window.SD_SEARCH=[{"u":"topics/advanced/data-structures-big-data.html","p":"Probabilistic Data Structures","i":"","h":"Overview","t":"Advanced Topics Probabilistic Data Structures for Big Data Trading a small, known error rate for huge memory savings — Bloom filters, HyperLogLog, Count-Min Sketch. 15-20 min Medium Say this in the first 2 minutes These exist because exact tracking at billion-event scale needs infeasible memory — a real hash set of 1B unique IDs costs tens of GB; HyperLogLog gets the same answer within ~0.81% error in ~12KB Each structure answers a different question: Bloom = \"have I seen this?\", HyperLogLog = \"how many unique things?\", Count-Min Sketch = \"how many times have I seen this?\" Bloom filters never produce false negatives, only false positives — \"definitely not present\" is always trustworthy, \"probably present\" needs a real lookup to confirm All three trade a small, bounded error for O(1) fixed memory instead of memory that grows with input size Name the concrete production use case immediately — Bloom filters in Cassandra/LSM-trees to skip disk reads, HLL in Redis for unique visitor counts, Count-Min Sketch for trending/heavy-hitter detection"},{"u":"topics/advanced/data-structures-big-data.html","p":"Probabilistic Data Structures","i":"mental-model","h":"Mental model","t":"Mental model These structures are like a bouncer with a bad memory for faces but a great memory for vibes — they can't tell you exactly who's inside, but they can say \"that person has definitely never been here\" with total confidence, using a notebook that never gets any bigger no matter how many people walk through the door."},{"u":"topics/advanced/data-structures-big-data.html","p":"Probabilistic Data Structures","i":"core-concepts","h":"Core concepts","t":"Core concepts Structure Question answered Memory profile Error characteristic Production use case Bloom filter Is this element possibly in the set? ~10 bits/element, fixed bit array False positives possible, false negatives impossible Cassandra/LevelDB SSTable lookups; CDN cache-miss avoidance HyperLogLog How many distinct elements have I seen? ~12KB fixed, regardless of input size ~0.81% standard error, both directions Redis PFCOUNT for unique visitors/DAU Count-Min Sketch Approximately how many times has X occurred? Small 2D array of counters (width x depth) One-directional overestimation only, never undercounts Top-K / trending hashtags, heavy-hitter detection in stream processing All three are write-once-read-many approximations — none of them support deletion cleanly (standard Bloom filters can't remove an element without risking false negatives for other elements that hash to the same bits)."},{"u":"topics/advanced/data-structures-big-data.html","p":"Probabilistic Data Structures","i":"how-it-works","h":"Bloom filter — bit array + multiple hashes","t":"Bloom filter — bit array + multiple hashes key: \"alice@...\" h1(k), h2(k), h3(k) → bit positions key: \"bob@...\" bit array, m=18 — 0 indexed left to right 1 1 1 1 1 query: \"carol@...\" all 3 bits already 1 → false positive Query \"dave@...\" → hashes to a 0 bit → \"definitely not present\" (always correct) Insert — set k bit positions to 1 per key, via k independent hash functions Query — if ANY bit is 0, the element is definitely absent; if all are 1, it's only probably present Allocate a fixed-size bit array of m bits, all initialized to 0, plus k independent hash functions. To insert a key, run it through all k hash functions and set each resulting bit position to 1. To query a key, run it through the same k hash functions — if any bit is 0, the key was definitely never inserted (no false negatives). If all k bits are 1, the key was probably inserted — but a collision from other keys hashing to the same bits can produce a false positive. Tune m (array size) and k (hash count) against expected element count to hit a target false-positive rate — more bits and more hashes lower the false-positive rate at the cost of memory and CPU. Key 'alice@example.com' is run through 3 independent hash functions Key 'bob@example.com' hashes to a different set of bit positions Bit flipped to 1 by hash function h1 of 'alice@...' Bit flipped to 1 by hash function h2 of 'bob@...' Bit flipped to 1 by hash function h2 of 'alice@...' Bit flipped to 1 by hash function h3 of 'bob@...' Bit flipped to 1 by hash function h3 of 'alice@...' Querying key 'carol@example.com' — all 3 of its hash positions happen to already be set to 1 by alice and bob so the filter reports a false positive even though carol was never inserted"},{"u":"topics/advanced/data-structures-big-data.html","p":"Probabilistic Data Structures","i":"hll-cms","h":"HyperLogLog and Count-Min Sketch — the other two","t":"HyperLogLog and Count-Min Sketch — the other two HyperLogLog Hashes each element, then tracks the longest run of leading zero bits seen across many buckets Longer zero-runs are exponentially rarer, so the longest observed run estimates how many distinct elements were hashed Redis's implementation uses ~12KB regardless of whether you've counted 1,000 or 1 billion unique items, at ~0.81% standard error Supports a merge operation — combine HLLs from multiple shards/days into one without re-scanning raw data Count-Min Sketch A small 2D array (width x depth); each row uses a different hash function Incrementing a key bumps one counter per row; querying a key takes the MINIMUM across all rows it maps to Taking the minimum cancels out most hash collisions — but the error is always an overestimate, never an undercount Used to find heavy hitters (top-K trending items) in an unbounded stream without storing a counter per unique item"},{"u":"topics/advanced/data-structures-big-data.html","p":"Probabilistic Data Structures","i":"tradeoffs","h":"Tradeoffs","t":"Tradeoffs Wins Fixed, predictable memory regardless of input cardinality — critical at billion-event scale O(1) insert and query time, independent of how many elements have been added Mergeable across shards/time windows without touching raw data again Error bounds are mathematically known in advance, not a surprise in production Costs Approximate by design — wrong tool if you need an exact, auditable count (e.g. billing) Standard Bloom filters can't delete elements without risking false negatives elsewhere Error compounds with poor hash function choice or under-sized structures — needs tuning against expected scale Debugging \"why did this say present when it shouldn't be\" is harder than with exact structures"},{"u":"topics/advanced/data-structures-big-data.html","p":"Probabilistic Data Structures","i":"when-to-use","h":"When to use which","t":"When to use which ✓ Use a Bloom filter as a pre-check before an expensive lookup — e.g. \"does this key possibly exist on disk?\" before hitting an SSTable, avoiding most unnecessary disk reads. ✓ Use HyperLogLog for \"how many unique X\" dashboards (DAU, unique IPs) where ~1% error is completely acceptable and exact counts would need gigabytes of hash sets. ✗ Avoid all three for anything requiring exact, auditable numbers — financial transaction counts, inventory levels, or anywhere a false positive/overestimate has real consequences."},{"u":"topics/advanced/data-structures-big-data.html","p":"Probabilistic Data Structures","i":"interview-moves","h":"Interview moves","t":"Interview moves 01 \"Exact unique-count over a billion events needs a real hash set — that's tens of GB. I'd reach for HyperLogLog instead, ~12KB for a ~0.81% error\" — justifies the tradeoff with concrete numbers 02 \"I'd put a Bloom filter in front of the disk lookup so 'definitely not present' never touches storage\" — names the exact LSM-tree/Cassandra pattern 03 \"No false negatives, only false positives — so a positive always needs a real lookup to confirm\" — shows precise understanding of the error direction 04 \"For trending hashtags I'd use a Count-Min Sketch — bounded memory, accepts slight overestimation, never undercounts a true heavy hitter\" — picks the right structure for the right question 05 \"These structures are mergeable — I can compute HLLs per shard and combine them at query time instead of re-scanning\" — shows awareness of distributed aggregation 06 \"I wouldn't use this for anything billing-related — approximate counts are wrong there by design\" — shows judgment about where NOT to apply the pattern"},{"u":"topics/advanced/data-structures-big-data.html","p":"Probabilistic Data Structures","i":"follow-ups","h":"Common follow-up questions","t":"Common follow-up questions Can you delete an item from a Bloom filter? Not from a standard one — clearing a bit could create false negatives for other keys sharing that bit. A Counting Bloom Filter (counters instead of bits) supports deletion at the cost of more memory. How does Redis implement HyperLogLog? PFADD / PFCOUNT / PFMERGE commands store a ~12KB sparse-then-dense encoded structure per key, giving unique counts at ~0.81% error with constant memory. Why does Count-Min Sketch only overestimate, never undercount? Taking the minimum across rows means a true count can only be inflated by hash collisions with other keys in some rows — it can never be deflated below the real value. How do you size a Bloom filter for a target false-positive rate? Use the standard formula relating bit array size (m), expected element count (n), and hash count (k) — more bits and an optimal k lower the false-positive probability; tune against expected n upfront. Why not just use a real HashSet if you have enough memory? You can — these structures only earn their complexity at scale where exact memory cost becomes the bottleneck (billions of keys) or where mergeability across distributed nodes matters. What's a concrete production example of each? Bloom: Cassandra/RocksDB skip-disk-read filters. HyperLogLog: Redis unique visitor counters, Google's original use for ad click dedup. Count-Min Sketch: streaming top-K trending detection (e.g. Twitter-scale hashtag trends)."},{"u":"topics/advanced/proximity-search.html","p":"Proximity Search","i":"","h":"Overview","t":"Advanced Topics Proximity Search How \"find nearby\" actually works at scale — geohashing, quadtrees, and R-trees. 15-20 min Medium Say this in the first 2 minutes Naive lat/lng range queries don't work — a degree of longitude shrinks toward the poles, so a fixed box isn't a fixed physical radius Geohashing turns 2D coordinates into a 1D string where shared prefix ≈ spatial proximity, so a normal B-tree index can do spatial range queries Geohash has a boundary problem — two points 10 meters apart across a cell edge can share zero prefix characters, so you always query neighboring cells too Quadtrees adapt to data density (fine-grained in cities, coarse in rural areas); R-trees group nearby objects into bounding boxes and are what production geo-databases like PostGIS actually use Always post-filter candidates with exact haversine distance — the index gets you \"probably close,\" not \"definitely within 5km\""},{"u":"topics/advanced/proximity-search.html","p":"Proximity Search","i":"mental-model","h":"Mental model","t":"Mental model Geohashing is like a postal address read left to right — \"USA, California, San Francisco, Mission St\" — each added character narrows the area, and two addresses sharing a long prefix are probably close together, even though the system has no idea what \"distance\" actually means."},{"u":"topics/advanced/proximity-search.html","p":"Proximity Search","i":"core-concepts","h":"Core concepts","t":"Core concepts Term Definition Geohash Base32 string encoding lat/lng; each extra char adds precision and shrinks the cell Prefix proximity Two geohashes sharing a long prefix are usually near each other (not guaranteed) Quadtree Recursively splits a square into 4 quadrants until each leaf holds few enough points R-tree Tree of nested minimum bounding rectangles (MBRs) grouping nearby geometries GiST index Postgres's generalized search tree — the index type PostGIS builds R-trees on top of Boundary problem Points near a cell edge can land in different cells with totally different prefixes Haversine distance Exact great-circle distance formula used to post-filter index candidates A 1° longitude box is ~111km wide at the equator but only ~78km wide at 45° latitude and 0km at the poles. Filtering with raw WHERE lng BETWEEN x AND y gives wildly inconsistent physical radii by latitude — this is why naive range queries fail."},{"u":"topics/advanced/proximity-search.html","p":"Proximity Search","i":"how-it-works","h":"Geohash cell subdivision","t":"Geohash cell subdivision LEVEL 1 — world split into a coarse 8x4 grid (1 char) 9 zoom in LEVEL 2 — cell '9' subdivided again (2 chars) 9q zoom in again — 4 more levels to \"9q8yyk\" (~3.7m precision) LEVEL 3 — fine cell, ~150m x 150m at 6 chars 9q8yyk 9q8yym ~30m apart, but split across a cell boundary Each level adds a base32 char and divides the cell again Boundary problem — adjacent points can land in sibling cells with different prefixes Encode lat/lng by interleaving binary subdivisions of latitude and longitude ranges into base32 characters. Each additional character roughly halves the cell's width and height — 1 char ≈ 5,000km, 6 chars ≈ 150m, 9 chars ≈ 5m. To find nearby points, query rows whose geohash starts with the user's prefix using a normal indexed LIKE 'prefix%' or range scan. Also query the 8 neighboring cells (geohash has a standard neighbor-calculation algorithm) to catch points split across a boundary. Post-filter every candidate with exact haversine distance and sort — the index narrows the field, it doesn't give exact distance. Top-level geohash cell '9' — roughly covers a quarter of North America at this resolution Geohash '9q' — covers roughly the US West Coast at 2-character precision Two restaurants 30m apart but on opposite sides of this cell edge — they get prefixes '9q8yyk' and '9q8yym' sharing only 5 of 6 characters despite being almost adjacent A point one cell over — looks unrelated by prefix alone even though it's physically next door"},{"u":"topics/advanced/proximity-search.html","p":"Proximity Search","i":"comparison","h":"Geohash vs quadtree vs R-tree","t":"Geohash vs quadtree vs R-tree Structure Precision control Query complexity Write cost Real-world usage Geohash Fixed steps via string length Simple prefix/range scan, needs neighbor lookups Cheap — just encode and insert Redis GEOADD / GEOSEARCH , Elasticsearch geo_point, Firestore Quadtree Adapts to point density automatically Tree traversal, naturally handles uneven density Moderate — may trigger node splits MongoDB 2d indexes (legacy), game engines, mapping tools R-tree Bounding boxes fit actual geometry, not a grid Best query performance for ranges/polygons, not just points Higher — box updates can cascade up the tree PostGIS (via GiST), most production spatial databases Redis specifically chose geohash because it can encode 2D location into a single 52-bit integer and reuse its existing sorted-set (ZSET) infrastructure — no new data structure needed. PostGIS chose R-trees/GiST because it already needed to index polygons and lines, not just points, where bounding boxes generalize better than a fixed grid."},{"u":"topics/advanced/proximity-search.html","p":"Proximity Search","i":"tradeoffs","h":"Tradeoffs","t":"Tradeoffs Wins Geohash reuses ordinary B-tree/string indexes — no custom spatial extension required Quadtrees self-adjust to density — dense cities get fine cells, empty deserts stay coarse R-trees give the tightest, most accurate candidate sets and support arbitrary shapes (polygons, routes) All three turn an O(n) full scan into a narrow indexed lookup Costs Geohash boundary problem means you must always check neighbor cells, adding query complexity Quadtree rebalancing on skewed inserts can be expensive and isn't trivially indexable in a normal RDBMS R-trees are the most complex to implement from scratch — you'll use a library/extension (PostGIS), not hand-roll one None of these give exact distance — every approach needs a post-filter pass"},{"u":"topics/advanced/proximity-search.html","p":"Proximity Search","i":"when-to-use","h":"When to use which","t":"When to use which ✓ Use geohash when you're building on Redis/Elasticsearch already, need \"nearby\" search with simple infra, and points (not polygons) are enough. ⚠ Watch out for uneven data density with a fixed-precision geohash — choose precision carefully, or pair with quadtree-style adaptive depth if density varies wildly (rural vs. downtown). ✗ Avoid hand-rolling R-trees yourself — use PostGIS, MongoDB geospatial indexes, or a managed geo-search service (Elasticsearch, Algolia) instead of building the tree from scratch."},{"u":"topics/advanced/proximity-search.html","p":"Proximity Search","i":"interview-moves","h":"Interview moves","t":"Interview moves 01 \"Naive lat/lng range filtering doesn't work — longitude degrees shrink toward the poles\" — shows you understand why this is a real problem, not just a lookup 02 \"I'd geohash the location so I can index it with a normal string prefix/B-tree, then range-query nearby prefixes\" — names the concrete mechanism 03 \"I'll also query the 8 neighbor cells to handle the boundary problem — two close points can hash totally differently near a cell edge\" — anticipates the classic gotcha 04 \"The index gets me candidates, not the answer — I post-filter with exact haversine distance and sort\" — clarifies precision vs. recall tradeoff 05 \"For polygons or routes I'd reach for PostGIS and R-trees instead — geohash is point-oriented\" — shows awareness of structure limits 06 \"At very large scale I'd shard by geohash prefix so nearby data colocates on the same node\" — connects geo-indexing to sharding strategy"},{"u":"topics/advanced/proximity-search.html","p":"Proximity Search","i":"follow-ups","h":"Common follow-up questions","t":"Common follow-up questions How do you pick geohash precision (string length)? Match it to your typical search radius — 6 chars (~150m cells) for \"restaurants nearby,\" 5 chars (~5km) for \"delivery zone.\" Shorter strings mean coarser cells and fewer rows scanned but more post-filtering. Why not just use a hash map keyed by rounded lat/lng? That's effectively a fixed-grid geohash with no prefix structure — you lose the ability to do range queries at multiple zoom levels with one index. How does Redis's GEO command work internally? It encodes lat/lng into a 52-bit geohash-like integer and stores it as the score in a sorted set (ZSET), reusing ZRANGEBYSCORE for proximity queries. How would you shard a geo-indexed dataset across multiple DB nodes? Shard by geohash prefix so physically nearby data lives on the same shard — keeps \"nearby\" queries from fanning out across every node. What's the actual distance error from using geohash prefix match alone? Unbounded in the worst case near a cell edge — two points meters apart can share zero prefix. That's exactly why neighbor-cell lookups plus an exact-distance post-filter are mandatory, not optional. When would a quadtree beat a geohash? When data density is extremely uneven (all points in cities, none elsewhere) — a quadtree naturally goes deep only where points cluster, while geohash precision is uniform everywhere by default."},{"u":"topics/advanced/time-series-databases.html","p":"Time-Series Databases","i":"","h":"Overview","t":"Advanced Topics Time-Series Databases Write-heavy, time-ordered data — downsampling, retention, and why metrics need their own database. 15-20 min Medium Say this in the first 2 minutes Time-series workloads are append-mostly, naturally ordered by time, and almost never updated after write — that shape enables compression a general-purpose OLTP DB can't exploit Row-per-event in a regular relational DB falls over at scale — index bloat and no time-aware compression mean storage and query cost grow linearly with every single data point Downsampling rolls up old high-resolution data into coarser aggregates over time — keep raw 1s data for days, 1min rollups for months, 1hr rollups forever Retention policies auto-expire raw data after a TTL, deleting old high-resolution rows once they've been downsampled or are no longer useful Cardinality explosion is the classic gotcha — too many unique tag/label combinations (e.g. tagging every request with a unique user ID) blows up a TSDB's index and kills performance"},{"u":"topics/advanced/time-series-databases.html","p":"Time-Series Databases","i":"mental-model","h":"Mental model","t":"Mental model A time-series database is like a security camera system that automatically degrades old footage from full HD to low-res thumbnails the further back in time you go — you keep perfect detail for \"what just happened\" and only a rough shape for \"what happened last year,\" because nobody needs frame-by-frame detail from six months ago."},{"u":"topics/advanced/time-series-databases.html","p":"Time-Series Databases","i":"core-concepts","h":"Core concepts","t":"Core concepts Term Definition Time-series data Timestamped, append-mostly data naturally ordered by time (metrics, sensor readings, logs) Downsampling Rolling up high-resolution old data into lower-resolution aggregates to save storage Retention policy Automatic expiry/deletion of raw data after a configured TTL Delta encoding Storing the difference between consecutive values instead of the full value — exploits slow-changing metrics Delta-of-delta Storing the change in the rate of change — exploits near-constant time intervals between points Cardinality Number of unique tag/label combinations — high cardinality blows up index size Tag/label Indexed metadata on a metric (host, region, endpoint) used for filtering and grouping Tagging every metric with a unique request ID or user ID turns a metric with a few hundred reasonable time series into millions — this is cardinality explosion, and it's the single most common way teams accidentally take down their own TSDB."},{"u":"topics/advanced/time-series-databases.html","p":"Time-Series Databases","i":"how-it-works","h":"Downsampling and retention tiers","t":"Downsampling and retention tiers TIME → (right edge = now) Raw — 1s resolution kept 7 days 1min rollup kept 90 days 1hr rollup kept forever downsample downsample retention TTL deletes raw >7d retention TTL deletes 1min rollup >90d storage cost stays bounded — total size doesn't grow unboundedly with time Recent data — full resolution for incident debugging Mid-term — downsampled for trend analysis Long-term — coarse rollups kept indefinitely at low cost Writes land at full resolution (e.g. 1s) in the hot tier — optimized for fast, append-only ingest. A background process periodically aggregates older raw points into coarser rollups (avg/min/max/p99 per minute, then per hour). Retention policies auto-delete each tier once it ages past its TTL and has already been captured in the next coarser tier. Queries automatically route to the appropriate tier — \"last 1 hour\" hits raw data, \"last 6 months\" hits hourly rollups — keeping query cost bounded regardless of time range. Delta and delta-of-delta encoding compress each tier further by storing changes instead of absolute values, since consecutive points and intervals barely change. Raw 1-second resolution data kept for 7 days — every individual data point retained for recent incident debugging 1-minute rollup aggregates (avg/min/max/p99 per minute) kept for 90 days — enough detail for weekly/monthly trend analysis 1-hour rollup aggregates kept forever — enough for year-over-year capacity planning at minimal storage cost Raw data older than the 7-day retention window is deleted entirely once it's been downsampled into the 1min tier 1min rollups older than 90 days are deleted once captured in the 1hr tier — storage cost stays bounded forever"},{"u":"topics/advanced/time-series-databases.html","p":"Time-Series Databases","i":"comparison","h":"Purpose-built TSDB vs general-purpose DB","t":"Purpose-built TSDB vs general-purpose DB Property TSDB (InfluxDB/TimescaleDB/Prometheus) General OLTP DB (Postgres/MySQL row-per-event) Compression Delta/delta-of-delta encoding — often 10-20x smaller Generic row storage — no time-aware compression Write pattern Optimized for high-throughput append-only ingest General-purpose, optimized for mixed read/write/update Query pattern Built-in \"last N hours aggregated by 1min\" time-bucket queries Manual GROUP BY + date_trunc, slower at scale Downsampling Built-in continuous aggregates / rollup policies Hand-rolled cron jobs and rollup tables Retention Built-in TTL/retention policies Hand-rolled delete jobs, risk of table bloat Index behavior at scale Time-partitioned, indexes stay small per partition Single growing index — bloats and slows down over time"},{"u":"topics/advanced/time-series-databases.html","p":"Time-Series Databases","i":"tradeoffs","h":"Tradeoffs","t":"Tradeoffs Wins Massive compression ratios from exploiting the time-ordered, slow-changing nature of metrics Built-in downsampling and retention remove a whole category of hand-rolled cron jobs Time-bucketed queries (e.g. Prometheus PromQL) are first-class, not bolted on Designed for the actual access pattern — append now, query recent at full detail, query old at low detail Costs Another specialized system to operate alongside your primary OLTP database Cardinality explosion from over-tagging can degrade performance badly and is easy to introduce by accident Not designed for arbitrary relational joins or transactional updates — wrong tool for general CRUD Downsampled historical data loses precision — can't recover exact raw values once rolled up and the raw tier expires"},{"u":"topics/advanced/time-series-databases.html","p":"Time-Series Databases","i":"when-to-use","h":"When to use","t":"When to use ✓ Use a TSDB for metrics, observability, IoT sensor data, or any workload that's overwhelmingly append-heavy, timestamp-ordered, and queried in time ranges/aggregates. ⚠ Watch out for cardinality explosion — never tag a metric with unbounded-cardinality fields like user ID or request ID; keep tags to bounded sets like host, region, or status code. ✗ Avoid a TSDB for general CRUD workloads needing relational joins, transactional updates, or strong consistency across unrelated entities — that's still a job for a regular OLTP database."},{"u":"topics/advanced/time-series-databases.html","p":"Time-Series Databases","i":"interview-moves","h":"Interview moves","t":"Interview moves 01 \"This is a metrics/observability workload — I'd reach for a purpose-built TSDB, not bolt time-series patterns onto a general OLTP DB\" — names the right tool immediately 02 \"Row-per-event in Postgres falls over here — no time-aware compression, and the index keeps growing forever\" — justifies the choice with a concrete failure mode 03 \"I'd set up tiered retention — raw at 1s for 7 days, 1min rollups for 90 days, hourly forever — so storage cost stays bounded\" — shows you can design the lifecycle, not just pick the DB 04 \"I'd watch cardinality carefully — tagging every event with a unique user or request ID would blow up the index\" — flags the most common real-world TSDB outage cause 05 \"Queries route by time range — recent queries hit raw data, historical queries hit rollups — so query cost doesn't scale with total retained history\" — connects downsampling to query performance directly 06 \"Delta-of-delta encoding works here because both the values and the time intervals between points barely change — that's specific to time-series, not general data\" — shows you understand why the compression works"},{"u":"topics/advanced/time-series-databases.html","p":"Time-Series Databases","i":"follow-ups","h":"Common follow-up questions","t":"Common follow-up questions Why does Postgres row-per-event fall over at scale specifically? No time-aware compression means storage grows linearly with raw events, and a single growing B-tree index degrades — TimescaleDB fixes this by automatically partitioning into time-based chunks. What exactly is cardinality explosion and why does it hurt? Each unique tag combination creates a separate indexed time series — millions of unique combinations (e.g. per-user tags) means millions of tiny series, blowing up index memory and query latency. How do continuous aggregates / rollup policies work? A background job periodically (or incrementally) computes aggregates like avg/p99 over fixed time buckets and stores them in a separate, much smaller rollup table or materialized view. What's lost when you downsample? Individual data points within a bucket — you can't recover the exact raw value once it's rolled into an aggregate and the raw tier's retention has expired. How would you handle a sudden spike in write throughput (e.g. IoT fleet doubling)? Shard by time + a bounded tag (e.g. device group), scale ingest nodes horizontally, and ensure the hot/raw tier's retention window is short enough to keep storage growth manageable. When would you NOT use Prometheus for metrics? When you need long-term, high-cardinality historical storage — Prometheus is built for short-term local retention; pair it with a remote-write long-term store (e.g. Thanos, Mimir) for that."},{"u":"topics/advanced/vector-databases.html","p":"Vector Databases","i":"","h":"Overview","t":"Advanced Topics Vector Databases Approximate nearest neighbor search over embeddings — the retrieval engine behind RAG. 15-20 min Medium Say this in the first 2 minutes Embeddings are dense vectors (often 768-1536 dimensions) where semantic similarity becomes geometric distance — \"nearby vectors mean similar meaning\" Exact nearest-neighbor search is O(n) per query and doesn't scale — ANN trades a small recall loss for orders-of-magnitude speedup HNSW is the dominant production ANN algorithm — a multi-layer graph where search descends from sparse long-range edges to dense local edges RAG exists because LLMs have a fixed context window and no built-in access to your private/current data — retrieval injects relevant facts at query time instead of fine-tuning The recall/latency knob is real and interview-relevant — wider search (higher ef_search) means better recall at the cost of latency, tune per use case"},{"u":"topics/advanced/vector-databases.html","p":"Vector Databases","i":"mental-model","h":"Mental model","t":"Mental model A vector database is a library where books aren't sorted by title or author, but placed physically close to other books about similar topics — you don't search by exact match, you walk to the right neighborhood on the shelf and grab everything nearby."},{"u":"topics/advanced/vector-databases.html","p":"Vector Databases","i":"core-concepts","h":"Core concepts","t":"Core concepts Term Definition Embedding Dense numeric vector from an ML model representing semantic meaning Cosine similarity Common distance metric — angle between two vectors, ignores magnitude kNN Exact k-nearest-neighbor search — brute-force compares the query to every vector ANN Approximate nearest neighbor — trades small recall loss for massive speed gains HNSW Hierarchical Navigable Small World graph — layered graph index, the dominant ANN algorithm ef_search Tunable parameter controlling how many candidates HNSW explores — higher = better recall, slower RAG Retrieval-Augmented Generation — fetch relevant context, inject into the LLM prompt before generation ANN/HNSW search — leans heavily toward speed over perfect exactness Exact (100% recall) Fast (95-99% recall)"},{"u":"topics/advanced/vector-databases.html","p":"Vector Databases","i":"how-it-works","h":"RAG pipeline with HNSW retrieval","t":"RAG pipeline with HNSW retrieval INDEX BUILD (offline) Knowledge base docs Embedding model HNSW index Pinecone/pgvector QUERY TIME (online) User query text Embedding model HNSW search top-K ANN Top-K chunks retrieved context Prompt assembly query + context → LLM LLM answer HNSW: top layer = sparse, long jumps. bottom layer = dense, local refine. Same embedding model must encode both docs and queries into one shared vector space HNSW index — built once offline, queried with low latency online Retrieved chunks ground the LLM in real data instead of pure model memory Offline: chunk source documents, embed each chunk, and insert vectors into an HNSW index (built incrementally, layer by layer). Online: embed the incoming user query with the same model so it lands in the same vector space as the indexed documents. HNSW search starts at the sparse top layer, takes long greedy hops toward the query vector, then drops down a layer and refines — like coarse-to-fine search. The bottom layer is dense and local — final hops here produce the approximate top-K nearest neighbors. Retrieved chunks are concatenated with the user's question into a single prompt, handed to the LLM to generate a grounded answer. Source documents — PDFs docs support tickets internal wiki pages Embedding model (e.g. OpenAI ada Cohere) converts each chunk of text into a dense vector Vectors inserted into an HNSW graph — multi-layer structure built once queried many times User asks a natural-language question Same embedding model encodes the query into the identical vector space as the indexed documents HNSW descends from sparse top layer to dense bottom layer returning the top-K nearest vectors approximately not exactly The K nearest document chunks retrieved as raw text to ground the LLM's answer in real source material Query + retrieved chunks assembled into a single prompt — grounds the model in real current data instead of relying on parametric memory LLM generates an answer grounded in the retrieved context reducing hallucination vs. answering from memory alone"},{"u":"topics/advanced/vector-databases.html","p":"Vector Databases","i":"comparison","h":"Exact kNN vs ANN/HNSW","t":"Exact kNN vs ANN/HNSW Property Exact kNN (brute force) ANN / HNSW Recall 100% — always correct top-K ~95-99%, tunable via ef_search Query latency O(n) — scans every vector O(log n)-ish — graph traversal, sub-millisecond to low-ms at scale Memory Just the raw vectors Vectors + graph edges — typically 1.5-2x raw vector size Index build cost None — no index to build Nontrivial — insertion builds graph layers, slower writes Scales to Thousands to low millions before too slow Billions of vectors in production"},{"u":"topics/advanced/vector-databases.html","p":"Vector Databases","i":"tradeoffs","h":"Tradeoffs","t":"Tradeoffs Wins Sub-linear query time at massive scale — the only way ANN search stays fast past millions of vectors ef_search gives a tunable recall/latency knob per query without rebuilding the index Purpose-built vector DBs (Pinecone, Weaviate, Qdrant) handle sharding, filtering, and hybrid search out of the box pgvector lets you keep vectors next to relational data when you don't need dedicated-DB scale Costs Approximate by design — a small fraction of true nearest neighbors can be missed HNSW graph memory overhead on top of raw vectors adds real infra cost at billions of vectors Index build/insert is slower than a flat array — not ideal for extremely high-churn data A dedicated vector DB is one more system to operate, secure, and keep in sync with the source of truth"},{"u":"topics/advanced/vector-databases.html","p":"Vector Databases","i":"when-to-use","h":"When to use","t":"When to use ✓ Use a dedicated vector DB when you're doing RAG, semantic search, or recommendation at scale (millions+ of vectors) and need fast, tunable ANN with metadata filtering. ⚠ Watch out for stale embeddings — if source documents change, you must re-embed and re-index, or RAG answers will be grounded in outdated context. ✗ Avoid a dedicated vector DB for small datasets (tens of thousands of vectors) — pgvector inside your existing Postgres, or even brute-force exact kNN, is simpler and fast enough."},{"u":"topics/advanced/vector-databases.html","p":"Vector Databases","i":"interview-moves","h":"Interview moves","t":"Interview moves 01 \"Exact nearest-neighbor search is O(n) per query — at millions of vectors that's too slow, so I'd reach for ANN via HNSW\" — justifies the approximation upfront 02 \"HNSW is a layered graph — sparse long-range edges at the top for fast coarse jumps, dense local edges at the bottom for fine refinement\" — shows you understand the mechanism, not just the name 03 \"ef_search is my recall/latency knob — I'd tune it per use case, wider for accuracy-critical paths, narrower for low-latency autocomplete-style search\" — names the actual tunable parameter 04 \"RAG exists because the LLM's context window is finite and its training data goes stale — retrieval grounds answers in current, real data instead of hallucinated memory\" — explains the why, not just the what 05 \"At our scale I'd justify a dedicated vector DB over pgvector — once we're past low millions of vectors, purpose-built sharding and ANN tuning start to matter\" — ties infra choice to scale, not default preference 06 \"Query and document embeddings must come from the same model version — I'd version-pin it and re-embed on model upgrades\" — anticipates a real production gotcha"},{"u":"topics/advanced/vector-databases.html","p":"Vector Databases","i":"follow-ups","h":"Common follow-up questions","t":"Common follow-up questions What happens if you increase ef_search? HNSW explores more candidates per layer, increasing recall toward exact kNN at the cost of higher query latency — it's a runtime knob, no reindex needed. How do you keep a RAG index fresh when source documents change? Re-embed changed documents and upsert into the index; for high-churn data, batch updates on a schedule rather than per-write to avoid index thrashing. Why not just fine-tune the LLM on your data instead of RAG? Fine-tuning is expensive, slow to update, and doesn't cite sources. RAG updates instantly by re-indexing and lets you point to the exact retrieved source for grounding. How do you combine vector search with traditional filters (e.g. \"only docs from 2024\")? Hybrid search — most vector DBs support metadata filtering alongside ANN search, pruning the candidate graph traversal to only matching metadata. What's the failure mode when embeddings are low quality? Semantically similar text won't cluster correctly, so retrieval returns irrelevant chunks — garbage in, garbage out; embedding model choice matters as much as the index. pgvector vs Pinecone — how do you decide? pgvector for smaller scale or when you want vectors colocated with relational data and one less system to operate. Pinecone/Weaviate/Qdrant once you need dedicated ANN performance at large scale with built-in sharding."},{"u":"topics/common-patterns.html","p":"Common Patterns","i":"","h":"Overview","t":"Foundations Common Patterns The 8 recurring architectural patterns that appear across almost every system design question. 15 min read Patterns Concept Map click to expand Pattern recognition cues Read-heavy (>10:1 R:W) → cache aggressively, read replicas, CDN Write-heavy (>10k/sec) → async writes, Kafka buffer, NoSQL \"Notify all followers\" → fan-out (push vs pull — decide at interview time) \"Never lose a transaction\" → saga pattern, idempotent retries, outbox \"Different read/write shapes\" → CQRS — separate read and write models Patterns Read-heavy Cache aggressively add read replicas put a CDN in front read-heavy Cache-aside Miss falls through backfills cache Read replicas Scale reads horizontally Write-heavy Buffer writes async batch to the DB write-heavy Kafka buffer Absorbs write spikes Batching Fewer larger DB writes Fan-out Push vs pull tradeoff for notifying many consumers fan-out Fan-out on write Push at write time fast reads Fan-out on read Pull at read time cheap for inactive users Saga Multi-step transaction with compensating actions on failure saga Orchestration Central coordinator drives steps Compensation Undo prior steps on failure CQRS Separate models for reads and writes when shapes diverge cqrs Write model Optimized for consistency Read model Denormalized for query speed Consistent Hashing Distributes keys so node changes remap minimal data tradeoffs Hash ring Keys map to the next node clockwise Virtual nodes Smooths load across heterogeneous machines"},{"u":"topics/common-patterns.html","p":"Common Patterns","i":"mental-model","h":"Mental model","t":"Mental model Patterns are the recurring moves in chess — you don't invent them mid-game, you recognize the position and apply the right pattern. Name the pattern out loud in the interview; it signals experience."},{"u":"topics/common-patterns.html","p":"Common Patterns","i":"read-heavy","h":"Pattern 1 — Read-heavy system","t":"Pattern 1 — Read-heavy system Triggered by: news feed, product catalog, URL redirect, DNS, CDN Client CDN / Redis hot path miss Read replica DB sync (~100ms lag) Primary DB source of truth Client / gateway Cache — hot path Database — source of truth Lever Effect Redis cache Serves hot keys in ~1ms; reduce DB load by 80-90% Read replicas Distributes read QPS across N nodes; adds ~100ms replication lag CDN Caches responses at edge; cross-region reads drop from ~100ms to ~5ms Denormalization Pre-compute joins into a single document; eliminates join overhead at read time"},{"u":"topics/common-patterns.html","p":"Common Patterns","i":"write-heavy","h":"Pattern 2 — Write-heavy system","t":"Pattern 2 — Write-heavy system Triggered by: metrics ingestion, log collection, IoT telemetry, click tracking Clients high volume Kafka write buffer Consumers batch write ClickHouse / Cassandra Client / gateway Database — source of truth Async — queue / worker Never write directly to a relational DB at >10k writes/sec. Buffer in Kafka, batch into a columnar store, or use Cassandra with a write-optimized LSM tree."},{"u":"topics/common-patterns.html","p":"Common Patterns","i":"fan-out","h":"Pattern 3 — Fan-out (notification / feed)","t":"Pattern 3 — Fan-out (notification / feed) Triggered by: Twitter-style feed, notification system, group chat Fan-out on write (push) When user posts, write to each follower's feed immediately Read is O(1) — precomputed feed Cost: write amplification — 1 post × 10M followers = 10M writes Use for: accounts with <~10k followers Fan-out on read (pull) When user opens feed, pull latest posts from all followed accounts Write is cheap — only 1 write per post Cost: read is expensive — merge N timelines on each load Use for: celebrity accounts with millions of followers Production answer: hybrid. Push for normal users, pull for mega-accounts (>1M followers). Twitter uses this exact approach."},{"u":"topics/common-patterns.html","p":"Common Patterns","i":"saga","h":"Pattern 4 — Saga (distributed transactions)","t":"Pattern 4 — Saga (distributed transactions) Triggered by: payment + inventory + order all needing to succeed or all roll back Order Service Payment Service Inventory Service Confirm compensating rollback on any failure Step: Each service completes its local transaction and publishes a success event Compensating transaction: If any step fails, each previous service executes a rollback operation (e.g., refund payment) Choreography vs orchestration: Choreography = services react to events (decoupled). Orchestration = a saga coordinator calls each service in order. Saga only gives eventual consistency — not ACID. Compensating transactions are complex to write correctly. If you need true atomicity, put everything in one DB."},{"u":"topics/common-patterns.html","p":"Common Patterns","i":"cqrs","h":"Pattern 5 — CQRS (Command Query Responsibility Segregation)","t":"Pattern 5 — CQRS (Command Query Responsibility Segregation) Triggered by: write and read shapes are very different; scale needs differ between reads and writes Write Write DB normalized async Read DB denormalized Read Client / gateway Cache — hot path Example: e-commerce writes normalized order data to Postgres; an async job denormalizes it into Elasticsearch for flexible search queries. Read and write models evolve independently."},{"u":"topics/common-patterns.html","p":"Common Patterns","i":"event-sourcing","h":"Pattern 6 — Event sourcing","t":"Pattern 6 — Event sourcing Triggered by: audit log required, need to replay state, financial ledger Concept Description Event log Store every state change as an immutable event — never update/delete Current state Computed by replaying events from the beginning (or from a snapshot) Snapshot Periodic materialized state to avoid replaying 10M events every read Projection A read model built by consuming the event log — one per query shape Event sourcing is powerful but complex. Only introduce it when audit trail or replay is a hard requirement. The default is CRUD — don't over-engineer."},{"u":"topics/common-patterns.html","p":"Common Patterns","i":"outbox","h":"Pattern 7 — Transactional outbox","t":"Pattern 7 — Transactional outbox Triggered by: must publish a message to Kafka reliably after a DB write — without dual-write risk single DB transaction Write to DB + Outbox table Polling relay reads outbox Publish to Kafka Cache — hot path Async — queue / worker Solves the dual-write problem: if you write to DB then publish to Kafka, you can't guarantee both succeed. Outbox makes the message part of the DB transaction — guaranteed to be published eventually."},{"u":"topics/common-patterns.html","p":"Common Patterns","i":"tradeoffs","h":"Pattern 8 — Consistent hashing","t":"Pattern 8 — Consistent hashing Triggered by: distributing keys across a cache cluster or sharded DB + Add node N5 Reset ring Node on the ring Key — stable, unaffected Key — remapped to the new node Click \"Add node N5\" — only keys in N5's new arc remap. Every other key stays put. Consistent hashing wins Adding/removing a node only remaps K/n keys (K=total, n=nodes) No global rehash — cache stampedes avoided Virtual nodes distribute load more evenly across heterogeneous machines Naive modulo hashing Adding 1 node remaps ~50% of all keys Cache stampede: all remapped keys are cache misses simultaneously Simple to implement — fine for static cluster size"},{"u":"topics/common-patterns.html","p":"Common Patterns","i":"when-to-use","h":"When to reach for each pattern","t":"When to reach for each pattern ✓ Use read-heavy/write-heavy patterns first: they're the cheapest fix and apply to almost every question — check the read:write ratio before anything else. ✓ Use saga + outbox together for money or inventory: any multi-service write that must never partially fail needs both compensating actions and reliable event delivery. ⚠ Watch out for CQRS overkill: only split read/write models when the shapes genuinely diverge — adding it \"for scale\" without a real shape mismatch is over-engineering. ✗ Avoid consistent hashing until resharding is a real pain point: a fixed shard count with modulo hashing is fine until you actually need to add/remove nodes live."},{"u":"topics/common-patterns.html","p":"Common Patterns","i":"interview-moves","h":"Interview moves","t":"Interview moves 01 \"This is a read-heavy system — I'd cache aggressively in Redis and add read replicas before considering any partitioning\" — name the pattern, state the implication 02 \"For the feed, I'd use fan-out on write for most users and fan-out on read for celebrity accounts above 1M followers — same hybrid Twitter uses\" — shows awareness of the tradeoff and real-world precedent 03 \"Across these three services I can't use a single DB transaction — I'd use the Saga pattern with compensating transactions for rollback\" — naming Saga explicitly signals distributed systems maturity 04 \"To guarantee the Kafka publish happens, I'd use the transactional outbox pattern — the event is written to a DB table in the same transaction as the state change\" — this is a differentiating answer most candidates don't know 05 \"For the cache cluster I'd use consistent hashing — adding nodes only remaps a small fraction of keys, avoiding stampedes\" — shows you know why, not just what"},{"u":"topics/common-patterns.html","p":"Common Patterns","i":"follow-ups","h":"Common follow-up questions","t":"Common follow-up questions When does fan-out on write break down? When a user has millions of followers — writing to 10M feeds per post creates unacceptable write amplification and latency. Switch to pull (fan-out on read) for accounts above a threshold (~500k followers). What's the difference between Saga choreography and orchestration? Choreography: services emit events and react to each other's events — no central controller, harder to trace. Orchestration: a saga coordinator calls each service in sequence — easier to observe but adds a single coordinator dependency. Is CQRS always worth the complexity? No. CQRS adds a second data model, async sync lag, and operational overhead. Use it when: read/write load is asymmetric by 100x, or when the read and write schemas genuinely diverge. For most CRUD apps, it's overkill. What's the difference between event sourcing and an audit log? An audit log is append-only but doesn't necessarily derive current state from events. Event sourcing makes the event log the source of truth — current state is always derived from replaying events. Much higher complexity. How do you handle the outbox polling delay? Polling interval is typically 100ms-1s — acceptable for most async workflows. For lower latency, use Debezium (CDC) which streams the DB transaction log directly to Kafka without polling. What are virtual nodes in consistent hashing? Each physical node maps to multiple points on the hash ring. This distributes load more evenly and allows heterogeneous nodes (stronger machines get more virtual nodes). Standard in Cassandra and Redis Cluster."},{"u":"topics/core-concepts.html","p":"Core Concepts","i":"","h":"Overview","t":"Foundations Core Concepts CAP theorem, consistency models, latency numbers, and back-of-envelope math — the vocabulary of every system design interview. 15 min read High-density Concept Map click to expand Say these with confidence CAP: you can only guarantee 2 of 3 — and during a partition, you choose CP or AP Eventual consistency: reads may return stale data; all replicas converge given time 1ms L1 cache → 100ms cross-datacenter — a 5-order-of-magnitude difference 10M DAU × 1 write/day ÷ 86,400 = ~115 writes/sec — memorize this formula A single machine handles ~10k concurrent connections; a single DB ~10k QPS Core Concepts CAP Theorem Pick 2 of Consistency Availability Partition tolerance core-concepts CP Consistent but can reject requests during a partition AP Always available may serve stale data Consistency How fresh a read is guaranteed to be consistency-models Strong Always returns the latest write Eventual Converges to latest given enough time Latency Order-of-magnitude cost of each operation type latency-numbers Memory vs disk ~100x latency gap Network hop Cross-datacenter dominates tail latency Estimation Back-of-envelope math that justifies every design decision how-it-works QPS math DAU ÷ seconds/day × actions/user Storage math Record size × writes/day × retention Scaling Vertical vs horizontal and the mechanisms that enable it tradeoffs Sharding Split data across nodes by key Replication Copy data for read scale and durability"},{"u":"topics/core-concepts.html","p":"Core Concepts","i":"mental-model","h":"Mental model","t":"Mental model Core concepts are the grammar of distributed systems. You don't need to quote papers — you need to use the words correctly when tradeoffs come up in your design."},{"u":"topics/core-concepts.html","p":"Core Concepts","i":"core-concepts","h":"CAP theorem","t":"CAP theorem C Consistency A Availability P Partition tol. click a corner — P is non-negotiable Click C, A, or P above See which two are guaranteed, what's sacrificed, and a real system that made that choice. Every read returns latest write, or errors Every request gets a non-error response Survives a network split between nodes In production, P is non-negotiable (networks always partition). So the real choice is: during a partition, do you return stale data (AP) or return an error (CP)? CP — financial systems, inventory, seat reservations CP: error over stale AP: stale over error AP — social feeds, caches, DNS, search indexes CP: error over stale AP: stale over error"},{"u":"topics/core-concepts.html","p":"Core Concepts","i":"consistency-models","h":"Consistency models (spectrum)","t":"Consistency models (spectrum) Strong consistency — every read sees latest write, always Strong (slow, safe) Eventual (fast, stale) Read-your-writes — you always see your own writes Strong (slow, safe) Eventual (fast, stale) Causal consistency — causally related ops appear in order Strong (slow, safe) Eventual (fast, stale) Monotonic reads — once seen, never see an older value Strong (slow, safe) Eventual (fast, stale) Eventual consistency — replicas converge given enough time Strong (slow, safe) Eventual (fast, stale)"},{"u":"topics/core-concepts.html","p":"Core Concepts","i":"latency-numbers","h":"Latency numbers every engineer must know","t":"Latency numbers every engineer must know L1 cache read ~1 ns L2 cache read ~4 ns RAM read ~100 ns Intra-DC round trip ~500 µs SSD random read ~100 µs Same-region, diff AZ ~1-2 ms HDD seek ~10 ms Cross-region (US-EU) ~100 ms Cross-continent ~200 ms Practical implication: Redis (in-memory) serves requests in ~1ms. A cold DB read from SSD takes ~10ms. Cross-region replication lag is ~100ms. These numbers justify every caching decision."},{"u":"topics/core-concepts.html","p":"Core Concepts","i":"how-it-works","h":"Back-of-envelope estimation","t":"Back-of-envelope estimation 86,400 seconds per day ~100k seconds per day (rounded) 10K max QPS per single DB 100K QPS per Redis instance Calculation Formula Example (10M DAU) Write QPS DAU × writes/user/day ÷ 86,400 10M × 1 ÷ 86,400 ≈ 115/s Read QPS Write QPS × R:W ratio 115 × 10 = 1,150/s Daily storage Write QPS × avg size × 86,400 115 × 1KB × 86,400 ≈ 10GB/day Yearly storage Daily storage × 365 10GB × 365 ≈ 3.5TB/yr Bandwidth QPS × avg request size 1,150 × 1KB = 1.15MB/s (trivial)"},{"u":"topics/core-concepts.html","p":"Core Concepts","i":"tradeoffs","h":"Scaling concepts","t":"Scaling concepts Vertical (≤100k QPS, use first) vs horizontal scaling (beyond that, or for HA) Vertical: bigger box Horizontal: more boxes Vertical scaling (scale up) Bigger machine — more CPU, RAM, disk Simple: no code changes needed Hard limit: largest machine available Single point of failure Good up to ~100k QPS; use it first Horizontal scaling (scale out) More machines behind a load balancer Complex: stateless services required No hard limit — add nodes as needed Higher operational overhead Required beyond ~100k QPS or HA Concept What it means Sharding Split DB rows across nodes by a key (user_id, region). Avoids hot spots. Complicates joins. Replication Copy data to multiple nodes. Primary handles writes; replicas handle reads. Consistent hashing Distribute keys across nodes such that adding/removing a node minimizes remapping Load balancing Distribute traffic across servers — L4 (TCP) or L7 (HTTP, content-aware) Rate limiting Cap requests per client per time window — token bucket or sliding window Circuit breaker Fail fast if a dependency is degraded — prevents cascading failures Idempotency Same request, same result if repeated — critical for retries in distributed systems"},{"u":"topics/core-concepts.html","p":"Core Concepts","i":"when-to-use","h":"Applying these concepts in an interview","t":"Applying these concepts in an interview ✓ State your CAP choice early: once you know AP vs CP, every downstream tradeoff (cache TTLs, replica reads, conflict handling) follows from that one decision. ✓ Anchor every capacity claim to a number: \"no sharding needed\" only lands if you've shown the QPS math that justifies it. ⚠ Watch for scale cues from the interviewer: \"assume 100M users\" versus \"assume 10K users\" should visibly change whether you reach for horizontal scaling at all. ✗ Don't reach for strong consistency by default: justify it against the specific feature — most consumer-facing reads tolerate eventual consistency fine."},{"u":"topics/core-concepts.html","p":"Core Concepts","i":"interview-moves","h":"Interview moves","t":"Interview moves 01 \"Given eventual consistency here, the read might be ~100ms stale — that's acceptable for a social feed but not for a payment\" — shows you connect consistency models to real scenarios 02 \"Back of envelope: 10M DAU, 1 write/day, gives ~115 writes/sec — single DB handles this easily\" — always say the implication, not just the number 03 \"Redis serves this in ~1ms vs a DB read at ~10ms — 10x faster, worth the operational cost for this hot path\" — justify cache decisions with latency numbers 04 \"This is an AP system — during a partition we serve stale data rather than error out\" — stating CAP preference explicitly shows distributed systems fluency"},{"u":"topics/core-concepts.html","p":"Core Concepts","i":"follow-ups","h":"Common follow-up questions","t":"Common follow-up questions When would you choose strong consistency over availability? Any time a stale read causes a wrong business decision: inventory (overselling), payments (double charge), seat reservations. For everything else — social, search, analytics — eventual is fine. What is PACELC and how does it extend CAP? PACELC says: even when there's no partition (E), you still trade off latency (L) vs consistency (C). It captures the everyday tradeoff that CAP only covers during failure. Mention it if the interviewer is probing deep. How do you handle hotspot keys in a sharded system? Add a random suffix to the key to distribute across shards (shard spreading), then aggregate reads. Or use consistent hashing with virtual nodes to rebalance load automatically. What's the difference between a cache and a replica? A replica is a consistent copy of the DB — writes go there, lag is bounded. A cache is a best-effort snapshot — eviction can make it stale, and writes don't necessarily go through it. Why does consistent hashing matter for distributed caches? With naive modulo hashing, adding 1 node remaps ~N/nodes keys — causing a cache stampede. Consistent hashing remaps only ~K/n keys (K = total keys, n = nodes added), minimizing disruption. What's the difference between rate limiting algorithms? Token bucket: smooth traffic, allows bursts up to bucket size. Sliding window: strict — counts requests in the last N seconds. Fixed window: simple but allows 2x burst at window boundaries. Token bucket is the most common production choice."},{"u":"topics/delivery-framework.html","p":"Delivery Framework","i":"","h":"Overview","t":"Foundations Delivery Framework The exact step-by-step structure to use in every system design interview — learn it once, use it every time. 12 min read Most important page Concept Map click to expand The 5-phase framework (memorize this) 1. Clarify — requirements, users, scale, exclusions (5 min) 2. Estimate — QPS, storage, bandwidth back-of-envelope (3 min) 3. API design — 3-4 core endpoints, request/response shape (5 min) 4. High-level design — components, data flow, data model (15 min) 5. Deep dive — bottlenecks, failure modes, scale tactics (15 min) Interview Framework Requirements Clarify functional + non-functional scope before designing anything mental-model Functional What the system must do Non-functional Scale latency availability targets Entities The core data objects and how they relate core-concepts Primary keys What uniquely identifies each entity Relationships Foreign keys and ownership API 3-4 endpoints that define the client contract api-design Request/response Shape of the payloads Auth model Who can call what Data Flow How a request moves through the system end to end how-it-works Read path Usually cache-first Write path Usually DB-first async fan-out after HLD The component diagram — services DB cache queues tradeoffs Components What boxes to draw and why Tech choices Justify SQL vs NoSQL cache etc. Deep Dive Go deep on the one thing the interviewer cares about when-to-use Bottlenecks Where the system breaks first at scale Failure modes What happens when a component dies"},{"u":"topics/delivery-framework.html","p":"Delivery Framework","i":"mental-model","h":"Mental model","t":"Mental model The framework is your GPS. You might deviate based on traffic (interviewer cues), but you always know where you are in the journey — and so does your interviewer. 1. Clarify 5 min 2. Estimate 3 min 3. API 5 min 4. High-level 15 min 5. Deep dive 15 min Client / gateway Cache — hot path Functional requirements users scale exclusions — get this wrong and everything downstream is wasted effort Back-of-envelope QPS storage bandwidth — these numbers justify every architecture decision you make next Define the core endpoints/contracts — forces precision about what the system actually exposes Draw the core components and data flow at a level everyone in the room can follow before zooming in Interviewer picks the hardest part — go deep on bottlenecks failure modes and tradeoffs here"},{"u":"topics/delivery-framework.html","p":"Delivery Framework","i":"core-concepts","h":"Phase 1 — Clarify (5 min)","t":"Phase 1 — Clarify (5 min) Ask about Example question Core feature \"Is this read-heavy or write-heavy? What's the primary user action?\" Users \"Are we building for consumers, businesses, or internal teams?\" Scale \"How many DAU should I assume? 1M, 10M, 100M?\" Exclusions \"Should I design auth, payments, and notifications — or focus on the core flow?\" SLA \"Is strong consistency required, or is eventual consistency acceptable?\" Geography \"Single-region or globally distributed?\" Stop after 5 minutes even if you haven't asked everything. State your assumptions and move forward. Over-clarifying is a time management red flag."},{"u":"topics/delivery-framework.html","p":"Delivery Framework","i":"how-it-works","h":"Phase 2 — Estimate (3 min)","t":"Phase 2 — Estimate (3 min) 10M DAU baseline ~115 writes/sec (10M × 1 write/day) ~1150 reads/sec (10:1 read:write) ~365GB storage/yr (1KB × 10M × 365) DAU ÷ 86,400 Write QPS × R:W ratio Read QPS Storage/yr size × QPS × 86400 × 365 Client / gateway Cache — hot path These numbers drive your decisions. A system at 100 writes/sec needs no sharding. At 100k writes/sec, you need sharding, Kafka, and a dedicated write path. Say this out loud. Start from daily active users — every other estimate in this chain derives from this one number Divide DAU by seconds/day to get average write throughput — tells you whether sharding is even on the table Apply the read:write ratio (often 10:1+) — read QPS usually dominates and drives caching decisions Multiply record size by writes/day by 365 — surfaces whether you need cold storage tiers or partitioning by age"},{"u":"topics/delivery-framework.html","p":"Delivery Framework","i":"api-design","h":"Phase 3 — API design (5 min)","t":"Phase 3 — API design (5 min) Define 3-4 core endpoints. RESTful by default unless the problem demands otherwise (streaming, bidirectional = WebSockets). What to define Example (URL Shortener) Method + path POST /urls Request body { long_url, custom_alias?, expires_at? } Response { short_url, short_code, expires_at } Auth Bearer token in header; anonymous for redirect endpoint API design shows you think from the client's perspective first. It also constraints your data model — define it early."},{"u":"topics/delivery-framework.html","p":"Delivery Framework","i":"tradeoffs","h":"Phase 4 — High-level design","t":"Phase 4 — High-level design Draw on the whiteboard while narrating. Cover these in order: Components to cover Client + load balancer + API layer Core service(s) and their responsibilities Database(s) — type, schema, why Cache layer — what's cached, eviction policy Async components (queues, workers) if needed Common omissions Forgetting to specify the data model Not saying why you chose SQL vs NoSQL Skipping the cache entirely Not labeling the read path vs write path Drawing components without explaining data flow"},{"u":"topics/delivery-framework.html","p":"Delivery Framework","i":"when-to-use","h":"Phase 5 — Deep dive","t":"Phase 5 — Deep dive ✓ Proactively identify bottlenecks: \"The hot path here is X — if this gets 100x traffic, here's what breaks and how I'd fix it.\" ✓ Cover failure modes: \"What happens if the DB goes down? If the cache is cold? If a downstream service is slow?\" Mention detection + recovery for each. ⚠ Follow the interviewer's lead: If they say \"tell me more about the DB design\", drop everything else and go deep. They're telling you where they want to probe. ✗ Don't go wide here: The deep dive is for depth on one component, not adding more components to your diagram. One thing, done well."},{"u":"topics/delivery-framework.html","p":"Delivery Framework","i":"interview-moves","h":"Interview moves","t":"Interview moves 01 \"Let me start by clarifying a few things before I design anything\" — say this verbatim as your first sentence, every single interview 02 \"I'll assume 10M DAU — quick math gives me ~115 writes/sec, so no sharding needed initially\" — shows you connect estimates to design decisions 03 \"Let me define the API first — it'll constrain my data model\" — interviewers love this sequencing; it shows design discipline 04 \"I'm going to draw the write path first, then the read path separately\" — separating paths shows clarity; most systems have different optimization needs per path 05 \"Before I go further — is there a component you'd like me to go deeper on?\" — check in at the 25-min mark; lets the interviewer steer the deep dive 06 \"The main bottleneck I see is [X]. Here's how I'd handle it at 10x scale...\" — proactive bottleneck identification is a strong senior signal"},{"u":"topics/delivery-framework.html","p":"Delivery Framework","i":"follow-ups","h":"Common follow-up questions","t":"Common follow-up questions What if the interviewer interrupts my framework flow? Follow them. The framework is your default path, not a script. If they say \"skip ahead to the DB design\", do it. You can always say \"I'll circle back to X if we have time.\" Do I need to cover all 5 phases in every interview? Clarify and estimate are always required. The interviewer may shortcut others. Watch for \"that's fine, let's move on\" — it means they're satisfied and want to go deeper elsewhere. How detailed should the data model be? Primary entities, key fields, and the main foreign key / partition key. Don't model every column. State your storage choice and justify it. That's enough at the high-level phase. What's the difference between a system design interview and a design review? Speed and ambiguity. A design review has a week of context and a 1-pager. An interview has 2 minutes of context and a blank whiteboard. The framework keeps you from spending time you don't have. When should I bring up monitoring and observability? In the deep dive, after bottlenecks and failure modes. Say: \"I'd instrument this with latency histograms and error rate alerts on the hot path.\" Proactively mentioning observability is a strong production engineering signal. How do I avoid running out of time? Set mental checkpoints: 5 min (requirements done), 8 min (estimates done), 13 min (API done), 28 min (high-level done), last 15 min for deep dive. Glance at the clock after each phase."},{"u":"topics/how-to-prepare.html","p":"How to Prepare","i":"","h":"Overview","t":"Foundations How to Prepare The highest-ROI study strategy — what to learn, in what order, and how to practice. 8 min read Foundations The 3 things that move the needle Practice delivering out loud — reading is not the same as doing Learn a delivery framework first; memorizing components without structure is useless Study 10-15 canonical questions deeply, not 50 questions shallowly Understand the WHY behind every technology choice, not just the WHAT Time yourself — 45 min is shorter than you think"},{"u":"topics/how-to-prepare.html","p":"How to Prepare","i":"mental-model","h":"Mental model","t":"Mental model Preparing for system design is like learning to drive: reading the manual helps, but the only thing that actually works is getting behind the wheel and narrating your decisions out loud."},{"u":"topics/how-to-prepare.html","p":"How to Prepare","i":"core-concepts","h":"Study phases","t":"Study phases Wk 1 Delivery framework Wk 1-2 Core concepts Wk 2 Key technologies Wk 3-4 Question deep-dives Wk 4+ Mock interviews Learn the 5-phase structure first — every later study phase hangs off this skeleton CAP consistency models latency numbers — the vocabulary you'll reuse in every deep dive When to reach for Redis Kafka S3 a CDN a load balancer — and what each one costs you Work through canonical questions end-to-end — this is where the framework and concepts actually click together Practice under real interview pressure with feedback — surfaces gaps that solo study can't"},{"u":"topics/how-to-prepare.html","p":"How to Prepare","i":"how-it-works","h":"The right practice loop","t":"The right practice loop Pick a question 45 min timer Deliver out loud Review gaps Repeat until smooth Pick a question: Start with URL Shortener (scope is well-defined, great for framework practice) Timer: Hard constraint — teaches you to manage the 5-phase structure under pressure Deliver out loud: Narrate as if there's an interviewer in the room; silence is a red flag in real interviews Review gaps: After the session, identify what you couldn't justify or didn't know Repeat: Same question until you can deliver it cleanly, then move to the next Start with Tier 1 questions — well-scoped problems that exercise the full framework Hard constraint — forces you to budget time across phases the same way a real interview does Narrate every decision as if an interviewer is listening — silence reads as a red flag in the real thing Identify exactly what you couldn't justify or didn't know — this is where the actual learning happens Same question again until delivery is smooth then move on — repetition builds the muscle memory"},{"u":"topics/how-to-prepare.html","p":"How to Prepare","i":"tradeoffs","h":"High-ROI vs low-ROI prep","t":"High-ROI vs low-ROI prep High ROI Practicing delivery with a timer Understanding tradeoffs for 5-6 key tech choices Learning latency numbers by heart Doing 1 mock interview per week with feedback Studying Hello Interview question breakdowns deeply Low ROI (avoid) Reading without practicing delivery Covering 50+ questions at surface level Memorizing architecture diagrams without understanding why Watching YouTube without pausing to answer yourself first Skipping back-of-envelope estimation"},{"u":"topics/how-to-prepare.html","p":"How to Prepare","i":"when-to-use","h":"Canonical questions to master (in order)","t":"Canonical questions to master (in order) ✓ Tier 1 (always asked): URL Shortener, Rate Limiter, Distributed Cache, Notification System, Chat System ✓ Tier 2 (frequently asked): News Feed, Search Autocomplete, Web Crawler, Payment System, Uber/Lyft ⚠ Tier 3 (role-specific): Ads bidding, Video streaming, Recommendation engine — study these if the role is ML or media"},{"u":"topics/how-to-prepare.html","p":"How to Prepare","i":"interview-moves","h":"Interview moves","t":"Interview moves 01 Practice the framework before the question bank — one week on delivery structure pays off more than five weeks on memorized answers 02 Learn the \"why\" for 6 core tech choices — SQL vs NoSQL, Redis, Kafka, CDN, consistent hashing, and sharding cover 90% of what comes up 03 Study one question per 2 days, not one per hour — depth over breadth; interviewers probe, not quiz 04 Record yourself once — watching a 10-min clip of yourself is more valuable than 3 hours of reading"},{"u":"topics/how-to-prepare.html","p":"How to Prepare","i":"follow-ups","h":"Common follow-up questions","t":"Common follow-up questions How long does it realistically take to get interview-ready? 4 weeks for most engineers with some distributed systems exposure. 6-8 weeks if you're starting from scratch. The bottleneck is always practice reps, not content consumption. Is Hello Interview enough, or do I need other resources? Hello Interview covers everything you need. The only supplement worth adding is 1-2 mock interviews with a human (peer or paid service) for feedback on communication. Should I study ML system design separately? Yes, if targeting Nvidia or Apple ML roles. Same framework, different component vocabulary: feature store, model serving, training pipeline, embedding index. Study 3-4 ML-specific questions. How do I know if I'm ready? You can deliver any Tier 1 question cold in 40 minutes — with justifications for every choice, a bottleneck analysis, and 3 failure modes — without consulting notes. What's the single highest-ROI thing to do in the last 48 hours before an interview? Do one timed mock on a question you haven't practiced in a week. Refresh latency numbers. Don't cram new topics — solidify what you already know. Does my production experience help? Yes — significantly. Frame your Oracle observability experience (Prometheus/Grafana at 37k tenants) as scale evidence. Interviewers weight real production judgment over textbook answers."},{"u":"topics/key-technologies.html","p":"Key Technologies","i":"","h":"Overview","t":"Foundations Key Technologies When to reach for SQL, NoSQL, Redis, Kafka, S3, CDN, and a load balancer — with the justifications interviewers expect. 15 min read Reference Concept Map click to expand Decision rules (memorize these) SQL if: relations matter, ACID required, schema is stable, <10k writes/sec NoSQL if: high write throughput, flexible schema, key-value or document access pattern Redis if: sub-millisecond reads, leaderboard, rate limiting, session, pub/sub Kafka if: async fan-out, event log, decoupling producer/consumer, >100k events/sec S3 if: storing binary blobs >100KB — never store images/video in a relational DB Client browser / app Load Balancer routes traffic App Server business logic Redis cache, ~1ms PostgreSQL source of truth Kafka async fan-out S3 blobs / files Client / routing Cache (hot path) Storage (DB / blobs) Async / queue Hover a box to trace its request path. Click a box to jump to the section that explains it. Tech Choices SQL Relational ACID use when relations and strong schema matter sql-vs-nosql Postgres Default choice for transactional data Joins + txns Strong consistency guarantees NoSQL Flexible schema high write throughput sql-vs-nosql DynamoDB Key-value at massive scale Wide-column Cassandra-style for write-heavy data Redis Sub-millisecond reads for hot data core-concepts Cache-aside Miss falls through to DB Rate limiting Sorted sets / token buckets Kafka Async event log for decoupling producers/consumers kafka-pattern Fan-out One event many consumers Buffering Absorbs write spikes S3 Object storage for blobs over ~100KB how-it-works Never in SQL Blobs bloat relational storage Presigned URLs Direct client upload/download CDN Edge caching that changes your whole read path tradeoffs Edge cache Requests never reach origin Cache invalidation TTL + explicit purge on change"},{"u":"topics/key-technologies.html","p":"Key Technologies","i":"mental-model","h":"Mental model","t":"Mental model Technologies are tools. The mistake is reaching for a tool because you know it — not because the problem needs it. Every choice must be followed by \"because the access pattern is X and the tradeoff I'm accepting is Y.\""},{"u":"topics/key-technologies.html","p":"Key Technologies","i":"sql-vs-nosql","h":"SQL vs NoSQL","t":"SQL vs NoSQL PostgreSQL — consistency vs availability Strong consistency Availability DynamoDB / Cassandra — consistency vs availability Strong consistency Availability Read-optimized vs write-optimized Read-optimized (SQL + replicas) Write-optimized (NoSQL sharded) SQL (PostgreSQL, MySQL) Structured, relational data with joins ACID transactions — critical for financial or inventory data Schema is known upfront and stable Complex queries (aggregations, filtering) Scales well to ~10k writes/sec with read replicas NoSQL (DynamoDB, Cassandra, MongoDB) High write throughput (>10k writes/sec) Flexible or evolving schema Simple key-value or document access pattern Horizontal sharding required from day one Eventual consistency is acceptable Common trap: using NoSQL because it sounds \"more scalable.\" SQL scales further than most systems need. Justify the choice with a specific scale number or access pattern requirement."},{"u":"topics/key-technologies.html","p":"Key Technologies","i":"core-concepts","h":"Technology decision matrix","t":"Technology decision matrix Technology Use when Don't use when Redis Sub-ms reads, session storage, rate limiting, leaderboards, pub/sub Data must survive restarts without persistence config; data > RAM Kafka Async fan-out, event streaming, decoupling services, audit log, >100k events/sec Simple task queues (use SQS/RabbitMQ); request-response patterns Cassandra Write-heavy, time-series, high availability across regions, no complex queries Complex joins; strong consistency required; ad-hoc analytics Elasticsearch Full-text search, fuzzy matching, autocomplete, log analytics Primary data store — always sync from a source of truth DB S3 / Object storage Binary blobs (images, video, files), backups, data lake Data you need to query — use Athena or load to a warehouse CDN Static assets, globally distributed reads, video streaming, API responses with low TTL Personalized or dynamic content that can't be cached Load balancer Distributing traffic across multiple service instances — always needed past 1 instance Single-instance internal services Latency by tier (log scale — note the gap) L1 cache ref ~1 ns RAM access ~100 ns Redis (same DC) ~1 ms SSD random read ~150 μs PostgreSQL query ~10 ms Same-region network ~0.5 ms Cross-region network ~150 ms S3 / internet round trip ~100-300 ms Redis vs PostgreSQL isn't \"a bit faster\" — it's ~10,000x faster than a cross-region call and ~10x faster than a local DB query. That gap is why hot-path caching changes your whole architecture."},{"u":"topics/key-technologies.html","p":"Key Technologies","i":"how-it-works","h":"Redis — what it actually does","t":"Redis — what it actually does ✓ Cache hit — ~1ms Client request Redis GET found ~1ms key exists → return immediately ▸ Replay ✗ Cache miss — ~10ms+ Client request Redis MISS write-back DB ~10ms+ miss → query DB → cache the result → return ▸ Replay Redis data structure Interview use case String / KV Session token → user_id, short_code → long_url Sorted Set (ZSET) Leaderboard, rate limiter (score = timestamp) Hash User profile object with multiple fields Set Unique visitors, blocked user IDs List Notification queue, recent activity feed HyperLogLog Approximate unique count (UV, DAU) at low memory cost Client sends GET /user/42 In-memory key lookup — O(1) no disk I/O Client sends GET /user/42 Key not found — falls through to DB then writes back Disk read / index scan — much slower than memory"},{"u":"topics/key-technologies.html","p":"Key Technologies","i":"kafka-pattern","h":"Kafka — when and why","t":"Kafka — when and why Producer user signs up Kafka topic durable log Consumer A email service Consumer B push notifications Producer (decoupled from consumers) Kafka topic (persisted, replayable) Independent consumer groups Fan-out: One event (user sign-up) triggers multiple consumers — email, analytics, fraud check — without the producer knowing about them Durability: Messages persisted to disk — consumers can replay from any offset Decoupling: Producer and consumer scale independently; consumer can be slow without blocking producer Ordering: Guaranteed within a partition — partition by user_id for per-user ordering Use Kafka when you need fan-out, replay, or >100k events/sec. For simple point-to-point task queues (<10k/sec), SQS or RabbitMQ is simpler and sufficient."},{"u":"topics/key-technologies.html","p":"Key Technologies","i":"tradeoffs","h":"CDN — when it changes your design","t":"CDN — when it changes your design CDN — latency vs freshness Always fresh (origin) Lowest latency (edge cache) What CDN solves Serves static assets from edge nodes (~5ms vs ~100ms) Absorbs read traffic — origin sees only cache misses Global availability without multi-region origin infra DDoS absorption at the edge HTTP redirect caching (URL shortener use case) CDN limitations Cache invalidation on update is slow or requires purge API Personalized content can't be cached Short TTL reduces cache hit rate Cost scales with egress bandwidth Not a substitute for proper DB or service design"},{"u":"topics/key-technologies.html","p":"Key Technologies","i":"when-to-use","h":"Choosing the right DB engine","t":"Choosing the right DB engine ✓ PostgreSQL: Default choice. Handles 99% of problems. JSONB for flexible fields. Read replicas for scale. Vertical scaling goes far. ✓ DynamoDB / Cassandra: When you need single-digit ms latency at millions of writes/sec. Access pattern must be key-value or range scan — no joins. ⚠ MongoDB: Good for document workloads with nested structure. Watch out: default eventual consistency and non-atomic multi-document operations. ✗ Never store images or video in a relational DB. Use S3 + CDN. Store only the S3 key/URL in the DB."},{"u":"topics/key-technologies.html","p":"Key Technologies","i":"interview-moves","h":"Interview moves","t":"Interview moves 01 \"I'd use PostgreSQL here — the data is relational, schema is stable, and we're at ~1k writes/sec which it handles easily\" — default to SQL and justify if you deviate 02 \"For the hot read path I'd put Redis in front — this cuts latency from ~10ms to ~1ms for the top 20% of keys that get 80% of traffic\" — always quantify the cache benefit 03 \"The user upload goes to S3 — I'd never store binary blobs in Postgres. The DB stores only the S3 key.\" — say this proactively; missing it is a common junior signal 04 \"I'd use Kafka here for fan-out — when a user posts, we need to notify their followers, update search index, and fire analytics without coupling the write path to all that work\" — name the specific reason 05 \"For global latency I'd put a CDN in front of the redirect service — edge nodes serve most requests in <10ms regardless of user location\" — CDN is often overlooked; mention it proactively for read-heavy systems"},{"u":"topics/key-technologies.html","p":"Key Technologies","i":"follow-ups","h":"Common follow-up questions","t":"Common follow-up questions When would you use Cassandra over DynamoDB? Cassandra when you need multi-region active-active writes (LWT for lightweight transactions) or when you want to avoid vendor lock-in. DynamoDB when you're on AWS and want zero ops overhead. Performance is comparable. What's a write-through vs write-behind cache? Write-through: write to cache and DB together — consistent but higher write latency. Write-behind (write-back): write to cache, DB updated asynchronously — lower latency but risk of data loss on crash. What happens if Redis goes down? Read traffic falls back to DB — latency increases but system stays up. Implement a circuit breaker on the cache layer. Redis Cluster with replication gives HA. Redis Sentinel for automatic failover. How does Kafka guarantee exactly-once delivery? Idempotent producers (dedup by sequence number) + transactional API for atomic produce + consumer offset commits. At-least-once is the practical default — make your consumers idempotent instead. When would you use a search index like Elasticsearch vs a DB full-text search? DB full-text (Postgres tsvector) works fine up to ~10M rows with infrequent queries. Elasticsearch for: fuzzy matching, autocomplete, faceted search, >10M docs, or >100 search QPS. Never use ES as your primary store. What's the difference between a message queue and an event log? Queue (SQS, RabbitMQ): messages consumed and deleted — each message processed once. Event log (Kafka): messages persisted, consumers maintain their own offset — any consumer can replay from any point in history."},{"u":"topics/patterns/dealing-with-contention.html","p":"Dealing with Contention","i":"","h":"Overview","t":"Patterns Dealing with Contention Race conditions, optimistic vs pessimistic locking, and distributed locks — how to stop two writers from stepping on each other. 15-20 min Medium Say this in the first 2 minutes Name the race condition explicitly: \"two requests read the same value, both write back, one update is lost\" Default to optimistic locking (version column) — it scales better because it doesn't hold a lock during I/O Reach for pessimistic locking only when conflict rate is high and retries would thrash Distributed locks (Redis/Zookeeper) are for cross-service coordination, not single-DB row updates Any distributed lock needs a TTL — and any TTL needs a fencing token, or you risk two holders at once"},{"u":"topics/patterns/dealing-with-contention.html","p":"Dealing with Contention","i":"mental-model","h":"Mental model","t":"Mental model Contention is two people grabbing the same shopping cart in a store. Optimistic locking lets both reach for it and only complains if they collide — cheap when collisions are rare. Pessimistic locking puts a \"do not touch\" sign on the cart the moment one person approaches — safer when collisions are common, but everyone else waits."},{"u":"topics/patterns/dealing-with-contention.html","p":"Dealing with Contention","i":"core-concepts","h":"Core concepts","t":"Core concepts Term Definition Race condition Outcome depends on the unpredictable timing of concurrent operations Lost update One write silently overwrites another because neither saw the other's change Optimistic locking Read with a version, write only if version still matches; retry on mismatch Pessimistic locking SELECT ... FOR UPDATE — acquire the lock before reading, hold until commit Compare-and-swap (CAS) Atomic primitive: \"set X to B only if X is currently A\" Distributed lock A mutex that works across processes/machines (Redis SETNX, Zookeeper) Fencing token Monotonically increasing number issued with a lock, so stale lock holders can be rejected Contention level → which lock strategy Low contention — optimistic High contention — pessimistic"},{"u":"topics/patterns/dealing-with-contention.html","p":"Dealing with Contention","i":"how-it-works","h":"Optimistic locking: read-modify-write with version check","t":"Optimistic locking: read-modify-write with version check Request A Read row (v=1) UPDATE WHERE v=1 success → v=2 Request B (concurrent) Read row (v=1) UPDATE WHERE v=1 0 rows affected — conflict! Retry: re-read v=2 re-apply change retry succeeds against v=2 Read with version Write succeeds (version matched) Conflict path — retry with fresh read Read: fetch the row along with its current version number Modify: compute the new value in application code Write: UPDATE ... SET value=?, version=version+1 WHERE id=? AND version=? Check: if 0 rows affected, someone else won the race — re-read and retry Both requests read row with version=1 A commits first — version becomes 2 B also read v=1 unaware A already committed DB rejects: WHERE v=1 matches 0 rows since v is now 2 B re-reads the new value (v=2) and retries the operation"},{"u":"topics/patterns/dealing-with-contention.html","p":"Dealing with Contention","i":"tradeoffs","h":"Tradeoffs","t":"Tradeoffs Optimistic locking wins No lock held during I/O — much higher throughput under low contention No deadlock risk between transactions Scales horizontally — works fine across DB replicas/shards Optimistic locking costs Wasted work on every conflict — must retry the whole read-modify-write Under high contention, retries can thrash and starve some requests Requires every writer to participate (a version/CAS check) — easy to bypass by mistake"},{"u":"topics/patterns/dealing-with-contention.html","p":"Dealing with Contention","i":"when-to-use","h":"When to use","t":"When to use ✓ Use optimistic locking as the default for most web app updates — profile edits, inventory counts, cart updates — where conflicts are rare. ✓ Use pessimistic locking for hot, narrow contention like seat booking or flash-sale checkout, where many requests target the exact same row at once. ⚠ Watch out with distributed locks — if a lock holder pauses (GC, network blip) past the TTL, another process can acquire the lock while the first still thinks it holds it. Use fencing tokens."},{"u":"topics/patterns/dealing-with-contention.html","p":"Dealing with Contention","i":"interview-moves","h":"Interview moves","t":"Interview moves 01 \"I'll add a version column and use it as a compare-and-swap on write — that's optimistic locking, and it's my default\" — names the pattern and justifies it as the baseline 02 \"This row gets hammered by concurrent writers, so I'd switch to SELECT FOR UPDATE here specifically — not everywhere\" — shows you scope pessimistic locking narrowly 03 \"Across services I can't take a DB lock, so I'd use a Redis-based distributed lock with a short TTL\" — shows you know locks don't cross service/DB boundaries 04 \"Any TTL-based lock can expire mid-operation — I'd attach a fencing token so a late writer gets rejected by the resource itself\" — the detail that separates senior answers from junior ones 05 \"On conflict I'd retry with exponential backoff and a retry cap, then surface an error rather than retry forever\" — shows you've thought about the failure path, not just the happy path"},{"u":"topics/patterns/dealing-with-contention.html","p":"Dealing with Contention","i":"follow-ups","h":"Common follow-up questions","t":"Common follow-up questions Why is optimistic locking usually the default in interviews? Most systems have low real contention — locks held during I/O block unrelated traffic and don't scale across replicas. Optimistic locking only pays a cost when conflicts actually happen. What's a fencing token and why does it matter? A monotonically increasing number issued alongside a distributed lock. The protected resource rejects any write carrying an older token than the last one seen — stops a \"zombie\" lock holder from corrupting state after expiry. When would you choose Zookeeper over Redis for distributed locks? Zookeeper gives strongly consistent, session-based locks with automatic release on disconnect — better correctness guarantees. Redis (Redlock) is faster and simpler but has known edge cases under clock drift and network partitions. How do you prevent deadlocks with pessimistic locking? Always acquire locks in a consistent global order across transactions, keep lock-held time short, and set a lock/statement timeout so a stuck transaction fails instead of hanging others. How does optimistic locking handle high contention gracefully? It often doesn't — retries pile up and some requests starve. At that point, switch that specific hot path to pessimistic locking or a queue that serializes writes to the contended resource. Can you combine optimistic locking with a queue? Yes — route writes to a contended key through a single-consumer queue (partitioned by key) so they're naturally serialized, eliminating the conflict instead of detecting and retrying it."},{"u":"topics/patterns/handling-large-blobs.html","p":"Handling Large Blobs","i":"","h":"Overview","t":"Patterns Handling Large Blobs Object storage, pre-signed URLs, chunked uploads, and CDN delivery — how to move big files without your app server choking. 15-20 min Medium Say this in the first 2 minutes Large files never flow through your application servers — that's the single most important sentence on this topic App server only issues a pre-signed URL; the client uploads bytes directly to object storage (S3) Chunked/multipart upload makes large files resumable on flaky connections Reads go through a CDN in front of object storage, not through the app server either Object storage gives you durability and infinite scale for free — don't store blobs in your relational DB"},{"u":"topics/patterns/handling-large-blobs.html","p":"Handling Large Blobs","i":"mental-model","h":"Mental model","t":"Mental model Your app server is a receptionist, not a loading dock. The receptionist hands you a signed pass (pre-signed URL) so you can drive your truck straight to the warehouse (object storage) — they never touch the cargo themselves."},{"u":"topics/patterns/handling-large-blobs.html","p":"Handling Large Blobs","i":"core-concepts","h":"Core concepts","t":"Core concepts Term Definition Object storage Durable, infinitely scalable blob store keyed by object name (S3, GCS) Pre-signed URL A time-limited URL granting temporary upload/download permission without exposing credentials Multipart upload Splitting a large file into chunks uploaded independently, then assembled server-side Resumability Ability to retry only the failed chunk instead of re-uploading the whole file CDN origin Object storage acts as the origin; CDN caches and serves reads at the edge Metadata record A DB row describing the blob (owner, size, status) — the blob itself never lives in the DB"},{"u":"topics/patterns/handling-large-blobs.html","p":"Handling Large Blobs","i":"how-it-works","h":"Upload and delivery flow","t":"Upload and delivery flow Client 1. request upload URL App Server issues pre-signed URL 2. returns URL 3. client PUTs file bytes directly to S3 — bypasses app server Object Storage multipart chunks CDN edge cache for reads origin 4. reads served from CDN, never re-hit app server Client uploads/downloads bytes directly App server — auth + URL minting only Object storage — durable blob store CDN — edge reads Request: client asks the app server for permission to upload a specific file Authorize: app server checks auth/quota, asks object storage to mint a pre-signed URL, returns it Upload: client PUTs the bytes directly to object storage — the app server's job is already done Serve: reads go through a CDN sitting in front of object storage, never through the app server Client requests an upload URL — no file bytes sent yet App server validates auth/quota asks S3 to mint a pre-signed PUT URL — never touches file bytes Large files split into chunks for resumable parallel upload CDN caches reads at the edge close to users"},{"u":"topics/patterns/handling-large-blobs.html","p":"Handling Large Blobs","i":"tradeoffs","h":"Tradeoffs","t":"Tradeoffs Pre-signed direct upload App server never holds large request bodies — no memory/bandwidth pressure Upload throughput scales with object storage, not your fleet size Client can upload in parallel chunks for speed and resumability Proxying uploads through app server Every upload consumes app server memory, CPU, and bandwidth App server becomes the bottleneck and a single point of failure for uploads Harder to scale — you're paying compute cost just to move bytes"},{"u":"topics/patterns/handling-large-blobs.html","p":"Handling Large Blobs","i":"when-to-use","h":"When to use","t":"When to use ✓ Use pre-signed URLs + object storage for any file upload over a few hundred KB — images, videos, documents, exports. ✓ Use multipart/chunked upload for files above ~50-100MB, or any upload from a mobile/unreliable network connection. ⚠ Watch for orphaned uploads — a pre-signed URL grants upload rights even if the client never tells your app it finished. Use storage event notifications (e.g. S3 → Lambda) to confirm and record completion."},{"u":"topics/patterns/handling-large-blobs.html","p":"Handling Large Blobs","i":"interview-moves","h":"Interview moves","t":"Interview moves 01 \"File bytes never flow through my app server — it only issues a pre-signed URL, the client uploads directly to S3\" — the single sentence that signals you know this pattern 02 \"For large files I'd use multipart upload, so a flaky connection only needs to retry the failed chunk, not the whole file\" — connects chunking to resumability concretely 03 \"I'd put a CDN in front of object storage so reads never round-trip to the app server or origin region either\" — extends the bypass principle to reads, not just writes 04 \"I'd store a metadata row in the DB — owner, size, status — and use a storage event webhook to flip status to 'uploaded' once S3 confirms\" — shows you've thought about the upload-confirmation gap 05 \"The pre-signed URL is scoped and time-limited so it can't be reused or shared beyond its purpose\" — addresses the obvious security follow-up before it's asked"},{"u":"topics/patterns/handling-large-blobs.html","p":"Handling Large Blobs","i":"follow-ups","h":"Common follow-up questions","t":"Common follow-up questions Why never proxy file bytes through the app server? It consumes app server memory and bandwidth per upload, turning a stateless compute tier into a data-transfer bottleneck. Direct-to-storage upload scales independently of your app fleet. How do you know an upload actually succeeded? Configure object storage to emit an event (e.g. S3 event notification) on object creation, consumed by a worker that updates the metadata record's status — don't trust the client to report success. How do you validate file type/size before allowing an upload? Pre-signed URLs can constrain content-type and max size via policy conditions. Re-validate server-side after upload (e.g. a Lambda that inspects the object) since client-side checks can be bypassed. How do you handle resumable uploads after a connection drop? Multipart upload tracks which chunk IDs have been acknowledged by storage; on reconnect, the client queries upload status and only resends missing chunks. How do you serve private files securely through a CDN? Use signed CDN URLs/cookies with short expiry, or keep private content origin-only and issue a fresh pre-signed download URL per request instead of caching it publicly. How do you reduce storage cost for old/rarely accessed blobs? Lifecycle policies that auto-transition objects to cheaper storage tiers (e.g. S3 Infrequent Access, Glacier) after N days of inactivity."},{"u":"topics/patterns/managing-long-running-processes.html","p":"Managing Long-Running Processes","i":"","h":"Overview","t":"Patterns Managing Long-Running Processes Async job queues, status polling, webhooks, and idempotency keys — how to handle work that doesn't fit in one request. 15-20 min Medium Say this in the first 2 minutes If work takes longer than a request timeout, don't block — accept the job, return immediately, process async Queue (SQS/Kafka) + worker pool decouples request latency from actual processing time Client either polls a status endpoint or registers a webhook for completion — name both and pick one with a reason Idempotency keys are mandatory the moment you have retries — a duplicate submission must not double-process Webhooks push complexity (retries, security, ordering) onto you; polling pushes latency and waste onto the client"},{"u":"topics/patterns/managing-long-running-processes.html","p":"Managing Long-Running Processes","i":"mental-model","h":"Mental model","t":"Mental model A long-running job is a dry cleaner ticket. You drop off your clothes (submit the job), get a ticket number (job ID), and don't stand in the shop waiting. You either call to check (\"is it ready?\" — polling) or they call you when it's done (webhook)."},{"u":"topics/patterns/managing-long-running-processes.html","p":"Managing Long-Running Processes","i":"core-concepts","h":"Core concepts","t":"Core concepts Term Definition Job queue Durable buffer (SQS/Kafka) holding submitted work until a worker picks it up Worker pool A fleet of processes that pull jobs off the queue and execute them Status store A DB/cache tracking each job's state: queued, running, done, failed Polling Client repeatedly calls a status endpoint until the job completes Webhook Your server calls a client-provided URL when the job finishes Idempotency key A client-supplied unique ID ensuring a retried submission isn't processed twice"},{"u":"topics/patterns/managing-long-running-processes.html","p":"Managing Long-Running Processes","i":"how-it-works","h":"Submit, process, and check status","t":"Submit, process, and check status Client submits job API 202 + job_id Queue SQS / Kafka Worker Pool idempotent exec writes status: running → done Status Store queued/running/done Client / gateway Database — source of truth Async — queue / worker Submit: client sends the job with an idempotency key, gets back 202 Accepted + job_id instantly Queue: job sits in a durable queue until a worker is free Process: worker executes the job, updating status as it progresses Notify: client either polls GET /jobs/{id} or receives a webhook callback on completion Client submits a job with an idempotency key gets a job_id back immediately Returns 202 Accepted + job_id does not wait for completion Durable buffer holds the job until a worker is free Pool of workers pulls jobs and executes them keyed by idempotency key Status record the client polls or that triggers a webhook on completion Status store — client polls this or it triggers the webhook"},{"u":"topics/patterns/managing-long-running-processes.html","p":"Managing Long-Running Processes","i":"tradeoffs","h":"Tradeoffs","t":"Tradeoffs Polling Simple to implement on both client and server — just a GET endpoint No need to expose a public callback URL or verify inbound webhook auth Cost: wasted requests and added latency between actual completion and detection Webhooks Near-instant notification, no wasted polling traffic Cost: you must handle retries, ordering, and signature verification for security Cost: client's callback endpoint must be reachable and reliable — added operational burden on them"},{"u":"topics/patterns/managing-long-running-processes.html","p":"Managing Long-Running Processes","i":"when-to-use","h":"When to use","t":"When to use ✓ Use polling for short-lived jobs (seconds to a couple minutes) or when the client is a browser that can't easily expose a webhook endpoint. ✓ Use webhooks for server-to-server integrations with long jobs (video processing, large exports) where polling would waste significant resources. ⚠ Watch for duplicate processing on retry — if the client times out and resubmits, an idempotency key must make the second submission a no-op, not a second job."},{"u":"topics/patterns/managing-long-running-processes.html","p":"Managing Long-Running Processes","i":"interview-moves","h":"Interview moves","t":"Interview moves 01 \"This work exceeds a normal request timeout, so I'd accept it async — return 202 and a job_id immediately, process in the background\" — names the trigger and the pattern together 02 \"I'd require an idempotency key on submission, so a client retry after a timeout doesn't create a duplicate job\" — the single most important correctness detail in this pattern 03 \"For this audience I'd offer a webhook, since these are server clients that can expose a callback — polling would just waste resources on both sides\" — justifies the choice instead of picking one by default 04 \"Webhook deliveries can fail, so I'd add retry with backoff and let the client fall back to polling the status endpoint if delivery fails repeatedly\" — shows you've thought about webhook failure modes 05 \"I'd sign webhook payloads so the receiver can verify the callback actually came from us\" — addresses webhook security before it's asked"},{"u":"topics/patterns/managing-long-running-processes.html","p":"Managing Long-Running Processes","i":"follow-ups","h":"Common follow-up questions","t":"Common follow-up questions Why are idempotency keys mandatory here? Any async system has retries — network blips cause clients to resubmit. Without a dedup key, that resubmission becomes a second, redundant (or harmful) job execution. How do you implement an idempotency key server-side? Store submitted keys with a TTL in a fast store (Redis/DB unique constraint). On a new submission, check if the key exists — if so, return the original job's result instead of enqueueing again. How do you handle a worker crashing mid-job? Use visibility timeouts (SQS) so the message becomes available again if not acknowledged within a window — another worker picks it up. Design job execution to be safely re-runnable (idempotent). How do you avoid polling becoming a load problem? Use exponential backoff on the client (start at 1s, cap at 30s), and serve status reads from a cache, not the primary job DB, since they're high-volume reads of low-churn data. How do you guarantee webhook delivery ordering? Generally you don't — include a sequence number or timestamp in the payload and let the receiver reorder/dedupe, rather than relying on delivery order. What if the client never polls and never receives a webhook successfully? Keep job results in the status store for a retention window (e.g. 7-30 days) so a late client request can still retrieve the outcome instead of losing it."},{"u":"topics/patterns/multi-step-processes.html","p":"Multi-Step Processes","i":"","h":"Overview","t":"Patterns Multi-Step Processes The saga pattern — chaining local transactions across services with compensating actions instead of distributed transactions. 15-20 min Medium Say this in the first 2 minutes Distributed transactions (2PC) don't scale — they hold locks across services and fail badly on partition Saga = sequence of local transactions, each with a compensating action to undo it Orchestration = central coordinator calls each step; choreography = services react to each other's events Compensating actions are business operations, not a generic rollback — \"refund payment\", not \"undo INSERT\" Sagas give eventual consistency only — say this explicitly so the interviewer knows you're not claiming ACID"},{"u":"topics/patterns/multi-step-processes.html","p":"Multi-Step Processes","i":"mental-model","h":"Mental model","t":"Mental model A saga is like booking a multi-leg trip yourself instead of through one travel agent: you book the flight, then the hotel, then the car. If the car rental fails, you don't get a magic global undo — you cancel the hotel and refund the flight, one call at a time."},{"u":"topics/patterns/multi-step-processes.html","p":"Multi-Step Processes","i":"core-concepts","h":"Core concepts","t":"Core concepts Term Definition Saga A sequence of local transactions across services, coordinated to act as one logical operation Local transaction A normal ACID transaction scoped to a single service's own database Compensating transaction A business-level action that semantically undoes a prior step (e.g. refund) Orchestration A central coordinator service explicitly calls each step and handles failures Choreography Each service publishes events; the next service reacts — no central coordinator 2PC Two-phase commit — a distributed transaction protocol that locks all participants until all agree"},{"u":"topics/patterns/multi-step-processes.html","p":"Multi-Step Processes","i":"how-it-works","h":"Orchestrated saga with compensating rollback","t":"Orchestrated saga with compensating rollback Saga Orchestrator coordinator Order Service local txn 1 Payment Service local txn 2 Inventory Service FAILS — out of stock Confirm happy path FAILURE → orchestrator runs compensations in reverse order Refund payment compensate txn 2 Cancel order compensate txn 1 Orchestrator / confirm Forward step (local transaction) Compensating transaction (rollback) Forward path: orchestrator calls order → payment → inventory in sequence, each a local ACID transaction Failure detected: inventory step fails (out of stock) — orchestrator stops the forward path Compensate: orchestrator calls compensating actions in reverse order — refund payment, then cancel order End state: system reaches a consistent \"cancelled\" state — eventually, not instantly Central coordinator drives the whole saga Step 1: create order in PENDING state Step 2: charge the customer Step 3: reserve stock — FAILS (out of stock) Only reached on success Compensating txn: refund the payment Compensating txn: cancel the order"},{"u":"topics/patterns/multi-step-processes.html","p":"Multi-Step Processes","i":"tradeoffs","h":"Tradeoffs","t":"Tradeoffs Orchestration One place to read the whole workflow — easier to understand and debug Centralized retry/timeout/compensation logic Easy to add monitoring and a saga state machine Cost: the orchestrator is a new service and a potential bottleneck Choreography No central coordinator — services stay fully decoupled No single point of failure for the workflow itself Cost: hard to see the overall flow — logic is smeared across services Cost: harder to add a new step without touching multiple services' event handlers"},{"u":"topics/patterns/multi-step-processes.html","p":"Multi-Step Processes","i":"when-to-use","h":"When to use","t":"When to use ✓ Use orchestration for complex workflows with many steps, conditional branches, or where you need a clear audit trail (e.g. order checkout, loan approval). ✓ Use choreography for simple 2-3 step flows where services are already event-driven and you want to avoid adding a new coordinator service. ✗ Avoid 2PC across microservices — it requires all participants to hold locks until every vote is in, which kills availability and doesn't survive a partition."},{"u":"topics/patterns/multi-step-processes.html","p":"Multi-Step Processes","i":"interview-moves","h":"Interview moves","t":"Interview moves 01 \"This spans three services with their own databases — I can't use one ACID transaction, so I'd use the saga pattern\" — names the trigger and the pattern together 02 \"I'd go with orchestration here since there are several steps and I want a single place to see the workflow state\" — justifies the choice instead of defaulting blindly 03 \"Each compensating action is a real business operation — refund the payment, release the inventory hold — not a generic database rollback\" — shows you understand sagas aren't free 04 \"Sagas only give eventual consistency — for a brief window the order exists in a partially-completed state, and I'd model that explicitly\" — preempts the \"what about consistency\" follow-up 05 \"Every step and compensation needs to be idempotent, since the orchestrator may retry after a timeout without knowing if the first call landed\" — connects sagas to retry-safety"},{"u":"topics/patterns/multi-step-processes.html","p":"Multi-Step Processes","i":"follow-ups","h":"Common follow-up questions","t":"Common follow-up questions Why not just use a distributed transaction (2PC)? 2PC holds locks on every participant until all vote commit — this blocks unrelated traffic and doesn't tolerate a coordinator or participant failure well. It doesn't scale to internet-scale microservices. What happens if a compensating transaction itself fails? Retry with backoff; if it keeps failing, push to a dead-letter queue for manual/ops intervention. Compensations must be designed to be retried safely (idempotent). How do you give the user a consistent view during a saga in progress? Show an explicit intermediate state like \"processing\" rather than a final state. Don't let the UI imply success until the saga's last step confirms. Can every step be compensated? No — some actions are irreversible (sending an email, an external non-refundable charge). Design those steps to be last in the sequence, after all reversible steps have succeeded. How does choreography avoid a single point of failure? Each service only knows about events it cares about and reacts independently via a message broker — there's no central process whose crash halts the whole workflow. How do you debug a choreographed saga gone wrong? Correlate all events with a single saga/trace ID and centralize logs (e.g. in a tracing system) — since there's no orchestrator state machine to inspect directly."},{"u":"topics/patterns/real-time-updates.html","p":"Real-Time Updates","i":"","h":"Overview","t":"Patterns Real-Time Updates How to push fresh data to clients — polling, long-polling, WebSockets, and SSE — and how to justify the choice. 15-20 min Medium Say this in the first 2 minutes Ask: is this one-way server push, or does the client also need to send frequent messages? One-way push (feeds, notifications, live scores) → SSE, not WebSockets — simpler infra, free reconnect Bidirectional + low-latency (chat, games, collab editing) → WebSockets WebSockets are stateful connections — horizontal scaling needs a pub/sub backplane (Redis) so any node can deliver to any client Long-polling is the fallback when WS/SSE are blocked by a corporate proxy, not the primary design"},{"u":"topics/patterns/real-time-updates.html","p":"Real-Time Updates","i":"mental-model","h":"Mental model","t":"Mental model Polling is calling the restaurant every 5 minutes to ask if your order is ready. Long-polling is staying on hold until they tell you. SSE is the restaurant texting you the moment it's done. WebSockets is a walkie-talkie — both sides can talk anytime, on the same open channel."},{"u":"topics/patterns/real-time-updates.html","p":"Real-Time Updates","i":"core-concepts","h":"Core concepts","t":"Core concepts Term Definition Polling Client repeatedly requests on a timer; mostly empty responses Long-polling Server holds the request open until data is ready, then responds SSE Server-Sent Events — one persistent HTTP connection, server streams events WebSocket Full-duplex socket over a single TCP connection after an HTTP upgrade Sticky session Load balancer routes a client to the same backend node every request Backplane Shared pub/sub layer (Redis) so any server node can broadcast to any connected client Heartbeat Periodic ping/pong to detect dead connections behind proxies and NATs"},{"u":"topics/patterns/real-time-updates.html","p":"Real-Time Updates","i":"how-it-works","h":"Connection lifecycle: polling vs WebSocket","t":"Connection lifecycle: polling vs WebSocket POLLING — N short-lived connections Client GET (empty) Server GET (empty) GET (data!) connection closes after every reply WEBSOCKET — one long-lived connection Client Upgrade: websocket Server socket held open push 1 push 2 (no new request needed) ≫ Client Persistent connection (WS / SSE) Polling: client opens and closes a connection on every tick — most responses carry nothing new Long-polling: server delays the response until data exists or a timeout hits, then the client immediately reopens SSE / WebSocket: one connection stays open; server pushes data the instant it's available, no re-request Client opens a new HTTP request Server responds immediately even if nothing changed Client opens one connection via HTTP Upgrade Connection stays open — server pushes whenever data is ready client can send anytime"},{"u":"topics/patterns/real-time-updates.html","p":"Real-Time Updates","i":"tradeoffs","h":"Tradeoffs","t":"Tradeoffs Dimension Polling Long-polling SSE WebSocket Direction Client pull Client pull, delayed Server push only Full duplex Latency Up to poll interval Near-instant Near-instant Near-instant Infra cost Low, but wasteful QPS Medium — many held requests Medium — 1 conn/client Higher — stateful, sticky sessions Browser support Universal Universal Good, no IE Universal (modern) Auto-reconnect N/A (stateless) Manual Built-in (browser native) Manual (you write it) SSE wins Just HTTP — works through normal proxies, load balancers, CDNs Browser auto-reconnects with Last-Event-ID for free Much less server-side state to manage than WebSockets Simple to reason about: it's a stream, not a protocol WebSocket costs Stateful — needs sticky sessions or a shared backplane to scale out No native reconnect or message replay — you build it Harder to load balance and harder to debug in prod Overkill if the client never needs to send much back"},{"u":"topics/patterns/real-time-updates.html","p":"Real-Time Updates","i":"when-to-use","h":"When to use","t":"When to use ✓ Use SSE for live feeds, notification badges, stock tickers, progress bars — anything where only the server speaks. ✓ Use WebSockets for chat, multiplayer games, collaborative editing — frequent bidirectional, low-latency messages. ⚠ Watch out for corporate proxies/firewalls that block long-lived connections — keep a long-polling fallback path for those clients."},{"u":"topics/patterns/real-time-updates.html","p":"Real-Time Updates","i":"interview-moves","h":"Interview moves","t":"Interview moves 01 \"Does the client need to send data back frequently, or just receive it?\" — this single question picks SSE vs WebSocket for you 02 \"I'd default to SSE — it's just HTTP, gets auto-reconnect for free, and is much easier to scale\" — shows you don't reach for WebSockets reflexively 03 \"WebSockets are stateful, so a load balancer alone can't route a push to the right client — I'd add a Redis pub/sub backplane\" — the single most important WS scaling fact 04 \"I'd keep long-polling as a fallback, not the primary path, for clients behind proxies that strip WebSocket upgrades\" — shows pragmatism about real-world networks 05 \"On reconnect, the client needs to resync missed state — either replay from a sequence number or just refetch a snapshot\" — addresses the gap nobody mentions until asked"},{"u":"topics/patterns/real-time-updates.html","p":"Real-Time Updates","i":"follow-ups","h":"Common follow-up questions","t":"Common follow-up questions How do you scale WebSockets across multiple servers? Sticky sessions route a client to the same node. Use Redis pub/sub (or Kafka) as a backplane so any node publishing an event reaches the node holding that client's socket. Why not just use WebSockets for everything? Stateful connections are expensive to scale, harder to load balance, and need custom reconnect/heartbeat logic. SSE gets you 90% of the value with HTTP-level simplicity for one-way push. How does a client recover missed messages after disconnect? SSE: send Last-Event-ID header on reconnect, server replays from there. WebSocket: you implement a sequence number and a \"resync\" message yourself. What's the failure mode of long-polling at scale? Each held request ties up a server thread/connection slot. At high concurrency this exhausts connection pools faster than short polling — needs async I/O servers (Node, Netty) to hold many idle connections cheaply. How do you detect a dead WebSocket connection? Ping/pong heartbeat every 30s. If no pong within a timeout, the server closes and the client reconnects. Without this, dead sockets behind NATs linger silently. Mobile clients drain battery on persistent connections — what do you do? Use platform push notifications (APNs/FCM) to wake the app instead of holding an open socket in the background; only open WS/SSE while the app is foregrounded."},{"u":"topics/patterns/scaling-reads.html","p":"Scaling Reads","i":"","h":"Overview","t":"Patterns Scaling Reads Read replicas, caching strategies, and CDNs — the standard toolkit for read-heavy systems. 15-20 min Medium Say this in the first 2 minutes Most systems are read-heavy — solve reads before you ever think about sharding writes Layer the levers: cache first (cheapest, fastest), then read replicas, then CDN for static/edge content Cache-aside is the default caching pattern — check cache, miss falls to DB, populate cache, return Read replicas introduce replication lag — call out the read-your-writes problem explicitly Cache invalidation is the hard part, not the cache itself — say this and you sound experienced"},{"u":"topics/patterns/scaling-reads.html","p":"Scaling Reads","i":"mental-model","h":"Mental model","t":"Mental model Scaling reads is like serving customers at a bakery: a sticky note on the counter (cache) answers the common \"do you have croissants\" question instantly. If the note's outdated, you check the back kitchen (replica). For city-wide chains, a nearby branch (CDN edge) is faster than calling headquarters (origin) every time."},{"u":"topics/patterns/scaling-reads.html","p":"Scaling Reads","i":"core-concepts","h":"Core concepts","t":"Core concepts Term Definition Read replica A copy of the primary DB that serves reads; updated via async/sync replication Replication lag Delay between a write on the primary and its visibility on a replica Cache-aside App checks cache first; on miss, reads DB and populates the cache itself Write-through Every write goes to cache and DB together, synchronously Write-behind Write hits cache immediately; DB write happens asynchronously after CDN Geographically distributed edge cache for static/semi-static content Read-your-writes Guarantee that a user always sees their own write immediately after making it CDN edge cache ~5ms Redis cache hit ~1ms Read replica (same region) ~5-15ms Primary DB, cold query ~50-100ms Cross-region DB read ~150ms+"},{"u":"topics/patterns/scaling-reads.html","p":"Scaling Reads","i":"how-it-works","h":"Read replicas + cache-aside","t":"Read replicas + cache-aside App Primary DB writes Read Replicas N nodes async replication, ~ms-100ms lag Redis cache-aside 1. check cache first 2. miss → query replica 3. populate cache, return Checked first — hot path Source of truth — fallback on miss Check cache: app looks up the key in Redis first Hit: return immediately, ~1ms, no DB touched Miss: query a read replica, get the value Populate: write the value into the cache with a TTL, then return it Application instances serving reads All writes go to the single primary Reads spread across replicas — lower load per node App checks Redis before hitting any DB"},{"u":"topics/patterns/scaling-reads.html","p":"Scaling Reads","i":"tradeoffs","h":"Tradeoffs","t":"Tradeoffs Wins Cache-aside: simple, only caches what's actually requested Read replicas: linearly scales read throughput, isolates read load from writes CDN: removes cross-region latency entirely for cacheable content Costs Replication lag breaks \"read your own write\" without extra handling Cache invalidation on update is easy to get wrong (stale reads) Write-through adds write latency; write-behind risks data loss on cache crash"},{"u":"topics/patterns/scaling-reads.html","p":"Scaling Reads","i":"when-to-use","h":"When to use","t":"When to use ✓ Use cache-aside + replicas as the default for read-heavy APIs — product pages, profiles, feeds — where slight staleness is acceptable. ✓ Use a CDN for static assets and rarely-changing API responses (e.g. public catalog data) to cut latency for geographically distant users. ⚠ Watch for read-your-writes right after a user submits a form — route that specific read to the primary, or read from cache populated by the write itself."},{"u":"topics/patterns/scaling-reads.html","p":"Scaling Reads","i":"interview-moves","h":"Interview moves","t":"Interview moves 01 \"This is read-heavy, so I'll layer caching, then read replicas, then a CDN before I even consider sharding writes\" — states the order of operations explicitly 02 \"I'd use cache-aside as the default — populate on miss, so we only cache what's actually being read\" — names the pattern and justifies it over write-through 03 \"Replicas introduce lag, so right after a write I'd either read from primary or rely on the cache I just populated, to avoid the read-your-writes problem\" — anticipates a classic follow-up 04 \"Cache invalidation is the hard part — I'd use a short TTL plus explicit invalidation on write, rather than relying on TTL alone\" — shows you know caching isn't free 05 \"For static or rarely-changing data I'd push it to a CDN so cross-region users aren't paying our DB's round-trip latency\" — connects CDN choice to actual latency numbers"},{"u":"topics/patterns/scaling-reads.html","p":"Scaling Reads","i":"follow-ups","h":"Common follow-up questions","t":"Common follow-up questions When does replication lag actually matter? Right after a write, when the same user (or a dependent process) reads it back immediately and the replica hasn't caught up yet — causes a confusing \"my change disappeared\" bug. How do you keep cache and DB in sync on update? On write, invalidate (delete) the cache key rather than updating it in place — the next read repopulates it correctly. Updating the cache directly risks races with concurrent writers. What's a cache stampede and how do you prevent it? Many requests miss the same hot key simultaneously and all hit the DB at once. Prevent with a short-lived lock/single-flight on cache repopulation, or stagger TTLs with jitter. Write-through vs write-behind — when would you pick write-behind? Write-behind when write latency matters more than durability guarantees (e.g. view counters, analytics) — accept a small risk of losing the last few writes if the cache crashes before flushing. How many read replicas can you add before it stops helping? Each replica adds replication overhead on the primary and increases lag variance. Past 5-10 replicas, consider sharding or a dedicated read-optimized store (e.g. Elasticsearch, a CQRS read model) instead. How does a CDN know when to refresh stale content? Cache-Control headers set a TTL; on expiry the CDN revalidates with the origin (or you actively purge/invalidate specific paths on deploy/update)."},{"u":"topics/patterns/scaling-writes.html","p":"Scaling Writes","i":"","h":"Overview","t":"Patterns Scaling Writes Sharding, write buffering, async writes, and batching — how to absorb write volume a single DB can't handle. 20-25 min Hard Say this in the first 2 minutes Reach for write scaling only after caching and replicas are maxed out — it's the most operationally expensive lever Sharding splits data across N databases by a shard key — pick the key based on your access pattern Hash sharding spreads load evenly but kills range queries; range sharding enables range queries but risks hot shards A queue in front of the DB absorbs write spikes — the DB drains it at a steady, sustainable rate Async writes trade some durability for throughput — be explicit about what you're willing to lose"},{"u":"topics/patterns/scaling-writes.html","p":"Scaling Writes","i":"mental-model","h":"Mental model","t":"Mental model Scaling writes is like a post office overwhelmed at one counter. Sharding opens multiple counters, each handling a fixed slice of the alphabet. A queue is the line that forms outside — it lets people arrive in bursts while clerks process at a steady pace."},{"u":"topics/patterns/scaling-writes.html","p":"Scaling Writes","i":"core-concepts","h":"Core concepts","t":"Core concepts Term Definition Sharding Splitting a dataset across multiple independent databases by a shard key Hash sharding shard = hash(key) % N — evenly distributes load, destroys ordering Range sharding Shard boundaries are key ranges — keeps order, risks a hot shard Hot partition One shard receives disproportionate traffic (e.g. a viral key) Key salting Append a random suffix to a hot key to spread its writes across shards Write buffering A queue absorbs bursts; consumers write to the DB at a sustainable rate Batching Grouping multiple writes into one DB round-trip to cut per-write overhead"},{"u":"topics/patterns/scaling-writes.html","p":"Scaling Writes","i":"how-it-works","h":"Hash sharding — write path demo","t":"Hash sharding — write path demo Write(key) hash(key) % 4 router Shard N1 Shard N2 Shard N3 Shard N4 Client / gateway Cache — hot path Write key: user:482 Write key: user:17 Write key: user:903 Write key: user:256 Click a key — the router lights up only the shard that owns it. Every other shard is untouched. Hash: compute hash(shard_key) % N to deterministically pick a shard Route: send the write directly to that shard's connection Isolation: every other shard is untouched by this write — no cross-shard coordination needed Incoming write request carries a key e.g. user_id=482 hash(key) % 4 picks the target shard deterministically Shard N1 — owns hash bucket 0 Shard N2 — owns hash bucket 1 Shard N3 — owns hash bucket 2 Shard N4 — owns hash bucket 3"},{"u":"topics/patterns/scaling-writes.html","p":"Scaling Writes","i":"tradeoffs","h":"Tradeoffs","t":"Tradeoffs Hash sharding Even distribution — no single shard naturally gets more traffic Simple router logic, predictable capacity planning Cost: range queries (\"all orders this week\") require fanning out to every shard Range sharding Range queries stay efficient — adjacent keys live on the same shard Easy to reason about and rebalance by splitting a range Cost: sequential/skewed keys (timestamps, celebrity IDs) create hot shards"},{"u":"topics/patterns/scaling-writes.html","p":"Scaling Writes","i":"when-to-use","h":"When to use","t":"When to use ✓ Use sharding once a single primary can't handle write QPS even after read offload, write buffering, and vertical scaling are exhausted. ✓ Use a write buffer/queue for bursty traffic (flash sales, IoT spikes) so the DB drains writes at a steady rate instead of falling over. ✗ Avoid sharding too early — it adds cross-shard query complexity, rebalancing operations, and multi-shard transaction headaches. Exhaust simpler levers first."},{"u":"topics/patterns/scaling-writes.html","p":"Scaling Writes","i":"interview-moves","h":"Interview moves","t":"Interview moves 01 \"Before sharding, I'd check if caching, replicas, and batching already solve this — sharding is the most expensive lever\" — shows you don't reach for the heaviest tool first 02 \"I'd shard by user_id since almost every query in this system is scoped to a single user\" — justifies the shard key against actual access patterns, not arbitrarily 03 \"Hash sharding here since we don't need range queries — it gives even distribution and avoids hot shards\" — names the tradeoff explicitly 04 \"If one key gets disproportionate traffic, I'd salt it — append a random suffix and fan writes across several sub-keys\" — the standard hot-partition mitigation 05 \"I'd put a queue in front of the writer so traffic spikes get absorbed instead of overwhelming the DB directly\" — connects write buffering to real burst scenarios"},{"u":"topics/patterns/scaling-writes.html","p":"Scaling Writes","i":"follow-ups","h":"Common follow-up questions","t":"Common follow-up questions How do you pick a shard key? Pick the field most queries filter by, with high cardinality and even distribution. A bad key (e.g. status with 3 values) creates massive imbalance. How do you handle a query that needs to join across shards? Avoid it where possible by denormalizing. Otherwise, fan out to all shards in parallel and merge in the application layer — accept the latency cost. What's key salting and when do you need it? Appending a random suffix (e.g. user:482#3) to spread a hot key's writes across multiple shards/partitions, then aggregating reads across the salted variants. Needed when one key (a viral post, a popular product) dominates traffic. How do you rebalance shards as data grows? Hash sharding: use consistent hashing so adding a shard only remaps a fraction of keys. Range sharding: split an oversized range into two and migrate that slice only. What's the durability tradeoff with async writes? A write acknowledged to the client before it's durably persisted can be lost on crash. Mitigate with a write-ahead log or by accepting the loss for non-critical data (e.g. view counts). When does batching writes help, and when does it hurt? Helps when per-write overhead (network round-trip, transaction commit) dominates — batch 100s of rows per insert. Hurts latency-sensitive single writes, since you wait for the batch window to fill or flush."},{"u":"topics/question-breakdowns.html","p":"Question Breakdowns","i":"","h":"Overview","t":"Questions Question Breakdowns 30 canonical system design questions, each walked through the same 6-step Hello Interview framework end to end. 30 questions All interview-ready How to use this page Start with Easy, then Medium, then Hard — each tier assumes the patterns from the tier before it Every page follows the same 6 steps: Requirements → Core Entities → API → Data Flow → High-Level Design → Deep Dives Read the flash card at the top of each page first — it's the 5 things a strong candidate says in the first 2 minutes Practice delivery out loud with a timer — the time badge on each card is the realistic interview length Come back to the Follow-up Q&A section after a first pass once the core design is solid"},{"u":"topics/question-breakdowns.html","p":"Question Breakdowns","i":"easy","h":"Easy — start here","t":"Easy — start here 01 Foundational URL Shortener ID generation as the core problem, not redirect logic — cache-first reads, CDN in front 30-40 min Easy"},{"u":"topics/question-breakdowns.html","p":"Question Breakdowns","i":"medium","h":"Medium — build core instincts","t":"Medium — build core instincts 02 Read-heavy Design Bitly <10ms global reads, Snowflake IDs, 301 vs 302 as a product decision, async click analytics 35-45 min Medium 03 Crawling News Aggregator Crawl scheduling and cross-source dedup as the real problem, not storage — freshness vs crawl cost 30-40 min Medium 04 Isolation LeetCode / Code Judge Untrusted code execution as an isolation problem — sandboxing is the design challenge, not CRUD 30-40 min Medium 05 Geospatial Design Yelp Geo search and ranking as the core problem — reviews and business profiles are standard CRUD 30-40 min Medium 06 Time-series Design Strava Write-heavy GPS time series, segment matching against a spatial index, live leaderboards 30-40 min Medium 07 Infrastructure Design a Rate Limiter A shared distributed counter, not per-instance local state — token bucket vs sliding window 30-40 min Medium 08 Search Facebook Post Search Near-real-time indexing that enforces per-viewer privacy — the hard part isn't full-text search 30-40 min Medium 09 Scraping Price Tracking Service Keeping a scraper fleet alive against sites actively trying to block it — anti-detection over storage 30-40 min Medium 10 Approximation Top-K Trending Videos Exact top-K breaks at global scale — Count-Min Sketch and heavy-hitters approximation instead of atomic counters 30-40 min Medium"},{"u":"topics/question-breakdowns.html","p":"Question Breakdowns","i":"hard","h":"Hard — distributed systems under pressure","t":"Hard — distributed systems under pressure 11 Storage sync Design Dropbox Chunked, content-addressed sync — dedup, delta sync, and conflict resolution across devices 40-50 min Hard 12 Geospatial Local Delivery Service Geohash-based driver matching, exactly-once order state machine, hot-partition handling 40-50 min Hard 13 Contention Design Ticketmaster Distributed locking under extreme write contention — millions fighting over the same seats in 60s 40-50 min Hard 14 Fan-out Facebook News Feed Hybrid fan-out on write vs read — push for normal users, pull for celebrities past 1M followers 40-50 min Hard 15 Geospatial Design Tinder Geospatial + preference filtering before any ranking model runs — retrieval before ranking 40-50 min Hard 16 Messaging Design WhatsApp At-least-once delivery and per-conversation ordering across millions of long-lived connections 40-50 min Hard 17 Contention Design an Online Auction Optimistic locking on bid contention and distributed coordination for auction close 40-50 min Hard 18 Fan-out Facebook Live Comments Fan-out to millions of concurrent viewers per stream — pub/sub at extreme scale 40-50 min Hard 19 Consensus Design Online Chess Game state as a distributed consensus problem — identical board state on both clients, even through packet loss New 40-50 min Hard 20 Fan-out Design Instagram Photo storage and feed generation are two separate scaling problems — write-once blob storage vs. fan-out feed delivery 40-50 min Hard 21 Geospatial Design Uber Write-heavy driver location vs. read-heavy ride matching — different stores, plus ETA and surge pricing deep dives 40-50 min Hard 22 Consistency Design Robinhood Trade execution must be idempotent and exactly-once — a retry must never double-execute a buy or sell 40-50 min Hard 23 Collaboration Design Google Docs Real-time collaborative editing is an Operational Transformation problem — last-write-wins doesn't converge 40-50 min Hard 24 Infrastructure Design a Distributed Cache Consistent hashing and replication are the real problem — not \"store key/value,\" but remap the fewest keys when nodes change 40-50 min Hard 25 Coordination Design a Distributed Job Scheduler Leader election and exactly-once dispatch — cron on one box is a SPOF, cron on every box double-fires 40-50 min Hard 26 Crawling Design a Web Crawler URL frontier and dedup at billions-of-URLs scale — politeness constraints, not HTTP fetching, are the hard part 40-50 min Hard 27 Streaming Design an Ad Click Aggregator Real-time exactly-once counting at billions of events/day — advertisers are billed on these numbers 40-50 min Hard 28 Consistency Design a Payment System Multi-party ledger settlement plus idempotent charges — coordinating state with an async external processor 40-50 min Hard 29 Observability Design a Metrics Monitoring System Time-series writes at extreme scale vs. flexible queries — plus a low-latency path just for alerting 40-50 min Hard 30 ML Infra Design ChatGPT GPU inference capacity is the scarce resource — continuous batching, token streaming, and context-window limits 40-50 min Hard"},{"u":"topics/questions/ad-click-aggregator.html","p":"Design an Ad Click Aggregator","i":"","h":"Overview","t":"Questions Design an Ad Click Aggregator Tests whether you can build a real-time stream-aggregation pipeline with financial-grade exactly-once correctness at extreme write volume, not just a generic \"count things with Kafka\" answer. 45-55 min interview Hard Concept Map click to expand Say this in the first 2 minutes The core insight: this is a real-time stream-aggregation problem where correctness is a financial requirement, not an analytics nicety — advertisers are billed off these numbers, so double- or under-counting is a bug, not noise Key scale number to anchor on: ~100B events/day is ~1.2M events/sec average, with peak traffic (evening, campaign launches) running several times higher — this rules out any single-node aggregation Primary tradeoff being probed: real-time streaming aggregates are fast but approximate under failure/replay; you need a batch reconciliation path against the immutable raw log to guarantee exactness Component most candidates forget: a dedup key (event_id) at ingestion — Kafka only guarantees at-least-once delivery, so without idempotent aggregation a retried producer double-counts a click Failure mode most candidates skip: late-arriving events after a window has \"closed\" — watermarks and a grace period are required or you silently undercount every window boundary Ad Click Aggregator Ad Server Emits a click event the instant an ad is clicked step4-dataflow Fire-and-forget Never blocks the ad redirect At-least-once Duplicates are possible downstream Kafka Durable ordered log absorbing extreme write volume step5-hld Partitioned By ad_id for parallel processing Replay source Backs both streaming and batch Stream Processor Windows and aggregates clicks in near real time step6-deepdive-windowing Event-time windows Groups by when the click happened Approximate fast Exact counts come from batch later Aggregate Store Serves fast approximate counts to the dashboard step5-hld Low latency reads Dashboard never touches raw events Overwritten by batch Reconciliation replaces estimates Batch Reconciler Recomputes exact counts from the immutable raw log step6-deepdive-reconciliation Source of truth Billing uses this number not the stream Exactly-once Idempotent replay from the log"},{"u":"topics/questions/ad-click-aggregator.html","p":"Design an Ad Click Aggregator","i":"step1-requirements","h":"Requirements","t":"Step 1 of 6 — Requirements Requirements 1 2 3 4 5 6 Arc 1 (steps 1-5) — Satisfy Functional Requirements 1 2 3 4 5 6 Arc 2 (all 6 steps) — Satisfy Non-Functional Requirements Functional Ingest click and impression events from ad servers at massive scale Aggregate counts and spend per ad and per campaign over time windows Serve near-real-time stats to an advertiser-facing dashboard Reconcile streaming aggregates against ground truth periodically Non-Functional ~100B events/day, ~1.2M events/sec average, multi-x at peak Dashboard numbers fresh within seconds to low minutes Financial-grade correctness — no double-counting on retry or replay Raw events durable and replayable for audits and reconciliation What NOT to build: ad-targeting or bidding logic, a fraud-detection ML pipeline (worth a one-line mention as a separate system), or the billing/invoicing system itself. This is event ingestion, aggregation, and correctness — not deciding which ad to show or how an advertiser gets charged. 100B/day click + impression events/day ~1.2M/s average events/sec ingested <1 min aggregation freshness target 0 dupes correctness requirement on replay"},{"u":"topics/questions/ad-click-aggregator.html","p":"Design an Ad Click Aggregator","i":"step2-entities","h":"Core entities","t":"Step 2 of 6 — Core Entities Core entities Raw events are an immutable append-only log — the source of truth. Aggregates are derived, rebuildable state that can always be recomputed from the log. ClickEvent Kafka event_id UUID — dedup key ad_id BIGINT, partition key campaign_id BIGINT event_type TEXT — click | impression event_ts TIMESTAMP — origin time bid_amount DECIMAL — spend basis Because a Kafka topic partitioned by ad_id gives ordered, replayable delivery with the parallelism aggregation needs — event_id is what makes every downstream consumer idempotent on redelivery. Tradeoff: at-least-once delivery means dedup logic is mandatory, not optional. AdAggregate Wide-column ad_id + window_start composite PK campaign_id BIGINT click_count BIGINT — upserted impression_count BIGINT — upserted spend_total DECIMAL — upserted reconciled BOOL — batch-confirmed Because dashboards need fast per-ad/per-window rollups, not raw events — a wide-column store keyed by (ad_id, window_start) gives O(1) writes and fast range scans for time-series queries. Tradeoff: it's a derived view, so it must be safely rebuildable, never treated as ground truth. RawEventLog S3 / cold event_id UUID event_date, hour partition columns full event payload Parquet, append-only Because this is the single source of truth advertisers can be billed against — an immutable, infinitely-replayable log lets the batch job recompute exact aggregates independent of any streaming-layer bug. Tradeoff: cheap and durable, but query-hostile — never serves live traffic directly. Primary key: ClickEvent.event_id , generated at the ad server (not the client) to survive retries. Critical index: AdAggregate 's composite key on (ad_id, window_start) — this is what makes windowed upserts idempotent and range-scannable for a campaign's time series."},{"u":"topics/questions/ad-click-aggregator.html","p":"Design an Ad Click Aggregator","i":"step3-api","h":"API design","t":"Step 3 of 6 — API / Interface API design Event ingestion is a Kafka produce call, not REST — it's a fire-and-forget high-frequency write. Stats queries are REST; live dashboard updates use a WebSocket push. PRODUCE kafka: topic=ad-events, key=ad_id Auth: signed ad-server client Message { event_id, ad_id, campaign_id, event_type, event_ts, bid_amount } Ack producer acks=all; retried on timeout (event_id makes retry safe) GET /api/v1/campaigns/{id}/stats?window=1m Auth: required (advertiser) Response { clicks, impressions, spend, as_of, approximate: true } Source streaming aggregate store — fast, not yet reconciled GET /api/v1/campaigns/{id}/stats?window=1d&reconciled=true Auth: required (advertiser) Response { clicks, impressions, spend, as_of, approximate: false } Source batch-recomputed from RawEventLog — exact, used for billing WEBSOCKET /ws/campaigns/{id}/live Auth: required (advertiser) Message out { window_start, clicks, impressions, spend } Cadence pushed once per closed window (~1 min) Two stats endpoints, same shape, different truth: the 1m window is the streaming aggregate — fast but labeled approximate . The reconciled=true query only serves windows the batch job has already corrected — this is the number billing systems should read, never the raw streaming aggregate."},{"u":"topics/questions/ad-click-aggregator.html","p":"Design an Ad Click Aggregator","i":"step4-dataflow","h":"Data flow","t":"Step 4 of 6 — Data Flow Data flow Ingestion (amber, async) and dashboard reads (mint) share the aggregate store but are otherwise independent — one writes continuously, the other reads on demand. INGESTION PATH — ~1.2M events/sec average Ad Server Kafka Stream Processor Aggregate Store DASHBOARD READ PATH Dashboard Query Service Ad server + dashboard — entry points Kafka + stream processor — continuous async write path Aggregate store — shared, written by ingestion, read by dashboard Ad server emits an event — every click/impression gets a unique event_id generated server-side Event lands on Kafka — partitioned by ad_id so all events for one ad are processed in order by one consumer Stream processor aggregates — dedups by event_id, folds the event into its 1-minute window's running counts Aggregate store is upserted — per-(ad_id, window_start) row updated in place, idempotent on replay Dashboard reads the aggregate — query service serves the latest window, labeled approximate until reconciled Emits a click/impression event with a unique event_id per view Partitioned by ad_id at-least-once delivery Windowed aggregation dedup by event_id checkpointed Per-ad per-window rollups — written by ingestion read by the dashboard Advertiser dashboard polls or subscribes via WebSocket Serves 1m and reconciled 1d windows"},{"u":"topics/questions/ad-click-aggregator.html","p":"Design an Ad Click Aggregator","i":"step5-hld","h":"High-level design","t":"Step 5 of 6 — High-Level Design High-level design Streaming gives fast approximate numbers; a batch job reads the immutable raw log and overwrites each window with the exact, reconciled value. Ad Server fleet, stateless Kafka event ingestion Stream Processor windowed agg + dedup Aggregate Store wide-column / time-series Dashboard via query svc Raw Event Log S3 / cold storage Batch Reconciler exact recompute overwrites/corrects streaming numbers Query Service stats API + WS Ad server + dashboard — entry/exit points Kafka, stream processor, batch reconciler — async/continuous work Aggregate store + raw event log — durable, one derived, one source of truth Component Role Tech + why Kafka Durable, ordered event ingestion Partitioned by ad_id — because ordering per ad matters for windowed aggregation, and partitions give horizontal write scaling; tradeoff is at-least-once delivery, pushing dedup downstream. Stream Processor Windowed aggregation, dedup Flink-style with checkpointing — because exactly-once semantics need periodic durable state snapshots; tradeoff is added operational complexity vs a naive stateless consumer. Aggregate Store Per-ad/campaign rollups Wide-column store (e.g. Cassandra/Bigtable-style) — because writes are keyed upserts at huge volume and reads are range scans by time; tradeoff is eventual consistency across replicas. Raw Event Log Immutable source of truth Append-only object storage (e.g. S3, Parquet) — because it must outlive and out-verify the streaming layer; tradeoff is high query latency, never used for live serving. Batch Reconciler Recomputes exact aggregates Scheduled batch job (e.g. Spark) over the raw log — because it's the only component that recomputes from first principles, catching any streaming-layer drift; tradeoff is hours of latency, acceptable since it's correction, not the live path. Scaling levers — cheapest to most complex Add Kafka partitions and stream-processor parallelism low complexity Batch small event writes at the ad server before producing low complexity Pre-aggregate at the edge (ad server) for coarse counts medium complexity Shard the aggregate store by ad_id hash range medium complexity Tiered windows — 1m live, 1h and 1d materialized incrementally high complexity — last resort Bottleneck Triggers at Exact fix Stream processor CPU/state TRIGGERS AT Peak load several times average (>5-6M events/sec) Scale processor parallelism horizontally by Kafka partition count Aggregate store write hot-spot TRIGGERS AT A viral ad/campaign concentrates writes on one key Salt the partition key or pre-aggregate at the stream layer before the final upsert Batch reconciliation runtime TRIGGERS AT Raw log grows faster than the job's throughput Partition the batch job by date/hour shard, run incrementally instead of full-table Aggregation freshness: real-time streaming vs reconciled batch Real-time streaming Reconciled batch Correctness approach: exactly-once complexity vs at-least-once + idempotent dedup Exactly-once pipeline At-least-once + dedup Serves ads emits click/impression events with a unique event_id Partitioned by ad_id or campaign_id for ordered parallel consumption Flink-style: windowed aggregation checkpointing idempotent sink Per-ad/per-campaign rollups upserted by window Advertiser-facing dashboard near-real-time Every raw event is also archived here immutable and append-only Nightly/hourly job — recomputes exact aggregates directly from the log Serves both the fast streaming window and the reconciled window labeled accordingly"},{"u":"topics/questions/ad-click-aggregator.html","p":"Design an Ad Click Aggregator","i":"step6-deepdive-exactlyonce","h":"Deep dive: exactly-once stream aggregation","t":"Step 6 of 6 — Deep Dives Deep dive: exactly-once stream aggregation Kafka is at-least-once — retries and rebalances redeliver events. Correctness = idempotent aggregation, not eliminating redelivery. Kafka (redelivers) at-least-once delivery — duplicates possible Dedup Check event_id seen before? new Apply to Window duplicate Drop (no-op) Checkpoint Idempotent Upsert Sink keyed on event_id, naturally replay-safe on failure: restore from checkpoint, replay since Kafka — source of at-least-once redelivery Dedup check + window state — hot-path processing Checkpoint + upsert sink — durable, idempotent by key Event arrives, possibly a redelivery — Kafka's at-least-once guarantee means this is expected, not exceptional Dedup check against event_id — a bounded window of recently-seen ids (backed by the checkpointed state) catches duplicates New events apply to the window — folded into the running count for that (ad_id, window_start) State is checkpointed periodically — on a crash, the processor restores from the last checkpoint and replays only what's since it The sink itself is upsert-based — even if a duplicate slips past dedup, writing the same (key, value) twice is a no-op, not a double-count Two independent layers of defense: dedup at the processing stage catches most duplicates cheaply, and an idempotent upsert sink keyed by window makes the rare slip-through harmless — correctness doesn't depend on either one being perfect alone. Same event_id redelivered after a producer timeout or consumer rebalance Checks event_id against a bounded recently-seen set before folding into the window Applies the event to the running window count exactly once Event is silently dropped — already counted no-op Snapshotted periodically so state survives a processor crash Upsert keyed by (ad_id window_start) — replaying the same event_id twice is a no-op by construction"},{"u":"topics/questions/ad-click-aggregator.html","p":"Design an Ad Click Aggregator","i":"step6-deepdive-windowing","h":"Deep dive: windowed aggregation and watermarks","t":"Deep dive: windowed aggregation and watermarks Events group into fixed 1-minute tumbling windows . Watermark tracks how far event-time has progressed. Grace period holds windows open for stragglers, then finalizes. EVENT-TIME TIMELINE Window [12:00–12:01) Window [12:01–12:02) Window [12:02–12:03) late event, timestamped 12:00:58 arrives late, routed to origin window Watermark: 12:02:00 grace period: 30s past window close closed + finalized in grace period actively accumulating Tumbling 1-minute windows — non-overlapping, fixed size Events are bucketed by event-time — the timestamp the ad server assigned, not when the processor receives it Watermark tracks progress — an estimate of \"no more events earlier than this will arrive,\" derived from observed event-time lag A window closes when the watermark passes its end — triggering emission of that window's aggregate A grace period keeps it updatable briefly after — late events within the grace window still get folded in via the idempotent upsert Events later than the grace period are dropped or routed to reconciliation — the batch job is what ultimately catches these instead Grace period is a direct latency-vs-completeness knob: a longer grace period catches more late/network-delayed events but delays when a window is considered \"final\" for the dashboard — a 0s grace period is fastest but systematically undercounts every window by its straggler rate."},{"u":"topics/questions/ad-click-aggregator.html","p":"Design an Ad Click Aggregator","i":"step6-deepdive-reconciliation","h":"Deep dive: streaming vs batch reconciliation","t":"Deep dive: streaming vs batch reconciliation Two paths compute the same numbers on different timelines. Streaming : fast, but drifts under replay or dedup edge cases. Batch : slow, reads the immutable log — always exact. STREAMING PATH — seconds, approximate Kafka Stream Processor Aggregate Store approximate: true Dashboard (live) BATCH PATH — hours later, exact Raw Event Log S3, immutable Batch Reconciler Aggregate Store approximate: false Billing System same store, streaming rows overwritten in place Streaming: Kafka + processor — fast, continuous, approximate Batch: raw log + reconciler — slow, exact, overwrites in place Streaming produces a live number within seconds — good enough for a dashboard graph, labeled approximate Every raw event is also durably archived — the same event that feeds Kafka lands in the immutable raw log A batch job runs on a schedule — hourly or nightly, reading directly from the raw log, independent of any streaming state It recomputes the exact aggregate per window — no dedup ambiguity, no checkpoint edge cases, just a full deterministic recompute The reconciled value overwrites the streaming value — same store, same key, marked approximate: false ; billing only ever reads this value Why not just pick one: streaming alone can silently drift — a rare checkpoint-recovery edge case or a dedup-window eviction under extreme skew can under- or over-count a window, and you'd never know without an independent check. Batch alone is correct but hours stale, useless for a live dashboard. You need both: streaming for speed, batch as the financial-grade backstop. Failure mode User impact Detection signal Mitigation Stream processor node fails mid-window Brief gap in live dashboard updates Consumer lag spike, checkpoint age alert Checkpoint-based recovery resumes from last snapshot — no data loss, only brief reprocessing Burst of duplicate events (buggy ad server or bot traffic) Inflated click counts if undetected event_id dedup-hit rate spikes; anomalous click-to-impression ratio Dedup by event_id at ingestion; route suspected bot traffic to a separate fraud pipeline, out of scope for core counting Aggregate store falls behind under peak load Dashboard numbers lag further behind real time Kafka consumer lag growing, backpressure signal from sink Backpressure throttles ingestion acceptance; stream layer scales horizontally by adding partitions/consumers Same source as ingestion Windowed checkpointed idempotent — but can drift under a rare replay edge case Holds the fast live-but-approximate numbers Reads the fast numbers for live charts Immutable append-only the one true record of every event Nightly/hourly Spark job — recomputes counts and spend directly from raw events Overwrites the streaming numbers for each window with the exact recomputed value Billing always reads reconciled never streaming"},{"u":"topics/questions/ad-click-aggregator.html","p":"Design an Ad Click Aggregator","i":"interview-moves","h":"Interview moves","t":"Interview moves 01 \"These numbers are what advertisers get billed on, so correctness isn't a nice-to-have — a double-counted click is a financial bug, not an analytics rounding error\" — frames the entire problem correctly in one sentence 02 \"Kafka only gives me at-least-once delivery, so I need every event to carry a unique event_id and make the aggregation idempotent on redelivery\" — shows you know exactly where duplicates come from and how to neutralize them 03 \"I'd use tumbling 1-minute windows with a watermark and a short grace period — that's a direct latency-vs-completeness tradeoff I can tune\" — names the specific mechanism instead of hand-waving \"windowing\" 04 \"Streaming alone can drift under replay or a checkpoint edge case, so I'd run a batch job against the immutable raw log to reconcile the exact numbers on a schedule\" — proactively introduces the dual-path architecture before being asked 05 \"The raw event log in cold storage is the actual source of truth — the aggregate store is just a fast, rebuildable derived view\" — demonstrates clear separation between ground truth and cache-like derived state 06 \"Fraud and bot-click detection is a related but separate system — I'd keep it out of the core counting pipeline and mention it as a follow-on concern\" — scopes the problem correctly instead of overbuilding"},{"u":"topics/questions/ad-click-aggregator.html","p":"Design an Ad Click Aggregator","i":"follow-ups","h":"Follow-up questions","t":"Follow-up questions How do you handle a stream processor crashing mid-window? Checkpointed state is restored from the last durable snapshot; the processor replays events since that checkpoint, and idempotent upserts make any reprocessed events a no-op. What if the same event_id somehow gets two different aggregate values applied? It shouldn't under correct upsert semantics — the sink is keyed by window, not by event, so applying the same event twice recomputes the same delta, not an additional one. How do you choose the grace period length? Measure observed event-time lag (p99 network/ad-server delay) and set the grace period just past that — too short undercounts stragglers, too long delays dashboard finality. Why not just make the batch job the only source of truth and skip streaming? Advertisers expect near-real-time spend visibility to manage budgets live; a batch-only system with hours of lag fails that requirement even though it's simpler. How would you detect bot or fraudulent click traffic? A separate fraud-detection pipeline scores events on signals like click velocity per IP/device and click-through anomalies, then flags or excludes them before billing — decoupled from core counting. How does this scale if event volume grows 10x? Add Kafka partitions and stream-processor parallelism first (cheap, linear), then shard the aggregate store by ad_id range before considering edge pre-aggregation."},{"u":"topics/questions/bitly.html","p":"Design Bitly","i":"","h":"Overview","t":"Questions Design Bitly Tests whether you can hit <10ms global read latency on a read-heavy system while treating 301 vs 302 as a product decision, not a coin flip. 35-45 min interview Medium Concept Map click to expand Say this in the first 2 minutes The core insight: 301 vs 302 is a business decision, not a technical one — it trades browser-cached speed against click analytics visibility Key scale number to anchor on: peak redirect traffic can hit 1M RPS on a single viral link, far above steady-state Primary tradeoff being probed: strong click-count consistency vs an async pipeline that never touches the redirect's critical path Component most candidates forget: CDN edge caching in front of Redis — Redis alone doesn't survive a true viral spike Failure mode most candidates skip: what happens when the Kafka analytics pipeline backs up — it must degrade silently, never block a redirect Bitly Redirect Svc Stateless service that looks up a short code and issues the redirect step5-hld Go stateless Low per-request overhead on the hottest path Fires click event Emits to Kafka after responding non-blocking Shorten Svc Validates the URL and checks the custom alias namespace on write step2-entities Stateless pods Scales horizontally with no shared state Alias check Separate table so aliases never collide with codes Redis Hot short_code to long_url cache in front of Postgres step6-deepdive-cache Cache-aside Miss falls through to Postgres sets back CDN in front Edge cache absorbs viral-link spikes Postgres Source of truth for URL rows with a unique index on short_code step2-entities Unique index Redirect is always a point lookup never a scan Read replicas Absorb cache-miss reads at high volume Snowflake Bit-packed ID generator that mints codes with zero DB round-trip step6-deepdive-idgen 64-bit ID Timestamp plus node ID plus sequence Clock drift risk Node refuses IDs if its clock moves backward Kafka to ClickHouse Fire-and-forget click analytics pipeline fully decoupled from redirects step6-deepdive-analytics Kafka buffers bursts Absorbs viral spikes without backpressure ClickHouse aggregates Column store built for group-by-and-count"},{"u":"topics/questions/bitly.html","p":"Design Bitly","i":"step1-requirements","h":"Requirements","t":"Step 1 of 6 — Requirements Requirements Functional Shorten a long URL into a unique short code Redirect a short code to its original long URL Support custom aliases and link expiration Track click analytics — count, referrer, geo, time Non-Functional 300M links created, billions of redirects/day Redirect latency <10ms p99 globally 99.99% availability for redirects (writes can tolerate less) Zero collisions on generated codes; analytics can be eventually consistent What NOT to build: a full marketing analytics suite (funnels, cohorts, A/B test integration), team/workspace collaboration features, or link-in-bio landing pages. This is shorten + redirect + click counting only. ~12k/s write QPS (shorten) ~120k/s read QPS (redirect, avg) ~1M/s read QPS (peak, single viral link) 7 chars base62 short code"},{"u":"topics/questions/bitly.html","p":"Design Bitly","i":"step2-entities","h":"Core entities","t":"Step 2 of 6 — Core Entities Core entities Url Postgres id BIGINT (Snowflake) short_code VARCHAR(10) UNIQUE long_url TEXT is_custom BOOLEAN expires_at TIMESTAMP, nullable Because writes need a strong uniqueness constraint and volume is low relative to reads (12k/s vs 120k/s) — a relational store fits. Tradeoff: single-writer ceiling without sharding. CustomAlias Postgres alias VARCHAR(50) url_id BIGINT → Url.id Because a user-chosen alias must never collide with a Snowflake-derived code — separate table makes collision-checking a simple existence query. Tradeoff: two lookups on redirect instead of one. ClickEvent ClickHouse short_code String ts DateTime country LowCardinality(String) referrer String Because billions of append-only rows/day with a group-by-and-count query pattern is exactly what a column store is built for. Tradeoff: not a fit for point lookups or transactional guarantees. Primary key: Url.id (Snowflake). Critical index: UNIQUE INDEX on short_code — the redirect hot path is a point lookup against this index and must never scan."},{"u":"topics/questions/bitly.html","p":"Design Bitly","i":"step3-api","h":"API design","t":"Step 3 of 6 — API / Interface API design POST /api/v1/shorten Auth: optional Request { long_url, custom_alias?, expires_at? } Response { short_url, short_code, expires_at } GET /{short_code} Auth: none Request path param only Response HTTP 302 + Location. 404 if not found/expired DELETE /api/v1/urls/{short_code} Auth: required (owner) Request path param only Response HTTP 204, triggers cache + CDN purge GET /api/v1/urls/{short_code}/stats Auth: required (owner) Request path param only Response { clicks, top_countries, referrers } — from ClickHouse"},{"u":"topics/questions/bitly.html","p":"Design Bitly","i":"step4-dataflow","h":"Data flow","t":"Step 4 of 6 — Data Flow Data flow READ PATH — redirect Client CDN Redirect Svc stateless Redis miss Postgres WRITE PATH — shorten Client Shorten Svc Snowflake ID gen Postgres ASYNC PATH — analytics, never touches read or write above Kafka → ClickHouse Read path — cache + CDN first Write path — low volume, strong uniqueness Analytics — fully decoupled, fire-and-forget Client requests /{short_code} — hits CDN edge first CDN hit — returns cached 302, origin never touched CDN miss → Redirect Svc → Redis — checks hot mapping cache Response sent to client — 302 issued immediately once the mapping is resolved Click event fired to Kafka — happens after the response is already sent, never adds latency Browser or app following a shortened link Caches the 302 at edge — most redirects never reach origin Thin stateless lookup service — logs a click event on the way out In-memory hot mapping cache — resolves in ~1-3ms Source of truth touched only on cache miss Submits a long URL to be shortened Validates URL checks custom alias namespace Mints a globally unique ID with zero DB round-trip Persists the new short_code → long_url row Redirect Svc fires-and-forgets a click event here after already responding to the client"},{"u":"topics/questions/bitly.html","p":"Design Bitly","i":"step5-hld","h":"High-level design","t":"Step 5 of 6 — High-Level Design High-level design Clients CDN API Gateway Redirect Svc stateless Shorten Svc stateless Redis Postgres Snowflake ID gen Kafka ClickHouse Client / gateway CDN — edge cache Redis — hot mappings Postgres + ClickHouse Snowflake + Kafka Component Role Tech + why Redirect Svc Looks up short_code, issues redirect, fires click event Go — because low per-request overhead matters on the hottest path in the system; tradeoff is a smaller library ecosystem. Snowflake Mints unique 64-bit IDs Bit-packed generator — because it needs zero DB round-trip at 12k writes/sec; tradeoff is provisioning a node ID per instance. Kafka Buffers click events Log-based queue — because it absorbs bursty viral-link traffic without backpressure on the redirect; tradeoff is added infra to operate. ClickHouse Aggregates click analytics Column store — because the query pattern is group-by-and-count over billions of rows; tradeoff is poor fit for point lookups. Scaling levers — cheapest to most complex Add Redis + Postgres read replicas near-zero complexity Put CDN in front of the redirect service low complexity, high payoff for viral links Scale Kafka consumer group partitions for analytics throughput medium complexity Add more Snowflake node IDs as write throughput grows medium complexity Shard Postgres by short_code prefix high complexity — last resort Bottleneck Triggers at Exact fix Redis overload TRIGGERS AT Cache hit rate <90% Shard Redis by short_code hash; raise maxmemory with tuned LRU Single viral link hotspot TRIGGERS AT One code gets 1M+ RPS CDN caches the 302 at edge; Redis local replica per region Kafka consumer lag TRIGGERS AT Click event backlog grows unbounded Scale out consumer partitions; batch ClickHouse inserts larger Redirect choice: analytics fidelity vs server load 302 (analytics) 301 (browser cache) Click analytics consistency Strong Eventual Both shorten and redirect requests originate here Clients Client Browser / app Origin of both shorten and redirect requests. 0ms — request origin Edge cache for redirect responses CDN CDN Edge cache for 302 responses Caches redirect responses at the edge so most traffic never reaches origin infrastructure. ~1-5ms edge-local Auth rate limiting routing API Gateway Service Auth rate limiting routing Single entry point for auth and rate limiting before requests reach internal services. ~1-2ms overhead Stateless Redirect Svc Service Go stateless pods Go — because low per-request overhead matters on the hottest path in the system; tradeoff is a smaller library ecosystem. ~1-3ms on cache hit Stateless Shorten Svc Service Stateless pods Validates the URL and checks the custom alias namespace before persisting. ~10-20ms write path Hot mappings LRU eviction Redis Cache In-memory cache LRU eviction Hot mapping cache in front of Postgres resolves the redirect lookup in ~1-3ms. ~1-3ms Source of truth primary + replicas Postgres Database Relational primary + read replicas Writes need a strong uniqueness constraint and volume is low relative to reads — a relational store fits; tradeoff is a single-writer ceiling without sharding. ~15-30ms on cache miss Node ID + timestamp + sequence bit-packed Snowflake Service Bit-packed ID generator Mints unique 64-bit IDs with zero DB round-trip at 12k writes/sec; tradeoff is provisioning a node ID per instance. <1ms no network call Buffers click event bursts Kafka Queue Log-based queue Absorbs bursty viral-link click traffic without adding backpressure on the redirect; tradeoff is added infra to operate. async no redirect-path latency Column store for click aggregation ClickHouse Database Column store Query pattern is group-by-and-count over billions of rows; tradeoff is a poor fit for point lookups. batched inserts every 1-5s"},{"u":"topics/questions/bitly.html","p":"Design Bitly","i":"step6-deepdive-idgen","h":"Deep dive: Snowflake ID generation","t":"Step 6 of 6 — Deep Dives Deep dive: Snowflake ID generation 1 sign 41 timestamp (ms) 10 node ID 12 sequence 1 bit unused, keeps ID positive 41 bits — ms since custom epoch, ~69 years 10 bits — up to 1024 nodes 12 bits — up to 4096 IDs/node/ms Read current timestamp — ms since a custom epoch, not Unix epoch Attach node ID — fixed per generator instance at boot Increment sequence — local counter reset every millisecond Bit-pack and return — no network call, no lock contention Base62-encode for the short code — the 64-bit int becomes the human-facing code Clock drift is the real failure mode: if a node's clock moves backward, it must refuse to generate IDs until it catches up, or two different timestamps could produce a colliding ID."},{"u":"topics/questions/bitly.html","p":"Design Bitly","i":"step6-deepdive-cache","h":"Deep dive: cache hit vs miss","t":"Deep dive: cache hit vs miss ✓ Cache hit — ~1-3ms Redirect GET code Redis key found ~1-3ms key exists → 302 immediately ▸ Replay ✗ Cache miss — ~15-30ms Redirect GET code Redis MISS SET back Postgres ~15-30ms miss → query → cache → return ▸ Replay Redirect Svc queries Redis In-memory lookup Redirect Svc queries Redis Falls through to Postgres SETs back Indexed lookup sub-30ms"},{"u":"topics/questions/bitly.html","p":"Design Bitly","i":"step6-deepdive-analytics","h":"Deep dive: analytics pipeline never blocks the redirect","t":"Deep dive: analytics pipeline never blocks the redirect Fire-and-forget emit — the redirect service publishes a click event after already sending the 302, non-blocking Kafka buffers the burst — a viral link spiking to 100k clicks/sec just queues, doesn't overwhelm consumers Consumer batches writes — analytics consumer bulk-inserts into ClickHouse every 1-5s instead of row-by-row ClickHouse aggregates on read — dashboards query grouped counts, exactly what a column store is built for Scenario User impact Detection signal Mitigation Redis cluster down Redirect latency spikes 10-30x Cache miss rate alert >50% Circuit breaker falls back to Postgres; auto-scale replicas Postgres primary down Writes fail; cache misses fail Write error rate + replication lag alarm Automated failover promotes a read replica Kafka broker outage None to redirects; analytics stalls Consumer lag climbing, producer errors Redirect drops click events after a short retry — analytics loss is acceptable, redirects are not CDN misconfiguration All redirect traffic hits origin, 10x+ spike Origin request rate anomaly Rate limit at gateway; redirect pods auto-scale Snowflake node clock drift Potential duplicate ID (rare) NTP drift alert on generator hosts Node refuses to generate IDs until clock catches up"},{"u":"topics/questions/bitly.html","p":"Design Bitly","i":"interview-moves","h":"Interview moves","t":"Interview moves 01 \"Before I design anything, let me clarify the read/write ratio — this is roughly 1000:1 read-heavy which changes the entire caching strategy\" — anchors every downstream decision to a stated number 02 \"302 vs 301 here is a business decision, not a technical one — 301 would get cached by the browser and we'd lose click visibility\" — ties a technical choice directly to a stated requirement 03 \"I'll decouple click tracking into Kafka so a slow analytics write never adds latency to the redirect's critical path\" — demonstrates instinct for isolating hot paths from side effects 04 \"Custom aliases go into a separate table from generated codes so a user-chosen alias can never silently collide with a Snowflake-derived one\" — a subtlety most candidates miss entirely 05 \"At 1M peak RPS on a single viral link, even Redis needs help — I'd lean on CDN edge caching so most of that traffic never reaches our infrastructure\" — shows caching has layers, not just one tier 06 \"If the Kafka pipeline backs up, I'd rather drop click events than let the redirect service block waiting on it\" — states a concrete, correct failure-mode priority unprompted"},{"u":"topics/questions/bitly.html","p":"Design Bitly","i":"follow-ups","h":"Follow-up questions","t":"Follow-up questions How do you prevent short code enumeration attacks? Rate limit per IP/user at the gateway. Base62 with 7+ chars gives 62^7 ≈ 3.5 trillion combinations, making brute-force scanning impractical. What if the same long URL is submitted by two different users? Each gets a distinct short code by default. Dedup by hashing long_url would save storage but adds a lookup on every write and complicates per-user ownership. How would you extend this to support 10x the scale? Shard Postgres by short_code prefix, extend the code to 8+ characters, add more Snowflake node IDs, make CDN edge caching mandatory for all traffic. How do you handle a malicious long URL (phishing, malware)? Check against a Safe Browsing API synchronously at shorten time, re-scan periodically, and allow user reports to flag and auto-disable a short code. Why not just use a DB auto-increment given the write QPS is relatively low? It works at 12k/s on a single writer but is a single point of contention with no horizontal scaling path — Snowflake removes that ceiling for a small added operational cost. How do you keep the CDN and Redis cache consistent when a URL is deleted? Short TTL (~60s) on both as a safety net, plus an explicit purge/invalidate call fired on delete rather than relying on TTL expiry alone."},{"u":"topics/questions/chatgpt.html","p":"Design ChatGPT","i":"","h":"Overview","t":"Questions Design ChatGPT Tests whether you recognize this as a GPU-capacity-and-scheduling problem, not a CRUD problem. The scarce resource is GPU compute — batching, streaming, and context management all exist to squeeze more out of it. 45-55 min interview Hard Concept Map click to expand Say this in the first 2 minutes The core insight: this is a GPU-inference-capacity-and-scheduling problem, not a storage problem — GPUs are the expensive, limited resource everything else is designed around Key scale number to anchor on: 100M weekly active users generating concurrent requests against a GPU fleet that can only run so many forward passes at once Primary tradeoff being probed: time-to-first-token (must feel instant, <1s) vs total generation time (can legitimately take several seconds) — solved by streaming, not by generating faster Component most candidates forget: continuous batching — naively running one request per GPU call wastes most of the GPU's capacity Failure mode most candidates skip: a conversation growing past the model's context window mid-session needs a graceful truncation/summarization strategy, not a hard error ChatGPT API Gateway Auth and rate limiting before a request reaches a GPU step3-api Rate limits Protects scarce GPU capacity Routing Sends requests to the batcher Scheduler / Batcher Groups concurrent requests into GPU-efficient batches step6-deepdive-batching Continuous batching New requests join a batch mid-flight GPU utilization The core scarce-resource problem GPU Worker Fleet Runs the actual model inference step5-hld Scarce resource Everything else exists to use it well Autoscaled Fleet size tracks demand Stream Router Pushes generated tokens back as they are produced step6-deepdive-streaming Token streaming User sees output incrementally Lower perceived latency First token matters more than total time Conversation DB Stores message history and context per conversation step2-entities Context window Bounds how much history is replayed Truncation policy Oldest turns drop first"},{"u":"topics/questions/chatgpt.html","p":"Design ChatGPT","i":"step1-requirements","h":"Requirements","t":"Step 1 of 6 — Requirements Requirements 1 2 3 4 5 6 Arc 1 (steps 1-5) — Satisfy Functional Requirements 1 2 3 4 5 6 Arc 2 (all 6 steps) — Satisfy Non-Functional Requirements Functional User sends a message and receives a generated response in the same conversation Response streams back token-by-token as it's generated, not all at once Conversation history persists so follow-up turns have context Input and output are checked by a content moderation/safety filter Non-Functional 100M weekly active users, bursty concurrent load across time zones Time-to-first-token <1s even though full generation takes several seconds Maximize GPU utilization — inference capacity is the scarce, expensive resource Long conversations must stay within the model's context window without abrupt context loss What NOT to build: the model training/fine-tuning pipeline, a plugin/tool-calling ecosystem, or billing/subscription tiers. This is the inference-serving path for an existing trained model only. 100M weekly active users <1s time-to-first-token target ~30-50/s tokens generated per second, per request 128k tokens context window size"},{"u":"topics/questions/chatgpt.html","p":"Design ChatGPT","i":"step2-entities","h":"Core entities","t":"Step 2 of 6 — Core Entities Core entities Conversation and Message are durable records in a relational store; InferenceRequest is ephemeral in-flight scheduler state that only exists for the seconds a generation is running. Conversation Postgres conversation_id UUID user_id BIGINT title TEXT — auto-generated model TEXT updated_at TIMESTAMP Because conversation metadata is small, low-write-frequency, and needs to support listing/sorting by recency — a standard relational row is the right fit. Tradeoff: needs a per-user index on updated_at for the conversation list view. Message Postgres message_id UUID conversation_id UUID → Conversation role TEXT — user/assistant/system content TEXT — full turn text seq INT — ordering within conversation Because every turn must be retrievable in order to reconstruct context for the next generation, and appended-only writes are cheap in a relational store at this scale. Tradeoff: long conversations mean a growing row set per conversation_id — needs the context-window strategy from Step 6. InferenceRequest Redis / in-memory request_id UUID conversation_id UUID prompt_tokens encoded input, in scheduler queue generated_tokens growing list, streamed as produced status queued / batching / streaming / done Because in-flight generation state lives only as long as the GPU worker holds it in memory — it's ephemeral scheduler/KV-cache state, not something worth persisting durably. Tradeoff: a worker crash loses this state entirely, which is why retry/resume design matters (see Step 6 failure modes). Primary key: Message.message_id with a composite index on (conversation_id, seq) for fast ordered retrieval of the last N turns. InferenceRequest has no durable index at all — it's scheduler state, not a queryable entity."},{"u":"topics/questions/chatgpt.html","p":"Design ChatGPT","i":"step3-api","h":"API design","t":"Step 3 of 6 — API / Interface API design Sending a message returns a streamed response over SSE so the client can render tokens as they arrive; everything else is plain REST since it's low-frequency metadata access. POST /api/v1/conversations/{id}/messages Auth: required — SSE streaming response Request { content: \"user message text\" } Response (stream) event: token → { text } ... event: done → { message_id, usage } GET /api/v1/conversations/{id} Auth: required Request path param: conversation id Response { conversation_id, title, messages: [...] } GET /api/v1/conversations Auth: required Request ?cursor&limit — paginated, sorted by updated_at Response { conversations: [{ id, title, updated_at }], next_cursor } DELETE /api/v1/conversations/{id} Auth: required (owner only) Request path param: conversation id Response 204 on success, 403 if not owner SSE over plain REST for the message endpoint: generation legitimately takes seconds, and the whole point of the design is that the user should see tokens the instant they're produced rather than staring at a blank screen until the full response is ready."},{"u":"topics/questions/chatgpt.html","p":"Design ChatGPT","i":"step4-dataflow","h":"Data flow","t":"Step 4 of 6 — Data Flow Data flow A new message triggers a write/batch path (blue write, amber async batching) that queues the request for GPU inference, and a read path (mint) that streams generated tokens back as they're produced. WRITE + BATCH PATH — new user message Client (Web/App) Moderation input filter Conversation DB Scheduler continuous batching STREAM PATH — tokens as they're generated GPU Worker Stream Router SSE fan-out Client (Web/App) Client — sends message, renders streamed tokens Conversation DB — durable message history Scheduler + GPU worker — batching and generation, off the synchronous request path Client sends the message, opens a stream — POST with an SSE connection kept open for the reply Moderation filters the input — blocks disallowed content before it reaches the model Conversation DB records the turn and assembles context — recent history is packed into the prompt Scheduler queues the request into a batch — joins an in-progress continuous batch rather than waiting for a dedicated GPU call GPU worker streams tokens as generated — each token is pushed to the client within milliseconds of being produced, not buffered until the end Sends the user's message opens an SSE connection for the reply Input safety filter runs before the prompt reaches the model Appends the message assembles conversation context for the prompt Queues the request joins it into an in-progress GPU batch Emits one token at a time as the forward pass produces it Forwards each token immediately over the open connection Renders each token the instant it arrives"},{"u":"topics/questions/chatgpt.html","p":"Design ChatGPT","i":"step5-hld","h":"High-level design","t":"Step 5 of 6 — High-Level Design High-level design Everything upstream of the GPU fleet exists to keep GPUs fed with work and to get tokens back to the client the instant they exist — that's the entire design philosophy. Client web / mobile app API Gateway auth, rate limit Moderation input + output filter Conversation DB Postgres Scheduler / Batcher continuous batching GPU Worker Fleet model + KV cache GPU Worker Fleet model + KV cache Stream Router SSE fan-out Stream Router SSE fan-out tokens streamed back to the same open client connection Client — holds the open SSE connection for the whole generation Conversation DB — durable history, read to build context each turn Scheduler + GPU fleet — the scarce resource, kept busy via batching Stream Router — the hot path this whole design optimizes for Component Role Tech + why API Gateway AuthN, rate limiting, request routing Standard edge gateway — because per-user rate limits protect scarce GPU capacity from any single abusive client; tradeoff is one more network hop before generation even starts. Moderation Filters input prompts and output tokens Lightweight classifier model, run inline on both sides — because letting disallowed content reach the model or the user is a product and legal risk; tradeoff is added latency on the critical path, so it must be fast and cheap relative to the LLM itself. Scheduler / Batcher Groups concurrent requests into GPU-efficient batches Continuous/dynamic batching queue — because GPUs are dramatically more efficient processing many requests together than one at a time; tradeoff is added scheduling complexity and a queuing delay under load spikes. GPU Worker Fleet Runs the model forward pass, generates tokens Model replicas across a GPU cluster, each holding per-request KV-cache — because this is the actual expensive resource; tradeoff is that worker state is not easily checkpointed, so a crash loses in-flight generations. Stream Router Delivers tokens to the client as produced SSE (or WebSocket) fan-out layer — because time-to-first-token matters far more than total transfer time; tradeoff is holding one open connection per active generation. Bottleneck Triggers at Exact fix GPU inference capacity TRIGGERS AT Concurrent request count exceeds what the fleet can batch and serve within latency targets Add GPU capacity; prioritize shorter/simpler requests; quantize the model for higher throughput per GPU Context assembly latency TRIGGERS AT Long conversations require reading and re-encoding large history on every turn Cache the KV-state across turns where possible; apply the summarization/truncation strategy from Step 6 Stream fan-out under load TRIGGERS AT Very high concurrent open-connection count Horizontally scale the stream router layer; connections are lightweight compared to GPU work Scaling levers — cheapest to most complex Increase max batch size within latency budget low complexity Add request prioritization (e.g. shorter prompts first) to smooth queue wait low complexity Add more GPU worker replicas behind the scheduler medium complexity — costly, but straightforward Quantize the model to trade a little quality for significantly higher tokens/sec per GPU medium complexity Speculative decoding with a smaller draft model to generate multiple tokens per full-model pass high complexity — last resort Batching: single-request latency vs GPU throughput Per-request latency GPU throughput Context management: full history vs summarized/truncated Full history Summarized/truncated Sends messages receives streamed tokens over SSE AuthN rate limiting routes to the moderation + scheduling path Checks both incoming prompts and outgoing tokens for disallowed content Durable conversation + message history used to build prompt context Continuous batching — groups concurrent requests to maximize GPU throughput Runs the model forward pass holds KV-cache per active request Runs the model forward pass holds KV-cache per active request Streams generated tokens back as they're produced fans out over SSE Same router handles the return path for all workers"},{"u":"topics/questions/chatgpt.html","p":"Design ChatGPT","i":"step6-deepdive-batching","h":"Deep dive: continuous batching for GPU efficiency","t":"Step 6 of 6 — Deep Dives Deep dive: continuous batching for GPU efficiency A naive one-request-per-GPU-call model wastes most of the GPU's capacity, because each forward pass can process many requests' tokens at once for nearly the same cost as one. NAIVE — one request per GPU call Request A (GPU idle for rest) Request B waits Request C waits total time = A + B + C, run one after another — GPU mostly idle relative to its batch capacity CONTINUOUS BATCHING — requests share the GPU concurrently Request A — tokens 1..N Request B — joins in-progress batch Request C — finishes, leaves Request D — joins as C leaves GPU batch stays near-full at all times; requests of different lengths enter and exit independently Cache — hot path Async — queue / worker New request arrives at the scheduler — it doesn't wait for a fresh batch to form It's inserted into the current in-flight batch — the next GPU forward pass processes it alongside whatever requests are already generating Each request advances one token per pass — requests are not forced to share the same total length A request that finishes generating leaves the batch immediately — its GPU slot is freed for the next arrival The batch composition changes continuously — this is what \"continuous\" or \"dynamic\" batching means, as opposed to static batches that wait to fill up before starting The key mental model: a GPU forward pass costs roughly the same whether it's processing 1 request's token or 64 requests' tokens simultaneously — so the entire scheduling problem is about keeping that batch as full as possible, at all times, without forcing requests to align in length. Request A occupies the whole GPU alone for its full duration Request B has to wait for A to fully finish before it can start Most of the GPU's capacity per call goes unused Joins the batch when it arrives generating tokens alongside whatever is already in-flight Joins mid-batch as soon as it arrives no need to wait for A or C to finish Finishes early and leaves; its GPU slot is immediately reused by the next arrival A new request slots into the batch the moment a GPU cycle is free no fixed batch boundary"},{"u":"topics/questions/chatgpt.html","p":"Design ChatGPT","i":"step6-deepdive-streaming","h":"Deep dive: token streaming to the client","t":"Deep dive: token streaming to the client Full generation can take several seconds, but the user should see the first token in well under a second — streaming decouples perceived latency from total generation time. GOOD — stream each token immediately Client GPU Worker prompt sent token 1 → <1s (time-to-first-token) token 2 → arrives moments later token 3, 4, 5... → each rendered as it lands user is reading tokens 1-3 while token 8 is still being generated BAD — wait for the full response first Client GPU Worker buffers full response full response → several seconds later, all at once user stares at a blank/loading screen for the entire generation time Client / gateway Async — queue / worker Client sends the prompt and opens a streaming connection — SSE or WebSocket, held open for the duration of generation GPU worker emits each token the instant it's produced — it does not wait to accumulate a full sentence or the full response Stream router forwards every token with no added buffering — its only job is to not introduce latency between worker and client Client renders tokens as they arrive — the user perceives the response as \"already happening,\" not \"loading\" A final event signals completion — carries metadata like token usage so the client can close the stream cleanly Time-to-first-token and total generation time are different metrics that get solved differently: TTFT is mostly about queue/batch wait time at the scheduler, while total time is bounded by tokens/sec throughput per request — optimizing one doesn't automatically fix the other. Client opens the connection and waits for the first byte Generates token 1 pushes it immediately — no buffering Client sends the prompt and gets nothing back until generation fully completes Buffers the entire response several seconds of generation before sending anything"},{"u":"topics/questions/chatgpt.html","p":"Design ChatGPT","i":"step6-deepdive-context","h":"Deep dive: conversation context management","t":"Deep dive: conversation context management Every new turn needs the prior conversation as context, but the model has a fixed context window — once a conversation grows past it, something has to give. EVERY TURN — retrieve history, assemble prompt New Message Conversation DB Context Assembler Fits context window? token budget check yes → send as-is no → apply a strategy Truncation drop oldest turns Summarization compress older turns Sliding Window keep last K turns loses early detail entirely keeps gist, loses exact wording simple, but abrupt cutoff Client / gateway Database — source of truth Async — queue / worker New message arrives for an existing conversation — the system needs prior turns to respond coherently Prior turns are retrieved in order — read from the Conversation DB by conversation_id Context is assembled and checked against the token budget — the model's context window is a hard limit, not a soft target If it fits, the prompt is sent as-is — the common case for most conversations If it doesn't fit, a strategy kicks in — truncation drops oldest turns, summarization compresses them, or a sliding window keeps only the most recent K turns — each trades detail for staying in budget Summarization has the best user experience — it approximates \"the model still remembers\" — but costs an extra inference call to produce the summary and risks compounding drift if summaries are repeatedly re-summarized across a very long conversation. Scenario User impact Detection signal Mitigation GPU worker crashes mid-generation Client sees a partial, cut-off response Worker heartbeat loss; stream connection drops mid-token Clean retry with the same prompt on a healthy worker — GPU/KV-cache state isn't easily recoverable, so resuming in-place isn't viable; a resumable protocol can replay already-streamed tokens if partial output is worth preserving Inference queue backs up under a traffic spike Requests wait far longer than the TTFT target Queue depth / wait-time percentiles cross threshold Show a visible queue position or wait estimate instead of a silent hang; shed load with a clear \"try again shortly\" error rather than an inexplicably slow response Conversation exceeds the context window mid-session Risk of abrupt, user-visible loss of earlier context Assembled prompt token count crosses the model's limit Trigger summarization/truncation automatically before the limit is hit, so the transition is invisible rather than a hard error on the next turn New user message arrives for an existing conversation Fetches prior turns for this conversation_id ordered by seq Packs retrieved turns plus the new message into the model's input Checks whether assembled context fits the model's token budget Drop the oldest turns entirely to make room for the newest ones Compress older turns into a short summary keep recent turns verbatim Keep only the most recent K turns always slide forward"},{"u":"topics/questions/chatgpt.html","p":"Design ChatGPT","i":"interview-moves","h":"Interview moves","t":"Interview moves 01 \"The scarce resource here isn't storage or bandwidth, it's GPU inference capacity — every design decision downstream exists to use that capacity more efficiently\" — states the core insight unprompted, reframes the whole problem correctly 02 \"I wouldn't run one request per GPU call — I'd use continuous batching so new requests join an in-progress batch as others finish, keeping the GPU near-fully utilized\" — names the single highest-leverage optimization 03 \"Time-to-first-token and total generation time are different problems — streaming solves perceived latency without needing to generate any faster\" — shows you're not conflating two separate metrics 04 \"A GPU worker crash mid-generation can't be resumed cleanly since KV-cache state isn't easily recoverable — I'd retry the same prompt on a healthy worker rather than trying to patch the broken stream\" — demonstrates you understand what's actually recoverable in this system 05 \"As a conversation grows past the context window, I'd rather summarize or slide the window than throw a hard error — the user should never feel an abrupt context cliff\" — surfaces a scale problem most candidates only discover when prompted 06 \"Under a load spike, I'd rather show the user a queue position than let requests silently time out — a predictable wait beats an inexplicable one\" — shows product sense about graceful degradation, not just system mechanics"},{"u":"topics/questions/chatgpt.html","p":"Design ChatGPT","i":"follow-ups","h":"Follow-up questions","t":"Follow-up questions Why not just give every request its own dedicated GPU? GPUs are expensive and mostly idle during a single request's memory-bound decode step — continuous batching lets one GPU serve many concurrent requests for nearly the cost of one. How do you keep time-to-first-token low when the queue is deep? Prioritize scheduling so new requests slot into the next batch cycle quickly, and shed or queue-signal load rather than letting wait times grow unbounded. What happens to in-flight generations if a GPU worker needs to be taken down for a deploy? Drain the worker — stop admitting new requests to its batch, let in-flight ones finish, then take it offline; abrupt kills should be reserved for actual crashes. How would you reduce moderation's added latency on the critical path? Run it as a small, fast classifier co-located with the gateway rather than a full model call, and run input and output checks in parallel with generation where safe. How do you decide between truncation, summarization, and a sliding window for context overflow? Sliding window is simplest and cheapest but loses early detail abruptly; summarization preserves gist at the cost of an extra inference call — most production systems use summarization once a conversation crosses a length threshold. How would you scale this to support much longer responses (e.g. long-form documents)? The streaming and batching model doesn't change — you'd mainly need higher per-request token budgets, longer-lived connections, and schedulers tolerant of more variance in request length within a batch."},{"u":"topics/questions/distributed-cache.html","p":"Design a Distributed Cache","i":"","h":"Overview","t":"Questions Design a Distributed Cache Tests whether you see this as a consistent-hashing + replication problem, not a \"store key/value in a hash map\" problem — the hard part is remapping keys cheaply and surviving node failure without a thundering herd. 40-50 min interview Hard Concept Map click to expand Say this in the first 2 minutes The core insight: distributing keys across nodes is a consistent-hashing problem — the goal is that adding/removing a node remaps the minimum number of keys, not the whole keyspace Key scale number to anchor on: 10M ops/sec across the cluster at sub-millisecond p99 — this rules out any design that hits disk or crosses a network hop more than once per request Primary tradeoff being probed: replication factor and consistency for replica reads vs raw throughput and memory cost Component most candidates forget: a coordination/membership layer that tracks ring topology — without it, nodes disagree about who owns which key Failure mode most candidates skip: thundering herd — when a hot key expires or a node dies, thousands of clients miss cache at once and hammer the database simultaneously Distributed Cache Client Library Ring-aware hashes keys locally and routes directly to the owner step6-deepdive-ring No proxy hop Skips a network hop for sub-ms latency Topology cached Holds a local copy of ring shape Cache Nodes In-memory key-value storage sharded via consistent hashing step5-hld Virtual nodes Spread ownership for even load RAM only No disk round trip on the hot path Replica Nodes Standby copy per shard so a primary failure does not lose data step5-hld Async or sync Consistency dial per write Failover target Promoted when primary dies Coordination Layer Gossip or etcd tracks ring topology and node health step6-deepdive-ring Gossip protocol Peer-to-peer topology sync Node status alive suspect or dead Backing Database Source of truth only touched on a cache miss step5-hld Miss path only Shielded from most traffic Herd protection Request coalescing guards it"},{"u":"topics/questions/distributed-cache.html","p":"Design a Distributed Cache","i":"step1-requirements","h":"Requirements","t":"Step 1 of 6 — Requirements Requirements 1 2 3 4 5 6 Arc 1 (steps 1-5) — Satisfy Functional Requirements 1 2 3 4 5 6 Arc 2 (all 6 steps) — Satisfy Non-Functional Requirements Functional GET/PUT/DELETE a key-value pair with sub-millisecond latency Keys support a TTL after which they expire automatically Cluster can grow or shrink by adding/removing nodes with minimal disruption A single node failure does not lose the whole cache or stall traffic Non-Functional 10M ops/sec sustained across the cluster Sub-millisecond p99 latency per operation Adding/removing a node remaps only a small fraction of keys, not the whole keyspace No thundering herd on the backing database when keys expire or a node fails What NOT to build: no persistent durability guarantees like a database (data loss on full outage is acceptable), no complex query language (no joins, no secondary indexes, no range scans), no cross-datacenter replication (single-region cluster only). 10M/s ops/sec across the cluster <1ms p99 latency target ~200 cache nodes in the cluster 2x replication factor per shard"},{"u":"topics/questions/distributed-cache.html","p":"Design a Distributed Cache","i":"step2-entities","h":"Core entities","t":"Step 2 of 6 — Core Entities Core entities Everything here is in-memory except the ring topology metadata, which needs to be durable enough that a coordination service restart doesn't lose cluster shape. CacheEntry In-memory key string, hashed onto ring value bytes, size-bounded ttl_expires_at epoch ms last_accessed_at for LRU tracking Because it's the hot-path unit of work — read and written far more than any other entity, so it lives entirely in RAM with no disk round-trip. Tradeoff: a node crash loses every entry it held until a replica takes over. RingTopology Coord service node_id string vnode_hashes array of ring positions status alive / suspect / dead version monotonic, for gossip convergence Because every client and node needs a consistent view of who owns which key range — this is metadata, not cached data, so it's held in a small, replicated coordination store, not the cache nodes themselves. Tradeoff: staleness here causes brief misrouting until gossip converges. ReplicaSet In-memory + coord shard_id ring range identifier primary_node node_id replica_nodes array of node_id, size = RF Because a shard needs at least one standby copy to survive a primary failure without a full cache-miss storm on that shard's key range. Tradeoff: every write now costs one extra network hop for replication. Primary key: CacheEntry.key , hashed via consistent hashing (not a plain modulo) to determine ring position. Critical metadata index: RingTopology.vnode_hashes — this sorted structure is what lets any client or node answer \"who owns this key\" in O(log n) without asking the whole cluster."},{"u":"topics/questions/distributed-cache.html","p":"Design a Distributed Cache","i":"step3-api","h":"API design","t":"Step 3 of 6 — API / Interface API design The client-facing API is a thin key-value contract; the interesting API is the internal one nodes use to gossip cluster membership. GET /cache/{key} Auth: service-to-service token Request key routed via client-side ring hash Response { value, ttl_remaining_ms } or 404 on miss PUT /cache/{key} Auth: service-to-service token Request { value, ttl_seconds } Response 200 once primary + at least one replica ack DELETE /cache/{key} Auth: service-to-service token Request no body Response 204 whether or not the key existed GOSSIP /internal/membership/sync Auth: node-to-node mTLS Message out { node_id, vnode_hashes, status, version } Cadence every ~1s to a random peer subset (gossip fanout) Clients embed a thin ring-aware library rather than calling a proxy — hashing the key locally and connecting directly to the owning node skips an extra network hop, which matters when the latency budget is sub-millisecond."},{"u":"topics/questions/distributed-cache.html","p":"Design a Distributed Cache","i":"step4-dataflow","h":"Data flow","t":"Step 4 of 6 — Data Flow Data flow Writes (blue) hash to the owning node then replicate to a standby; reads (mint) hash to the same owning node and return directly — no replication hop needed on the read path. WRITE PATH — PUT /cache/{key} Client Lib Ring Hash Primary Node Replica Node READ PATH — GET /cache/{key} Client Lib Ring Hash Primary Node Client library — hashes locally, no proxy hop Cache nodes — primary owns the shard, replica is standby Client hashes the key — onto the consistent-hashing ring using the locally cached topology Request routes directly to the owning node — no intermediate proxy or load balancer hop Write replicates to the shard's replica(s) — synchronously or asynchronously depending on the consistency dial Read returns straight from the primary's memory — sub-millisecond since it's a single RAM lookup On primary failure, client retries against the promoted replica — using the same ring lookup with updated topology Ring-aware client library holds a copy of the topology Hashes key onto the ring finds next node clockwise Owning node for this key's ring position Async or sync replicate before ack per consistency setting Same client library same ring lookup logic Same deterministic hash — reads and writes agree on ownership Returns value directly from RAM no replication hop needed"},{"u":"topics/questions/distributed-cache.html","p":"Design a Distributed Cache","i":"step5-hld","h":"High-level design","t":"Step 5 of 6 — High-Level Design High-level design Client library, sharded cache nodes with primary/replica pairs, a gossip-based membership layer for topology, and a backing database that the cache shields from load. Client Library consistent-hash aware Cache Node 1 shard A — primary Cache Node 2 shard B — primary Cache Node 3 shard C — primary Replica Nodes 1 per shard, RF=2 Coordination / Membership gossip protocol or etcd/ZooKeeper Backing Database source of truth, miss path only membership also tracks DB health for failover routing Client library — ring-topology aware, routes directly Cache nodes — sharded via consistent hashing, primary + replica Coordination layer — decoupled from the hot read/write path Backing database — only touched on a miss Component Role Tech + why Client Library Ring-aware routing Embedded consistent-hashing client — because routing at the client skips a proxy hop, which matters directly against a sub-millisecond p99 budget; tradeoff is every client needs topology updates pushed to it. Cache Nodes In-memory key-value storage, sharded Consistent hashing ring with virtual nodes — because it bounds key remapping to a small arc on add/remove instead of the whole keyspace; tradeoff is extra memory and gossip overhead per vnode. Replica Nodes Standby copy per shard Async or semi-sync replication — because losing a primary shouldn't mean losing that shard's data or availability; tradeoff is replica lag under strict eventual consistency. Coordination / Membership Tracks ring topology and node health Gossip protocol (or a config service like ZooKeeper/etcd) — because nodes and clients need a shared, eventually-consistent view of who owns what; tradeoff is convergence lag during topology changes. Backing Database Source of truth on a miss Whatever durable store the cache sits in front of — because the cache is not durable by design; tradeoff is it must be protected from simultaneous miss storms. Bottleneck Triggers at Exact fix Single shard hot key One key gets disproportionate traffic vs the rest of its shard Client-side local caching for that key, or split it into N sub-keys hashed to different shards Gossip convergence lag Large cluster (200+ nodes) or high churn (frequent adds/removes) Increase gossip fanout, shorten gossip interval, or move to a centralized but replicated config service Coordination service overload Every node polling it instead of gossiping peer-to-peer Push to gossip-based membership so nodes only sync with a random peer subset, not a central service Scaling levers — cheapest to most complex Increase virtual nodes per physical node for smoother key distribution low complexity Add read replicas per shard for read-heavy hot shards low complexity Client-side local (L1) cache for the hottest handful of keys medium complexity Grow the ring by adding physical nodes, remapping only adjacent arcs medium complexity Move from centralized coordinator to gossip-based membership at very large cluster sizes high complexity — last resort Consistency: strong vs eventual for replica reads Strong Eventual Eviction policy: LRU vs LFU under memory pressure LRU LFU Ring-aware holds cached copy of cluster topology Owns shard A's key range Owns shard B's key range Owns shard C's key range Standby copies — one per shard replication factor 2 Gossip protocol or ZooKeeper/etcd — tracks ring topology node health Only hit on a cache miss — request coalescing shields it from thundering herd"},{"u":"topics/questions/distributed-cache.html","p":"Design a Distributed Cache","i":"step6-deepdive-ring","h":"Deep dive: consistent hashing ring","t":"Step 6 of 6 — Deep Dives Deep dive: consistent hashing ring Nodes and keys hash onto the same circular keyspace. Key owner = first node clockwise from the key. Vnodes scatter each node's ownership so load stays even. HASH RING — 0 to 2^32, wraps around A-v1 A-v2 A-v3 B-v1 B-v2 B-v3 C-v1 C-v2 C-v3 key:\"user:42\" key:\"sess:9a1\" key owned by next vnode found clockwise Node A's 3 vnodes — spread around the ring for even load Node B's 3 vnodes Node C's 3 vnodes Every physical node gets many vnodes — hashing the node ID with several salts places dozens-to-hundreds of points per node around the ring A key is hashed to a single ring position — the same hash function used for nodes, so keys and nodes share one keyspace Ownership is \"next vnode clockwise\" — walk the ring from the key's position until a vnode is found; that vnode's physical node owns the key Adding a node only remaps its adjacent arc — new vnodes slice into existing arcs, and only the keys that fell in the sliced portion move; everything else is untouched Removing a node merges its arc into the next vnode clockwise — again, only that node's keys remap, bounded to roughly 1/N of the keyspace, not all of it This is the entire reason to use consistent hashing over a plain hash(key) % N scheme: with modulo hashing, changing N reshuffles almost every key. With a ring, changing node count only touches the keys owned by the adjacent arc — typically ~1/N of the keyspace."},{"u":"topics/questions/distributed-cache.html","p":"Design a Distributed Cache","i":"step6-deepdive-eviction","h":"Deep dive: eviction and invalidation","t":"Deep dive: eviction and invalidation Two separate questions: which entries get evicted under memory pressure (LRU vs LFU), and how the cache stays consistent with the backing store (write-through vs write-back vs cache-aside). Eviction policy Evicts Good for Weak point LRU (Least Recently Used) Oldest-accessed entry first Simple, cheap to implement (linked list + hash map) A single large scan can evict genuinely hot keys LFU (Least Frequently Used) Lowest access-count entry first Resists scan pollution, protects truly hot keys More bookkeeping; slow-to-decay counts can keep stale-but-once-popular keys too long Write pattern Consistency behavior Tradeoff Cache-aside App checks cache, on miss reads DB and populates cache; writes go straight to DB and the cache entry is invalidated Simplest pattern, but a brief inconsistency window exists between DB write and cache invalidation. Write-through Every write goes to the cache first, which synchronously writes through to the DB before acknowledging Cache and DB never disagree, but every write pays DB latency — no write-speed benefit from the cache. Write-back Write lands in the cache and is acknowledged immediately; DB write happens asynchronously afterward Fastest writes, but a cache node failure before flush can lose data the client thinks is durable. TTL expiry and eviction are independent triggers for the same outcome — a key can be evicted early under memory pressure even with time left on its TTL, or it can sit untouched at low memory and still expire exactly at its TTL. Both paths must trigger the same invalidation/refetch logic, or a candidate leaves consistency bugs at the boundary."},{"u":"topics/questions/distributed-cache.html","p":"Design a Distributed Cache","i":"step6-deepdive-thunderingherd","h":"Deep dive: thundering herd protection","t":"Deep dive: thundering herd protection A hot key expiring or a node dying can turn thousands of simultaneous cache misses into thousands of simultaneous identical database queries — the database sees a spike that looks like an attack. WITHOUT PROTECTION 1,000s of Clients Cache (miss) Database overwhelmed WITH REQUEST COALESCING 1,000s of Clients Cache + Coalescer single-flight per key Database 1 query, not 1,000s Fan-out result to waiters Clients — many simultaneous requesters for one key Cache + coalescer — collapses concurrent misses into one fetch Database — protected from duplicate simultaneous load First miss on a key registers an in-flight marker — request coalescing/single-flight: subsequent misses for the same key see the marker and wait instead of re-querying Only one request actually reaches the database — its result is cached and fanned out to every client that was waiting TTLs are staggered with jitter — instead of every replica of a dataset expiring at exactly T+60s, each gets T+60s ± random jitter so misses spread out over time Stale-while-revalidate serves the old value during refresh — a client gets the slightly-stale cached value immediately while one background request refreshes it, instead of everyone blocking on a fresh fetch Node failure triggers the same coalescing on the promoted replica — the replica that takes over a dead primary's shard applies identical single-flight logic rather than accepting every re-routed request as a fresh miss Request coalescing must be scoped correctly — coalescing across different keys (not just the same key) either does nothing useful or, worse, serves the wrong value to the wrong request. The single-flight lock key must be the exact cache key. Failure mode User impact Detection signal Mitigation Cache node fails, shard temporarily unavailable Brief latency spike, not full unavailability Node health check fails in gossip/membership layer Replica promotion for that shard; client library re-routes using updated ring topology within one gossip cycle Hot key overwhelms a single shard One shard's latency degrades while others stay healthy Per-shard QPS metrics show one shard far above the mean Client-side local caching for that key, or split it into N sub-keys hashed across multiple shards Coordination/membership service becomes a bottleneck Slow or stale topology updates cluster-wide Rising node-to-coordinator request latency or a growing gossip convergence delay Move from a centralized coordinator to gossip-based membership so nodes sync peer-to-peer instead of all hitting one service 1 000s of clients all requesting the same expired hot key Every request misses the same key at the same instant All 1 000s of misses hit the DB simultaneously — thundering herd Same 1 000s of clients same expired key Single-flight: first miss registers an in-flight marker for the key Only ONE request per key reaches the DB; rest wait on it Result fans back out to every waiting client from the single fetch"},{"u":"topics/questions/distributed-cache.html","p":"Design a Distributed Cache","i":"interview-moves","h":"Interview moves","t":"Interview moves 01 \"The hard part here isn't storing key/value — it's distributing keys across nodes so adding or removing one remaps the minimum number of keys, which means consistent hashing, not modulo hashing\" — states the core insight unprompted 02 \"I'd use virtual nodes so each physical node owns many small arcs of the ring instead of one big one — that keeps load even as the cluster grows\" — shows you know the practical refinement, not just the textbook algorithm 03 \"A node failure should cause a brief latency spike from replica promotion, not full unavailability of that shard's keys\" — frames failure handling around replication, not just retries 04 \"When a hot key expires, I don't want a thundering herd — I'd add request coalescing so only one request per key reaches the database while the rest wait on it\" — names the specific failure and the specific mechanism 05 \"I'd jitter TTLs so a batch of keys written together don't all expire at the exact same millisecond\" — proactively surfaces a subtle correlated-failure mode 06 \"The coordination layer tracking cluster topology can't be a single point of failure itself — I'd lean toward gossip over a single centralized coordinator at this scale\" — extends the design's own failure-tolerance principle to its own control plane"},{"u":"topics/questions/distributed-cache.html","p":"Design a Distributed Cache","i":"follow-ups","h":"Follow-up questions","t":"Follow-up questions How do virtual nodes actually improve load distribution? Without vnodes, one unlucky hash placement can give a node a disproportionate arc; with dozens of vnodes per physical node, individual bad placements average out across the whole node. How would you handle a client with a stale view of the ring topology? The owning node detects it doesn't actually own the key, returns a redirect/hint with the correct node, and the client refreshes its topology copy from the coordination layer. Why not just always use strong consistency for replica reads? Strong consistency means every read waits on cross-replica confirmation, which conflicts directly with a sub-millisecond latency target — eventual consistency is the deliberate tradeoff for speed. What happens if the coordination service itself goes down? With gossip-based membership, nodes keep operating on their last-known topology and keep converging peer-to-peer; a centralized coordinator instead needs its own HA setup (e.g. a Raft-backed quorum). How do you decide between write-through and write-back? Write-through when losing an unflushed write is unacceptable; write-back when write latency matters more and a short data-loss window on cache-node crash is tolerable. How would you detect a hot key before it becomes a problem? Sample per-key access counts on each node with a rolling window; a key crossing a threshold relative to its shard's average triggers client-side caching or key-splitting proactively."},{"u":"topics/questions/dropbox.html","p":"Design Dropbox","i":"","h":"Overview","t":"Questions Design Dropbox Tests whether you can design chunked, content-addressed file sync without a naive re-upload-everything approach. 40-50 min interview Hard Concept Map click to expand Say this in the first 2 minutes The core insight: chunked upload with content-hash dedup means the same file uploaded by 1M users is stored exactly once Key scale number to anchor on: at 700M users, even a 40% dedup rate on raw storage is the difference between feasible and impossible cost Primary tradeoff being probed: metadata and block storage are two separate systems with different access patterns — coupling them serves neither well Component most candidates forget: the notification service is a lightweight signal, not a push of file content — the client still pulls the delta Failure mode most candidates skip: two offline devices editing the same file concurrently — silently picking a winner is a data-loss bug Dropbox Block Service Checks chunk hash existence and handles chunk upload and download step6-deepdive-upload Stateless pods Chunk operations are independent scale horizontally Batch existence check One request checks many hashes at once Metadata Svc Owns the file tree versions and permissions step2-entities Postgres-backed ACID guarantees matter for a version commit Commits last Only writes after every chunk is confirmed durable S3 Content-addressed object storage for immutable chunks step2-entities Hash as key Same content uploaded twice is a free no-op ref_count Tracks references for garbage collection PostgreSQL Relational store for the file tree and version rows step2-entities parent_dir index Fast directory listing via self-reference Strong consistency Not eventual unlike the block layer Notification Svc Long-poll signal service that tells a client something changed step5-hld Signal only Client still pulls the actual delta Debounced fan-out Batches rapid edits into one signal CDN Edge-caches frequently downloaded blocks in front of S3 step4-dataflow Accelerates reads Popular blocks skip a direct S3 hit Download path only Uploads never touch the CDN"},{"u":"topics/questions/dropbox.html","p":"Design Dropbox","i":"step1-requirements","h":"Requirements","t":"Step 1 of 6 — Requirements Requirements Functional Upload and download files of arbitrary size Sync file changes across a user's devices Support offline edits with conflict detection on reconnect Version history per file Non-Functional 700M users, hundreds of billions of files Deduplicate identical content across all users Sync latency <5s from change to notification Resumable uploads on unreliable/mobile networks What NOT to build: real-time collaborative co-editing (Google Docs style), in-browser document rendering/preview, or third-party app integrations. This is storage, sync, and versioning only. ~4MB chunk size ~50k/s chunk upload QPS (peak) ~10 EB raw block storage/yr (pre-dedup) SHA-256 chunk content hash"},{"u":"topics/questions/dropbox.html","p":"Design Dropbox","i":"step2-entities","h":"Core entities","t":"Step 2 of 6 — Core Entities Core entities File Postgres file_id UUID parent_dir UUID, self-ref owner_id BIGINT latest_version_id UUID Because the file tree is small, relational, and read constantly. Tradeoff: hot rows for large shared folders need sharding by owner_id. FileVersion Postgres version_id UUID file_id UUID → File chunk_hashes TEXT[] device_id UUID Because a version is an ordered list of chunk hashes, not the file bytes themselves — the file is never reassembled server-side until download. Tradeoff: reconstructing a file requires N chunk fetches. Block S3 hash CHAR(64), SHA-256 s3_key TEXT ref_count BIGINT Because chunks are large, immutable once written, and accessed by hash — content-addressing makes dedup a free existence check. Tradeoff: garbage collection needed when ref_count hits zero. Primary key: Block.hash is the content hash itself — this is what makes \"1M users upload the same PDF\" cost one chunk of storage. Critical index: File.parent_dir for fast directory listing."},{"u":"topics/questions/dropbox.html","p":"Design Dropbox","i":"step3-api","h":"API design","t":"Step 3 of 6 — API / Interface API design POST /api/v1/blocks/exists Auth: required Request { hashes: [sha256, ...] } Response { missing: [sha256, ...] } POST /api/v1/blocks/{hash} Auth: required Request raw chunk bytes, content-addressed by hash Response HTTP 201, idempotent — safe to retry POST /api/v1/files/{file_id}/versions Auth: required Request { chunk_hashes: [...], device_id } Response { version_id, created_at } LONG-POLL /api/v1/sync/wait?since={cursor} Auth: required Connection held open up to 30s, or until a change exists Message { file_id, new_version_id } — signal only, no content Long-poll instead of WebSocket: sync events are infrequent per device and one-directional (server → client signal), so holding a full-duplex socket open per idle device isn't worth the infra cost. Long-poll gives near-real-time delivery at a fraction of the connection overhead."},{"u":"topics/questions/dropbox.html","p":"Design Dropbox","i":"step4-dataflow","h":"Data flow","t":"Step 4 of 6 — Data Flow Data flow UPLOAD PATH — chunked, deduplicated Client Block Service S3 Metadata Svc PostgreSQL DOWNLOAD PATH Client Metadata Svc CDN Upload path — only new chunks transfer Download path — CDN accelerates popular blocks Client splits and hashes file locally — before any network call Batch existence check against Block Service — server returns which hashes are missing Only missing chunks upload — chunks already in S3 are skipped entirely Metadata commit — once every chunk is confirmed durable, a new file version is written Download reverses it — metadata gives the chunk list, chunks fetch in parallel through CDN Splits file into 4MB chunks locally hashes each with SHA-256 Checks which chunk hashes already exist before accepting any bytes Content-addressed by hash — re-uploads of the same content are no-ops Commits the file's chunk-list + version once all chunks are confirmed File tree chunk lists versions Requests a file — metadata gives chunk list Returns the chunk-hash list for the requested version Edge-caches frequently downloaded blocks"},{"u":"topics/questions/dropbox.html","p":"Design Dropbox","i":"step5-hld","h":"High-level design","t":"Step 5 of 6 — High-Level Design High-level design Clients API Gateway Block Service S3 Metadata Svc PostgreSQL Notification Svc CDN Clients + gateway S3 + Postgres — split by access pattern Notification Svc — long-poll signal only CDN — accelerates popular block downloads Component Role Tech + why Block Service Hash existence checks, chunk upload/download Stateless pods — because chunk operations are independent and horizontally scale; tradeoff is none, this is the easy side. Metadata Svc File tree, versions, permissions Postgres-backed — because ACID guarantees matter for a version commit; tradeoff is sharding needed for hot shared folders. Notification Svc Fans out \"file changed\" signals Long-poll — because sync events are infrequent per device; tradeoff is stateful connection-holding infra. S3 Durable content-addressed block storage Object storage — because blocks are immutable and accessed by hash; tradeoff is eventual consistency on some read-after-write edge cases. Scaling levers — cheapest to most complex Cache file-tree reads in Redis in front of Postgres low complexity Batch and debounce notification fan-out for rapid edits low complexity Shard Postgres metadata by owner_id or folder_id medium complexity Cross-region S3 replication for durability and latency medium complexity Move sync from long-poll to persistent WebSocket at very large idle-connection scale high complexity — last resort Bottleneck Triggers at Exact fix Hash existence check latency TRIGGERS AT Large files with thousands of chunks Batch checks with a Bloom filter pre-check to skip obvious misses Metadata DB hot rows TRIGGERS AT Popular shared folder edited by many users Shard by owner_id/folder_id; cache file-tree reads Notification fan-out storms TRIGGERS AT A folder with 10k+ collaborators changes Batch delivery, debounce rapid successive edits into one signal Sync signaling: infra simplicity vs efficiency at scale Simple polling Stateful long-poll Driver/chunk data durability vs write speed Durable Fast, ephemeral Splits hashes and reassembles files locally Clients Client Desktop/mobile/web sync client Splits files into chunks and hashes them locally so only new content ever needs to cross the network. ~0ms — local compute only Auth routing to block/metadata/sync services API Gateway Service Stateless pods Single entry point for auth and routing to block metadata and notification services. ~5-10ms Hash existence checks chunk upload/download Block Service Service Stateless pods — because chunk operations are independent and horizontally scale Chunk operations are independent and horizontally scale; tradeoff is none this is the easy side. ~10-30ms per batch check Content-addressed object storage S3 Database Object storage — because blocks are immutable and accessed by hash Blocks are immutable and accessed by hash; tradeoff is eventual consistency on some read-after-write edge cases. ~20-80ms per chunk read/write File tree versions permissions Metadata Svc Service Postgres-backed — because ACID guarantees matter for a version commit ACID guarantees matter for a version commit; tradeoff is sharding needed for hot shared folders. ~10-20ms per commit Structured file tree and version rows PostgreSQL Database Relational ACID-compliant store File tree and version rows need relational structure and strong consistency not eventual consistency. ~1-5ms per query Holds long-poll connections fans out change signals Notification Svc Queue Long-poll — because sync events are infrequent per device Sync events are infrequent per device; tradeoff is stateful connection-holding infra. <5s to notify from change Edge-caches popular blocks for download CDN CDN Edge cache in front of S3 Accelerates popular block downloads by serving from the edge instead of hitting S3 directly every time. ~5-20ms on cache hit"},{"u":"topics/questions/dropbox.html","p":"Design Dropbox","i":"step6-deepdive-upload","h":"Deep dive: chunked upload sequence","t":"Step 6 of 6 — Deep Dives Deep dive: chunked upload sequence File → 3 chunks Hash each chunk Check existence batch hash lookup 2 of 3 exist only chunk 2 is new Upload only chunk 2 the other 2 chunks: zero bytes sent Commit metadata chunk_hashes = [h1,h2,h3] Client / gateway Cache — hot path Database — source of truth Async — queue / worker Split — client divides the file into ~4MB chunks locally Hash — SHA-256 each chunk, becomes its identity and dedup key Check existence — one batched request against the block service Upload only what's missing — this is the mechanism that makes 1M identical uploads cost one chunk Commit — a new version is written referencing the ordered chunk-hash list The upload isn't \"done\" from the metadata layer's view until every referenced chunk hash is confirmed durable in S3 — committing metadata earlier risks a version pointing at a chunk that never landed. 10MB file split locally into three chunks SHA-256 each chunk before any network call Batch hash lookup against the block index Server responds: 2 of 3 already exist Only the missing chunk's bytes are transferred Commit once every chunk hash is confirmed present"},{"u":"topics/questions/dropbox.html","p":"Design Dropbox","i":"step6-deepdive-conflict","h":"Deep dive: conflict resolution","t":"Deep dive: conflict resolution Both clients edit offline — each queues its own new version locally, tagged with its device_id Both reconnect and attempt to commit — the second commit's parent version no longer matches the current latest_version_id Server detects divergence — the version vector comparison shows two branches from the same parent, not a linear history Keep both — the losing client's version is saved as \"file (conflicted copy — device B).txt\" rather than discarded User resolves manually — both versions remain fully recoverable, no silent data loss Scenario User impact Detection signal Mitigation Chunk upload interrupted mid-transfer Partial chunk never confirmed Client-side timeout, no server ack Idempotent per-chunk retry — safe to resend since it's content-addressed Metadata service down No new versions can commit; sync halts Write error rate spike Clients queue pending commits locally, retry with backoff Two devices edit concurrently offline Conflicting versions on reconnect Version vector shows divergent branches Keep both as separate files rather than discarding one S3 region outage Uploads/downloads for affected blocks fail Elevated 5xx from block storage Cross-region replication, failover reads to secondary region Notification service outage No real-time updates on other devices Long-poll connection drop rate spike Clients fall back to periodic low-frequency polling"},{"u":"topics/questions/dropbox.html","p":"Design Dropbox","i":"interview-moves","h":"Interview moves","t":"Interview moves 01 \"The core insight here is content-addressed chunking — I'd split files into 4MB blocks, hash each with SHA-256, and only transfer chunks the server hasn't seen before\" — names the actual hard problem unprompted 02 \"I'm keeping metadata and block storage as two completely separate systems since their access patterns don't match\" — shows you won't force one database to do two incompatible jobs 03 \"Sync is notify-then-pull, not poll-then-diff — a long-poll tells the client something changed, and the client decides what to fetch\" — rules out a naive polling design 04 \"On conflict, I'd keep both versions rather than pick a winner — silently discarding a user's edit is worse than a visible duplicate file\" — a mature, user-first tradeoff call 05 \"Every chunk upload needs to be idempotent since mobile clients are on flaky connections — retrying a chunk should always be safe\" — proactively addresses a realistic failure mode 06 \"The same PDF uploaded by a million users should cost one chunk of storage, not a million copies — that's what the content hash buys us\" — makes the dedup payoff concrete"},{"u":"topics/questions/dropbox.html","p":"Design Dropbox","i":"follow-ups","h":"Follow-up questions","t":"Follow-up questions How do you handle a file with millions of chunks? Store the chunk-hash list as a separate blob rather than an inline array past a threshold, and paginate existence checks in batches. What stops a malicious user from claiming they have a chunk they don't? The server verifies hashes server-side after any real upload and can spot-check with a byte-range proof before trusting an existence claim for sensitive dedup paths. How do you delete storage when the last reference is removed? Decrement ref_count on file/version deletion; a background garbage collector reclaims chunks whose ref_count hits zero, avoiding synchronous delete-time S3 calls. How would you support very large team folders shared across thousands of users? Shard metadata by folder_id, batch and debounce notification fan-out, and consider a pub/sub tree so updates propagate through fewer hops than one service fanning out to everyone. How do you keep chunk boundaries stable when a user inserts a byte at the start of a file? Fixed-size chunking shifts every later boundary. Content-defined chunking (rolling hash) picks boundaries based on content, so only chunks actually touched by the edit change. How is version history stored without duplicating unchanged chunks per version? Each version stores an ordered chunk-hash list; unchanged chunks are simply referenced again by hash — only new/changed chunks physically exist once."},{"u":"topics/questions/fb-live-comments.html","p":"Design Facebook Live Comments","i":"","h":"Overview","t":"Questions Design Facebook Live Comments Tests whether you can fan a single comment out to millions of concurrent viewers in under a second — this is pub/sub at extreme scale, not a CRUD comments feature. 40-50 min interview Hard Concept Map click to expand Say this in the first 2 minutes The core insight: this is fan-out delivery at extreme scale, not a comments CRUD problem — writes are cheap, broadcast is the hard part Key scale number to anchor on: a single hot stream can hold 1M+ concurrent viewers, each needing every new comment pushed within ~1 second Primary tradeoff being probed: strict comment ordering per stream vs horizontal write throughput — solved by partitioning on stream_id Component most candidates forget: a connection server layer that holds the long-lived WebSockets, decoupled from the stateless ingestion path Failure mode most candidates skip: a fan-out worker falling behind on a viral stream — it must shed load gracefully, never crash the whole pipeline FB Live Comments Ingestion Svc Validates a comment persists it then publishes it for fan-out step4-dataflow Stateless Scales independent of delivery 202 response Fan-out happens async after accept Cassandra Durable append-only comment history partitioned by stream_id step2-entities Partition key stream_id keeps a stream on one node Clustering key TIMEUUID preserves post order Kafka Ordered durable log partitioned by stream_id for fan-out step6-deepdive-partitioning Per stream order One partition per stream_id Hot partition risk A viral stream can saturate one partition Fan-out Workers Consume the ordered log and push to the right connection servers step6-deepdive-connservers Consumer group Horizontal scaling by adding consumers Redis lookup Resolves which servers hold viewers Redis routing Maps a stream_id to the set of connection servers holding its viewers step5-hld O(1) membership Fast lookup on every fan-out Ephemeral Rebuilt on restart as clients reconnect Connection Servers Hold millions of long-lived WebSockets the one stateful tier step6-deepdive-connservers ~50k sockets Per instance sized for failure headroom Local fan-out Each server pushes to its own sockets"},{"u":"topics/questions/fb-live-comments.html","p":"Design Facebook Live Comments","i":"step1-requirements","h":"Requirements","t":"Step 1 of 6 — Requirements Requirements 1 2 3 4 5 6 Arc 1 (steps 1-5) — Satisfy Functional Requirements 1 2 3 4 5 6 Arc 2 (all 6 steps) — Satisfy Non-Functional Requirements Functional Post a comment on a live stream Broadcast new comments to every connected viewer in real time Load recent comment history when a viewer joins mid-stream Support moderation — delete/hide a comment for all viewers instantly Non-Functional 1M+ concurrent viewers on a single hot stream, millions of streams overall Fan-out delivery latency <1s p99 from post to viewer render Comments within a stream must render in a consistent order for every viewer 99.9% delivery availability; brief comment loss during overload is acceptable, silent crash is not What NOT to build: comment threading/replies, reactions/emoji bursts, spam/toxicity ML models, or the video transcoding pipeline itself. This is post-a-comment + broadcast-it-to-everyone-watching only. 1M+ concurrent viewers, single hot stream ~50k/s comments/sec at peak, platform-wide <1s post-to-render fan-out latency, p99 ~50k open WebSockets per connection server instance"},{"u":"topics/questions/fb-live-comments.html","p":"Design Facebook Live Comments","i":"step2-entities","h":"Core entities","t":"Step 2 of 6 — Core Entities Core entities Three entities across two stores, split by access pattern — durable comment history is a different shape than in-memory viewer routing. Stream Postgres id BIGINT (Snowflake) host_id BIGINT → User.id status ENUM(live, ended) started_at TIMESTAMP Because stream metadata is low-volume and needs strong consistency for status transitions (live → ended). A relational store fits; tradeoff is irrelevant to the hot comment path so it never sits on it. Comment Cassandra stream_id BIGINT (partition key) comment_id TIMEUUID (clustering key) author_id BIGINT text VARCHAR(500) is_hidden BOOLEAN Because writes are append-only and the only read pattern is \"last N comments for this stream_id, in order\" — a wide-column store with stream_id as partition key gives that for free. Tradeoff: no ad-hoc queries across streams. ViewerConnection Redis stream_id BIGINT connection_server_id SET member viewer_count INT, approximate Because viewer routing is pure in-memory, ephemeral state — a viewer's socket lives only as long as they're watching. Redis gives O(1) membership lookups for \"which connection servers hold viewers of this stream.\" Tradeoff: state is lost on Redis restart, acceptable since clients reconnect and re-register. Primary key: Comment(stream_id, comment_id) — a composite/partition key. Critical index: partitioning on stream_id guarantees every comment for a stream lives on one partition, which is what makes in-order delivery possible without a distributed sort."},{"u":"topics/questions/fb-live-comments.html","p":"Design Facebook Live Comments","i":"step3-api","h":"API design","t":"Step 3 of 6 — API / Interface API design Posting a comment and joining a stream are REST; the live comment feed itself pushes over a persistent WebSocket — polling can't hit sub-second fan-out at this scale. POST /api/v1/streams/{stream_id}/comments Auth: required Request { text } Response { comment_id, accepted: true } — HTTP 202, fan-out happens async GET /ws/streams/{stream_id}/comments Auth: required Request WebSocket upgrade; server registers viewer in Redis on connect Response stream of { comment_id, author, text, ts } frames, pushed as posted GET /api/v1/streams/{stream_id}/comments?limit=50 Auth: optional Request query params: limit, before_comment_id Response { comments: [...] } — last N, backfilled once on join before the socket takes over DELETE /api/v1/streams/{stream_id}/comments/{comment_id} Auth: required (moderator/host) Request path params only Response HTTP 204, broadcasts a delete event over the same socket to hide it live"},{"u":"topics/questions/fb-live-comments.html","p":"Design Facebook Live Comments","i":"step4-dataflow","h":"Data flow","t":"Step 4 of 6 — Data Flow Data flow Write path (blue) — post a comment. Read/fan-out path (mint) — that comment delivered to every viewer's open socket. WRITE PATH — post a comment Client Ingestion Svc stateless Kafka topic per stream Cassandra READ / FAN-OUT PATH — delivered to viewers Kafka Fan-out Worker reads Redis routing Connection Svr ~50k sockets/instance Viewer JOIN PATH — new viewer registers before the first push arrives register in Redis + REST backfill Client / viewer Kafka — durable, ordered by stream_id Connection servers — hold the sockets Cassandra — comment history Client posts a comment — Ingestion Svc validates and returns 202 immediately Comment persisted to Cassandra — durable history, keyed by stream_id + comment_id Comment published to Kafka — partitioned by stream_id, so order is preserved per stream Fan-out worker consumes and looks up routing — Redis tells it which connection servers hold this stream's viewers Pushed to every connection server holding a viewer — each pushes down its open WebSockets, target <1s end-to-end Viewer submits a comment while watching Validates and persists the comment then publishes for fan-out Partitioned by stream_id — preserves per-stream ordering Durable comment history for backfill on join Fan-out workers consume from the same partitioned topic Looks up which connection servers hold this stream's viewers pushes to each Holds long-lived WebSockets one shard of total viewers per instance Comment renders in the live feed target <1s from post On connect: viewer registered in Redis last 50 comments backfilled via REST"},{"u":"topics/questions/fb-live-comments.html","p":"Design Facebook Live Comments","i":"step5-hld","h":"High-level design","t":"Step 5 of 6 — High-Level Design High-level design Full system: ingestion, ordered fan-out, and the connection server pool that actually holds the millions of open sockets. Clients API Gateway Ingestion Svc stateless Cassandra Kafka topic per stream Fan-out Workers consumer group Redis viewer routing Connection Svr pool, horizontally scaled Viewers (millions) Join backfill (REST) Clients / gateway / viewers Cassandra — durable comment history Kafka + fan-out workers — ordered delivery Redis routing + connection server pool Component Role Tech + why Ingestion Svc Validates and accepts new comments Stateless HTTP service — because it needs to scale horizontally with write volume independent of everything downstream; tradeoff is an extra network hop before persistence. Kafka Ordered, durable transport for fan-out Partitioned log, one partition per stream_id — because ordering only needs to hold within a stream, not globally; tradeoff is a hot stream can still overload a single partition. Fan-out Workers Push comments to the right connection servers Kafka consumer group — because horizontal scaling is just adding consumers; tradeoff is consumer lag directly becomes viewer-facing delivery lag. Redis (routing) Maps stream_id → connection servers holding its viewers In-memory set per stream — because it's a hot, high-QPS lookup on every fan-out; tradeoff is ephemeral state that must be rebuilt on restart. Connection Server pool Holds millions of long-lived WebSockets Horizontally sharded stateful pool — because a single process can only hold so many open sockets; tradeoff is it's the one genuinely stateful tier in the whole system. Cassandra Durable comment history for backfill Wide-column store keyed by stream_id — because the only read is \"last N comments for this stream, in order\"; tradeoff is no secondary-index queries across streams. Scaling levers — cheapest to most complex Add more connection server instances to the pool near-zero complexity Add more fan-out consumer instances per stream's partition group low complexity Batch comment pushes into ~200ms windows on very hot streams medium complexity Split a single hot stream across extra Kafka partitions keyed by comment_id sub-ranges once volume outgrows one partition medium-high complexity Regional connection server pools to cut cross-region push latency high complexity — last resort Bottleneck Triggers at Exact fix Connection server socket limit ~50k sockets on one instance Add instances; Redis routing set spreads new viewers across the pool Fan-out consumer lag Push latency exceeds 1s p99 on a hot stream Scale out consumer group; batch pushes into short windows instead of one push per comment Single Kafka partition hotspot One viral stream saturates its partition's throughput Sub-partition that stream's topic; accept slightly relaxed ordering only on that stream Comment ordering guarantee Best-effort Strict per-stream Fan-out delivery under load Never drop Shed gracefully Commenters and viewers same client app Auth rate limiting routes writes vs socket upgrades Validates writes to Cassandra publishes to Kafka Durable comment history partitioned by stream_id One topic per stream partitioned by stream_id for ordering Consumes ordered comments resolves routing pushes to connection servers stream_id → set of connection_server_id holding its viewers Each instance holds a shard of viewer WebSockets ~50k sockets/instance Pushes comment frames down open sockets New viewer backfills last ~50 comments via REST before socket push takes over"},{"u":"topics/questions/fb-live-comments.html","p":"Design Facebook Live Comments","i":"step6-deepdive-connservers","h":"Deep dive: connection server architecture","t":"Step 6 of 6 — Deep Dives Deep dive: connection server architecture Millions of long-lived WebSockets can't live on one box — the pool is sharded, and Redis is the map from stream to shard. Fan-out Worker 1 comment event Redis routing set Conn Svr #1 ~50k sockets Conn Svr #2 ~50k sockets Conn Svr #N ~50k sockets Viewer A Viewer B Viewer C 1M viewers on one stream = spread across ~20 connection server instances Fan-out worker — one comment, many targets Redis routing + connection server pool Viewers — sharded across the pool Viewer opens the WebSocket — load balancer assigns it to whichever connection server has headroom Server registers itself in Redis — adds its ID to the set for that stream_id Fan-out worker never talks to sockets directly — it asks Redis which servers to notify, then pushes once per server, not once per viewer Each connection server fans out locally — iterates its own in-memory socket map for that stream, writes the frame to each On disconnect, the server removes itself from the Redis set — if it was the last one for that stream, the set is cleaned up The connection server is the one genuinely stateful tier in this system — a single instance dying drops every socket it held. Clients must detect the drop and reconnect (with backoff), and the load balancer must route the reconnect to a healthy instance. This is why capacity planning targets ~50k sockets/instance, not the theoretical OS-level max — headroom for a full instance failure without cascading reconnect storms. Fan-out worker looks up which servers hold this stream's viewers SMEMBERS stream: id :servers Holds ~50k viewer sockets for streams it was assigned Holds a different shard of viewer sockets for the same stream Pool scales horizontally as concurrent viewers grow A viewer of this stream connected to server #1 A different viewer of the same stream connected to server #2 A viewer connected to server #N — same comment same instant"},{"u":"topics/questions/fb-live-comments.html","p":"Design Facebook Live Comments","i":"step6-deepdive-partitioning","h":"Deep dive: Kafka partition strategy","t":"Deep dive: Kafka partition strategy Partitioning by stream_id is what makes \"every viewer sees comments in the same order\" possible without a distributed sort. Producer key = stream_id Partition 0 stream_id=42: c1 → c2 → c3 Partition 1 stream_id=77: c1 → c2 Partition 2 stream_id=13: c1 Consumer (fan-out) stream 42 always in-order Consumer (fan-out) stream 77 always in-order Consumer (fan-out) stream 13 always in-order Kafka partitions — one ordered log each Consumers — each owns a fixed set of partitions Producer hashes stream_id — picks the partition deterministically, same stream always lands on the same partition Kafka preserves order within a partition — comments for one stream are appended in the exact order they were produced One consumer instance owns a partition at a time — guarantees a single stream's comments are never processed out of order or in parallel A viral stream can still overload its single partition — the tradeoff for ordering is a per-partition throughput ceiling Mitigation for a hot stream: sub-key by comment_id ranges — relaxes strict ordering only for that one stream, in exchange for parallelism Scenario User impact Detection signal Mitigation Fan-out consumer group falls behind Comments arrive late, feels laggy on a hot stream Kafka consumer lag metric climbing per partition Auto-scale consumer instances; batch pushes into short windows to shed per-comment overhead Connection server instance crashes Every viewer on that instance is disconnected Instance health check failure, socket count drop to zero Client auto-reconnects with backoff; load balancer routes to a healthy instance, Redis re-registers Redis routing store unavailable Fan-out workers can't resolve which servers to push to Redis error rate spike on routing lookups Workers buffer briefly and retry; Redis runs as a replicated cluster to survive single-node loss Single stream goes viral mid-broadcast One Kafka partition and its consumer become a bottleneck Partition lag isolated to one stream_id Sub-partition that stream's topic on the fly; accept relaxed ordering only there Cassandra write latency spikes None to live fan-out; join backfill may be briefly stale Write p99 latency alarm on the comments table Fan-out reads from Kafka, not Cassandra — persistence and delivery are decoupled by design hash(stream_id) selects the partition — deterministic always the same partition for this stream Every comment for stream_id=42 lands here in post order Comments from a different stream ordered independently A third stream its own ordered log One consumer owns partition 0 — reads c1 c2 c3 in that exact order always A different consumer instance owns partition 1 A third consumer instance owns partition 2"},{"u":"topics/questions/fb-live-comments.html","p":"Design Facebook Live Comments","i":"interview-moves","h":"Interview moves","t":"Interview moves 01 \"The hard part here isn't storing comments — it's fanning one comment out to a million open connections in under a second\" — names the real problem before diving into a familiar CRUD shape 02 \"I'll partition Kafka by stream_id so ordering only needs to hold within a stream, not globally — that's the scope the requirement actually needs\" — shows the ordering guarantee is scoped deliberately, not accidentally weak 03 \"Connection servers are the one stateful tier in this system — everything else stays stateless so it can scale freely around it\" — demonstrates architectural awareness of where state actually lives 04 \"Fan-out workers never talk to individual sockets — they push once per connection server, and each server fans out locally to its own viewers\" — a concrete detail that shows the fan-out isn't O(viewers) at the messaging layer 05 \"If a fan-out worker falls behind on a viral stream, I'd rather batch pushes into short windows than let latency creep unbounded\" — proactively states a degrade-gracefully strategy before being asked 06 \"A connection server dying drops every socket it holds — I size instances at ~50k sockets, not the OS max, specifically to leave headroom for that failure\" — ties a concrete capacity number directly to a stated failure mode"},{"u":"topics/questions/fb-live-comments.html","p":"Design Facebook Live Comments","i":"follow-ups","h":"Follow-up questions","t":"Follow-up questions Why not just have every viewer poll for new comments every second? At 1M concurrent viewers that's 1M requests/sec just for one stream's polling — a persistent WebSocket amortizes that into a single push per comment instead. How do you handle a viewer switching between streams rapidly? Each switch closes the old socket registration in Redis and opens a new one; the connection server itself can often be reused, only the routing entry changes. How would you extend this to support 10x the concurrent viewers? Add more connection server instances (near-linear scaling), scale the fan-out consumer group, and sub-partition Kafka topics for any individual stream that outgrows one partition. What happens to in-flight comments if a fan-out worker crashes mid-broadcast? Kafka retains the offset; a new consumer in the group picks up the partition from the last committed offset, so comments are delayed, not lost. How do you moderate a comment out in real time across a million viewers? Delete publishes a lightweight \"hide comment_id\" event through the same Kafka/fan-out path, so every connected client removes it client-side within the same latency budget. Why Cassandra for comment history instead of Postgres? The access pattern is purely append + range-scan by stream_id, which a wide-column store handles at far higher write throughput than a relational store without sharding."},{"u":"topics/questions/fb-news-feed.html","p":"Design Facebook News Feed","i":"","h":"Overview","t":"Questions Design Facebook News Feed Tests whether you can reason about fan-out on write vs read at the tail of the follower-count distribution — one design breaks the moment a celebrity posts. 40-50 min interview Hard Concept Map click to expand Say this in the first 2 minutes The core insight: fan-out on write vs fan-out on read is not either/or — it's a hybrid keyed on follower count Key scale number to anchor on: a celebrity with 50M followers makes push fan-out physically impossible on a single post Primary tradeoff being probed: write amplification (push) vs read latency (pull) — and who pays the cost Component most candidates forget: the feed is a precomputed, ranked cache (Redis sorted set), not a live query over the Post table Failure mode most candidates skip: what happens when a fan-out worker falls behind during a viral moment — feed staleness must degrade gracefully, not block posting FB News Feed Post Svc Validates and persists a new post returns fast while fan-out runs async step2-entities Stateless pods Scales independently of fan-out load Fast write Post commit under 50ms Fan-out Worker Pushes new post IDs into follower feed caches below a follower threshold step6-deepdive-fanout Push threshold Skips push above roughly 1M followers Kafka-driven Consumes post_created events async Social Graph Wide-column store resolving follower lists for fan-out step2-entities Cassandra Partitioned by user_id for fast scans Eventual consistency Follow/unfollow lags slightly Redis Feed Cache Precomputed sorted set serving the ranked feed per user step6-deepdive-fanout ZSET O(log N) inserts and ranged reads Depth cap About 800 items kept per user Feed Svc Stateless reader that merges cache with a live pull for celebrity authors step4-dataflow Pull merge Live fetch for followed celebrities Hydration Resolves post IDs into content Ranking Svc Scores each candidate post before it is cached into a feed step6-deepdive-ranking rank_score Single float used as the ZADD score Scored at write Never runs on the read path"},{"u":"topics/questions/fb-news-feed.html","p":"Design Facebook News Feed","i":"step1-requirements","h":"Requirements","t":"Step 1 of 6 — Requirements Requirements 1 2 3 4 5 6 Arc 1 (steps 1-5) — Satisfy Functional Requirements 1 2 3 4 5 6 Arc 2 (all 6 steps) — Satisfy Non-Functional Requirements Functional Publish a post (text, image, video) to a user's followers/friends Fetch a ranked, paginated feed for the home page Rank feed items by recency, affinity, and predicted engagement Support likes/comments updating feed item state in near-real-time Non-Functional 2B users, avg 300 friends/follows, celebrities up to 100M followers Feed load latency <200ms p99 Post-to-feed-visible latency <5s for 99% of posts Eventually consistent feed is fine; strong consistency on the post itself is not What NOT to build: Stories/ephemeral content, Messenger/DMs, group-specific feeds, ads auction/insertion logic, or the full ML training pipeline for ranking. This is publish + fan-out + rank + serve only — ranking signals are treated as inputs, not derived from scratch. ~5k/s write QPS (new posts) ~400k/s read QPS (feed loads, avg) ~10M fan-out writes for one celebrity post 800 items feed cache depth kept per user"},{"u":"topics/questions/fb-news-feed.html","p":"Design Facebook News Feed","i":"step2-entities","h":"Core entities","t":"Step 2 of 6 — Core Entities Core entities Four entities across three stores, split by access pattern — the feed itself is a derived cache, not a queryable table. Post Postgres id BIGINT (Snowflake) author_id BIGINT → User.id content TEXT media_url TEXT, nullable → S3 created_at TIMESTAMP Because a post is the durable source of truth and needs relational integrity with the author — writes are moderate (5k/s) so a single well-indexed table handles it. Tradeoff: not the shape the feed reads from directly. SocialGraph Cassandra user_id BIGINT, partition key follower_id BIGINT, clustering key relation_type ENUM(friend, follow) Because \"who follows this user\" must resolve in single-digit milliseconds for fan-out at write time, at massive read volume across a huge dataset — wide-column stores partition this cleanly by user_id. Tradeoff: no cross-partition joins, so friend-of-friend queries need a separate service. FeedCache Redis feed:{user_id} Sorted Set (ZSET) member post_id score rank_score (float, recency + affinity) Because the feed read path can't afford to compute ranking on every page load — a precomputed sorted set gives O(log N) inserts and O(log N + K) ranged reads for pagination. Tradeoff: ~800 items/user capped, costs real memory at 2B users (~30TB cluster-wide at 15KB/user). EngagementEvent ClickHouse post_id String user_id BIGINT event_type ENUM(like, comment, share, dwell) ts DateTime Because engagement signals feed the ranking model as append-only, aggregate-heavy analytics — a column store handles billions of daily events for feature computation. Tradeoff: not usable for a synchronous point read on the serving path. Primary key: Post.id (Snowflake). Critical index: SocialGraph partitioned on user_id so \"give me all followers of X\" is a single-partition scan — this is the query fan-out workers run millions of times per celebrity post."},{"u":"topics/questions/fb-news-feed.html","p":"Design Facebook News Feed","i":"step3-api","h":"API design","t":"Step 3 of 6 — API / Interface API design Three endpoints — publish, fetch feed, and engage. Feed fetch is the hottest and most latency-sensitive. POST /api/v1/posts Auth: required Request { content, media_url? } Response { post_id, created_at } — 201, fan-out happens async GET /api/v1/feed?cursor={cursor}&limit=20 Auth: required Request cursor (opaque rank_score offset), limit Response { posts: [{post_id, author, content, score}], next_cursor } POST /api/v1/posts/{post_id}/engage Auth: required Request { event_type: like|comment|share } Response HTTP 202 — event queued, async fan-in to ranking signals DELETE /api/v1/posts/{post_id} Auth: required (owner) Request path param only Response HTTP 204 — tombstoned; workers lazily drop it from ZSETs on next read"},{"u":"topics/questions/fb-news-feed.html","p":"Design Facebook News Feed","i":"step4-dataflow","h":"Data flow","t":"Step 4 of 6 — Data Flow Data flow Write path (blue) fans a post out to followers asynchronously; read path (mint) merges the precomputed feed with a live pull for celebrity authors. READ PATH — load feed Client Feed Svc stateless Redis merge Pull Merge celeb authors Postgres WRITE PATH — publish + fan-out Client Post Svc Postgres Fan-out Worker async, Kafka-driven Redis ASYNC PATH — ranking signals, never touches read or write above Engagement → ClickHouse Read path — precomputed cache + celebrity pull merge Write path — durable post, fan-out decoupled Fan-out + ranking — fully async, never blocks either path Client publishes a post — Post Svc writes to Postgres and returns 201 in under 50ms Post event queued to Kafka — fan-out worker picks it up, never blocks the publish response Worker checks author follower count — under 1M: push to every follower's Redis ZSET; over 1M: skip push entirely Client loads feed — Feed Svc reads the precomputed ZSET, then merges in a live pull for any followed celebrity authors Response hydrated and ranked — post IDs resolved against Postgres/CDN for content, sorted by rank_score, paginated back App opens home feed Stateless feed service — reads cache merges celebrity pulls Precomputed ZSET — read for normal follows ~2-5ms Live fetch of latest posts from followed celebrities Post content hydration by ID User publishes a new post Validates and persists the post returns 201 immediately Source of truth for the post Reads follower list decides push vs skip per author size ZADD post_id into each follower's feed ZSET Likes/comments/shares stream into ranking feature computation"},{"u":"topics/questions/fb-news-feed.html","p":"Design Facebook News Feed","i":"step5-hld","h":"High-level design","t":"Step 5 of 6 — High-Level Design High-level design Full system: publish, hybrid fan-out, ranking signal collection, and feed serving together with every supporting component. Clients API Gateway Post Svc stateless Feed Svc stateless Postgres Kafka Fan-out Worker push if <1M followers Social Graph Cassandra Redis Feed Cache Ranking Svc score model Engagement Kafka ClickHouse Client / gateway Redis — precomputed feed Postgres + Social Graph + ClickHouse Fan-out worker + Kafka + Ranking Svc Component Role Tech + why Fan-out Worker Pushes new post IDs into follower feed caches Kafka-consumer worker pool — because it needs to absorb bursty publish traffic and scale horizontally independent of the write path; tradeoff is added lag under sustained load. Social Graph Resolves follower lists for fan-out Cassandra — because a single-partition read of millions of followers must stay under ~10ms; tradeoff is eventual consistency on follow/unfollow. Redis Feed Cache Serves the precomputed, ranked feed Sorted sets — because ZADD/ZREVRANGE give O(log N) writes and ranged reads without recomputing rank on every load; tradeoff is real memory cost at 2B users. Ranking Svc Scores each candidate post before it's cached Feature-store-backed scoring service — because recency alone produces a bad feed; tradeoff is added latency on the fan-out write path (mitigated by being async). Scaling levers — cheapest to most complex Add Redis + Postgres read replicas near-zero complexity Raise the celebrity threshold's fan-out worker pool size during known viral events low complexity Shard Redis feed cache by user_id hash medium complexity Cap feed cache depth (e.g. 800 → 400 items) to cut memory footprint under pressure medium complexity Move the celebrity threshold dynamically based on live fan-out worker lag high complexity — last resort Bottleneck Triggers at Exact fix Fan-out worker lag Kafka consumer lag >30s during a viral post Scale out consumer partitions; temporarily lower the celebrity push threshold so more authors fall back to pull Redis memory pressure Cluster memory >80% Shard by user_id hash; trim ZSET depth per user; evict cold users' feeds and rebuild on next login Celebrity pull merge slow Feed load p99 >200ms for users following many celebrities Cache each celebrity's latest N posts separately with a short TTL so the merge is a cache hit, not a DB query Fan-out strategy: write amplification vs read latency Push (fan-out on write) Pull (fan-out on read) Feed freshness consistency Strong Eventual Publish and feed-load requests originate here Auth rate limiting routing Stateless Stateless merges pull for celebrity authors Source of truth for posts Kafka topic — post_created events Reads SocialGraph decides push vs skip by follower count Wide-column follower/friend index Per-user sorted-set feed cache Ranking signals feed a score into each ZADD Engagement events buffered for feature computation Column store for engagement feature aggregation"},{"u":"topics/questions/fb-news-feed.html","p":"Design Facebook News Feed","i":"step6-deepdive-fanout","h":"Deep dive: fan-out write amplification and the hybrid model","t":"Step 6 of 6 — Deep Dives Deep dive: fan-out write amplification and the hybrid model Pure push fan-out is simple and fast to read — until a single post has to write into 10 million sorted sets. 1 post published by a celebrity 10M followers to fan out to 10M ZADDs writes fired from one publish event ~15 min time to fully fan out at 11k writes/sec/worker ✓ Normal user — push, ~2ms felt latency Publish 800 followers Redis 800 ZADDs done in ~70ms feed already warm when they open the app ▸ Replay ✗ Celebrity (10M followers) — push would take ~15 min Publish 10M followers 1M and skips push entirely\"> Redis push SKIPPED not written here Pulled at read time follower's Feed Svc merges this in on load ▸ Replay The hybrid threshold (commonly ~1M followers) isn't arbitrary: below it, push cost is bounded and every follower gets an instantly warm feed; above it, the write amplification (10M+ ZADDs from one post) would starve the fan-out worker pool and delay every other user's fan-out behind it in the same queue. 800 followers worker fans out in milliseconds ZADD into each follower's cached feed Skipped — too expensive to push Fan-out worker sees follower_count > 1M and skips push entirely Follower's Feed Svc pulls this celebrity's recent posts live at read time"},{"u":"topics/questions/fb-news-feed.html","p":"Design Facebook News Feed","i":"step6-deepdive-ranking","h":"Deep dive: feed ranking signal pipeline","t":"Deep dive: feed ranking signal pipeline Every candidate post gets a rank_score before it's written into (or merged into) a feed — recency alone produces a feed nobody wants to scroll. Recency Affinity score Engagement prediction Ranking Model logistic regression / GBM rank_score float, e.g. 0.842 ZADD Input signals Ranking model Score written as ZSET rank Recency decays exponentially — a post from 30 minutes ago outranks one from 3 days ago by default, half-life tuned to ~6 hours Affinity score computed offline — built from historical interaction rate between the specific viewer and author, refreshed periodically not per-request Engagement prediction from ClickHouse features — a lightweight model estimates P(engage) per viewer/post pair using aggregated signals Signals combine into one rank_score — a single float so it can be used directly as a Redis ZSET score, no re-ranking needed at read time Score is recomputed only on fan-out — not on every feed read, keeping the read path a pure sorted-set range query Ranking must not run synchronously on the feed-read path — if the model call is on the critical path, a slow feature lookup directly adds to feed load latency. Score at fan-out/write time instead, and treat the feed read as a dumb, fast ZREVRANGE. Scenario User impact Detection signal Mitigation Fan-out worker pool falls behind New posts missing from followers' feeds for minutes Kafka consumer lag alert >60s Auto-scale worker pool; temporarily lower celebrity push threshold to shed load Redis feed cache node down Affected users see empty/stale feed Cache error rate spike on feed reads Fall back to on-demand rebuild from Social Graph + recent Postgres posts, slower but correct Social Graph partition hot (mega-celebrity) Fan-out for that author stalls, delays queue for others Per-partition write latency alarm on Cassandra Route celebrity authors permanently to pull-only, skip fan-out entirely by policy Ranking Svc outage None to availability; feed quality degrades Ranking Svc error rate / timeout alert Fall back to recency-only scoring — feed still loads, just less personalized ClickHouse ingestion backlog None immediately; engagement features go stale over hours Kafka consumer lag on engagement topic Ranking model uses last-known feature snapshot; alert if staleness exceeds a few hours Time since posted — decays quickly half-life ~6 hours Historical interaction strength between viewer and author Model-predicted P(like/comment/share/dwell) for this viewer Weighted combination — a logistic model not a hand-tuned formula Single float used as the ZADD score Score becomes the ZSET member's sort key"},{"u":"topics/questions/fb-news-feed.html","p":"Design Facebook News Feed","i":"interview-moves","h":"Interview moves","t":"Interview moves 01 \"The feed is a precomputed cache, not a live query — I'll rank and materialize it ahead of time so the read path is a cheap sorted-set range scan\" — signals you understand the read/write asymmetry immediately 02 \"Pure push fan-out breaks the moment someone with 10 million followers posts — that's 10 million writes fired from one event\" — names the exact failure mode before being asked 03 \"I'd use a hybrid: push for normal accounts under roughly a million followers, and merge in a live pull for celebrity accounts at read time\" — the single answer that separates strong candidates on this question 04 \"Ranking has to happen at fan-out/write time, not on the read path — otherwise every feed load pays for a model inference\" — shows awareness that read latency budgets are non-negotiable 05 \"I'll decouple engagement events into their own async pipeline so a slow ranking feature computation never blocks a like or comment from confirming\" — demonstrates the same hot-path-isolation instinct as write/read separation 06 \"If the fan-out workers fall behind during a viral spike, I'd rather show a slightly stale feed than block or slow down publishing\" — states a concrete, correct failure-mode priority unprompted"},{"u":"topics/questions/fb-news-feed.html","p":"Design Facebook News Feed","i":"follow-ups","h":"Follow-up questions","t":"Follow-up questions How do you decide the exact follower-count threshold for push vs pull? Based on fan-out worker throughput budget — pick the count where worst-case fan-out time stays under a few seconds, typically in the hundreds of thousands to low millions. What happens when a user follows 500 celebrities? The pull merge at read time queries a small, per-celebrity cached \"latest posts\" list rather than hitting Postgres directly, keeping the merge itself cache-backed and fast. How do you handle a user with zero cached feed (new account, cold cache)? Fall back to an on-demand build: pull recent posts from their follow list directly from Postgres/Social Graph, rank inline, and populate the ZSET for next time. How do you prevent the feed from being dominated by one hyperactive friend? Cap posts per author per feed window and fold repetition into the affinity signal so the ranking model naturally discounts over-posting from a single source. How would you support real-time-ish updates (new post appears without refresh)? A lightweight WebSocket/long-poll notifying the client a new item exists, prompting a background refetch — not pushing full content over the socket itself. How do you extend this to 10x the user base? Shard Redis and Social Graph further by user_id, push more authors into pull-only status, and move ranking feature computation to precomputed batch snapshots refreshed more frequently."},{"u":"topics/questions/fb-post-search.html","p":"Design Facebook Post Search","i":"","h":"Overview","t":"Questions Design Facebook Post Search Tests whether you can build a near-real-time search index while enforcing per-viewer privacy — the hard part isn't full-text search, it's never showing a post to someone who can't see it. 30-40 min interview Medium Concept Map click to expand Say this in the first 2 minutes The core insight: this isn't a full-text search problem, it's a privacy-filtered full-text search problem — the ACL check is the hard part, not the tokenizer Key scale number to anchor on: 500M posts/day created, but search must reflect a new post within ~5-10 seconds or results feel stale Primary tradeoff being probed: filter privacy at index time (fast, can go stale on ACL changes) vs at query time (always correct, costs latency) Component most candidates forget: a durable event stream (Kafka) between the post service and the indexer, so indexing never blocks post creation Failure mode most candidates skip: what happens when a post's audience changes (public → friends-only) after it's already indexed FB Post Search Post Service Validates and persists a post returns immediately without waiting on indexing step4-dataflow Stateless Scales independent of the indexer 201 response Indexing is fully async Postgres Source of truth for post text author and visibility step2-entities Snowflake id Post.id is the primary key Visibility enum public friends or custom Kafka Streams post created and visibility changed events to the indexer step4-dataflow Decouples write Indexing lag never blocks posting Replay safe Crashed worker resumes from offset Indexing Worker Tokenizes post text and snapshots the audience into the index doc step6-deepdive-invindex Consumer group Scales with post creation bursts visible_to snapshot Denormalized ACL at index time Elasticsearch Inverted index serving relevance ranked queries over billions of docs step6-deepdive-invindex Posting lists term maps to sorted post_id list BM25 ranking Term frequency weighted by rarity Privacy Filter Re-validates the live friend and block cache before returning results step6-deepdive-privacy Redis backed Avoids a graph DB call per query Query time check Belt and suspenders over index time"},{"u":"topics/questions/fb-post-search.html","p":"Design Facebook Post Search","i":"step1-requirements","h":"Requirements","t":"Step 1 of 6 — Requirements Requirements 1 2 3 4 5 6 Arc 1 (steps 1-5) — Satisfy Functional Requirements 1 2 3 4 5 6 Arc 2 (all 6 steps) — Satisfy Non-Functional Requirements Functional Search posts by keyword and return relevance-ranked results Index a new post's text within seconds of creation Only return posts the searching user is allowed to see Support basic filters — author, date range, post type Non-Functional 500M posts/day created, ~10B searchable posts in the index Indexing lag <10s p99 from post creation to searchable Search latency <200ms p99 including privacy filtering Zero privacy leaks — a false negative (missed result) beats a false positive (leaked post) What NOT to build: semantic/vector search, comment or photo search, typeahead/autocomplete, or a ranking ML pipeline with personalization signals. This is keyword search over post text with correct privacy enforcement only. ~6k/s post creation rate (avg) ~40k/s search query rate (peak) <10s indexing lag target, p99 ~10B documents in the live index"},{"u":"topics/questions/fb-post-search.html","p":"Design Facebook Post Search","i":"step2-entities","h":"Core entities","t":"Step 2 of 6 — Core Entities Core entities Three entities: the source-of-truth post, the search-optimized index entry, and the privacy scope that gates every query. Post Postgres id BIGINT (Snowflake) author_id BIGINT text TEXT visibility ENUM(public, friends, custom) created_at TIMESTAMP Because a post is a transactional write with strong durability needs and moderate volume (6k/s) — a relational store fits and stays the single source of truth. Tradeoff: not queryable for full-text at this scale. IndexEntry Elasticsearch post_id keyword text analyzed text (inverted index) author_id keyword visible_to keyword[] — denormalized ACL snapshot created_at date Because relevance-ranked keyword search over billions of documents needs an inverted index, which Postgres full-text search doesn't scale to at this volume. Tradeoff: a second, eventually-consistent copy of the data that can drift from Postgres. PrivacyScope Redis user_id BIGINT friend_ids Set<BIGINT>, cached blocked_ids Set<BIGINT>, cached Because query-time privacy filtering needs the searching user's social graph on every request — caching it in Redis avoids a graph-DB round trip per search. Tradeoff: cache can be briefly stale after a friend/block change. Primary key: Post.id (Snowflake). Critical index: IndexEntry.text inverted index plus a filter on visible_to — every query must intersect the term match with the viewer's allowed audience before ranking."},{"u":"topics/questions/fb-post-search.html","p":"Design Facebook Post Search","i":"step3-api","h":"API design","t":"Step 3 of 6 — API / Interface API design Three endpoints — create, search, and delete. Indexing itself is never a direct API call; it's driven by events. POST /api/v1/posts Auth: required Request { text, visibility, custom_audience? } Response { post_id, created_at } — 201, indexing happens async GET /api/v1/search Auth: required Request ?q=&author_id=&since=&cursor= Response { results[], next_cursor } — pre-filtered to viewer's audience DELETE /api/v1/posts/{post_id} Auth: required (owner) Request path param only Response HTTP 204, emits a delete event that tombstones the index entry PUT /api/v1/posts/{post_id}/visibility Auth: required (owner) Request { visibility, custom_audience? } Response HTTP 200, emits a re-index event to refresh visible_to"},{"u":"topics/questions/fb-post-search.html","p":"Design Facebook Post Search","i":"step4-dataflow","h":"Data flow","t":"Step 4 of 6 — Data Flow Data flow Write path (blue) — post created and streamed to the indexer — and read path (mint) — a search query filtered by privacy before ranking. WRITE PATH — post created → indexed Client Post Svc stateless Postgres Kafka post events Index Worker ES READ PATH — search query Client Search API Privacy Filter Redis-backed ES cluster ASYNC PATH — visibility change re-indexes affected posts Visibility change → Kafka → re-index Write path — post commits, then streams to the indexer Read path — privacy filter applied before ranking ACL changes re-enter the same pipeline as new posts Client posts text — Post Svc writes to Postgres and returns 201 immediately, indexing is not on this path Post-created event streamed to Kafka — fired after the DB commit, decoupling indexing latency from write latency Index Worker consumes the event — tokenizes text and snapshots the current audience into visible_to Document written to Elasticsearch — searchable within the <10s lag target Search request hits Privacy Filter first — the viewer's friend/block sets gate the query before it ever reaches ranking User submits a new post Validates and persists returns 201 immediately Source of truth for the post Post-created event streamed here after the DB commit Tokenizes text snapshots the audience builds the doc Inverted index sharded by post_id hash User submits a search query Parses query orchestrates the privacy-filtered search Fetches the viewer's friend/block sets to build the ACL filter Term match ANDed with viewer's audience filter then ranked Visibility change fires the same event pipeline as a new post"},{"u":"topics/questions/fb-post-search.html","p":"Design Facebook Post Search","i":"step5-hld","h":"High-level design","t":"Step 5 of 6 — High-Level Design High-level design Full system: post creation, the async indexing pipeline, and privacy-filtered search together with every supporting component. Clients API Gateway Post Service stateless Postgres Kafka event stream Indexing Worker Elasticsearch Search API stateless Privacy Filter Redis-backed Client / gateway Postgres — source of truth Kafka + indexing worker Elasticsearch — inverted index Privacy filter — gates every query Component Role Tech + why Post Service Validates and persists new posts, returns immediately Stateless service over Postgres — because writes are transactional and must never wait on indexing; tradeoff is a second async system to keep in sync. Kafka Streams post-created and visibility-changed events Log-based queue — because it decouples indexing throughput from post-write throughput and replays cleanly if a worker crashes; tradeoff is added operational surface. Indexing Worker Tokenizes post text, snapshots audience, writes the doc Horizontally-scaled consumer group — because indexing throughput must track post-creation bursts; tradeoff is eventual consistency between Postgres and the index. Elasticsearch Stores the inverted index, serves ranked queries Sharded/replicated cluster — because relevance-ranked full-text search over 10B docs needs an inverted index at this scale; tradeoff is operational complexity (shard rebalancing, cluster sizing). Privacy Filter Resolves the viewer's allowed audience before ranking Redis-cached friend/block sets — because a graph-DB call on every search would blow the 200ms budget; tradeoff is a brief staleness window after a social graph change. Scaling levers — cheapest to most complex Add Elasticsearch replica shards for read throughput near-zero complexity Scale Kafka consumer group / indexing workers horizontally low complexity Cache hot query results (trending terms) at the Search API layer medium complexity Increase Elasticsearch primary shard count as document volume grows medium complexity — requires reindexing Move privacy filtering into the ES query itself via a filtered alias per audience tier high complexity — last resort Bottleneck Triggers at Exact fix Indexing lag exceeds target Kafka consumer lag >10s sustained Add indexing worker instances; increase Kafka partition count for parallelism Elasticsearch query latency spikes p99 search latency >200ms Add replica shards; cache Privacy Filter results more aggressively Privacy Filter cache miss storm Redis miss rate >20% after a mass social-graph update Pre-warm cache for active users; fall back to graph DB with a short circuit breaker Privacy enforcement point: index time vs query time Index time (fast, can go stale) Query time (always correct) Search index freshness Strong Eventual Both post creation and search requests originate here Auth rate limiting routing Validates persists returns immediately Source of truth Buffers post-created and visibility-changed events Tokenizes text snapshots ACL writes the doc Sharded inverted index replicated Query parsing orchestrates privacy-filtered search Cached friend/block sets gates every query"},{"u":"topics/questions/fb-post-search.html","p":"Design Facebook Post Search","i":"step6-deepdive-invindex","h":"Deep dive: the inverted index","t":"Step 6 of 6 — Deep Dives Deep dive: the inverted index The data structure that makes \"find every post containing this word\" fast instead of a full scan across 10B rows. \"launching our new dashboard\" post_id: 9931 Tokenizer stem + lowercase terms: launch, dashboard TERM → POSTING LIST (doc_ids containing that term) \"launch\" [9931, 9877, 9812, 9701, ...] \"dashboard\" [9931, 9650, 9502, ...] multi-term query = intersect posting lists, then rank by relevance (TF-IDF/BM25) Client / gateway Cache — hot path Database — source of truth Async — queue / worker Post text is tokenized — lowercased, stemmed (\"launching\" → \"launch\"), stopwords dropped Each term maps to a posting list — a sorted list of every post_id containing that term A multi-term query intersects posting lists — \"launch dashboard\" only matches posts in both lists The privacy filter ANDs against visible_to — narrows the intersected set to the viewer's allowed audience before ranking Surviving docs are scored and ranked — BM25 (term frequency weighted by rarity) orders the final results The posting list is what makes privacy filtering at query time affordable: it's a set intersection (term matches ∩ viewer's audience), not a per-document ACL check against every candidate. Raw post text is tokenized into terms at index time Lowercased stemmed stopwords removed Every post mentioning 'launch' or its stem Sorted list of post IDs enables fast intersection with an ACL filter A different query term same structure A different posting list intersected with the first for multi-term queries"},{"u":"topics/questions/fb-post-search.html","p":"Design Facebook Post Search","i":"step6-deepdive-privacy","h":"Deep dive: privacy filtering — index time vs query time","t":"Deep dive: privacy filtering — index time vs query time Where you enforce \"who can see this post\" changes the entire correctness/latency tradeoff of the system. Filter at index time Each document stores a denormalized visible_to list computed when it's indexed. Queries filter against it directly — fast, no extra hop. Risk: audience shrinks after indexing (unfriend, visibility change). Stale visible_to keeps serving the post — privacy leak. Leak persists until the next re-index event fires. Filter at query time Every search resolves the viewer's live audience (friend/block sets) and intersects it with matched posts at query time — always reflects the current ACL state. Cost: an extra Redis (or graph-DB) round trip per query, and the intersection itself adds compute versus a pre-baked field. This is the safer default for a \"zero leaks\" requirement. This system uses both: visible_to is denormalized into the index as a coarse pre-filter (cuts the candidate set fast), then Privacy Filter re-validates against the live friend/block cache at query time before results are returned — belt and suspenders, because a false positive here is worse than a false negative. Scenario User impact Detection signal Mitigation Post visibility changed after indexing Stale visible_to briefly over- or under-shares a post Re-index event lag >10s on visibility-changed topic Query-time Privacy Filter re-validates every result before returning it Elasticsearch cluster degraded Search unavailable or slow Query latency p99 spike, node health check failures Serve from replica shards; circuit-break to a \"search temporarily unavailable\" state rather than skip the privacy filter Kafka consumer lag builds up New posts take longer than 10s to become searchable Consumer group lag alert on the post-events topic Auto-scale indexing worker pool; add topic partitions Redis privacy cache miss storm Search latency spikes as filter falls back to source Cache hit rate drop below 80% Fall back to the social graph service with a circuit breaker; never skip the check itself Delete event lost or delayed Deleted post still appears in search results Reconciliation job diff between Postgres and ES doc counts Nightly reconciliation sweep tombstones orphaned index entries"},{"u":"topics/questions/fb-post-search.html","p":"Design Facebook Post Search","i":"interview-moves","h":"Interview moves","t":"Interview moves 01 \"The hard part here isn't full-text search, it's making sure a search result never leaks a post the viewer isn't allowed to see\" — names the actual constraint being tested before diving into architecture 02 \"I'll decouple indexing from post creation with Kafka so a slow indexer never adds latency to posting\" — shows the instinct to isolate the write path from a downstream async system 03 \"I'd rather show fewer results than leak one — so query-time privacy re-validation is non-negotiable even though it costs latency\" — states a concrete, defensible priority ordering unprompted 04 \"Denormalizing the audience into the index gives us a cheap pre-filter, but I still re-check against the live friend/block cache at query time\" — demonstrates the index-time vs query-time tradeoff isn't binary 05 \"A multi-term query is just a set intersection of posting lists — that's why the inverted index scales to billions of documents\" — grounds the abstract search claim in a concrete mechanism 06 \"If a post's visibility changes from public to friends-only, that has to re-enter the same indexing pipeline as a brand-new post\" — catches a subtlety most candidates miss: ACL changes, not just creates/deletes, must trigger re-indexing"},{"u":"topics/questions/fb-post-search.html","p":"Design Facebook Post Search","i":"follow-ups","h":"Follow-up questions","t":"Follow-up questions How do you rank results when many posts match the same terms? BM25 (term frequency weighted by inverse document frequency) as the base signal, with recency and engagement as secondary boosts — Elasticsearch supports this natively. What happens if the indexing worker crashes mid-batch? Kafka consumer offsets aren't committed until the ES write succeeds, so the event is simply replayed on restart — indexing is idempotent by post_id. How do you handle a user who blocks someone after their posts are already indexed? The block event invalidates that pair in the Privacy Filter cache immediately; the denormalized visible_to field catches up on the next re-index sweep. Why not just filter privacy in the Post Service before results are ever sent to Elasticsearch? Filtering must happen per-viewer at query time, not per-post at write time — the same post is visible to different audiences depending on who's searching. How would you support typo tolerance or fuzzy matching? Elasticsearch's built-in fuzzy query (edit-distance matching) on the analyzed field — costs extra query latency, so it's usually a fallback when exact-match returns few results. How do you scale this to 10x the document volume? Increase Elasticsearch primary shard count (requires reindexing), add more Kafka partitions and indexing workers in lockstep, and push hot-query caching harder at the Search API layer."},{"u":"topics/questions/google-docs.html","p":"Design Google Docs","i":"","h":"Overview","t":"Questions Design Google Docs Tests whether you can reason about real-time collaborative editing as a convergence problem. Naive last-write-wins destroys concurrent edits — you need Operational Transformation or CRDTs so every client converges. 50-60 min interview Hard Concept Map click to expand Say this in the first 2 minutes The core insight: this is a convergence problem, not a storage problem — two users editing the same region of text simultaneously will produce conflicting ops, and last-write-wins silently corrupts one user's edit You need either Operational Transformation (server transforms concurrent ops against each other) or CRDTs (ops carry enough metadata that math guarantees convergence without a central arbiter) — pick one and justify it Key scale numbers to anchor on: 1B documents, 100M DAU, ~3 concurrent editors per active document, and a <500ms propagation target for one editor's keystroke to appear for everyone else Component most candidates forget: the revision log + snapshot/compaction worker — without it, reconstructing a document replays every operation since creation, which gets slower forever Failure mode most candidates skip: an OT server crashing mid-transform — clients must resend un-acked ops after reconnect via a sequence-number protocol, or edits silently vanish Google Docs Client OT Engine Applies edits optimistically in-browser before server ack step6-deepdive-ot Optimistic apply Typing feels instant no round trip wait Rebase on ack Reapplies transformed ops from server Doc Service Stateless WebSocket gateway routing ops to the right OT server step5-hld WS gateway Persistent connection per editing session Routes by doc_id Forwards to the owning OT server OT Server Transforms concurrent ops so every client converges to one state step6-deepdive-ot One owner per doc Single agreed order for transforms transform function Adjusts op position against concurrent ops Revision Log Append-only ordered history of every accepted operation step2-entities seq_no ordering Server-assigned source of truth for order Replay source Feeds both live delivery and history Snapshot Worker Periodically materializes full document state to bound replay cost step6-deepdive-snapshots Compaction Every N ops trims old replay chains Bounded replay Reconstruction cost stays constant Presence Service Tracks live cursors and avatars via heartbeat and TTL step4-dataflow Heartbeat + TTL Self-heals after ungraceful disconnects Ephemeral state Not durable just current viewers"},{"u":"topics/questions/google-docs.html","p":"Design Google Docs","i":"step1-requirements","h":"Requirements","t":"Step 1 of 6 — Requirements Requirements 1 2 3 4 5 6 Arc 1 (steps 1-5) — Satisfy Functional Requirements 1 2 3 4 5 6 Arc 2 (all 6 steps) — Satisfy Non-Functional Requirements Functional Multiple users edit the same document simultaneously Every editor's changes appear for every other editor in near real time Presence: see who else is currently in the doc, with live cursor/avatar View revision history and restore an earlier version Non-Functional Changes propagate to other editors in <500ms All clients must converge to the identical final document state, no exceptions Client can type optimistically without waiting on a server round trip Survive a server crash mid-edit without losing or duplicating an operation What NOT to build: rich commenting/suggestion-mode review workflows, a full offline-first sync engine (extended disconnected editing with local persistence), or a granular per-paragraph permission/sharing ACL system. This is real-time concurrent editing and convergence only. 1B documents 100M daily active users ~3 avg concurrent editors / active doc <500ms change-propagation target"},{"u":"topics/questions/google-docs.html","p":"Design Google Docs","i":"step2-entities","h":"Core entities","t":"Step 2 of 6 — Core Entities Core entities The document itself is a small, frequently-read metadata pointer; the real write volume lives in an append-only operation log, with periodic snapshots to bound replay cost. Document Firestore-style doc_id UUID owner_id BIGINT title TEXT current_rev BIGINT — latest sequence # latest_snapshot_id UUID → Snapshot Because metadata is read constantly (every doc open, every listing) but written rarely — a per-doc document store optimized for point reads is the right fit. Tradeoff: the actual content isn't stored here, only a pointer to the latest snapshot + rev number. Operation Revision log op_id UUID doc_id, seq_no composite — ordered by server seq client_id who authored it op_type, pos, payload insert/delete, position, text transformed_against seq_no[] — OT lineage Because every edit must be durable, ordered, and replayable — this is the append-only source of truth that both live clients and history reconstruction depend on. Tradeoff: grows unbounded per document, which is exactly why snapshots exist. Snapshot Compacted state snapshot_id UUID doc_id, seq_no rev this snapshot represents content full materialized document body created_at TIMESTAMP Because replaying every operation since doc creation gets slower forever on an old, hot document — a snapshot every N ops gives a cheap recent starting point. Tradeoff: storage cost for periodic full copies vs. unbounded replay latency. Primary key: Operation.doc_id + seq_no — the server-assigned sequence number is the single source of truth for ordering, and it's what every OT transform is computed relative to. Critical index: doc_id, seq_no range scans are what both live delivery and history replay depend on."},{"u":"topics/questions/google-docs.html","p":"Design Google Docs","i":"step3-api","h":"API design","t":"Step 3 of 6 — API / Interface API design Editing and presence are both bidirectional, high-frequency, low-latency — persistent WebSocket connections. Initial load and history are simple point reads over REST. GET /api/v1/doc/{id} Auth: required (viewer/editor) Request (none — doc_id in path) Response { content, current_rev, snapshot_id } WEBSOCKET /ws/doc/{id}/edit Auth: required (editor) Message in { op: insert|delete, pos, payload, base_rev, client_seq } Message out { op' (transformed), server_seq, ack_client_seq } GET /api/v1/doc/{id}/history?at={revision} Auth: required (editor) Request (query param: target revision or timestamp) Response { content_at_revision, nearest_snapshot_seq, ops_replayed } WEBSOCKET /ws/doc/{id}/presence Auth: required (editor/viewer) Message in { cursor_pos, selection_range } heartbeat every ~5s Message out { active_users: [{ user_id, cursor_pos, color }] } WebSockets over REST for editing and presence: every keystroke needs a sub-500ms round trip and the server needs to push transformed ops back to every other connected client — a persistent connection avoids reconnect overhead on every single character typed."},{"u":"topics/questions/google-docs.html","p":"Design Google Docs","i":"step4-dataflow","h":"Data flow","t":"Step 4 of 6 — Data Flow Data flow Op submission (blue) is the hot write path that must complete in under 500ms; doc load and presence (mint) are simpler reads that don't touch the OT transform logic. WRITE PATH — op submission, <500ms target Client A Doc Service (WS) OT Server Revision Log READ PATH — doc load / presence Client B Document Store Snapshot Store Clients — local OT engine applies ops optimistically OT Server — the hot-path convergence logic Revision log + snapshots — durable, replayable history Client applies the op locally — optimistic update, no waiting on the server, so typing feels instant Op is sent over the open WebSocket — tagged with the client's base revision and local sequence number OT server transforms it — against any concurrent ops it has already accepted since that base revision Transformed op is appended to the revision log — durable, ordered by server-assigned sequence number Transformed op is broadcast to every other connected client — each applies it locally, all converge to the same state Applies op_A optimistically to the local view before server ack Persistent connection forwards op to the OT server Transforms op against any concurrent ops already applied Append-only ordered by server sequence number Opens a document requests current content Looks up current_rev + latest_snapshot_id Nearest snapshot + replay ops after it = current content"},{"u":"topics/questions/google-docs.html","p":"Design Google Docs","i":"step5-hld","h":"High-level design","t":"Step 5 of 6 — High-Level Design High-level design Local OT engine on each client, stateless doc service in front. One authoritative per-document OT server + durable revision log. Snapshot/compaction worker; separate presence service for cursors. Client A local OT engine Client B local OT engine Doc Service WebSocket gateway OT Server 1 logical owner per doc Document Store Revision Log Snapshot Worker compaction Snapshots Presence Service cursors, avatars Clients — optimistic local apply, the thing users actually feel OT Server — the convergence brain, one logical instance per doc Document store, revision log, snapshots — durable state Snapshot worker + presence — decoupled from the edit hot path Component Role Tech + why Client OT engine Applies ops optimistically, rebases on transformed acks Runs in-browser — because waiting on a server round trip for every keystroke would blow the <500ms feel budget; tradeoff is client-side complexity to handle rebasing. OT Server Serializes and transforms concurrent ops per document One logical owner per active doc, in-memory op buffer — because transform correctness requires a single agreed-upon order; tradeoff is it's a stateful hot spot that needs careful failover. Revision Log Durable, ordered append-only operation history Log-structured store keyed by (doc_id, seq_no) — because every client replay and every history view derives from this; tradeoff is unbounded growth without compaction. Snapshot/Compaction Worker Periodically materializes full document state Background worker, every N ops — because reconstructing a doc by replaying from op #0 gets slower forever; tradeoff is snapshot storage cost. Presence Service Tracks who is currently viewing/editing In-memory, heartbeat + TTL — because \"who's here\" is ephemeral and must self-heal from ungraceful disconnects; tradeoff is a few seconds of staleness after a crash. Scaling levers — cheapest to most complex Batch small, rapid keystrokes client-side before sending low complexity Shard OT servers by doc_id so each doc has one logical owner low complexity Increase snapshot frequency for hot documents specifically medium complexity Move presence heartbeats off the OT server's critical path onto a dedicated service medium complexity Migrate from server-serialized OT to CRDTs for offline/peer-to-peer support high complexity — last resort Bottleneck Triggers at Exact fix OT server hot spot on one doc Many concurrent editors piling ops onto a single logical owner Cap concurrent editors per doc; batch small ops before transform Revision log unbounded growth Very old or very hot documents accumulate millions of ops More frequent snapshotting; archive/trim ops older than the oldest retained snapshot Presence fan-out under many viewers Large docs with dozens of simultaneous viewers Coalesce cursor updates, throttle broadcast rate per viewer Conflict resolution: server-serialized OT vs peer-to-peer CRDT Server-serialized OT Peer-to-peer CRDT Snapshot frequency: storage cost vs replay speed Cheap storage, slow replay More storage, fast replay Local OT engine applies ops optimistically before ack Second concurrent editor same document Persistent WebSocket connection routes to the doc's OT server Serializes and transforms concurrent ops for this document Per-doc metadata: current_rev latest_snapshot_id Append-only ordered by server sequence number Every N ops materializes and stores a full snapshot then can trim old ops Materialized full document bodies at known revisions Tracks who is currently in the doc — heartbeat + TTL expiry"},{"u":"topics/questions/google-docs.html","p":"Design Google Docs","i":"step6-deepdive-ot","h":"Deep dive: Operational Transformation — the hardest diagram on this site","t":"Step 6 of 6 — Deep Dives Deep dive: Operational Transformation — the hardest diagram on this site Two users type near the same position concurrently. Server transforms one operation against the other. Result: every client converges to the same final string. Setup: document is \"Hello orld\" (10 characters, missing the \"w\" in World). User A inserts \"W\" at position 6. User B, at the same instant, inserts \"!\" at position 10 (end of string). Neither has seen the other's op yet — both applied their own op locally and sent it to the server optimistically. CLIENT A TIMELINE CLIENT B TIMELINE SERVER TIMELINE — single authoritative order Base doc, rev 40 \"Hello orld\" op_A: insert(\"W\", 6) applied locally, optimistic A's local view \"Hello World\" op_A sent to server Base doc, rev 40 \"Hello orld\" op_B: insert(\"!\", 10) applied locally, optimistic B's local view \"Hello orld!\" op_B sent to server Server accepts op_A applies at rev 40 → rev 41 op_B' = transform(op_B, op_A) position 10 → 11 (A's insert shifted it) rev 42 canonical op_A' is broadcast back to Client B; op_B' is broadcast back to Client A Client / gateway Cache — hot path Database — source of truth Async — queue / worker The transform function, concretely: op_A = insert(\"W\", pos=6) , op_B = insert(\"!\", pos=10) . Since A's insert happens before B's position in the string, B's operation needs its position shifted right by the length of A's inserted text: op_B' = transform(op_B, op_A) = insert(\"!\", pos=11) . Meanwhile A's own operation is untouched relative to B, since B inserted after A's position: op_A' = transform(op_A, op_B) = insert(\"W\", pos=6) . BOTH CLIENTS CONVERGE TO THE SAME FINAL STRING, APPLYING OPS IN OPPOSITE ORDERS Client A applies [op_A, op_B'] insert(W,6) then insert(!,11) \"Hello World!\" rev 42 Client B applies [op_B, op_A'] insert(!,10) then insert(W,6) \"Hello World!\" rev 42 — IDENTICAL CONVERGED Client / gateway Cache — hot path Database — source of truth Both clients apply their own op immediately and optimistically — this is what makes typing feel instant even before the server responds Both ops are sent to the server tagged with their base revision (40) — the revision the client had last synced against The server picks an arrival order — say op_A arrives first, is applied cleanly, doc moves to rev 41 op_B references a now-stale base revision — the server transforms it: op_B' = transform(op_B, op_A) , shifting its position to account for op_A already being applied The server also computes op_A' = transform(op_A, op_B) — needed so Client B, which already applied op_B locally, can correctly apply A's change on top Server broadcasts the transformed complement to each client — Client A receives op_B' (not raw op_B), Client B receives op_A' (not raw op_A) Both clients converge to the identical final document — despite applying operations in opposite local orders, because the transform function guarantees it mathematically This is the entire reason naive last-write-wins fails: if the server just applied whichever op arrived second and discarded position awareness, op_B's \"!\" would land at raw position 10 in a document that now has an extra \"W\" — landing one character too early, silently corrupting the insert. The transform step is what prevents this. Shared starting document before either edit User A types W where they see the gap before seeing op_B Client A's own screen updates instantly — no server wait Same shared starting document B's perspective User B adds punctuation at the end concurrently before seeing op_A Client B's own screen updates instantly — no server wait Server received op_A first at revision 40 unmodified op_B arrives referencing stale rev 40 — server must transform it against op_A first Both transformed ops now apply cleanly in server order Client A already has op_A applied — receives op_B' from server and applies it Final converged state on Client A Client B already has op_B applied — receives op_A' from server and applies it Final converged state on Client B — identical to Client A despite opposite apply order"},{"u":"topics/questions/google-docs.html","p":"Design Google Docs","i":"step6-deepdive-crdt","h":"Deep dive: CRDT vs OT","t":"Deep dive: CRDT vs OT OT : central server picks an order and transforms. CRDT : metadata in each op makes replicas converge mathematically. CRDTs need no server and tolerate any delivery order. OPERATIONAL TRANSFORMATION Client A Client B OT Server required, central Client C Client D All clients depend on server ordering — no server, no convergence CRDT Client A Client B Client C Client D Peer-to-peer — no central serialization point, math guarantees convergence OT server — mandatory central authority that serializes and transforms CRDT clients — can sync with any peer, in any order, and still converge Dimension Operational Transformation CRDT Ordering authority Central server required to pick order + transform None needed — metadata makes order irrelevant Offline / peer-to-peer support Weak — needs a round trip to the server to resolve conflicts Strong — two peers can sync directly, whenever they reconnect Metadata overhead Low — position + text, transform logic lives server-side Higher — every character/op carries a unique fractional or sequence ID Implementation complexity Transform functions are notoriously hard to get exactly right for all op-type pairs Convergence proof lives in the data structure, less bespoke logic per op-type pair OT: server receives concurrent ops — picks an arrival order and computes a transform for every out-of-order pair CRDT: no server decision needed — each character/op is tagged with a unique identifier (e.g. a fractional position between neighbors) at creation time CRDT convergence is structural — merging two replicas' op sets, in any order, deterministically produces the same result because the identifiers form a total order on their own Tradeoff in practice — Google Docs uses a server-centered OT-style model since it already assumes an always-connected client and a central service; CRDTs shine when offline-first or fully peer-to-peer sync matters more than metadata size Every client must route through the server Every client must route through the server Single point that serializes and transforms every op — a required central authority Cannot converge without going through the server first Cannot converge without going through the server first Ops carry unique fractional/sequence identifiers baked in Ops carry unique fractional/sequence identifiers baked in Ops carry unique fractional/sequence identifiers baked in Ops carry unique fractional/sequence identifiers baked in"},{"u":"topics/questions/google-docs.html","p":"Design Google Docs","i":"step6-deepdive-snapshots","h":"Deep dive: revision history + snapshots","t":"Deep dive: revision history + snapshots The operation log grows forever. Reconstructing a document at any point in time uses the nearest prior snapshot plus replaying only the ops after it — never replaying from the beginning. OPERATION LOG — grows indefinitely, one entry per edit Snapshot @ op 500 Snapshot @ op 1000 op 1047 (latest, \"now\") To reconstruct \"now\": load snapshot @ 1000, replay ops 1001-1047 only 47 ops replayed — NOT all 1047 Request: doc @ revision 620 nearest prior snapshot is @ 500 Load snapshot @ 500 + replay ops 501-620 120 ops replayed, bounded cost Document @ rev 620 Cache — hot path Database — source of truth Every accepted operation is appended to the log — durable, ordered by server sequence number, never mutated Every N operations (e.g. 500), the snapshot worker materializes a full document body — stored alongside the sequence number it represents To reconstruct the document at any revision — find the nearest snapshot at or before that revision, then replay only the ops between the snapshot and the target Old ops before the oldest retained snapshot can be safely archived or trimmed — nothing downstream needs to replay past a snapshot boundary Why not always replay from op #0? Because replay cost is O(ops since creation) — on a document that's been live for years with tens of thousands of edits, every single page load or history view would get slower and slower forever. Snapshotting bounds replay cost to O(ops since the nearest snapshot), which is a controllable, tunable constant. Snapshot frequency is the dial: snapshotting every 100 ops means faster reconstruction but more storage and worker overhead; every 5,000 ops is cheap to store but means longer replay chains. Hot documents (high edit velocity) should get more frequent snapshots than a document nobody has touched in a year. Failure mode User impact Detection signal Mitigation OT server crash mid-transform In-flight ops could be lost or silently duplicated on reconnect WebSocket disconnect + missing ack for a sent op Clients track un-acked ops by sequence number and resend on reconnect; server dedupes by client_seq so no op is double-applied Stale \"still editing\" presence indicator Other editors see a ghost collaborator who already left No heartbeat received within TTL window Presence entries expire automatically after a short TTL (e.g. 10-15s) without a heartbeat refresh Snapshot/compaction worker falls behind on a hot doc Document load and history views get progressively slower Revision log length per doc and replay-to-reconstruct latency both trending up Monitor per-doc op velocity; dynamically increase snapshot frequency for docs exceeding a threshold Snapshot taken at op #A63D40 — a full materialized copy of the document Snapshot taken at op #1000 Ops since the last snapshot — not yet compacted Reconstructing an arbitrary earlier revision works the same way 120 ops replayed on top of the snapshot not 620 Reconstructed document body at exactly revision 620"},{"u":"topics/questions/google-docs.html","p":"Design Google Docs","i":"interview-moves","h":"Interview moves","t":"Interview moves 01 \"This is fundamentally a convergence problem — last-write-wins silently destroys concurrent edits, so I need either Operational Transformation or a CRDT to guarantee every client ends up in the same state\" — states the core insight unprompted 02 \"Each client applies its own operation optimistically before the server responds — that's what makes typing feel instant despite a network round trip\" — signals awareness of the client-side UX requirement, not just server correctness 03 \"When two ops arrive concurrently, the server transforms each against the other — op_B' = transform(op_B, op_A) — so applying them in opposite local orders still produces the identical final document\" — proves you actually understand OT mechanics, not just the buzzword 04 \"I wouldn't replay from operation zero to reconstruct a document — I'd snapshot every N ops so replay cost stays bounded regardless of how old the doc is\" — shows you've thought about unbounded growth before being asked 05 \"If the OT server crashes mid-transform, clients need an ack/sequence-number protocol so they know exactly which ops to resend — no op lost, none duplicated\" — proactively raises a concrete failure mode with a concrete fix 06 \"Presence is a separate, more relaxed system from editing — a stale 'still editing' indicator is a UX annoyance, not a correctness bug, so heartbeat + TTL is good enough\" — demonstrates you can triage which components need strict guarantees and which don't"},{"u":"topics/questions/google-docs.html","p":"Design Google Docs","i":"follow-ups","h":"Follow-up questions","t":"Follow-up questions Why not just use a global lock so only one person can edit at a time? It defeats the entire point of collaborative editing — users expect simultaneous typing, and a lock would serialize what should feel real-time and concurrent. What happens if the OT server itself becomes a single point of failure for a very popular document? Its state is minimal and reconstructible from the revision log, so a replacement instance can rebuild in-memory state from the last known sequence number and resume. How do you handle formatting operations (bold, font size) alongside text insert/delete? Each formatting change is its own operation type with its own transform rules against every other op type — the transform function grows combinatorially, which is why CRDTs are often preferred as op-type variety increases. Why doesn't Google Docs use a fully peer-to-peer CRDT model? It already assumes an always-connected client talking to a central service for storage, permissions, and history — a central OT server fits that model with lower per-op metadata overhead. How would you support a user editing offline for an extended period? That's explicitly out of scope for this design — a true offline-first engine would push further toward CRDTs, since OT's server-dependent transform doesn't handle long offline divergence well. How do you keep revision history storage from growing without bound? Archive or delete raw operations older than the oldest snapshot you intend to keep retrievable — anything before that boundary is never needed for replay again."},{"u":"topics/questions/instagram.html","p":"Design Instagram","i":"","h":"Overview","t":"Questions Design Instagram Tests whether you split photo storage (write-once, read-many blob storage) from feed generation (a fan-out problem) instead of treating \"Instagram\" as one monolithic system. 40-50 min interview Hard Concept Map click to expand Say this in the first 2 minutes The core insight: photo storage and feed generation are two completely separate scaling problems — storage is write-once read-many (durable blob store + CDN), feed generation is a fan-out problem Feed generation is the same shape as Facebook News Feed: push (fan-out-on-write) for normal users, pull-on-read (fan-out-on-read) for celebrity accounts, merged at read time Key scale to anchor on: 2B users, 100M photos/day uploaded, 500M DAU viewing feed — feed reads dwarf photo writes by orders of magnitude Component most candidates forget: the upload call must return immediately — thumbnailing, CDN pre-warm, and feed fan-out all happen asynchronously via a queue Failure mode most candidates skip: a celebrity with 100M followers posting — fan-out-on-write for that one post would be a catastrophic write storm, so celebrity posts are pulled at read time instead Instagram Upload Service Accepts chunked uploads writes the blob returns fast without waiting on fan-out step6-deepdive-upload 202 Accepted Returns before thumbnailing or fan-out runs Sync only to S3 The one latency-sensitive write in the pipeline S3 + CDN Durable write-once-read-many blob storage with edge delivery step2-entities Blob pointer Metadata row holds only the s3_key CDN pre-warm Avoids a cold origin fetch on first viral view Feed Generation Worker Decides push versus pull fan-out per post based on follower count step6-deepdive-feedfanout Push threshold Below it fan-out-on-write to every follower Pull threshold Above it marked pull-only to avoid a write storm Follow Graph Cassandra adjacency list partitioned by follower_id step6-deepdive-followgraph Partition key Follower lookups are a single-partition read No joins One query shape beats a relational join table Feed Cache Redis per-user sorted set holding the precomputed home feed step2-entities Capped size About 800 entries per user oldest evicted Score by time Sorted set ranks entries for cheap reads Feed Read Service Merges pushed entries with live-pulled celebrity posts at read time step6-deepdive-feedfanout Merge and rank One unified feed regardless of source Bounded pull A user follows only a handful of celebrities"},{"u":"topics/questions/instagram.html","p":"Design Instagram","i":"step1-requirements","h":"Requirements","t":"Step 1 of 6 — Requirements Requirements 1 2 3 4 5 6 Arc 1 (steps 1-5) — Satisfy Functional Requirements 1 2 3 4 5 6 Arc 2 (all 6 steps) — Satisfy Non-Functional Requirements Functional User uploads a photo with an optional caption User follows/unfollows other users User views a home feed of photos from people they follow User views a single photo, likes and comments on it Non-Functional 100M photo uploads/day, 500M DAU viewing feed Upload-to-visible latency <2s (photo readable by the uploader's own feed viewers) Feed reads are 100-1000x more frequent than photo writes — optimize for read path Highly available for reads; feed can tolerate a few seconds of staleness What NOT to build: Stories/Reels (ephemeral or short-video content, a different storage and feed model), direct messages, e-commerce/shopping tags, or the ad delivery/ranking system. This is photo upload, follow graph, and home feed only. 100M photos uploaded/day 500M daily active users viewing feed ~600k/s estimated peak feed reads/sec <2s upload-to-visible latency target"},{"u":"topics/questions/instagram.html","p":"Design Instagram","i":"step2-entities","h":"Core entities","t":"Step 2 of 6 — Core Entities Core entities The photo blob and its metadata are split across two stores; the follow graph lives in a wide-column store built for adjacency lookups; the feed itself is a precomputed cache, not a live query. Photo S3 + Postgres photo_id UUID owner_id BIGINT s3_key TEXT — blob pointer caption TEXT created_at TIMESTAMP Because the pixel bytes are an immutable, write-once-read-many blob (belongs in S3) while queryable attributes (owner, caption, timestamps) belong in a row store — one entity, two physical stores by access pattern. Tradeoff: every photo read is a metadata lookup plus a CDN-fronted blob fetch, not one query. FollowGraph Cassandra follower_id partition key followee_id clustering key followed_at TIMESTAMP is_celebrity BOOL — on followee row Because at billions of edges the only query that matters is \"give me everyone X follows\" — a partition-key lookup, no joins — and a wide-column store serves that at a fraction of the cost of a relational table with foreign keys. Tradeoff: no ad-hoc relational queries; every access pattern needs its own table. FeedEntry Redis user_id key — sorted set photo_id member score created_at, for ranking cap ~800 entries/user Because the feed is read hundreds of times more often than it changes — precomputing it into a per-user sorted set turns \"what's my feed\" from a fan-in query into an O(1) cache read. Tradeoff: storage cost of duplicating photo_ids across every follower's cache, and a cold-start problem for brand-new users. Primary key: Photo.photo_id with s3_key as the indirection to the blob. Critical index: FollowGraph partitioned by follower_id so \"who does X follow\" is a single-partition read, never a scan."},{"u":"topics/questions/instagram.html","p":"Design Instagram","i":"step3-api","h":"API design","t":"Step 3 of 6 — API / Interface API design Upload is a REST call that returns immediately — heavy lifting (thumbnailing, fan-out) happens after the response, off a queue. POST /api/v1/photos Auth: required Request multipart chunked upload + { caption } Response 202 Accepted { photo_id, status: \"processing\" } GET /api/v1/feed Auth: required Request { cursor, limit } Response { photos: [...], next_cursor } POST /api/v1/follow/{userId} Auth: required Request { } (userId in path) Response 200 { following: true } GET /api/v1/photos/{id} Auth: required Request { id } (path param) Response { photo_id, owner, caption, cdn_url, created_at } The upload endpoint returns 202 Accepted , not 201 Created , with the photo already visible on the owner's own profile but processing (thumbnails, CDN pre-warm, feed fan-out) happening asynchronously — this is what keeps upload latency low regardless of downstream fan-out cost."},{"u":"topics/questions/instagram.html","p":"Design Instagram","i":"step4-dataflow","h":"Data flow","t":"Step 4 of 6 — Data Flow Data flow Upload writes (blue) hit S3 synchronously then hand off to async workers (amber); feed reads (mint) hit the precomputed feed cache, never the follow graph directly. UPLOAD PATH — sync write, async fan-out Client Upload Svc S3 Queue Thumb Worker CDN FEED READ PATH Client Feed Read Svc Feed Cache NOTIFICATION PATH — separate, decoupled Notification Service (push, off same queue) Client apps — upload and feed viewing S3 — durable blob storage Queue + workers — async thumbnailing, fan-out, notifications Feed cache — precomputed, read hot-path Client uploads in chunks — resumable multipart upload over HTTPS Upload Svc writes the blob to S3 — then immediately returns 202 to the client, photo already visible on owner's own profile A message is pushed to the queue — decouples the fast synchronous write from slow downstream work Thumbnail worker generates sizes and pre-warms the CDN — runs entirely in the background Feed fan-out and notifications happen off the same queue — feed reads always hit the precomputed cache, never the follow graph live Chunked upload over HTTPS Validates writes blob returns 202 immediately Durable blob storage write-once read-many Decouples upload response from processing Generates thumbnail sizes runs moderation Pre-warms edge caches ahead of first view Opens the home feed Reads the precomputed feed no fan-in query Per-user sorted set of recent photo_ids Notifies followers of a new post independent of feed fan-out"},{"u":"topics/questions/instagram.html","p":"Design Instagram","i":"step5-hld","h":"High-level design","t":"Step 5 of 6 — High-Level Design High-level design Upload path (top), feed generation path (middle), and follow graph (bottom) as three largely independent subsystems joined only at fan-out time. Client App Upload Service returns 202 fast S3 CDN Queue + Workers Feed Generation Worker Follow Graph Cassandra Per-User Feed Cache Redis, fan-out-on-write Celebrity Pull Svc fan-out-on-read Feed Read Service merge + rank Notification Svc Client app — upload + feed viewing S3 + Follow Graph — durable storage CDN — pre-warmed edge cache for photo blobs Feed cache — precomputed hot-path for normal users Queues, workers, celebrity pull, notifications Component Role Tech + why Upload Service Accepts chunked uploads, writes blob, returns fast Stateless HTTP service in front of S3 — because the client should never wait on downstream processing; tradeoff is the client sees \"processing\" state briefly. S3 + CDN Durable photo storage + edge delivery Object storage is write-once-read-many by nature; CDN pre-warm avoids a cold origin fetch on first viral view — tradeoff is CDN cache invalidation complexity if a photo is deleted. Feed Generation Worker Decides push vs pull per post Reads follower count from the Follow Graph — under a threshold, fans out to feed caches; over it, marks the post for pull-only. Tradeoff: threshold tuning is a judgment call, not a hard science. Follow Graph (Cassandra) Adjacency list — who follows whom Wide-column store partitioned by follower_id — because the only query is a fast key lookup, never a join; tradeoff is duplicated data if reverse lookups (followers of X) are also needed. Feed Cache (Redis) Precomputed per-user feed Sorted set per user, capped at ~800 entries — because reading a feed should never require a live fan-in query; tradeoff is storage cost from duplicating photo_ids across followers. Bottleneck Triggers at Exact fix CDN origin overload A photo goes viral, thousands of concurrent cache misses hit S3 at once Request coalescing at the origin + CDN pre-warm on upload so the first real viewer is already a cache hit Feed cache cold start Brand-new user with an empty or sparse follow graph Fall back to a trending/popular pull query until the follow graph and feed cache warm up Celebrity fan-out storm An account with tens of millions of followers posts Route celebrity posts to pull-on-read instead of pushing to every follower's cache Scaling levers — cheapest to most complex Add CDN edge PoPs and increase TTL for immutable photo blobs low complexity Cap feed cache size per user (~800 entries) instead of storing full history low complexity Tune the celebrity-threshold follower count that switches push to pull medium complexity Shard the Follow Graph by follower_id across more Cassandra nodes medium complexity Pre-rank and cache merged feeds for celebrity-heavy users to avoid recomputing the merge on every read high complexity — last resort Feed freshness: real-time vs cached Real-time Cached, seconds stale Photo blob storage: cost vs redundancy Cheapest storage tier Multi-region redundancy Uploads photos views feed Accepts chunked upload returns immediately Durable blob storage Edge cache pre-warmed for viral spread Async thumbnailing moderation fan-out trigger Decides push vs pull per post based on author's follower count Adjacency list — who follows whom at scale Normal users: fan-out-on-write pushes entries here at post time Celebrity accounts: pulled live at read time merged with cache Merges pushed + pulled entries serves the client Notifies followers of new posts decoupled from feed cache"},{"u":"topics/questions/instagram.html","p":"Design Instagram","i":"step6-deepdive-upload","h":"Deep dive: photo upload pipeline","t":"Step 6 of 6 — Deep Dives Deep dive: photo upload pipeline The upload call is synchronous only up to the S3 write. Everything after that — thumbnailing, CDN propagation — is async, off a queue, and invisible to the client's latency budget. Client Upload Svc S3 Queue Thumb Worker chunked upload (multipart) PUT blob S3 write ack 202 Accepted returned immediately enqueue processing job (async) dequeue reads original, writes thumbnail sizes CDN pre-warm Client sends the photo in chunks — resumable multipart upload, tolerant of a flaky mobile connection Upload Svc streams the blob to S3 — the only synchronous, latency-sensitive write in the whole pipeline 202 Accepted returns to the client immediately — the original photo is already viewable on the owner's own profile at full resolution A processing job is enqueued — decouples the fast path from everything downstream Thumbnail worker generates multiple sizes and pre-warms the CDN — happens entirely in the background, typically completing well under the 2s \"visible to followers\" target The key move: the upload call's latency is bounded by one S3 write, not by thumbnailing or fan-out. Anything that can happen after the client stops caring about the response belongs on the queue, not in the request path."},{"u":"topics/questions/instagram.html","p":"Design Instagram","i":"step6-deepdive-feedfanout","h":"Deep dive: feed generation — push vs pull hybrid","t":"Deep dive: feed generation — push vs pull hybrid Same pattern as the Facebook News Feed problem: fan-out-on-write for normal users, fan-out-on-read for celebrity accounts, merged at serve time. Normal User Post Fan-out-on-Write push at post time Each Follower's Feed Cache Celebrity Post Marked Pull-Only no fan-out write Fan-out-on-Read pulled when a follower opens feed Feed Read Service merges both sources, ranks, returns page Follower's Feed Push (fan-out-on-write) — normal users, written at post time Pull (fan-out-on-read) — celebrity accounts, fetched at view time Feed Generation Worker checks follower count — below the celebrity threshold, pushes a feed entry into every follower's cache immediately Above the threshold, the post is marked pull-only — no write fan-out happens; writing to tens of millions of caches for one post would be catastrophic Follower opens their feed — Feed Read Service reads their precomputed cache (push-based entries already there) Feed Read Service also live-queries the small set of celebrities they follow — cheap because a user follows only a handful of celebrities, not millions Both sources are merged and ranked by recency — the follower sees one unified feed, unaware of which posts were pushed vs pulled The threshold isn't a hard science — it's tuned against write amplification: a user with 5,000 followers fanning out on write costs 5,000 cache writes; a user with 100M followers doing the same would be a full write storm across the fleet. Pick a threshold (e.g. ~1M followers) where push cost stops being negligible. A post from a normal user (few thousand followers) Follower count under threshold — push now Entry written into every follower's feed cache A post from a celebrity account (tens of millions of followers) Follower count over threshold — writing to millions of caches at once would be a storm Fetched live at read time instead of pushed at post time Combines pushed entries + live-pulled celebrity posts ranks by time Sees one merged ranked feed regardless of source"},{"u":"topics/questions/instagram.html","p":"Design Instagram","i":"step6-deepdive-followgraph","h":"Deep dive: follow graph storage","t":"Deep dive: follow graph storage Modeled as an adjacency list in a wide-column store, not a relational join table — because the only query that matters at billions of edges is a fast single-partition lookup. App Server Cassandra partition key: follower_id PARTITION follower_id = 42 followee_id: 7 followed_at: t1 followee_id: 91 followed_at: t2 followee_id: 156 followed_at: t3 ... clustered by followed_at, all in one partition Why not relational (foreign-key join table)? Billions of edges, one access pattern (\"who does X follow\") — a join buys nothing here, and costs index maintenance + join overhead at a scale relational engines don't handle cheaply. Client / gateway Database — source of truth Model as an adjacency list — one row per (follower, followee) edge, partitioned by follower_id \"Who does X follow\" becomes a single-partition read — no join, no cross-partition scan, sub-millisecond A separate table, partitioned by followee_id, answers \"who follows X\" — denormalized on purpose, written twice per follow action Celebrity flag stored on the followee's row — cheap to check at fan-out decision time without a separate lookup No multi-row transactions needed — a follow/unfollow is a single-partition write, naturally atomic The relational instinct is to model this as a join table with foreign keys — but at billions of edges, joins don't help because there's exactly one query shape (\"give me all edges for this key\"). A wide-column store turns that into what it actually is: a fast key-value lookup, denormalized in both directions to avoid ever needing a join. Scenario User impact Detection signal Mitigation CDN origin overload on a viral photo Thundering herd of cache misses hits the S3 origin at once Spike in S3 GET latency + origin error rate Request coalescing at the CDN edge; pre-warm on upload so viral spread rarely reaches a cold origin Feed cache cold start for a new user Empty feed — no follow graph yet, no cached entries Feed cache miss + empty follow graph on read Fall back to a trending/popular-content pull query until the user builds a follow graph Celebrity post fan-out storm A 100M-follower account posting would trigger 100M cache writes if pushed Follower count check at post time exceeds threshold Route to pull-on-read instead of push; followers fetch the post live at feed-view time Query: who does user 42 follow? Single-partition read on follower_id=42 — no join no scan"},{"u":"topics/questions/instagram.html","p":"Design Instagram","i":"interview-moves","h":"Interview moves","t":"Interview moves 01 \"Photo storage and feed generation are two completely separate scaling problems — one's a write-once-read-many blob problem, the other's a fan-out problem\" — states the core insight unprompted 02 \"The upload call should return the instant the blob hits S3 — thumbnailing and fan-out happen async off a queue\" — signals you understand sync vs async latency budgets 03 \"Feed generation here is the same shape as the Facebook News Feed problem — push for normal users, pull for celebrities, merged at read time\" — connects to a well-known pattern instead of reinventing it 04 \"I wouldn't model the follow graph relationally — at billions of edges there's one query shape, so a wide-column adjacency list beats a join table\" — justifies a NoSQL choice with a concrete reason, not a buzzword 05 \"A celebrity posting to 100M followers can't fan out on write — that's a write storm, so I'd pull those posts at read time instead\" — proactively surfaces the hardest failure mode before being asked 06 \"A brand-new user has no follow graph and no cached feed — I'd fall back to trending content rather than showing an empty screen\" — handles the cold-start edge case unprompted"},{"u":"topics/questions/instagram.html","p":"Design Instagram","i":"follow-ups","h":"Follow-up questions","t":"Follow-up questions How do you decide the follower-count threshold for push vs pull? Tune it against write amplification cost — pick the count where fan-out write volume per post starts to meaningfully strain the cache fleet, typically in the hundreds of thousands to low millions. What happens if a user follows several celebrities — does every feed read get slow? No — a user follows only a handful of celebrities even with a huge follow graph, so the live pull query touches a small, bounded set of accounts, not their whole feed. How do you delete a photo and make sure it disappears everywhere, including the CDN? Mark it deleted in Postgres immediately (stops new reads), issue a CDN cache invalidation for that key, and let feed caches skip it lazily on next read via a soft-delete check. How would you support re-ordering the feed by engagement instead of pure recency? Swap the sorted-set score from timestamp to a computed ranking score, recomputed periodically by a ranking worker — the cache structure doesn't change, only what \"score\" means. How do you avoid unbounded growth of a user's feed cache? Cap each per-user sorted set at a fixed size (e.g. ~800 entries) and evict the oldest on insert — old enough posts are past the point anyone scrolls to them anyway. What's the impact of a follow-graph write during an active fan-out? Fan-out is computed from a snapshot of followers at post time; a follow that lands mid-fan-out simply misses that one post and catches up on the next one — acceptable staleness, not a correctness bug."},{"u":"topics/questions/job-scheduler.html","p":"Design a Distributed Job Scheduler","i":"","h":"Overview","t":"Questions Design a Distributed Job Scheduler Tests whether you can turn \"run this job at this time\" into a leader-election and exactly-once-dispatch problem instead of quietly relying on a single cron box. 45-55 min interview Hard Concept Map click to expand Say this in the first 2 minutes The core insight: this is a leader-election + exactly-once-dispatch problem — cron on one box is a single point of failure, cron on every box double-fires every job Key scale number to anchor on: 10M scheduled jobs with sub-minute recurrence granularity means the scheduler must scan and claim due jobs continuously, not just once a minute Primary tradeoff being probed: at-least-once dispatch with idempotent workers vs true exactly-once — most production schedulers pick the former because it's dramatically simpler to build correctly Component most candidates forget: a durable \"next_run_time\" index so a scheduler doesn't have to replay history to figure out what's due Failure mode most candidates skip: what happens to missed jobs when the entire scheduler tier is down for an hour during a deploy or outage Job Scheduler Job Store Durable record of every job and its next_run_time step2-entities next_run_time index Drives which jobs are due Durable Survives a full scheduler restart Scheduler Shards Each shard owns a partition of jobs to reduce contention step6-deepdive-sharding Partitioned by hash Spreads load across shards Independent leaders One leader claims jobs per shard Coordination Svc Elects the leader per shard and tracks liveness step5-hld Leader election Prevents double-dispatch Health checks Detects a dead leader fast Dispatch Queue Buffers claimed jobs before workers pick them up step6-deepdive-dispatch Decouples claim from run Claim does not block on worker capacity At-least-once Workers must be idempotent Worker Pool Executes the actual job logic step4-dataflow Idempotent execution Safe to retry a claimed job Execution Log Records outcome for catch-up"},{"u":"topics/questions/job-scheduler.html","p":"Design a Distributed Job Scheduler","i":"step1-requirements","h":"Requirements","t":"Step 1 of 6 — Requirements Requirements 1 2 3 4 5 6 Arc 1 (steps 1-5) — Satisfy Functional Requirements 1 2 3 4 5 6 Arc 2 (all 6 steps) — Satisfy Non-Functional Requirements Functional Create a scheduled job: one-off (\"run at T\") or recurring (cron-style, sub-minute granularity) Dispatch each due job to exactly one worker at (approximately) its scheduled time Update or delete a job's schedule at any time before it fires Retry failed executions with backoff; support manual/on-demand trigger Non-Functional 10M scheduled jobs live at once, sub-minute recurrence supported Dispatch latency <5s from scheduled time, even under scheduler node failure No duplicate execution under normal operation; no permanently lost jobs on any single failure Scheduler tier itself must be horizontally scalable, not a single leader bottleneck What NOT to build: a full DAG/workflow-dependency engine like Airflow's task graphs, a scheduling UI/dashboard, or multi-tenant billing/quota enforcement. This is the dispatch guarantee problem — reliably firing individual jobs exactly once at the right time — not workflow orchestration. 10M scheduled jobs live <5s dispatch latency target ~2,000/s dispatches/sec at peak 64 scheduler shards"},{"u":"topics/questions/job-scheduler.html","p":"Design a Distributed Job Scheduler","i":"step2-entities","h":"Core entities","t":"Step 2 of 6 — Core Entities Core entities Job and Execution are durable Postgres rows because dispatch correctness depends on ACID claims; Worker registration is ephemeral and lives in Redis because it's just a liveness signal. Job Postgres job_id UUID schedule cron expr or one-off TIMESTAMP next_run_time TIMESTAMPTZ, indexed claimed_by scheduler_shard_id, nullable claimed_until TIMESTAMPTZ lease, nullable overlap_policy skip / queue / allow_concurrent Because job state is the source of truth for \"what should fire when\" — losing or duplicating a row means either a missed job or a double-fired one. Tradeoff: needs sharding by job_id hash at 10M rows to keep the next_run_time index scan fast. Execution Postgres execution_id UUID job_id UUID → Job attempt_number INT status dispatched / running / succeeded / failed / dead_letter worker_id which worker ran it Because an append-only log of every run attempt is what makes retries, dead-lettering, and audit/debugging possible — a job's current state is derivable but the history must never be overwritten. Tradeoff: grows unbounded, needs periodic archival past a retention window. Worker Redis worker_id key capacity available execution slots ttl ~15s heartbeat Because worker liveness is a fast-changing, ephemeral signal used only for load balancing — it self-heals every heartbeat and doesn't need durability. Tradeoff: a Redis blip drops the worker pool view briefly, self-corrects within one heartbeat interval. Primary key: Job.job_id . Critical index: (next_run_time, claimed_until) on Job — this is what lets a scheduler shard cheaply find \"jobs due now that no one else has claimed\" without scanning all 10M rows."},{"u":"topics/questions/job-scheduler.html","p":"Design a Distributed Job Scheduler","i":"step3-api","h":"API design","t":"Step 3 of 6 — API / Interface API design Job management is plain REST — low-frequency, latency-tolerant. The interesting guarantees live in dispatch, not this API surface. POST /api/v1/jobs Auth: required (service) Request { schedule: \"cron(*/5 * * * *)\" | run_at, payload, overlap_policy } Response { job_id, next_run_time } GET /api/v1/jobs/{id}/executions Auth: required (service) Request ?limit=20&status=failed Response { executions: [{ execution_id, status, attempt_number, ran_at }] } DELETE /api/v1/jobs/{id} Auth: required (service) Request no body Response 204 — job removed from next scan onward; in-flight dispatch unaffected POST /api/v1/jobs/{id}/trigger Auth: required (service) Request { } — manual/on-demand run, bypasses schedule Response { execution_id, status: \"dispatched\" } Notice the API itself doesn't need to expose claiming, leases, or shard ownership — those are internal dispatch mechanics. A well-designed API hides the exactly-once machinery entirely from the caller."},{"u":"topics/questions/job-scheduler.html","p":"Design a Distributed Job Scheduler","i":"step4-dataflow","h":"Data flow","t":"Step 4 of 6 — Data Flow Data flow Dispatch is a write path (claim + enqueue, blue/amber) that races against no one else because of the claim; status lookups are a separate read path (mint) against the Execution log. DISPATCH PATH — claim + enqueue due jobs Job Store Scheduler Shard Claim (CAS on Job row) Dispatch Queue Worker Pool STATUS READ PATH — execution history lookup Calling Service Job API Execution Log Job Store / Execution Log — durable Postgres, source of truth Scheduler shard — the component doing the claiming Queue + workers — decoupled async execution Scheduler shard scans for due jobs — queries its partition of the next_run_time index for rows due within the next lease window Shard claims each due job — conditional UPDATE sets claimed_by/claimed_until, only succeeds if unclaimed or lease expired Claimed job is enqueued — pushed onto the durable dispatch queue, decoupling claim success from actual execution Worker pulls and executes — runs the job payload, reports success/failure back to the Execution log next_run_time is advanced — for recurring jobs, computed from the cron expression and written back so the next scan picks it up correctly next_run_time index scanned continuously Shard leader scans jobs due within its lease window Conditional claim: only wins if claimed_until has lapsed Durable queue decouples claim success from worker execution Pulls job executes idempotently reports result Service checking whether a job ran and how it went GET /jobs/ id /executions Append-only run history read-only for this path"},{"u":"topics/questions/job-scheduler.html","p":"Design a Distributed Job Scheduler","i":"step5-hld","h":"High-level design","t":"Step 5 of 6 — High-Level Design High-level design Job metadata and schedules are sharded across scheduler nodes; a coordination service handles leader election per shard so exactly one node scans and claims each partition at a time. Job Definition API Job Store Scheduler Shard 0 leader Scheduler Shard 1 leader Scheduler Shard N leader Coordination Svc ZooKeeper / etcd Dispatch Queue Worker Pool Execution Log Dead-Letter Queue Job Definition API — entry point for schedule CRUD Scheduler shards — partitioned, each with an elected leader Job Store / Coordination Svc / Execution Log — durable truth Queue, workers, dead-letter — decoupled async execution Component Role Tech + why Job Definition API CRUD for job metadata and schedules Stateless REST service — because schedule authoring is low-frequency and needs no special guarantees beyond normal API durability. Job Store Source of truth for job state + next_run_time Postgres, sharded by job_id hash — because claims need transactional compare-and-swap semantics; tradeoff is cross-shard queries (e.g. \"all jobs for tenant X\") need a secondary index or fan-out. Scheduler Shard Scans its partition, claims and dispatches due jobs One elected leader per shard — because only one process should scan a partition at a time; tradeoff is a brief dispatch gap during leader failover (bounded by lease timeout). Coordination Svc Leader election, lease renewal, shard membership ZooKeeper or etcd — because it gives strongly-consistent, fault-tolerant consensus for \"who owns this shard right now\"; tradeoff is added operational complexity of running a consensus cluster. Worker Pool Executes job payloads Stateless, horizontally scalable pool pulling from the dispatch queue — because execution is embarrassingly parallel and idempotency, not coordination, is the correctness lever here. Scaling levers — cheapest to most complex Add worker pool capacity horizontally, stateless low complexity Widen the scan lookahead window to smooth bursty due-job spikes low complexity Increase scheduler shard count to shrink each shard's job partition medium complexity Read replicas for the Job Store to offload status/list queries from the claim path medium complexity Re-shard the Job Store itself as job count grows past a single Postgres cluster's comfort zone high complexity — last resort Bottleneck Triggers at Exact fix next_run_time index scan contention >10M rows, many due in the same second Partition the index by shard range; widen scan batches instead of per-job polling Coordination service load Very high shard count doing frequent lease renewal Lengthen lease duration; batch renewals; fewer, larger shards Worker pool saturation Dispatch rate exceeds available worker capacity Autoscale worker pool on queue depth; backpressure via queue max length Dispatch guarantee: at-least-once vs exactly-once Exactly-once At-least-once + idempotent Catch-up policy after extended scheduler outage Run-all-missed Skip-to-latest Creates/updates/deletes job definitions Source of truth: schedule next_run_time claim state Shard 0: owns hash(job_id) % N == 0 Shard 1: owns hash(job_id) % N == 1 Shard N: same pattern repeats across all partitions ZooKeeper/etcd — leader election per shard lease renewal Durable at-least-once delivery to workers Executes job payload reports result must be idempotent Success/failure written per attempt append-only After max retries exhausted routed here for manual/alerted handling"},{"u":"topics/questions/job-scheduler.html","p":"Design a Distributed Job Scheduler","i":"step6-deepdive-sharding","h":"Deep dive: leader election and sharding","t":"Step 6 of 6 — Deep Dives Deep dive: leader election and sharding Jobs are partitioned across scheduler shards by hash(job_id) mod N. Each shard elects exactly one leader via the coordination service; only the leader scans and claims that shard's jobs. 10M jobs partitioned by hash(job_id) % N shards Shard 0 — Leader node A, scanning Shard 0 — Standby Shard 1 — Leader node B, scanning Shard 1 — Standby Shard 2 — Leader node C, crashed Shard 2 — Standby promoting… Coordination Svc lease per shard New leader re-scans Job Store's next_run_time index Active leader — the only node scanning/claiming its shard Coordination Svc / Job Store — durable truth both sides trust Standby promoting after a detected leader failure Jobs are partitioned — hash(job_id) mod N assigns every job to exactly one shard, deterministically Each shard elects a leader — nodes race to acquire an ephemeral lock/lease in the coordination service; loser becomes standby Leader renews its lease continuously — a short TTL (a few seconds) means failure is detected fast without needing explicit heartCheck failure signals Leader crashes → lease lapses — the coordination service releases the lock, standby nodes race to acquire it New leader re-scans from the durable index — because next_run_time lives in the Job Store, not in the old leader's memory, no scheduling state is lost on failover The key design choice: scheduler shards are stateless with respect to \"what's due\" — all of that lives in the durable Job Store's next_run_time index. A leader failing just means a brief pause in scanning that partition, never lost knowledge of what to run. Leader currently owns this shard actively scanning + claiming Standby replica ready to take over if the leader's lease lapses Leader currently owns this shard actively scanning + claiming Standby replica ready to take over if the leader's lease lapses This leader just crashed — lease will expire and trigger re-election Will be promoted once the coordination service detects the lapsed lease Holds the lease record per shard: which node until when New leader resumes by scanning next_run_time from the durable store — no job is permanently lost"},{"u":"topics/questions/job-scheduler.html","p":"Design a Distributed Job Scheduler","i":"step6-deepdive-dispatch","h":"Deep dive: exactly-once dispatch via conditional claim","t":"Deep dive: exactly-once dispatch via conditional claim Failover can briefly produce two leaders (lapsed lease unnoticed). Real guard: compare-and-swap claim on the job row itself. TWO REPLICAS SCAN THE SAME DUE JOB — only one wins the claim Scheduler Replica A Scheduler Replica B Job row — CAS claim claimed_by + claimed_until Replica A: WINS claim Replica B: 0 rows affected Dispatch Queue (once) Replica B continues scanning — no retry needed Job row — the single point of truth the CAS operates on Winning replica — proceeds to enqueue Dispatch queue — receives exactly one enqueue for this job/run Both replicas find the same due job — a race is possible during failover windows or clock skew between shard scans Both issue a conditional UPDATE — SET claimed_by = self, claimed_until = now() + lease WHERE job_id = X AND claimed_until < now() Only one UPDATE matches the WHERE clause — the database's row-level locking guarantees exactly one writer sees an affected-rows count of 1 The winner enqueues the job — exactly one dispatch reaches the queue for this run The loser detects 0 affected rows and moves on — no error, no retry storm, just proceeds to the next due job in its batch This only works if the claim and the \"is this job due\" check happen against the same durable, transactional store. If next_run_time lived in a cache instead of Postgres, a stale cache read could let both replicas believe they won. Believes it's still leader scans the due job independently Newly elected also scans the same due job UPDATE ... WHERE claimed_until < now() — conditional atomic Replica A's UPDATE matched the WHERE clause first — claim succeeds Replica B's UPDATE affected 0 rows — job already claimed backs off Only the winner enqueues the job for execution Loser simply moves on to the next due job in its scan batch"},{"u":"topics/questions/job-scheduler.html","p":"Design a Distributed Job Scheduler","i":"step6-deepdive-catchup","h":"Deep dive: scheduler drift and catch-up","t":"Deep dive: scheduler drift and catch-up Extended scheduler outage → backlog of missed runs. Durable next_run_time index means no full-history replay. Catch-up is just a policy decision for what's overdue. TIMELINE — scheduler tier down for 40 minutes during a deploy tier healthy tier goes down 3 missed runs accumulate — but only next_run_time advances conceptually, no rows written tier restored Job Store next_run_time still points to first miss run-once-immediately default for most jobs skip-to-latest default for high-frequency jobs run-all-missed rare, explicit opt-in Job Store — one stale next_run_time value, not a log of every miss Run-once-immediately — the sane default for most recurring jobs Run-all-missed — expensive, only for jobs where every interval has meaning Scheduler tier goes down — no shard is scanning or claiming; due jobs simply accumulate against their existing next_run_time Nothing is lost, nothing is duplicated during the outage — because next_run_time is a single durable field per job, not an append-only log of every missed tick Tier comes back and resumes scanning — every job whose next_run_time is now in the past is \"due,\" possibly by a large margin Catch-up policy decides what happens next — run-once-immediately (fire once, then resume normal cadence), skip-to-latest (recompute next_run_time strictly forward, don't fire for missed ticks), or run-all-missed (fire once per missed interval — expensive, rarely correct) Policy is set per job, not globally — a metrics-rollup cron job should skip-to-latest; a billing-cycle job may need run-all-missed Run-all-missed is a trap for anything sub-minute: 40 minutes down on a 1-minute job means 40 backlogged executions firing in a burst the moment the tier recovers, which can itself take the system down again. Default to skip-to-latest or run-once-immediately unless a job explicitly needs every interval accounted for. Failure mode User impact Detection signal Mitigation Worker crashes mid-execution Job appears stuck in \"running\" state Execution row exceeds expected runtime with no completion signal Idempotent job design + retry with exponential backoff; route to dead-letter queue after max retries Job runtime exceeds its own interval Overlapping runs of the same job, resource contention New run due while previous attempt still marked running Per-job overlap_policy: skip the new run, queue it behind the current one, or explicitly allow concurrent execution Coordination service (ZooKeeper/etcd) unavailable Shards can't confirm or renew leadership Lease renewal calls start failing or timing out Shards degrade to a safe default — pause new dispatches rather than risk two nodes believing they're both leader Still holds the original next_run_time — nothing to replay just one stale timestamp Fire once immediately then resume normal cadence Recompute next_run_time strictly in the future skip everything missed Fire once per missed interval in order — used only for jobs where every run matters (e.g. billing)"},{"u":"topics/questions/job-scheduler.html","p":"Design a Distributed Job Scheduler","i":"interview-moves","h":"Interview moves","t":"Interview moves 01 \"This is fundamentally a leader-election and exactly-once-dispatch problem, not a cron-syntax problem\" — states the core insight unprompted, before diving into scheduling algorithms 02 \"I wouldn't run cron on one box — single point of failure — and I wouldn't run it on every box either, since that double-fires every job\" — shows you understand why the naive approaches both fail 03 \"The claim itself needs to be a conditional compare-and-swap on the job row — that's what survives two schedulers racing on the same due job\" — names the specific mechanism, not just \"use locking\" 04 \"A scheduler shard should be stateless about what's due — all of that lives in a durable next_run_time index, so a leader failing never loses scheduling state\" — separates transient compute from durable truth 05 \"I'd default to at-least-once dispatch with idempotent workers rather than chase true exactly-once — it's dramatically simpler and the failure mode is a safe retry, not silent loss\" — demonstrates pragmatic tradeoff judgment 06 \"After an extended outage I wouldn't run every missed interval by default — I'd pick a per-job catch-up policy, because run-all-missed can cause a dispatch storm the moment the system recovers\" — proactively surfaces a second-order failure mode"},{"u":"topics/questions/job-scheduler.html","p":"Design a Distributed Job Scheduler","i":"follow-ups","h":"Follow-up questions","t":"Follow-up questions How do you handle clock skew between scheduler nodes? Use the database's own clock (e.g. Postgres now()) inside the claim's WHERE clause rather than each node's local clock — the CAS still only lets one writer win regardless of skew. What happens if a worker claims a job but then dies before reporting status? The execution row's claimed_until lease expires; a monitor requeues it as a retry attempt. Idempotent job design ensures a partial or duplicate run doesn't corrupt state. How would you support 10M jobs without the scan becoming the bottleneck? Shard the Job Store itself, keep a tight (next_run_time, claimed_until) composite index per shard, and scan in small time-windowed batches instead of scanning the full table. Why not just use the coordination service (ZooKeeper/etcd) to store job state directly? Coordination services are optimized for small, low-churn config/consensus data, not millions of rows with high write throughput — Postgres with proper indexing scales dispatch state far better. How do you prevent a \"thundering herd\" when many jobs share the same schedule (e.g. hourly at :00)? Jitter next_run_time by a small random offset per job at creation time, spreading dispatch load across a few seconds instead of one instant. How would this design change to support Airflow-style task dependencies? It wouldn't fit cleanly — that needs a DAG execution engine tracking upstream/downstream task state, which is a different problem layered on top of this dispatch primitive, not a variation of it."},{"u":"topics/questions/leetcode.html","p":"Design LeetCode","i":"","h":"Overview","t":"Questions Design LeetCode (Online Code Judge) Tests whether you can safely run untrusted user code at scale — sandboxing is the actual system design problem, the CRUD around it is a distraction. 30-40 min interview Medium Concept Map click to expand Say this in the first 2 minutes The core insight: this is an untrusted-workload isolation problem, not a CRUD problem — sandboxing is the whole design, not a detail Key scale number to anchor on: ~5k submissions/sec at peak (contest start), each fanning out into 20-100 test case runs Primary tradeoff being probed: sandbox isolation strength (VM-level) vs spin-up latency (container-level) — you can't max both Component most candidates forget: a job queue between submission and execution — synchronous execution on the request thread cannot survive a contest-start spike Failure mode most candidates skip: infinite loops and fork bombs — every sandbox needs hard CPU/memory/wall-clock caps enforced by the host, not the guest LeetCode Judge Submit Svc Validates a submission and persists it before enqueueing the job step2-entities Stateless pods Does intake only no execution Fast 202 Returns queued status in ms Kafka Buffers submission jobs so a contest-start spike never overwhelms workers step6-deepdive-sandbox Burst absorption Handles 100x steady-state traffic Language partitions Avoids one runtime blocking others Judge Workers Pool that runs each test case inside an isolated sandbox step6-deepdive-sandbox gVisor Userspace kernel intercepts syscalls cgroup caps Hard CPU memory wall-clock limits Postgres Stores Submission and Problem metadata with strong consistency step2-entities Status transitions queued to running to passed or failed UUID keys Client-safe optimistic generation ClickHouse Column store holding per-test-case verdicts and runtimes step2-entities Append-only One row per test case per submission Aggregate queries Pass rate and runtime distribution WS Gateway Pushes live grading status to the still-open client socket step3-api Push over poll Sub-second updates without polling Sticky routing Per submission_id connection"},{"u":"topics/questions/leetcode.html","p":"Design LeetCode","i":"step1-requirements","h":"Requirements","t":"Step 1 of 6 — Requirements Requirements 1 2 3 4 5 6 Arc 1 (steps 1-5) — Satisfy Functional Requirements 1 2 3 4 5 6 Arc 2 (all 6 steps) — Satisfy Non-Functional Requirements Functional Submit code (multiple languages) against a problem's test cases Run submission against all test cases and return pass/fail + runtime/memory per case Stream live status (queued → running → passed/failed) to the browser Rank submissions on a contest leaderboard by correctness + speed Non-Functional Untrusted code execution must never compromise the host or other tenants ~5k submissions/sec at peak (contest start), ~50/sec steady-state Judge result returned in <10s p99 for a typical submission Every submission's resource use is hard-capped — no single job can starve the fleet What NOT to build: a full IDE with autocomplete/debugging, a general-purpose CI/CD platform, or a public code-hosting service. This is submit → sandbox-execute → grade → return only. ~5k/s submissions/sec (contest-start peak) ~150ms sandbox cold-start latency 2s / 256MB default CPU time / memory cap 20-100 test cases fanned out per submission"},{"u":"topics/questions/leetcode.html","p":"Design LeetCode","i":"step2-entities","h":"Core entities","t":"Step 2 of 6 — Core Entities Core entities Four entities — problem definition is relatively static and relational; submissions and their per-test-case results are high-write and split by access pattern. Problem Postgres problem_id BIGINT title TEXT time_limit_ms INT memory_limit_mb INT test_cases_uri TEXT → S3 Because problems are read constantly and written rarely — a relational store with a CDN-cached read path fits. Tradeoff: schema changes to limits require careful backfill for in-flight submissions. Submission Postgres submission_id UUID user_id BIGINT problem_id BIGINT → Problem language ENUM status ENUM (queued/running/passed/failed) Because a submission's row is small and needs strong consistency for status transitions the UI polls/subscribes to. Tradeoff: high write rate at contest start needs write buffering ahead of Postgres. TestCaseResult ClickHouse submission_id UUID test_case_id INT verdict ENUM (AC/WA/TLE/MLE/RE) runtime_ms INT memory_kb INT Because it's append-only, one row per test case per submission, and queried in aggregate (pass rate, runtime distribution) far more than by single row. Tradeoff: not the source of truth for \"did the submission pass overall\" — that's a rollup back onto Submission. SourceCode S3 submission_id UUID code_uri TEXT size_bytes INT Because raw source blobs are large relative to metadata and immutable once submitted — object storage is cheaper than a relational TEXT column at this volume. Tradeoff: an extra fetch on the judge worker's critical path. Primary key: Submission.submission_id (UUID, client-safe to generate optimistically). Critical index: (user_id, problem_id) on Submission — powers both \"my submission history\" and duplicate-submission throttling."},{"u":"topics/questions/leetcode.html","p":"Design LeetCode","i":"step3-api","h":"API design","t":"Step 3 of 6 — API / Interface API design Submit and fetch-problem are REST; live grading status is a WebSocket push — polling for a job that takes seconds wastes both client and server cycles. GET /api/v1/problems/{problem_id} Auth: optional Request path param only Response { title, description, time_limit_ms, memory_limit_mb, sample_cases } POST /api/v1/submissions Auth: required Request { problem_id, language, source_code } Response HTTP 202 + { submission_id, status: \"queued\" } WS /ws/submissions/{submission_id} Auth: required Connection opened right after submit, held until a terminal status Message { status, cases_passed, cases_total, verdict? } GET /api/v1/submissions/{submission_id} Auth: required (owner) Request path param only Response { status, verdict, runtime_ms, memory_kb, per_case_results } WebSocket over polling here: a submission takes 1-10s end to end and the client wants sub-second status updates during that window — polling every 500ms per active submission multiplies request volume for no benefit over a push."},{"u":"topics/questions/leetcode.html","p":"Design LeetCode","i":"step4-dataflow","h":"Data flow","t":"Step 4 of 6 — Data Flow Data flow Write path (blue) queues the job and returns immediately; read path (mint) is the async judge execution that pushes results back over the open socket. WRITE PATH — submit Client Submit Svc S3 Kafka READ PATH — judge execution, async Kafka Judge Worker Sandbox gVisor + seccomp Result Store WS Gateway Write path — submit returns 202 immediately Kafka — decouples submit from execution Sandbox — the actual hard part Client submits code — Submit Svc writes a queued Submission row and enqueues a job, returns 202 in ms Client opens a WebSocket — subscribes to that submission_id for live status Judge worker dequeues the job — pulls source from S3 and test cases from the problem's bundle Sandbox executes each test case — isolated, resource-capped, no network access Result written and pushed — verdict lands in the result store, WS gateway pushes the update to the open socket Submits source code for a problem Validates language size limits writes Submission row as queued Raw source code persisted before queueing the job Absorbs contest-start bursts decouples submit from execution Same queue as above consumed by the judge worker pool Pulls source + test cases spins up an isolated sandbox gVisor sandbox hard CPU/memory/time caps enforced by host Per-test-case verdict + runtime/memory written back Pushes status transitions to the still-open client socket"},{"u":"topics/questions/leetcode.html","p":"Design LeetCode","i":"step5-hld","h":"High-level design","t":"Step 5 of 6 — High-Level Design High-level design Full system: submission intake, the queue that absorbs bursts, the judge worker fleet, and result delivery back to the browser. Clients API Gateway Submit Svc Postgres Kafka Judge Workers gVisor sandboxes ClickHouse WS Gateway Client / gateway Kafka — burst absorption Postgres + ClickHouse WS Gateway — pushes final verdicts Component Role Tech + why Submit Svc Validates and persists a submission, enqueues the job Stateless pods — because it does no execution itself, just intake; tradeoff is it can't reject obviously-bad code without a slow static check, so it defers to the sandbox. Kafka Buffers submission jobs Log-based queue — because contest-start traffic can spike 100x steady-state in seconds and workers can't scale that fast; tradeoff is added latency during backlog drains. Judge Workers Runs each test case inside an isolated sandbox gVisor (userspace kernel) over raw containers — because untrusted code needs a real security boundary, not just namespace isolation; tradeoff is ~2-3x syscall overhead vs bare Docker. ClickHouse Stores per-test-case results Column store — because the query pattern is \"pass rate / runtime distribution across millions of case results\"; tradeoff is poor fit for the single \"did this submission pass\" point read, which is why that rolls up onto Postgres. WS Gateway Delivers live status back to the browser Persistent connection pool — because a submission's grading window (1-10s) is short enough that push beats poll; tradeoff is stateful infra that needs sticky routing per submission_id. Scaling levers — cheapest to most complex Autoscale judge worker pool on queue depth near-zero complexity Pre-warm a pool of idle sandboxes ahead of scheduled contests low complexity, cuts cold-start latency Partition Kafka by language so a slow-to-start runtime (e.g. JVM) doesn't head-of-line block others medium complexity Parallelize test cases within a single submission across multiple workers medium complexity Dedicated worker pools per trust tier (contest vs practice vs untrusted new accounts) high complexity — last resort Bottleneck Triggers at Exact fix Judge worker pool saturated Queue depth grows unbounded at contest start Pre-warmed sandbox pool + aggressive autoscaling triggered on queue depth, not CPU Slow-runtime head-of-line blocking JVM/compiled-language cold starts stall the queue Partition the queue by language so Python submissions don't wait behind Java compiles Single submission hogs a worker Infinite loop or fork bomb in submitted code Host-enforced hard wall-clock timeout + cgroup memory/CPU caps that kill the sandbox, not just the process Sandbox isolation strength vs spin-up latency Fast (raw container) Safe (microVM) Result delivery: push vs poll WebSocket push Client polling Browser IDE — submits code holds a WS for live status Auth rate limiting routing Validates and persists the submission enqueues the job Submission + Problem metadata Buffers contest-start bursts decouples submit from execution Autoscaled pool one sandbox per test case run Per-test-case verdicts runtime memory Fans the final verdict back to the client's open socket"},{"u":"topics/questions/leetcode.html","p":"Design LeetCode","i":"step6-deepdive-sandbox","h":"Deep dive: sandbox isolation layers","t":"Step 6 of 6 — Deep Dives Deep dive: sandbox isolation layers Untrusted code gets four stacked layers of containment — any single layer failing shouldn't compromise the host. Host OS Container (no network, read-only rootfs) gVisor userspace kernel seccomp-bpf syscall filter cgroup limits: 2s CPU · 256MB mem · 64 procs Host OS — never directly reachable Container — filesystem + network isolation gVisor — intercepts every syscall seccomp + cgroups — the hard numeric caps Container boundary — read-only root filesystem, no network namespace at all, so exfiltration and outbound abuse are structurally impossible, not just blocked by policy gVisor userspace kernel — reimplements the Linux syscall surface in userspace, so even a kernel exploit in the submitted code hits gVisor's sandbox, not the real host kernel seccomp-bpf filter — denies an explicit blocklist of dangerous syscalls (ptrace, mount, reboot, clone with CLONE_NEWUSER) before gVisor even has to interpret them cgroup resource caps — 2s CPU time, 256MB memory, 64 max processes/threads — enforced by the host scheduler, so a spinning process gets killed even if application-level timeouts fail Sandbox teardown — every sandbox is destroyed after one submission's run, never reused across tenants, so no state or memory leaks between users The timeout must be enforced by the host (cgroup wall-clock + CPU quota), never by a timer inside the submitted code's own process — an infinite loop or intentionally hostile submission will never call its own cleanup code. Bare metal or VM running the judge worker fleet Isolated filesystem network namespace with no egress gVisor intercepts syscalls in userspace — the untrusted code never talks to the real kernel directly Denies dangerous syscalls outright — ptrace mount reboot raw sockets cgroups enforce the numbers that actually stop abuse"},{"u":"topics/questions/leetcode.html","p":"Design LeetCode","i":"step6-deepdive-parallelism","h":"Deep dive: test case runner parallelism","t":"Deep dive: test case runner parallelism A single submission with 100 test cases can't run them serially against a 10s p99 target — they fan out to independent sandboxes and results aggregate back. Judge worker claims the job — reads the test case bundle manifest for the problem (count, inputs, expected outputs) Fan out N sandboxes in parallel — one sandbox per test case, up to a per-submission concurrency cap (typically 10-20 at once) to avoid one submission monopolizing the worker's local resources Each sandbox runs independently — own CPU/memory/wall-clock caps, one case's TLE doesn't affect siblings Results stream back as each case finishes — the WS gateway can push \"42/100 passed\" incrementally rather than waiting for all 100 Aggregation rule: first failure can short-circuit — for a straight pass/fail verdict, stop dispatching remaining cases once one fails, saving compute; contest judges instead run all cases for partial-credit scoring Short-circuiting on first failure is a product decision, not just an optimization — LeetCode-style practice mode wants a fast fail signal, but a scored contest needs every test case's result for tie-breaking and partial credit, so it must run to completion regardless of an early failure. Scenario User impact Detection signal Mitigation Infinite loop in submitted code Would hang a worker indefinitely cgroup wall-clock cap hit Host kills the sandbox at 2s regardless of internal state; verdict returns TLE Fork bomb / thread explosion Could exhaust worker host resources cgroup pids.max threshold hit Process count hard-capped at 64; excess forks fail to spawn, sandbox killed Kafka backlog at contest start Submission status stuck at \"queued\" longer than expected Consumer lag alert, queue depth spike Pre-warmed worker autoscaling on queue depth; UI shows an honest queue position rather than a fake spinner Judge worker crashes mid-execution Submission appears stuck at \"running\" Missed heartbeat from worker to orchestrator Orchestrator requeues the job after a heartbeat timeout; job processing is idempotent per test case Malicious code attempts network egress None — structurally blocked seccomp denial log entry (informational, not urgent) No network namespace exists in the sandbox at all, so the syscall fails regardless of policy correctness"},{"u":"topics/questions/leetcode.html","p":"Design LeetCode","i":"interview-moves","h":"Interview moves","t":"Interview moves 01 \"The core challenge here isn't the CRUD around problems and submissions — it's safely executing code I don't trust, so I want to spend most of our time on the sandbox\" — names the real hard problem before diving into architecture 02 \"I'd put a queue between submission and execution — at contest start, submissions can spike 100x steady-state in seconds, and workers physically can't scale that fast\" — justifies the async architecture with a concrete number 03 \"Every resource limit — CPU, memory, wall-clock — has to be enforced by the host via cgroups, never trusted to code running inside the sandbox\" — shows you understand who the adversary actually is 04 \"I'd fan out a submission's test cases to run in parallel across sandboxes rather than serially, since 100 sequential runs would blow past our latency target\" — catches an obvious latency trap before being asked 05 \"Whether to short-circuit on the first failing test case is a product decision — fast feedback for practice mode, but a scored contest needs every case's result for partial credit\" — reframes an implementation detail as a requirements question 06 \"gVisor over raw Docker here — a container alone shares the host kernel, and that's not a strong enough boundary for code I don't trust at all\" — justifies a specific, correct technology choice with the actual reason"},{"u":"topics/questions/leetcode.html","p":"Design LeetCode","i":"follow-ups","h":"Follow-up questions","t":"Follow-up questions Why not just use Docker containers without gVisor? Containers share the host kernel — a container-escape kernel exploit compromises the host directly. gVisor intercepts syscalls in userspace, so an exploit hits gVisor's sandboxed kernel instead. How do you keep sandbox spin-up latency low without compromising isolation? Pre-warm a pool of idle, already-isolated sandboxes ahead of demand (especially before scheduled contests) so a submission claims one instead of paying the ~150ms cold-start cost per request. How do you support multiple languages without N different judge images? One base sandbox image per language runtime, selected at dispatch time; compiled languages (Java, C++) get a separate compile step with its own timeout before the run step even starts. How do you prevent a user from submitting the same solution thousands of times to game the leaderboard? Rate limit submissions per user per problem (e.g. one every few seconds), and hash-dedupe identical source against a user's own recent submissions to short-circuit obvious repeats. What happens if the judge worker fleet is completely saturated during a flash-contest spike? Submissions queue in Kafka and the UI shows an honest queue position rather than fabricating progress; autoscaling adds workers on queue depth, and pre-warmed capacity absorbs the first burst before scale-up lands. How do you stop test case data from leaking to users through the submitted code's output? The sandbox only returns stdout/stderr up to a size cap and a pass/fail verdict — raw expected-output test data is never mounted anywhere the submitted process can read it directly."},{"u":"topics/questions/local-delivery.html","p":"Design a Local Delivery Service","i":"","h":"Overview","t":"Questions Design a Local Delivery Service Tests whether you separate a write-heavy geospatial problem (driver location) from a read-heavy one (order matching) instead of forcing both into one store. 40-50 min interview Hard Concept Map click to expand Say this in the first 2 minutes The core insight: driver location is a write-heavy geospatial problem, order matching is a read-heavy geospatial problem — they need different stores Key scale number to anchor on: 10k drivers pinging every ~4s is 2,500 location writes/sec, an entirely separate hot path from order volume Primary tradeoff being probed: durability vs write speed for driver location — losing one ping is fine since the next one is 4s away Component most candidates forget: the order state machine needs atomic, exactly-once transitions or you get double-dispatched drivers Failure mode most candidates skip: a hot geohash cell during lunch rush — one neighborhood can get 5-10x the load of the rest of the city Local Delivery Order Service Owns the order state machine with optimistic-locked transitions step6-deepdive-statemachine version column Prevents double-dispatch via optimistic lock Postgres-backed ACID guarantees for a financial transaction Matching Svc Finds the nearest available driver by expanding outward from a geohash cell step6-deepdive-geohash Ring expansion Own cell first then neighbors then next ring Blends idle time Ranking is not distance-only Redis GEO In-memory geospatial index shared by the write and read paths step2-entities GEOADD writes Ephemeral driver position TTL around 30s Sub-ms radius query No durability needed per single ping Order DB Postgres store of order rows the transactional source of truth step2-entities Strict state Delivery is a financial transaction Sharded by metro_id At high order volume per region ETA Svc Streaming consumer that composes distance traffic prep time and driver load step5-hld Off Kafka Consumes the same location stream continuously Recomputed live Not a single static formula Notification Svc Pushes order state changes to customer and driver apps over WebSocket step5-hld Async push Decoupled from the order-state change itself No polling Clients receive pushes not pulls"},{"u":"topics/questions/local-delivery.html","p":"Design a Local Delivery Service","i":"step1-requirements","h":"Requirements","t":"Step 1 of 6 — Requirements Requirements Functional Customer places an order from a nearby merchant System matches the order to the nearest available driver Track order state from placed through delivered Real-time ETA shown to customer and driver Non-Functional 10k active drivers/metro, location updates every ~4s Order-to-dispatch matching latency <2s Exactly-once state transitions — no double-dispatch, no lost orders Handle a 5-10x demand spike in a single neighborhood at lunch What NOT to build: merchant inventory management, driver payroll/tax systems, or in-app chat between customer and driver. This is order placement, matching, and delivery tracking only. ~2,500/s location writes/sec (10k drivers) ~50/s new orders/sec (metro peak) <2s match latency target 7 chars geohash precision (~150m cell)"},{"u":"topics/questions/local-delivery.html","p":"Design a Local Delivery Service","i":"step2-entities","h":"Core entities","t":"Step 2 of 6 — Core Entities Core entities Order Postgres order_id UUID customer_id BIGINT driver_id BIGINT, nullable status TEXT — state machine version INT — optimistic lock Because a delivery is a financial transaction with strict state and exactly-once requirements — ACID guarantees matter more than raw write throughput. Tradeoff: needs sharding by metro_id at high order volume. DriverLocation Redis GEO driver_id member key lng, lat GEOADD coordinates ttl ~30s Because positions are ephemeral and read/written constantly at 2,500 writes/sec — durability of any single ping is irrelevant, only the current position and query speed matter. Tradeoff: a node failure loses recent positions, self-heals within one ~4s ping cycle. OrderStateLog Postgres order_id UUID → Order from_state TEXT to_state TEXT Because an append-only audit trail is needed for dispute resolution and debugging — every transition is logged, never overwritten. Tradeoff: grows unbounded, needs periodic archival. Primary key: Order.order_id with a version column for optimistic locking. Critical index: Redis GEO's internal sorted-set structure on driver coordinates — this is what makes a radius query fast without a full scan."},{"u":"topics/questions/local-delivery.html","p":"Design a Local Delivery Service","i":"step3-api","h":"API design","t":"Step 3 of 6 — API / Interface API design POST /api/v1/orders Auth: required (customer) Request { merchant_id, dropoff_geo } Response { order_id, status: \"placed\", eta_seconds } POST /api/v1/orders/{id}/transition Auth: required (driver) Request { to_status, version } Response 200 on match, 409 on version conflict WEBSOCKET /ws/driver/{id}/location Auth: required (driver) Message in { lat, lng } every ~4s Message out { order_offer } when matched WEBSOCKET /ws/orders/{id}/track Auth: required (customer) Message out { status, driver_location, eta_seconds } Cadence pushed on every state or location change WebSockets over REST for location and tracking: both are high-frequency, bidirectional-in-spirit (driver sends location, receives offers; customer receives pushes) — a persistent connection avoids the overhead of a new HTTP request every 4 seconds per driver."},{"u":"topics/questions/local-delivery.html","p":"Design a Local Delivery Service","i":"step4-dataflow","h":"Data flow","t":"Step 4 of 6 — Data Flow Data flow WRITE PATH — driver location, ~2,500/s Driver App Location Svc Redis GEO READ PATH — order matching Customer App Order Service Matching Svc Redis GEO Driver/customer apps — persistent WebSocket Redis GEO — shared index, written by one path, read by the other Driver pings location every ~4s — over the already-open WebSocket connection Location Svc writes to Redis GEO — GEOADD updates the driver's position, TTL refreshed Customer places an order — Order Service creates the order row in \"placed\" state Matching Svc queries Redis GEO — finds nearby available drivers by expanding outward from the order's cell Best candidate is offered the order — pushed over their WebSocket connection Sends a GPS ping every ~4s over a persistent WebSocket Thin ingestion — writes position forwards to ETA pipeline GEOADD writes current position TTL ~30s Places an order from a nearby merchant Creates the order kicks off matching Expands outward from the order's geohash cell Same GEO index the location write path populates"},{"u":"topics/questions/local-delivery.html","p":"Design a Local Delivery Service","i":"step5-hld","h":"High-level design","t":"Step 5 of 6 — High-Level Design High-level design Customer App Driver App Order Service state machine Order DB Matching Svc Redis GEO Kafka ETA Svc Notification Svc Customer + driver apps Redis GEO — single geospatial index, shared Order DB — transactional source of truth Kafka + ETA/Notification — decoupled from hot path Component Role Tech + why Order Service Owns order state machine Postgres-backed, optimistic locking — because exactly-once transitions matter more than raw throughput; tradeoff is sharding needed at high order volume. Matching Svc Nearest-driver query Geohash ring expansion — because it turns 2D proximity into a cheap prefix lookup; tradeoff is edge effects at cell boundaries. Redis GEO Live driver positions In-memory geospatial index — because 2,500 writes/sec of ephemeral data needs sub-ms radius queries; tradeoff is no durability guarantee per ping. ETA Service Composes distance, traffic, prep time, driver load Streaming consumer off Kafka — because inputs update independently and continuously; tradeoff is added pipeline complexity vs a single static formula. Scaling levers — cheapest to most complex Add Redis GEO read replicas per region low complexity Pre-warm wider search radius during known peak windows low complexity Shard orders by metro_id medium complexity Batch client-side location writes over the 4s interval medium complexity Dynamic geohash cell-splitting in dense areas high complexity — last resort Bottleneck Triggers at Exact fix Hot geohash cell (lunch rush) TRIGGERS AT One dense cell gets far more orders/drivers than others Dynamic cell-splitting — finer geohash precision in dense areas Location write throughput TRIGGERS AT >5k writes/sec regionally Shard Redis GEO by metro; batch client-side writes Order DB write contention TRIGGERS AT High order volume in one metro during surge Shard by metro_id; optimistic locking avoids row-level blocking Driver location data: durability vs write speed Durable Fast, ephemeral Order state consistency Strong Eventual Places orders and views tracking Customer App Client Mobile app WebSocket + REST client Entry point for order placement and live tracking; needs a persistent connection for pushed status updates. ~20-50ms network RTT Sends location receives order offers Driver App Client Mobile app persistent WebSocket Streams GPS pings and receives order offers over one open connection avoiding a new HTTP request every ~4s. ~20-50ms network RTT Owns the order state machine Order Service Service Postgres-backed optimistic locking Exactly-once transitions matter more than raw throughput; tradeoff is sharding needed at high order volume. ~10-30ms per transition ACID-compliant order records Order DB Database Postgres Order is a financial transaction with strict state — ACID guarantees matter more than write throughput. ~5-15ms per write Nearest-driver query expanding geohash rings Matching Svc Service Geohash ring expansion Turns 2D proximity into a cheap prefix lookup; tradeoff is edge effects at cell boundaries. ~5-20ms per query Live driver positions shared by both flows Redis GEO Cache In-memory geospatial index 2 500 writes/sec of ephemeral data needs sub-ms radius queries; tradeoff is no durability guarantee per ping. sub-millisecond Every ping also streams here for ETA + analytics Kafka Queue Streaming log driver location topic Decouples the hot location-write path from downstream ETA and analytics consumers. ~5-10ms produce latency Combines distance traffic prep time driver load ETA Svc Service Streaming consumer off Kafka Inputs update independently and continuously; tradeoff is added pipeline complexity vs a single static formula. ~50-200ms compute Pushes state changes to both apps Notification Svc Service Async push over WebSocket Decouples order-state changes from delivery to clients; pushes rather than requiring clients to poll. ~50-150ms push delivery"},{"u":"topics/questions/local-delivery.html","p":"Design a Local Delivery Service","i":"step6-deepdive-geohash","h":"Deep dive: geohashing for driver matching","t":"Step 6 of 6 — Deep Dives Deep dive: geohashing for driver matching CITY GRID — each cell ≈ 150m × 150m (7-char geohash) ▸ Simulate driver ping ▸ Simulate order query (expand outward) Driver ping lands in a cell — GPS coordinate encoded to a geohash string, GEOADD places the driver Order arrives with its own cell — customer/merchant location encoded the same way Query expands outward ring by ring — own cell first, then 8 neighbors, then the next ring Rank candidates — actual road distance plus current driver load, not just geohash proximity Dispatch and lock — top candidate offered with a short accept window, next candidate tried on decline A pure distance-only match can repeatedly offer orders to the same nearby driver, ignoring one who just finished two blocks further out — ranking should blend distance with idle time to avoid this."},{"u":"topics/questions/local-delivery.html","p":"Design a Local Delivery Service","i":"step6-deepdive-statemachine","h":"Deep dive: order state machine","t":"Deep dive: order state machine Placed order created Matched driver assigned Picked Up at merchant En Route to customer Delivered terminal Cancelled terminal only from Placed or Matched Transition Reversible? Guard condition Placed → Matched Yes (revert on driver decline) Matching service finds and confirms an available driver Placed / Matched → Cancelled No — terminal Customer or system-initiated cancellation before pickup Matched → Picked Up No — irreversible Driver confirms pickup at merchant (geofence or manual tap) En Route → Delivered No — terminal Driver confirms drop-off (geofence + explicit confirmation) Once an order reaches \"Picked Up,\" cancellation is no longer offered — food is already in the driver's possession. This must be enforced at the state machine level, not just in the UI, or a race condition could let a cancel request slip through after pickup. Scenario User impact Detection signal Mitigation Redis GEO node failure Matching can't find nearby drivers temporarily GEO query error rate spike Replica promotion; drivers self-heal the index within one ping cycle Double-dispatch race condition Two customers matched to the same driver Optimistic lock version conflict Version-checked conditional update; losing request retries next candidate Driver app stops pinging Driver appears unavailable TTL expiry on driver's GEO key Key expires from index after ~30s; auto re-enters pool on reconnect Order service partial outage New orders can't be placed Write error rate spike on order creation Circuit breaker shows unavailable in-app; in-flight orders unaffected"},{"u":"topics/questions/local-delivery.html","p":"Design a Local Delivery Service","i":"interview-moves","h":"Interview moves","t":"Interview moves 01 \"Before I design anything, let me separate driver location from order matching — one is write-heavy geospatial, the other is read-heavy geospatial, and they need different stores\" — states the core insight unprompted 02 \"Finding the nearest driver can't be a full scan — I'd geohash-index positions so proximity becomes a prefix lookup\" — signals you know the core data structure problem 03 \"Order state transitions need to be atomic and exactly-once — I'd use optimistic locking with a version column to prevent double-dispatch\" — names a specific, correct mechanism 04 \"I'd expect a hot-partition problem during lunch rush in dense neighborhoods — I'd plan for dynamic geohash cell-splitting rather than treating it as an edge case\" — proactively surfaces a scale problem 05 \"ETA isn't one distance calculation — it's a live composition of routing, traffic, prep time, and driver load, recomputed as state changes\" — shows systems thinking over ML hand-waving 06 \"Once an order hits 'picked up,' cancellation should be blocked at the state machine level, not just hidden in the UI\" — demonstrates invariants hold even under racy client behavior"},{"u":"topics/questions/local-delivery.html","p":"Design a Local Delivery Service","i":"follow-ups","h":"Follow-up questions","t":"Follow-up questions How do you prevent two orders from being matched to the same driver simultaneously? Optimistic locking on the driver assignment — the UPDATE only succeeds if the driver is still unassigned; a losing request immediately retries the next candidate. What happens if a driver goes offline mid-delivery? The order enters a stalled state after a location-staleness timeout; the system waits for reconnect within a grace period or reassigns to a backup driver. How would you detect a hot geohash cell in real time? Stream order/driver counts per cell through a rolling window aggregator; a crossed threshold triggers both surge pricing and dynamic cell-splitting for that region. How do you avoid always sending orders to the same closest driver? Blend distance with idle time in the ranking function so drivers waiting longer get preference among similarly-close candidates. How is surge pricing computed transparently? A bounded multiplier tied to a real demand-density signal (orders per available driver per cell), capped at a maximum, shown to the customer before confirming. How would this change for scheduled (non-immediate) orders? Scheduled orders skip immediate matching and enter a time-based queue that triggers matching shortly before the scheduled window, reusing the same state machine."},{"u":"topics/questions/metrics-monitoring.html","p":"Design a Metrics Monitoring & Alerting System","i":"","h":"Overview","t":"Questions Design a Metrics Monitoring & Alerting System Tests whether you separate a write-scale time-series storage problem (millions of points/sec) from a latency-critical alerting problem instead of serving both off the same slow query path. 45-55 min interview Hard Concept Map click to expand Say this in the first 2 minutes The core insight: this is a time-series data problem at extreme write scale — write throughput and query flexibility pull in opposite directions, which forces specialized TSDB storage instead of a general-purpose database Key scale number to anchor on: 10M metric data points/sec across the fleet, which rules out row-oriented storage and demands columnar, compressed, downsampled layout Primary tradeoff being probed: query freshness vs storage cost — high-resolution data is expensive to keep forever, so old data gets progressively downsampled Component most candidates forget: alerting cannot share the same query path as dashboards — a <1 minute evaluation latency target means a separate low-latency streaming path is mandatory Failure mode most candidates skip: cardinality explosion — one bad label (like user_id) can take down the whole TSDB index, not just one metric Metrics Monitoring Ingestion Tier Kafka buffers write bursts decouples agents from TSDB write rate step4-dataflow Backpressure Absorbs uneven producer rate spikes Never drops Bursts queue rather than get lost TSDB raw tier Columnar compressed sharded by series id at 10s resolution step2-entities series_id key Hash of metric name plus labels Write-optimized Handles 10M points per second Downsample Worker Compacts old high-resolution data into coarser long-term tiers step6-deepdive-downsampling 3 tiers 10s 1min and 1hr resolutions Cost + speed Solves storage cost and query latency together Alerting Engine Streams the ingestion firehose directly never queries the dashboard path step6-deepdive-alerting In-memory state Tracks per-series breach windows Sub-minute No polling interval to wait out AlertRule Store Postgres holds low-volume rule config with relational integrity step2-entities Cached in memory Engine polls config not per evaluation Threshold + window Comparator plus eval_window seconds Notification Svc Fires pages Slack or email within a minute of a breach step5-hld Breach trigger Fired the moment a window condition is met No query hop Never waits on the analytical query path"},{"u":"topics/questions/metrics-monitoring.html","p":"Design a Metrics Monitoring & Alerting System","i":"step1-requirements","h":"Requirements","t":"Step 1 of 6 — Requirements Requirements 1 2 3 4 5 6 Arc 1 (steps 1-5) — Satisfy Functional Requirements 1 2 3 4 5 6 Arc 2 (all 6 steps) — Satisfy Non-Functional Requirements Functional Agents/services emit metric data points (name, value, labels, timestamp) Dashboards query arbitrary time ranges and label-dimension aggregations Users define alert rules (threshold + evaluation window) on any metric Alerts fire notifications within a tight latency bound of a threshold breach Non-Functional 10M metric data points/sec ingested across the fleet Months of retention, with resolution degrading gracefully over time Alert evaluation latency <1 minute from threshold breach to notification Ingestion must not silently drop data during traffic bursts — especially during incidents What NOT to build: a full distributed tracing system (spans/traces are a different data shape and product), log aggregation/search (logs are unstructured text, not numeric time series), or synthetic/uptime monitoring (external blackbox probing is a separate concern from metrics emitted by the fleet itself). 10M/s metric data points ingested 13 mo retention (downsampled) <1 min alert evaluation latency target 3 tiers downsampling resolutions (10s/1min/1hr)"},{"u":"topics/questions/metrics-monitoring.html","p":"Design a Metrics Monitoring & Alerting System","i":"step2-entities","h":"Core entities","t":"Step 2 of 6 — Core Entities Core entities Raw and rolled-up points live in a specialized TSDB sharded by series identity; alert rule config lives in Postgres because it's low-volume and needs relational integrity. MetricDataPoint TSDB (columnar) series_id hash(metric_name + labels) timestamp TIMESTAMP, 10s resolution value DOUBLE labels MAP<TEXT,TEXT> — host, region, service Because 10M writes/sec of append-only numeric data needs columnar layout and compression, not row-oriented storage — a general-purpose DB would fall over on write volume alone. Tradeoff: sharded by metric name + labels, so a single high-cardinality metric can still overload one shard. RollupSeries TSDB (long-term, S3-backed) series_id same key space as raw bucket_start TIMESTAMP, resolution-tagged resolution ENUM — 10s / 1min / 1hr agg avg, min, max, p99 (pre-aggregated) Because keeping 13 months at 10s resolution is both cost-prohibitive and pointless — nobody zooms into a second-level spike from 6 months ago. Tradeoff: querying a long historical range only ever sees aggregated values, never the original raw points. AlertRule Postgres rule_id UUID metric_selector TEXT — name + label matchers threshold DOUBLE, comparator (>, <, ==) eval_window INT seconds — e.g. breach for 60s Because rule configuration is low-volume and relational (owned by teams, versioned, needs joins to notification channels) — ACID guarantees matter far more here than throughput. Tradeoff: the streaming alerting engine caches rules in memory and only polls Postgres for config changes, never per-evaluation. Primary key: series_id , a hash of metric name plus the full label set — this is the unit of sharding. Critical index: (series_id, timestamp) for the raw tier, and (series_id, resolution, bucket_start) for rollups — this is what lets a query pick the right resolution tier without scanning raw data."},{"u":"topics/questions/metrics-monitoring.html","p":"Design a Metrics Monitoring & Alerting System","i":"step3-api","h":"API design","t":"Step 3 of 6 — API / Interface API design Ingestion is a high-frequency push/scrape endpoint; queries and alert rule management are ordinary REST since they're comparatively low-volume. POST /api/v1/metrics Auth: required (agent API key) Request { points: [{ metric, labels, value, ts }] } — batched Response 202 Accepted, or 400 with rejected series on cardinality limit GET /api/v1/query?metric=&range=&step= Auth: required (dashboard) Request metric=cpu.usage, range=6h, labels=region:us-east Response { series: [{ ts, value }], resolution_used } POST /api/v1/alerts/rules Auth: required (team owner) Request { metric_selector, threshold, comparator, eval_window } Response { rule_id, status: \"active\" } GET /api/v1/alerts/active Auth: required Request team_id (optional filter) Response [{ rule_id, series_id, breached_since, severity }] Ingestion accepts batches, not single points: at 10M points/sec, one HTTP request per data point would collapse under connection overhead alone — agents buffer briefly and flush in batches of hundreds to thousands."},{"u":"topics/questions/metrics-monitoring.html","p":"Design a Metrics Monitoring & Alerting System","i":"step4-dataflow","h":"Data flow","t":"Step 4 of 6 — Data Flow Data flow Ingestion (blue, fanning into an async downsampling path in amber) and dashboard queries (mint) are fully separate flows that only meet at the TSDB. WRITE PATH — ingestion, ~10M points/sec Agent/Exporter Ingestion (Kafka) TSDB (raw) Downsample Worker READ PATH — dashboard query Dashboard UI Query Service TSDB (raw + rollup) Agents + dashboard UI — entry points Kafka + downsample worker — absorb bursts, compact off the hot path TSDB — shared store, written by ingestion, read by queries Agent emits a batch of points — pushed (or scraped, pull-model) every 10-15s from every host/service Ingestion tier buffers the burst — Kafka decouples producer rate from TSDB write rate TSDB writes raw points — sharded by series_id, compressed columnar layout Downsample worker compacts in the background — rolls old high-resolution data into lower-resolution long-term tiers Dashboard queries the query service — which picks raw or rolled-up data depending on the requested range Scrapes or pushes metric points on an interval Ingestion tier — buffers bursts absorbs backpressure Sharded write of raw high-resolution points Background worker compacts old data into lower resolutions Requests a metric over a time range Picks the resolution tier based on requested time range Same physical store the write path populates"},{"u":"topics/questions/metrics-monitoring.html","p":"Design a Metrics Monitoring & Alerting System","i":"step5-hld","h":"High-level design","t":"Step 5 of 6 — High-Level Design High-level design Two consumers of the same ingestion stream: the TSDB/query path for dashboards, and a separate low-latency alerting engine that never waits on the analytical query path. Agents / Exporters push or scrape Ingestion Tier Kafka TSDB (raw tier) 10s resolution Downsample Worker 1min / 1hr rollups TSDB (rollup tier) S3-backed, months Query Service dashboards, ad-hoc Dashboard UI seconds-old to months-old Alerting Engine streaming evaluation AlertRule Store Postgres Notification Svc <1min from breach Agents + dashboard UI Alerting engine — the low-latency path, separate from dashboards TSDB tiers + AlertRule store — durable storage Kafka, downsample worker, notification service — async/background Component Role Tech + why Ingestion Tier Absorbs write bursts, decouples producer rate from TSDB rate Kafka — because 10M points/sec arriving unevenly needs a buffer, or the TSDB itself becomes the bottleneck; tradeoff is added end-to-end latency of tens to hundreds of ms. TSDB (raw tier) Stores high-resolution recent data Columnar, compressed, sharded by series_id — because row-oriented general-purpose DBs can't sustain this write volume with compression this good; tradeoff is limited ad-hoc query flexibility (no arbitrary joins). Downsample Worker Compacts old data into lower resolutions Background batch job over cold shards — because keeping 10s resolution for 13 months is cost-prohibitive and unnecessary; tradeoff is old data loses fine-grained detail permanently. Alerting Engine Evaluates rules against live incoming data Stream processor consuming directly off ingestion — because a <1 minute target rules out polling the same query path dashboards use; tradeoff is a second, parallel consumer of the same firehose. Scaling levers — cheapest to most complex Add TSDB read replicas per region for dashboard fan-out low complexity Increase Kafka partition count to raise ingestion parallelism low complexity Shard TSDB by metric name + label hash to spread hot metrics medium complexity Pre-aggregate common dashboard queries as materialized rollups medium complexity Federate the alerting engine per shard so one hot shard can't stall global alert evaluation high complexity — last resort Bottleneck Triggers at Exact fix Single TSDB shard overload TRIGGERS AT One metric's label combinations concentrate writes on one shard Cardinality guardrails at ingestion reject the offending series before they land Query latency on long ranges TRIGGERS AT Dashboard queries months of raw data directly Query service auto-selects the rollup tier instead of scanning raw points Alert evaluation lag TRIGGERS AT Alerting engine falls behind the ingestion stream Backpressure monitoring on the alerting pipeline itself, independent scaling from the TSDB write path Query freshness: real-time vs downsampled historical Seconds-old, raw Months-old, downsampled Alerting latency: streaming evaluation vs periodic polling Streaming Polling Push or pull/scrape model batched emission Buffers ingestion bursts decouples producers from TSDB write rate Sharded by metric name + labels compressed columnar writes Background compaction to lower resolutions over time Long-term rollup storage cheaper and coarser Serves dashboards picks resolution tier by requested range Renders time-series charts and long-range history Streams every point as it arrives evaluates rules in real time — never touches the historical query path Threshold + window rules cached in memory from Postgres Pages Slack email — fired on rule breach"},{"u":"topics/questions/metrics-monitoring.html","p":"Design a Metrics Monitoring & Alerting System","i":"step6-deepdive-downsampling","h":"Deep dive: time-series storage + downsampling","t":"Step 6 of 6 — Deep Dives Deep dive: time-series storage + downsampling Raw 10s-resolution data is compacted over time into progressively coarser tiers — this is what makes both storage cost and long-range query performance tractable. RETENTION TIERS — resolution degrades as data ages Raw — 10s resolution retained 24 hours Rollup — 1min resolution retained 7 days Rollup — 1hr resolution retained 13 months Query Service — auto-selects resolution range <24h → raw, <7d → 1min, >7d → 1hr Cache — hot path Database — source of truth Async — queue / worker Agent writes at 10s resolution — every point kept exactly as emitted for the first 24 hours Compaction worker runs on a schedule — reads a window of raw points, aggregates (avg/min/max/p99), writes one rolled-up point 1-minute tier retained for 7 days — enough detail for \"what happened yesterday,\" at 1/6th the storage 1-hour tier retained for 13 months — enough for long-range trend and capacity charts, at a fraction of raw storage cost Query auto-picks the tier — a 6-hour dashboard query hits raw data; a 6-month capacity chart hits the 1hr tier automatically, transparent to the user This isn't just a cost optimization — querying 13 months of 10s-resolution points would also be too slow to render a dashboard interactively. Downsampling solves storage cost and query latency at the same time. Every point as written kept for the most recent 24h Compaction worker averages/aggregates 6 raw points into one Coarsest tier — cheap S3-backed still enough for a 6-month trend chart Query service inspects the requested range and picks the coarsest tier that still covers it"},{"u":"topics/questions/metrics-monitoring.html","p":"Design a Metrics Monitoring & Alerting System","i":"step6-deepdive-cardinality","h":"Deep dive: cardinality explosion","t":"Deep dive: cardinality explosion A single high-cardinality label turns one metric into millions of unique time series, overwhelming the TSDB's index — the classic way a monitoring system takes itself down. SAFE vs DANGEROUS LABEL CHOICES http.requests{region, host, status_code} ~10^4 unique series — bounded, healthy http.requests{..., user_id, request_id} ~10^9 unique series — unbounded, fatal TSDB index overwhelmed memory exhaustion, slow queries fleet-wide A label is added without thinking about its value space — user_id or request_id looks harmless per-metric, but each unique value is a brand-new time series Series count multiplies combinatorially — every existing label combination now multiplies by every distinct user_id ever seen The TSDB index grows unbounded — the index tracking which series exist, not the data itself, is what runs out of memory first Every query against that metric slows down — and because shards are shared, other unrelated metrics on the same shard degrade too Mitigation is enforced at ingestion, not after the fact — reject or drop series once a per-metric cardinality limit is hit, and alert the emitting team immediately High-cardinality dimensions like user_id or request_id belong in logs or traces, which are built to index arbitrary high-cardinality fields — not in a metrics system, which is optimized for a bounded, slowly-changing set of series. Bounded set: a handful of regions a few hundred hosts Unbounded set: one new series per unique user forever Index has to track one entry per unique label combination — this is what falls over"},{"u":"topics/questions/metrics-monitoring.html","p":"Design a Metrics Monitoring & Alerting System","i":"step6-deepdive-alerting","h":"Deep dive: alerting pipeline separate from dashboards","t":"Deep dive: alerting pipeline separate from dashboards Alert evaluation reads the ingestion stream directly. Never the dashboard TSDB path — too slow, query-contended. WRONG — polling the dashboard query path Alert Poller polls every 30-60s TSDB Query Path shared, contended, seconds of latency RIGHT — dedicated streaming evaluation Alerting Engine stream processor In-memory Rule State per-series breach windows Notification Svc <1min end-to-end Cache — hot path Database — source of truth Async — queue / worker Ingestion stream is tapped twice — once into the TSDB for storage, once directly into the alerting engine Alerting engine keeps rule state in memory — per series, tracking how long a threshold has been breached against the configured evaluation window Evaluation happens as data arrives — no polling interval to wait out, no query queue to sit behind Breach triggers immediately — once the window condition is met, notification fires without a query round-trip Dashboards remain free to run expensive queries — because they no longer share a path with the latency-critical alerting workload Polling-based alerting has two independent failure modes: the poll interval itself adds latency that can blow past a <1 minute target, and an expensive ad-hoc dashboard query can starve the same query path an alert poll is waiting on — exactly when an incident is generating more dashboard traffic too. Scenario User impact Detection signal Mitigation High-cardinality metric overwhelms a TSDB shard Query latency spikes for every metric on that shard, not just the offender Per-metric series count crosses a cardinality limit Ingestion rejects/drops the offending series past the limit and alerts the emitting team Alerting engine itself lags behind real-time Alerts fire late or not at all — silent blind spot exactly when needed most Consumer lag metric on the alerting engine's own stream offset Dedicated backpressure monitoring and alerting on the alerting pipeline — \"who alerts on the alerting system\" Ingestion burst during an incident The moment monitoring matters most is when the system emits far more data than usual Ingestion queue depth and per-agent emission rate spike together Headroom and backpressure handling that queues and drains rather than silently dropping points Naive design: alerting runs the same query dashboards do on a timer Same path serving expensive ad-hoc dashboard queries — contention and slow scans Consumes the raw ingestion stream directly no query round-trip Evaluates each rule's window as points arrive — no polling no shared contention Fired the moment a window's breach condition is satisfied"},{"u":"topics/questions/metrics-monitoring.html","p":"Design a Metrics Monitoring & Alerting System","i":"interview-moves","h":"Interview moves","t":"Interview moves 01 \"This is fundamentally a time-series data problem at extreme write scale — the tension is write throughput vs query flexibility, and that's why it needs a specialized TSDB instead of a general-purpose database\" — states the core insight unprompted 02 \"At 10M points/sec, keeping raw resolution forever is both a storage cost problem and a query performance problem — I'd downsample old data into progressively coarser tiers\" — shows you understand downsampling solves two problems at once 03 \"Alerting can't share the same query path as dashboards — a sub-minute latency target means it needs its own streaming evaluation off the ingestion firehose\" — the single most commonly missed architectural split in this problem 04 \"I'd put hard cardinality limits at ingestion — a metric that accidentally includes user_id as a label can take down an entire TSDB shard, not just itself\" — names the classic TSDB failure mode by name 05 \"High-cardinality data like user IDs or request IDs belongs in logs or traces, not metrics — metrics are optimized for a bounded, slowly-changing set of series\" — draws a clean boundary between observability pillars 06 \"I'd monitor the alerting pipeline itself with the same rigor as the system it watches — if the alerting engine falls behind, that's a silent blind spot at the worst possible time\" — surfaces a second-order failure mode most candidates never mention"},{"u":"topics/questions/metrics-monitoring.html","p":"Design a Metrics Monitoring & Alerting System","i":"follow-ups","h":"Follow-up questions","t":"Follow-up questions How do you decide the right downsampling schedule? Balance query patterns against cost: keep raw resolution only as long as engineers actually zoom into second-level detail, then step down tiers where storage savings outweigh lost precision. What happens if the downsample worker falls behind? Raw data is retained until compaction catches up — queries transparently fall back to scanning raw data for that window, slower but not lossy, with lag surfaced as its own monitored metric. How would you enforce a cardinality limit without silently losing important data? Reject only the excess series past the limit, keep the rest flowing, and immediately alert the emitting team with the exact label combination that tripped the limit. Why not just run alert queries more frequently to hit the latency target? Frequent polling of an analytical query path multiplies load on shared TSDB infrastructure and still adds interval-length latency — streaming evaluation removes both problems at once. How do you scale the alerting engine independently from ingestion? Partition rule evaluation by series_id shard, same key space as the TSDB, so alerting horizontal scale tracks ingestion scale without a shared bottleneck. How would this design change for push (agent-initiated) vs pull (scrape) metric collection? Push suits ephemeral or high-cardinality-risk sources needing immediate delivery; pull suits stable long-running services and centralizes scrape-interval and target discovery control."},{"u":"topics/questions/news-aggregator.html","p":"Design a News Aggregator","i":"","h":"Overview","t":"Questions Design a News Aggregator Tests whether you can turn crawl scheduling and cross-source dedup into a first-class design problem instead of hand-waving \"we scrape the internet.\" 30-40 min interview Medium Concept Map click to expand Say this in the first 2 minutes The core insight: crawl scheduling and dedup are the hard problems here, not storage — any store handles the write volume fine Key scale number to anchor on: ~150k source feeds, ~40M new articles/day, but re-crawl checks dwarf that at ~2B fetches/day Primary tradeoff being probed: freshness (crawl aggressively) vs crawl cost (bandwidth, politeness, source rate limits) Component most candidates forget: a URL frontier that prioritizes re-crawls by source velocity, not a flat round-robin queue Failure mode most candidates skip: a Bloom filter false positive silently drops a real article — must state the accepted error rate out loud News Aggregator URL Frontier Priority queue deciding what to crawl next per source step6-deepdive-frontier Per-source cadence A wire feed crawls far more often than a blog Priority scoring Freshness need vs crawl cost Crawler Fleet Fetches articles from thousands of registered sources step4-dataflow Distributed workers Parallel fetch across sources Politeness Respects per-domain rate limits Bloom Filter Cheap membership check before a full dedup lookup step6-deepdive-bloom Memory win Fraction of a real set at same scale False positives ok Tunable rare and cheap to accept Elasticsearch Indexes article text for search and clustering step5-hld Full-text search Powers the reader-facing search Feeds clustering Groups near-duplicate coverage Ranker Batch-scores stories for the personalized feed step6-deepdive-ranking Runs periodically Not computed per-request Redis (ranked) Serves the precomputed feed fast"},{"u":"topics/questions/news-aggregator.html","p":"Design a News Aggregator","i":"step1-requirements","h":"Requirements","t":"Step 1 of 6 — Requirements Requirements 1 2 3 4 5 6 Arc 1 (steps 1-5) — Satisfy Functional Requirements 1 2 3 4 5 6 Arc 2 (all 6 steps) — Satisfy Non-Functional Requirements Functional Crawl a registered set of source feeds (RSS/Atom + HTML fallback) on a schedule Deduplicate the same story reported by multiple outlets into one cluster Rank and serve a personalized feed of recent, relevant articles Support keyword/topic search over the last 30 days of articles Non-Functional 150k source feeds crawled, ~40M new articles/day ingested Breaking news surfaced in the feed within 2-5 min of publish Feed read latency <200ms p99; crawl pipeline can lag by minutes, never blocks reads Eventual consistency on ranking/dedup; strong consistency not required anywhere What NOT to build: a full-text summarization/LLM pipeline, comment threads or social features, or a generic web-scale crawler that indexes the entire internet. This is a bounded set of known news sources, crawled, deduped, ranked, and served. 150k registered source feeds ~40M/day new articles ingested ~2B/day re-crawl fetch checks ~80k/s feed read QPS (peak)"},{"u":"topics/questions/news-aggregator.html","p":"Design a News Aggregator","i":"step2-entities","h":"Core entities","t":"Step 2 of 6 — Core Entities Core entities Four entities split by access pattern: raw content is huge and immutable, metadata is queried constantly, clusters group duplicates, and the frontier drives crawl order. Article Postgres id BIGINT (Snowflake) url_hash CHAR(64) UNIQUE — SHA-256(canonical_url) source_id BIGINT → Source.id title, published_at TEXT, TIMESTAMP cluster_id BIGINT, nullable content_key VARCHAR(200) → S3 object Because metadata needs indexed point lookups and range scans by time/source, while the actual HTML/text body is large and rarely touched after indexing. Tradeoff: every read of full content is a second hop to S3. RawContent S3 content_key S3 object key raw_html bytes, gzip-compressed extracted_text bytes, gzip-compressed Because 40M articles/day at ~50KB avg raw HTML is ~2TB/day — cheap blob storage with no per-object query needs beats a row-oriented DB by an order of magnitude on cost. Tradeoff: no server-side filtering, must fetch whole object. Source Postgres id BIGINT feed_url, domain TEXT avg_publish_rate FLOAT — articles/hour, rolling last_crawled_at TIMESTAMP Because it's a small (150k row), low-write reference table that the frontier's priority calculation reads on every scheduling pass. Tradeoff: none significant at this size — a single Postgres instance handles it easily. SeenUrl Redis (Bloom) bloom_filter RedisBloom, ~2B capacity fp_rate 0.1% target Because checking \"have we seen this URL before\" needs to happen ~2B times/day at sub-millisecond latency — a probabilistic set trades a small false-positive rate for a 1000x memory reduction over a real set. Tradeoff: can wrongly skip a genuinely new article. Primary key: Article.id (Snowflake). Critical index: UNIQUE INDEX on url_hash plus a composite index on (source_id, published_at) — the crawl-dedup check and the feed-ranking scan both depend on these never falling back to a table scan."},{"u":"topics/questions/news-aggregator.html","p":"Design a News Aggregator","i":"step3-api","h":"API design","t":"Step 3 of 6 — API / Interface API design Four endpoints — feed, article detail, search, and source registration. All request-response; crawling itself is internal, not client-facing. GET /api/v1/feed Auth: required Request { cursor?, topics[]?, limit=20 } Response { articles: [{id, title, source, cluster_size, published_at}], next_cursor } GET /api/v1/articles/{id} Auth: optional Request path param only Response { title, body, source, related_cluster_articles[] } GET /api/v1/search Auth: optional Request { q, from?, to?, limit=20 } Response { results: [{id, title, snippet, score}] } — from Elasticsearch POST /api/v1/sources Auth: required (admin) Request { feed_url, initial_priority? } Response { source_id, status: \"pending_first_crawl\" }"},{"u":"topics/questions/news-aggregator.html","p":"Design a News Aggregator","i":"step4-dataflow","h":"Data flow","t":"Step 4 of 6 — Data Flow Data flow Write path (blue) is the crawl pipeline — frontier, fetch, dedup, store. Read path (mint) is the feed request, fully decoupled from crawling. READ PATH — fetch feed Client Feed Svc stateless Redis (ranked) miss Postgres WRITE PATH — crawl pipeline URL Frontier priority queue Crawler Fleet Bloom Filter dedup check S3 + Postgres ASYNC PATH — indexing + clustering, never blocks crawl or read Kafka → Indexer + Clusterer Read path — pre-ranked cache first Write path — frontier drives crawl order Indexing/clustering — fully decoupled Frontier pops the next due source — priority computed from publish velocity and staleness Crawler fetches feed/page — respects robots.txt, per-domain rate limits, conditional GET (ETag) Bloom filter check on url_hash — likely-duplicate URLs are dropped before any parsing happens New article persisted — raw HTML to S3, metadata row to Postgres, event fired to Kafka Async consumers index and cluster — Elasticsearch gets the text, clusterer groups same-story duplicates App or web client requesting the home feed Applies personalization fans out to cache/index Pre-ranked top-N articles per topic refreshed every ~60s Fallback for cold topics or cursor pagination past the cached window Priority queue of (source due_time) picks what to crawl next Fetches feed/HTML respects robots.txt and rate limits Checks url_hash against Bloom filter before doing any real work Raw HTML to S3 metadata row to Postgres indexed into Elasticsearch New-article events fan out to the indexer and the dedup clustering worker"},{"u":"topics/questions/news-aggregator.html","p":"Design a News Aggregator","i":"step5-hld","h":"High-level design","t":"Step 5 of 6 — High-Level Design High-level design Full system: crawl, dedup/index, ranking, and read paths together with every supporting component. URL Frontier priority queue Crawler Fleet Bloom Filter Redis S3 Postgres Kafka Elasticsearch Clusterer worker pool Ranker writes pre-ranked lists Redis (ranked) Feed / API Svc Clients Clients Bloom filter + ranked-feed cache S3 + Postgres + Elasticsearch Frontier, Kafka, Clusterer, Ranker Component Role Tech + why URL Frontier Decides what to crawl next and when Redis sorted set keyed by due_time — because a priority pop needs to be O(log n) at 2B ops/day; tradeoff is it's an extra stateful component to keep warm. Bloom Filter Fast reject of already-seen URLs RedisBloom — because it does ~2B membership checks/day in under 1ms each at a fraction of the memory of a real set; tradeoff is a tunable false-positive rate that can silently drop real articles. S3 Stores raw HTML and extracted text Object storage — because ~2TB/day of write-once, rarely-read blobs is exactly what object storage is priced and built for; tradeoff is no query capability, metadata must live elsewhere. Elasticsearch Full-text search over recent articles Inverted index — because keyword search over tens of millions of documents needs relevance scoring a relational DB doesn't do well; tradeoff is eventual consistency and added operational surface. Clusterer Groups duplicate stories across sources Worker pool doing title-shingle + embedding similarity — because syntactic dedup alone misses reworded stories; tradeoff is added compute cost per article. Ranker Scores and pre-sorts articles per topic Batch job writing to Redis every ~60s — because computing personalized rank at request time is too slow at 80k QPS; tradeoff is up to 60s staleness on ranking signals. Scaling levers — cheapest to most complex Add Redis + Postgres read replicas for feed reads near-zero complexity Add more crawler fleet workers horizontally, bounded by per-domain politeness limits low complexity Widen Kafka partitions for indexer/clusterer throughput medium complexity Shard Elasticsearch by time range so old indices can be dropped/archived cheaply medium complexity Move clustering to streaming embeddings instead of batch nearest-neighbor high complexity — last resort Bottleneck Triggers at Exact fix Frontier starvation High-velocity sources crowd out slow ones Cap max concurrent fetches per source; age-boost long-waiting sources Bloom filter saturation False-positive rate creeps above 0.1% target Resize filter or rotate to a fresh filter with a rolling TTL window Ranker lag Pre-ranked cache staler than 5 min during breaking news Shorten the ranker batch interval for high-velocity topics only Crawl strategy: freshness vs crawl cost Cheap, infrequent Aggressive, real-time Dedup precision vs recall Aggressive merge Conservative, allow near-dupes Scheduling loop picks sources by priority Horizontally scaled fetch workers ~2B checks/day sub-ms Raw HTML ~2TB/day Article metadata source registry Buffers new-article events for async fanout Full-text index for search Groups same-story articles via title/embedding similarity Scores freshness source trust engagement signals Pre-ranked top-N per topic ~60s refresh Stateless serves feed/article/search requests Web and mobile clients"},{"u":"topics/questions/news-aggregator.html","p":"Design a News Aggregator","i":"step6-deepdive-frontier","h":"Deep dive: URL frontier priority queue","t":"Step 6 of 6 — Deep Dives Deep dive: URL frontier priority queue 150k sources can't be polled on a flat round-robin — a wire feed publishing every 30s and a monthly blog need wildly different crawl cadences. Track rolling publish rate per source — exponential moving average of articles/hour, updated after every crawl Compute next due_time — due_time = now + clamp(3600 / avg_publish_rate, 30s, 6h) , so a high-velocity wire gets checked every 30s and a quiet blog every few hours Push (source_id, due_time) into a Redis sorted set — score is due_time, giving an O(log n) priority pop Scheduler pops due sources in batches — dispatches to the crawler fleet respecting a per-domain concurrency cap Re-score after crawl — a source that just published gets re-prioritized; a source with zero new articles across 5 checks backs off further Politeness beats greed: a source with no rate cap will get hammered by an eager frontier and rate-limit or block the crawler entirely. Every source carries a max-concurrency and min-interval floor that the priority score can never override."},{"u":"topics/questions/news-aggregator.html","p":"Design a News Aggregator","i":"step6-deepdive-bloom","h":"Deep dive: Bloom filter dedup — the false-positive math","t":"Deep dive: Bloom filter dedup — the false-positive math A Bloom filter trades a small, tunable false-positive rate for a massive memory win over storing every seen URL as a real set. 2B expected inserted URLs (n) ~34.2 Gb bit array size (m) for target FP rate 7 optimal hash functions (k) 0.1% target false-positive rate (p) Sized from the standard formulas m = -(n · ln p) / (ln 2)² and k = (m/n) · ln 2 . At n=2B and p=0.1%, that's m ≈ 2.87×10¹⁰ bits ≈ 3.4GB . Versus roughly 128GB+ to store 2B full SHA-256 hashes (32 bytes each) in a real set — a ~40x memory reduction for a 1-in-1000 chance of a false duplicate. A false positive here is silent by design — the article just never gets crawled, with no error and no retry. Mitigate by rotating the filter on a 30-day TTL window (old URLs age out, keeping n and thus m bounded) and by treating the Bloom check as advisory-only for high-priority sources, backed by a real url_hash lookup in Postgres before a final drop."},{"u":"topics/questions/news-aggregator.html","p":"Design a News Aggregator","i":"step6-deepdive-ranking","h":"Deep dive: feed ranking pipeline — signals → score → rank","t":"Deep dive: feed ranking pipeline — signals → score → rank Ranking runs as a batch job every ~60s per topic, not per-request — computing a personalized score for 80k QPS in real time doesn't fit the latency budget. ✓ Pre-ranked cache hit — ~2-5ms Feed Svc GET topic Redis ranked list found ~2-5ms list exists → paginate and return ▸ Replay ✗ Cold/niche topic miss — ~40-80ms Feed Svc GET topic Redis MISS fall through Postgres ~40-80ms miss → recency-ordered fallback query ▸ Replay Collect signals per article — recency, source trust score, cluster size (how many outlets ran the story), early click-through rate Compute a weighted score — score = 0.4·recency + 0.25·source_trust + 0.2·cluster_size + 0.15·ctr , weights tuned offline Sort and truncate per topic — top 500 articles/topic written to Redis, far more than any single page needs Feed Svc paginates from the cached list — cursor-based, no re-ranking per request Scenario User impact Detection signal Mitigation Redis ranked-cache cluster down Feed latency spikes 10-20x for all topics Cache miss rate alert >50% Fallback to recency-ordered Postgres query; auto-scale replicas Ranker batch job stalls Feed goes stale, breaking news delayed Ranked-list age >5 min alert Auto-restart job; page on-call if staleness exceeds 10 min Bloom filter unavailable Crawler can't dedup, risk of duplicate storms Bloom check error rate spike Crawler fails closed — pauses ingestion rather than writing unfiltered duplicates Elasticsearch cluster degraded Search unavailable; feed reads unaffected Query error rate + cluster health yellow/red Serve feed and article pages normally, disable search endpoint with a clear error Clusterer falls behind Duplicate stories briefly show as separate articles Kafka consumer lag on clustering topic Scale worker pool; dedup is corrective, not blocking, so late merges are acceptable Feed Svc queries the ranked list for the requested topic Pre-sorted list refreshed every ~60s Feed Svc queries the ranked list for a rarely-requested topic No pre-ranked list exists yet for this niche topic Recency-only query no personalized score"},{"u":"topics/questions/news-aggregator.html","p":"Design a News Aggregator","i":"interview-moves","h":"Interview moves","t":"Interview moves 01 \"The hard part here isn't storage, it's crawl scheduling and dedup — any reasonable store handles 40M articles/day fine\" — immediately signals you know where the real complexity lives 02 \"I'd prioritize the crawl frontier by each source's publish velocity, not a flat round-robin — a wire feed and a monthly blog shouldn't be polled the same way\" — shows scheduling insight most candidates skip entirely 03 \"A Bloom filter trades a small false-positive rate for a huge memory win on the dedup check — at 2B checks/day that tradeoff is clearly worth it\" — demonstrates comfort quantifying a probabilistic structure, not just naming it 04 \"Ranking runs as a batch job every ~60 seconds, not per-request — computing a personalized score live for 80k QPS doesn't fit the latency budget\" — ties an architectural choice directly to a stated number 05 \"Dedup and clustering are corrective, not blocking — if the clusterer falls behind, duplicate articles just show up separately for a while, which is a fine degradation\" — states a concrete, correct failure-mode priority unprompted 06 \"Every source carries a hard politeness cap the frontier's priority score can never override, or an eager crawler gets us rate-limited or blocked entirely\" — a subtlety that separates a working crawler from a naive one"},{"u":"topics/questions/news-aggregator.html","p":"Design a News Aggregator","i":"follow-ups","h":"Follow-up questions","t":"Follow-up questions How do you detect that two articles from different outlets are the same story? Title/text shingling (n-gram overlap) catches near-identical wire copy cheaply; embedding similarity on the extracted text catches reworded coverage the shingling misses. What happens if a source's RSS feed goes down but the site still publishes? Frontier tracks consecutive feed failures and falls back to an HTML sitemap/homepage crawl after a threshold, flagging the source for manual review if that also fails. How would you extend this to support 10x the source count? Shard the frontier's sorted set by source_id hash, horizontally scale the crawler fleet, and shard Elasticsearch by time range so old indices can be archived off the hot cluster. How do you avoid re-processing the same article if the crawler retries after a timeout? The Bloom filter check happens before any parsing or storage write, and the final Postgres insert is guarded by the unique constraint on url_hash, making the write path idempotent. Why not rank every feed request live instead of batching every 60 seconds? Live ranking means recomputing scores across thousands of candidate articles per request at 80k QPS — the freshness lost by batching is a better tradeoff than that compute cost. How do you handle a source that's flagged as low-quality or spammy? Source.avg_publish_rate and a separate trust score feed directly into the ranker's weighting, and a trust score below threshold can drop a source from the frontier entirely."},{"u":"topics/questions/online-auction.html","p":"Design an Online Auction","i":"","h":"Overview","t":"Questions Design an Online Auction Tests whether you treat concurrent bidding as a compare-and-swap problem and auction close as a distributed coordination problem, not just another CRUD write. 40-50 min interview Hard Concept Map click to expand Say this in the first 2 minutes The core insight: bid submission under contention is an optimistic-locking problem — two bids racing for the same auction must resolve via compare-and-swap, never last-write-wins Key scale number to anchor on: a hot auction in its final 10 seconds can see 200-400 bid attempts/sec, almost all against the same row Primary tradeoff being probed: strong consistency on the current highest bid vs low-latency real-time updates to every watching bidder Component most candidates forget: the auction close worker — deciding the exact moment an auction ends and settling exactly once is a distributed coordination problem, not a cron job Failure mode most candidates skip: \"bid sniping\" — a bid arriving in the last millisecond before close must not be dropped just because the clock ticked over mid-flight Online Auction Bid Service Validates the increment and issues a conditional UPDATE to place a bid step4-dataflow Stateless pods No shared state between instances CAS write Compare and swap on the version column Postgres Source of truth for Auction and Bid rows with optimistic locking step2-entities Version column Every bid write checks it first Append only Bid Every attempt is recorded for audit Auction State Machine Enforces scheduled to active to closing to closed transitions step6-deepdive-statemachine Guarded transitions Each move validated in one transaction Irreversible steps Active to closing cannot revert Auction Close Worker Detects ends_at has passed and settles the winner exactly once step6-deepdive-bidrace Redis lock Only one worker may close an auction Idempotent settle Safe to resume after a crash Redis Pub Sub Broadcasts the new high bid to watchers without blocking the write step5-hld Fire and forget Decoupled from the CAS response Fan out Feeds the WebSocket gateway WebSocket Gateway Holds live connections and pushes bid updates to every watcher step3-api 10k watchers On the hottest auctions Reconnect resync Client re-fetches state on drop"},{"u":"topics/questions/online-auction.html","p":"Design an Online Auction","i":"step1-requirements","h":"Requirements","t":"Step 1 of 6 — Requirements Requirements 1 2 3 4 5 6 Arc 1 (steps 1-5) — Satisfy Functional Requirements 1 2 3 4 5 6 Arc 2 (all 6 steps) — Satisfy Non-Functional Requirements Functional Seller lists an item with a starting price and close time Bidder places a bid that must exceed the current highest by a minimum increment All watchers see the current highest bid update in real time Auction closes automatically at its end time and declares a winner Non-Functional 500k concurrent live auctions, some with 10k+ watchers each Bid acceptance latency <150ms p99, no lost or double-applied bids Exactly-once auction close — never settle twice, never miss a last-second bid Bid submission stays correct under 300+ concurrent bids/sec on one auction What NOT to build: payment processing/escrow, seller reputation and review systems, or fraud/shill-bidding detection ML. This is listing, bidding, real-time broadcast, and close/settlement only. 500k concurrent live auctions ~350/s peak bid attempts on one hot auction (final 10s) <150ms bid acceptance latency, p99 ~10k concurrent WebSocket watchers, hottest auction"},{"u":"topics/questions/online-auction.html","p":"Design an Online Auction","i":"step2-entities","h":"Core entities","t":"Step 2 of 6 — Core Entities Core entities Auction and Bid live in Postgres for transactional correctness; item media sits in object storage — split by access pattern and consistency need. Auction Postgres auction_id UUID seller_id BIGINT status TEXT — state machine current_high_bid_cents BIGINT ends_at TIMESTAMPTZ version INT — optimistic lock Because the current-high-bid field is read and conditionally updated by every competing bid — a relational store with row-level compare-and-swap semantics is exactly the correctness guarantee needed. Tradeoff: a single hot row becomes a write bottleneck on a viral auction. Bid Postgres bid_id BIGINT (Snowflake) auction_id UUID → Auction bidder_id BIGINT amount_cents BIGINT accepted BOOLEAN Because every bid attempt — accepted or rejected — must be durably recorded for dispute resolution and audit, append-only. Tradeoff: high-volume auctions generate a lot of rejected-bid noise that needs periodic archival. Item S3 item_id UUID → Auction title, description TEXT (metadata in Postgres) image_urls S3 object keys Because large binary media has no place in a row store and benefits from CDN-fronted delivery independent of auction state. Tradeoff: an extra round trip to resolve object keys to signed URLs. Primary key: Auction.auction_id with a version column for optimistic locking. Critical index: UNIQUE INDEX on Auction(auction_id, version) — every bid write is a conditional UPDATE ... WHERE version = $current , which is what makes concurrent bidding safe without a distributed lock."},{"u":"topics/questions/online-auction.html","p":"Design an Online Auction","i":"step3-api","h":"API design","t":"Step 3 of 6 — API / Interface API design Listing and bidding are REST; live bid updates use a persistent connection since 10k watchers per auction makes polling infeasible. POST /api/v1/auctions Auth: required (seller) Request { title, description, starting_price_cents, ends_at, image_urls } Response { auction_id, status: \"scheduled\" } POST /api/v1/auctions/{id}/bids Auth: required (bidder) Request { amount_cents, expected_version } Response 200 + new high bid on success, 409 on version conflict / too low GET /api/v1/auctions/{id} Auth: none Request path param only Response { item, current_high_bid_cents, ends_at, status, version } WEBSOCKET /ws/auctions/{id}/watch Auth: none Message out { current_high_bid_cents, bidder_id_masked, ends_at } Cadence pushed on every accepted bid and on close WebSocket over polling for the watch endpoint: at 10k concurrent watchers on a hot auction, even a 2s poll interval is 5k req/sec of pure waste — a single push on state change scales with actual event rate, not watcher count."},{"u":"topics/questions/online-auction.html","p":"Design an Online Auction","i":"step4-dataflow","h":"Data flow","t":"Step 4 of 6 — Data Flow Data flow Read path (mint) — viewing an auction — and write path (blue) — submitting a bid — share the same Auction row but diverge everywhere else. READ PATH — viewing an auction Client CDN Auction Svc stateless Redis miss Postgres WRITE PATH — submitting a bid Client Bid Svc Postgres Redis Pub/Sub → WebSocket Read path — cache + CDN first Write path — CAS on Postgres, no distributed lock needed Broadcast — decoupled from the write's response Client submits a bid with expected_version — the version it last saw for this auction Bid Svc validates the increment — must exceed current high bid by the minimum step Conditional UPDATE against Postgres — WHERE version = expected_version , atomically bumps version Loser gets a 409 — client re-fetches the new current_high_bid and version, may re-bid immediately Winner's bid is published to Redis Pub/Sub — every WebSocket-connected watcher receives the new high bid within milliseconds Browser or app loading an auction page Serves item images and static page shell Thin stateless read service for auction detail Caches current high bid + auction metadata short TTL Source of truth touched only on cache miss Submits a bid amount with the last-known version Validates increment issues conditional UPDATE Compare-and-swap on version — only one concurrent writer wins Broadcasts the new high bid to every watcher over WebSocket"},{"u":"topics/questions/online-auction.html","p":"Design an Online Auction","i":"step5-hld","h":"High-level design","t":"Step 5 of 6 — High-Level Design High-level design Full system: bid service, auction state machine, bid store, real-time broadcast, and the auction close worker together. Clients API Gateway Bid Service stateless Postgres Auction State Machine Auction Close Worker Redis lock (close mutex) Redis Pub/Sub WebSocket Gateway Client / gateway Postgres — Auction + Bid, source of truth Redis — close mutex + pub/sub Close worker + WebSocket fan-out Component Role Tech + why Bid Service Validates and applies bids via CAS Stateless Go/Java service — because it needs to issue a conditional UPDATE with minimal per-request overhead under contention; tradeoff is all correctness logic must live in the DB transaction, not in-memory. Auction State Machine Enforces valid status transitions Embedded in the Auction row's status column with guarded transitions — because auction state is small and transactional; tradeoff is it can't easily fan out to a separate workflow engine without a rewrite. Auction Close Worker Detects ends_at has passed, settles the winner exactly once Polling worker pool + Redis distributed lock per auction_id — because multiple worker instances must never both close the same auction; tradeoff is added operational surface for lock expiry tuning. Redis Pub/Sub + WebSocket Gateway Broadcasts live bid updates to watchers Pub/sub fan-out — because 10k watchers per hot auction makes per-connection DB polling impossible; tradeoff is pub/sub is fire-and-forget, so a disconnected client must re-fetch state on reconnect. Scaling levers — cheapest to most complex Add Postgres read replicas for auction browsing/search near-zero complexity Put Redis cache in front of auction detail reads low complexity Scale WebSocket gateway instances behind sticky routing medium complexity Partition the close worker pool by auction_id hash so lock contention only happens within a shard medium complexity Shard Postgres by auction_id for bid writes at extreme scale high complexity — last resort Bottleneck Triggers at Exact fix Hot auction row contention >200 concurrent bid attempts/sec on one auction CAS retry with jittered backoff on the client; queue-ahead bidding for extreme cases WebSocket fan-out overload One auction crosses ~10k concurrent watchers Shard watchers across multiple gateway nodes subscribed to the same Redis channel Close worker lock expiry mid-settlement Settlement takes longer than the lock TTL Extend lock via heartbeat during settlement; make settlement idempotent regardless Current high bid: consistency vs write availability Strong Eventual Watcher broadcast freshness vs infra load Poll (simple) Push (real-time) Bidders and watchers REST + WebSocket Auth rate limiting routing Validates increment issues the CAS write Auction + Bid rows compare-and-swap on version Enforces scheduled → active → closing → closed transitions Polls ends_at promotes closing → closed exactly once Distributed lock so only one worker closes a given auction Publishes new high bid on every accepted write Fans out to every connection watching that auction"},{"u":"topics/questions/online-auction.html","p":"Design an Online Auction","i":"step6-deepdive-bidrace","h":"Deep dive: bid race condition","t":"Step 6 of 6 — Deep Dives Deep dive: bid race condition Two bids arrive within milliseconds of each other for the same auction. Only one can win — a compare-and-swap on the version column decides, not arrival order at the network layer. ✓ Winning bid — CAS succeeds Bidder A v=7 → $105 Postgres version=7 match 200 OK, v=8 CAS matches → highest bid updated ▸ Replay ✗ Losing bid — CAS fails, clean retry Bidder B v=7 → $102 Postgres version=7 stale 0 rows affected Bid Svc 409, re-fetch CAS misses → 409 → client retries with new version ▸ Replay This is why the client sends expected_version , not a raw amount comparison: comparing against a possibly-stale current_high_bid read on the client is itself a race. The database's atomic conditional UPDATE is the only safe arbiter. Bid at $105 expected_version=7 UPDATE ... WHERE version=7 matches row updated to version=8 Bid at $102 expected_version=7 — but v is already 8 version already advanced to 8 by Bidder A WHERE clause matches zero rows Client re-fetches current_high_bid=105 version=8"},{"u":"topics/questions/online-auction.html","p":"Design an Online Auction","i":"step6-deepdive-statemachine","h":"Deep dive: auction state machine","t":"Deep dive: auction state machine Every transition is guarded and enforced in the same transaction as the triggering write. Dashed transitions are irreversible. Scheduled listing created Active accepting bids Closing ends_at passed Closed / Sold winner settled Closed / Unsold no bids met reserve from Closing, zero valid bids Transition Reversible? Guard condition Scheduled → Active Yes (seller can still edit before start) Current time reaches the listing's start_at Active → Closing No — irreversible Close worker acquires the auction's Redis lock exactly once past ends_at Closing → Closed/Sold No — terminal Highest accepted bid exists and meets reserve price Closing → Closed/Unsold No — terminal No bids, or highest bid below reserve Bid sniping is the classic edge case: a bid that starts processing at t-5ms before ends_at must still be evaluated against the correct pre-close version. The close worker must only flip Active → Closing after acquiring the row lock, so an in-flight CAS either completes first or is correctly rejected — never silently dropped. Scenario User impact Detection signal Mitigation Two close workers race on the same auction Auction could be double-settled, winner charged twice Redis lock acquisition failure rate per auction_id Distributed lock keyed on auction_id; only the lock holder may transition to Closing Bid arrives during the Active→Closing transition A legitimate last-second bid could be lost CAS attempts against an auction already in Closing state Closing only begins after the lock is held; bids mid-flight complete their CAS against the prior state first Redis pub/sub broker outage Watchers stop seeing live bid updates Publish error rate, WebSocket message lag Clients fall back to polling GET /auctions/{id} on detected staleness; bids themselves are unaffected Close worker crash mid-settlement Auction stuck in Closing indefinitely Auctions in Closing state past a settlement timeout threshold Lock TTL expires, another worker picks up and resumes idempotent settlement Hot auction row contention at close Final bids see elevated latency/409 rate CAS conflict rate spike in the last 10s window Client-side jittered retry; this is expected and bounded, not a system failure"},{"u":"topics/questions/online-auction.html","p":"Design an Online Auction","i":"interview-moves","h":"Interview moves","t":"Interview moves 01 \"Bid submission under contention is fundamentally a compare-and-swap problem — I'll use a version column so only one of two racing bids can win\" — states the core insight before being asked 02 \"The client shouldn't compare its own view of the current price against a new bid — that comparison itself can be stale. The conditional UPDATE in Postgres is the only safe arbiter\" — shows understanding of why CAS beats client-side checks 03 \"Auction close needs a distributed lock per auction_id so two worker instances can never both settle the same auction\" — names the exact mechanism for exactly-once coordination 04 \"I'd decouple the real-time bid broadcast from the bid write itself — Redis pub/sub fans out to watchers after the CAS succeeds, never blocking the write\" — demonstrates instinct for isolating hot paths from side effects 05 \"Bid sniping is a real edge case — a bid in flight at the exact close moment must be resolved against the correct pre-close state, not silently dropped because the clock ticked over\" — proactively surfaces a subtle correctness bug 06 \"At 300+ bid attempts/sec on one auction, some 409s are expected and healthy — I'd rather have a clear, bounded retry loop than pretend I can eliminate contention entirely\" — sets realistic expectations instead of over-engineering"},{"u":"topics/questions/online-auction.html","p":"Design an Online Auction","i":"follow-ups","h":"Follow-up questions","t":"Follow-up questions Why optimistic locking instead of a row-level pessimistic lock (SELECT ... FOR UPDATE)? Pessimistic locks serialize all bidders on one auction, worsening latency under contention. Optimistic CAS lets rejected bidders fail fast and retry instead of queuing behind a lock. How do you prevent shill bidding (seller bidding on their own item)? Out of scope for this design's core, but the guard would live in Bid Service validation: reject any bid where bidder_id equals the auction's seller_id. How would you support proxy/auto-bidding (bid up to a max on my behalf)? Store a max_amount per bidder; on any new competing bid, a background rule auto-submits the next valid increment on behalf of any proxy bidder whose max still exceeds it, up to their cap. What happens if the auction close worker pool falls behind during a traffic spike? Auctions queue in Closing slightly longer than ends_at, which is acceptable since bids are already frozen by the state transition — settlement delay doesn't affect bid correctness. How do you handle a WebSocket client that misses updates due to a dropped connection? On reconnect the client calls GET /auctions/{id} to resync current_high_bid and version before resubscribing — pub/sub is best-effort, REST is the consistency backstop. How would this change for a \"Buy It Now\" hybrid auction? Buy It Now is itself just a bid at a fixed amount that triggers an immediate CAS-guarded transition straight to Closing, reusing the same compare-and-swap and state machine."},{"u":"topics/questions/online-chess.html","p":"Design Online Chess","i":"","h":"Overview","t":"Questions Design Online Chess Tests whether you treat two-player game state as a distributed consensus problem — both clients must see an identical board with zero divergence, even across packet loss and reconnects. 40-50 min interview Hard Concept Map click to expand Say this in the first 2 minutes The core insight: this is a distributed consensus problem in miniature — two clients must converge on one authoritative board state, and packet loss must never let them diverge Key scale number to anchor on: 10M concurrent games, each a stateful WebSocket session pinned to one game server — this is not a stateless request/response system Primary tradeoff being probed: strong consistency on the two-player write path vs eventual consistency on the spectator read path — they should not share an architecture Component most candidates forget: idempotency on the move message — an ack can be lost even when the move itself was applied, so the client must be able to safely retry without double-submitting Failure mode most candidates skip: a network partition between the two players isn't a bug to catch, it's a rule to define — someone needs an adjudication policy, not just a retry loop Online Chess Game Server Stateful sticky-session process owning both sockets for one game step5-hld Sticky session Reconnect must rejoin the same instance Stateful Not a stateless request/response service Move Validation Pure legality check against current FEN and turn before any write step6-deepdive-movevalidation Synchronous Runs in the hot path with no external calls Idempotency key Lets a lost ack be retried safely Game State Store Redis key per game_id holding the authoritative live board step2-entities move_count version Rejects stale writes as an optimistic lock No durability Recovered from the append-only move log Move Log DB Postgres append-only history for replay and disputes step2-entities Off hot path Written async never blocks a live move Cheat detection Feeds a post-game engine-analysis pipeline Matchmaking Svc Buckets players by rating and widens the search window over time step6-deepdive-matchmaking Rating buckets Groups by ELO for a cheap bucket scan Widening window Trades match quality for bounded wait time Spectator Fan-out Pub/sub plus CDN edge layer fully decoupled from the players write path step6-deepdive-spectators One publish Single event per move not one per viewer Edge nodes Absorb up to 1M viewer connections"},{"u":"topics/questions/online-chess.html","p":"Design Online Chess","i":"step1-requirements","h":"Requirements","t":"Step 1 of 6 — Requirements Requirements 1 2 3 4 5 6 Arc 1 (steps 1-5) — Satisfy Functional Requirements 1 2 3 4 5 6 Arc 2 (all 6 steps) — Satisfy Non-Functional Requirements Functional Two players are matched and play a real-time game of chess Every move is validated against the rules and the current board state Both players see an identical, up-to-date board at all times Spectators can watch a live game without affecting either player's connection Non-Functional 10M concurrent games, each a long-lived stateful WebSocket session Move validation + broadcast to opponent in <150ms Zero board-state divergence between players — no split-brain, ever Spectator fan-out must scale to 1M viewers on a single popular match What NOT to build: tournament bracket system, puzzle/training mode, or in-app voice chat. This is 1v1 real-time gameplay, matchmaking, move validation, and spectator viewing only. 10M concurrent games ~350k/s moves/sec at peak (10M games, ~1 move/30s/game avg) <5s matchmaking latency target 1M peak spectators on one popular match"},{"u":"topics/questions/online-chess.html","p":"Design Online Chess","i":"step2-entities","h":"Core entities","t":"Step 2 of 6 — Core Entities Core entities Live board state → Redis , sub-millisecond hot path. Player identity + rating → Postgres , durability. Every move appended to an immutable log — replay, disputes, analysis. Game Redis game_id UUID fen TEXT — current board (FEN notation) turn ENUM — white | black white_id, black_id BIGINT → Player move_count INT — monotonic, doubles as version clocks {white_ms, black_ms} Because every move reads-then-writes this object under <150ms pressure, thousands of times a second across the fleet — an in-memory store with atomic ops beats a disk-backed one. Tradeoff: a Redis node loss mid-game needs a recovery path, since this copy is the only live copy. Player Postgres player_id BIGINT rating INT — ELO rating_bucket INT — indexed for matchmaking games_played INT Because rating is a durable, disputed-often value that needs ACID guarantees and survives independent of any single game or connection. Tradeoff: rating updates are async after game end, not on the hot move path. Move Postgres (append-only) game_id UUID → Game seq INT — move number, append-only san TEXT — e.g. \"Nf3\" idempotency_key UUID — client-generated Because the full move history is the source of truth for replay, cheat-detection analysis, and dispute resolution — it is never overwritten, only appended to. Tradeoff: written off the hot path (async from the Redis write) so it never adds latency to a live move. Primary key: Game.game_id , with move_count doubling as an optimistic version so a stale client write is rejected rather than silently applied out of order. Critical index: Player.rating_bucket — this is what makes matchmaking a cheap bucket scan instead of a full table sort."},{"u":"topics/questions/online-chess.html","p":"Design Online Chess","i":"step3-api","h":"API design","t":"Step 3 of 6 — API / Interface API design Matchmaking = REST — one-shot request. Gameplay + spectating = WebSockets — long-lived, low-latency streams. Two separate connection pools , never shared. POST /matchmaking/queue Auth: required (player) Request { rating, time_control } Response { queue_token } — game_id pushed later over WebSocket WEBSOCKET /ws/game/{id} Auth: required (one of the two players) Message in { move_san, idempotency_key, move_count } Message out { type: \"ack\"|\"opponent_move\"|\"clock_sync\", fen, move_count } GET /game/{id}/history Auth: none (public) Request path param only Response { moves: [san...], result, players } WEBSOCKET /ws/game/{id}/spectate Auth: none (public, read-only) Message out { fen, last_move, clocks } on every applied move Cadence fan-out layer, never touches the game server directly The move message carries a client-generated idempotency_key : if the ack is lost and the client retries the same move, the server recognizes the key and resends the ack instead of re-applying (or rejecting) the move."},{"u":"topics/questions/online-chess.html","p":"Design Online Chess","i":"step4-dataflow","h":"Data flow","t":"Step 4 of 6 — Data Flow Data flow Move write (blue): game server + Redis, directly. Applied move fans out to spectators (amber) via broadcast layer. Broadcast layer never talks to the authoritative game server. WRITE PATH — move submission + validation, ~350k/s fleet-wide Player Client Game Server Move Validator Game State (Redis) FAN-OUT PATH — up to 1M spectators, fully decoupled from the write path Pub/Sub Fan-out Spectators Player client / spectators — separate connection pools Redis — single authoritative board, written by one path, read by the fan-out layer Pub/sub fan-out — decoupled from the hot game-write path Player submits a move — sent over the already-open game WebSocket with an idempotency key Game Server forwards to Move Validator — checks legality against the current FEN and whose turn it is Valid move is written to Redis — atomic update of FEN, turn, move_count (acts as version) Ack sent to the mover, move broadcast to the opponent — both over their own persistent connections Applied move is published to the fan-out layer — spectators receive it seconds or milliseconds later, never touching the game server Sends a move over the persistent game WebSocket Stateful sticky-session server owning this game's connection Checks legality against current FEN + turn Atomic read-modify-write of board state keyed by game_id Broadcast layer subscribed to applied-move events never the game server itself Read-only viewers no connection to the authoritative game server"},{"u":"topics/questions/online-chess.html","p":"Design Online Chess","i":"step5-hld","h":"High-level design","t":"Step 5 of 6 — High-Level Design High-level design Players hold a sticky session to one game server. That server is the sole writer to the game's Redis state. Matchmaking + spectator fan-out never sit on the hot path. Client (White) Client (Black) Game Server sticky session Move Validation Svc Game State Store Redis, per-game key Move Log DB Postgres, append-only Spectator Fan-out Spectators Matchmaking Svc assigns game_id, hands off to a Game Server Players + spectators — separate connection pools Game State Store — single authoritative Redis key per game Move Log DB — durable, append-only, off the hot path Matchmaking + Fan-out — decoupled services, never on the two-player write path Component Role Tech + why Game Server Owns the live WebSocket for both players in one game Stateful process, sticky session by game_id — because a reconnect needs to rejoin the exact instance holding in-memory connection state; tradeoff is failover requires explicit recovery, not just a retry to any node. Move Validation Svc Checks legality before any state mutation Pure function over FEN + move — because it must run synchronously in the hot path with no external dependency; tradeoff is chess rule edge cases (en passant, castling rights) add real complexity here. Game State Store Authoritative live board per game Redis, one key per game_id, move_count as version — because 350k moves/sec fleet-wide need sub-ms atomic read-modify-write; tradeoff is it's a single point of truth per game with no built-in durability guarantee. Matchmaking Svc Buckets players by rating, assigns a Game Server Rating-bucketed queue with a widening window — because an exact-rating match is rare at low queue depth; tradeoff is wait time vs match quality. Bottlenecks Bottleneck Triggers at Exact fix Game Server connection limit ~10k sticky WebSocket sessions per instance Horizontal scale-out with consistent hashing on game_id at the load balancer Popular match spectator surge >10k viewers on one game_id CDN-backed pub/sub fan-out layer, never scale the game server itself Matchmaking queue depth Thin player pool at extreme rating tails Widen the rating/latency window on a timer until a match is found Scaling levers — cheapest to most complex Add Game Server instances behind consistent hashing low complexity Cache validated-move results per FEN prefix for repeated opening theory low complexity Separate spectator fan-out onto its own CDN/pub-sub tier medium complexity Shard the Move Log DB by game_id range medium complexity Cross-region sticky-session failover with state replication high complexity — last resort Game state consistency: strong vs eventual Strong Eventual Sticky session: durability vs failover speed Durable, slow failover Fast failover, less state White player's persistent connection Black player's persistent connection One stateful instance sticky-session pinned owns both sockets for this game Legality check against current FEN + turn before any write Authoritative live board keyed by game_id Every applied move is also appended here off the hot path Publishes applied-move events for spectators decoupled from the game server Up to 1M read-only viewers on a popular match Bucket by rating + widen search window over time"},{"u":"topics/questions/online-chess.html","p":"Design Online Chess","i":"step6-deepdive-movevalidation","h":"Deep dive: move validation and delivery","t":"Step 6 of 6 — Deep Dives Deep dive: move validation and delivery Validated once, applied once, delivered twice (ack + broadcast). Lost ack → client retries with the same idempotency key . Never resubmit as a fresh move. SEQUENCE — mover, game server, opponent Mover 1. submit move Game Server 2. ack { fen, move_count } 3. broadcast opponent_move Opponent ack lost in transit Client retry timer fires resends move with same idempotency_key Server sees known idempotency_key re-sends stored ack, does not re-apply 4. duplicate-safe ack delivered Client / gateway Cache — hot path Async — queue / worker Mover submits a move — includes a client-generated idempotency key and the move_count it expects to apply against Game Server validates and applies — legality check, then atomic Redis update, move_count increments Ack goes to the mover, broadcast goes to the opponent — two independent sends over two independent sockets If the ack never arrives — the mover's client retry timer fires and resends the identical move with the same idempotency key Server detects the duplicate key — it does not re-validate or re-apply the move, it simply re-sends the stored ack, so the board never double-advances Without the idempotency key, a lost ack plus a naive retry would submit the same move twice — the second submission would either be rejected as illegal (confusing the client) or, worse, silently advance the board an extra ply if validation is sloppy about turn order. Sends move_san idempotency_key move_count Validates legality applies to Redis move_count++ Persistent WebSocket receives the applied move Client sees no ack within timeout retries with the SAME idempotency_key Recognizes the key was already applied — does not re-validate or re-apply just resends the ack"},{"u":"topics/questions/online-chess.html","p":"Design Online Chess","i":"step6-deepdive-matchmaking","h":"Deep dive: ELO-bucketed matchmaking","t":"Deep dive: ELO-bucketed matchmaking Players are placed into a rating bucket on queue entry. The matcher first looks for an opponent inside a narrow rating/latency window, then widens that window on a timer if no match is found. RATING LADDER — buckets of ~100 ELO, widening search window over time 800-900 900-1000 1000-1100 1100-1200 1200-1300 Player A (1050) t=0s, window ±50 t=5s, window ±150 Player B (1080) Client / gateway Cache — hot path Database — source of truth Async — queue / worker Player enters the queue — assigned to a rating bucket based on current ELO Matcher searches a narrow window first — e.g. ±50 ELO and a tight latency/region constraint, for match quality No match after a few seconds — the rating window widens on a timer (±50 → ±150 → ±300...) Latency constraint can relax too — a distant region becomes acceptable rather than leaving the player waiting indefinitely Match found — both players are handed off to the same Game Server instance, which creates the Game record and opens both sockets The widening schedule is itself a tradeoff knob: a steeper widening curve favors wait time , a flatter one favors match quality — worth stating explicitly if asked \"how do you pick the widening rate?\" Player queues at rating 1050 lands in the 1000-1100 bucket t=0s: window is +/-50 ELO — no match found yet t=5s: window widens to +/-150 ELO if still unmatched t=15s: window widens further latency constraint may also relax Player queues at rating 1080 within the widened window of Player A"},{"u":"topics/questions/online-chess.html","p":"Design Online Chess","i":"step6-deepdive-spectators","h":"Deep dive: spectator fan-out at 1M viewers","t":"Deep dive: spectator fan-out at 1M viewers Player write path and spectator read path never share infrastructure . A viral 1M-viewer match must add zero latency for the players. WRITE PATH — untouched by spectator load 2 Players Game Server Game State + Event READ PATH — scales independently to 1M viewers Pub/Sub Broker CDN Edge Node A CDN Edge Node B Spectators up to 1M viewers Players — fixed at 2 sockets regardless of spectator count Game State + Event — single publish point per applied move Pub/Sub + CDN edge — the only tier that scales with viewer count Move is applied on the Game Server — exactly as it would be for an unwatched game, no extra cost per spectator One event is published — a single fan-out message per move, not one per viewer Pub/sub broker distributes to CDN edge nodes — regional nodes absorb the bulk of connection fan-out close to viewers Edge nodes hold the spectator connections — 1M viewers spread across many edge nodes, not one origin server Spectators never connect to the Game Server — a viral match adds zero load to the two players' authoritative connection The single most common mistake here is routing spectators through the same server that holds the players' sockets \"because it already has the state\" — that couples an unbounded, bursty read load to a latency-critical write path and is exactly the kind of decision an interviewer is listening for you to avoid. Chess-specific failure modes Failure Impact Detection signal Mitigation Game Server dies mid-game Both players disconnected simultaneously Health-check failure / connection drop on both sockets at once Board state is recoverable from the Redis game-state store — a new Game Server instance reloads the FEN + move_count and both clients reconnect to it Network partition between the two players One player can't tell if the other is still connected or thinking Missed heartbeat / clock running past a grace threshold Disconnect-clock rule: opponent's clock keeps running; if it expires or the disconnect exceeds a timeout, the arbiter auto-adjudicates a win/loss (or draw by mutual disconnect) Cheating (engine assistance) Unfair outcome, rating pollution Post-game move-match-rate vs top engine lines, flagged async Async engine-analysis pipeline runs after the game ends — never blocks or delays the live move path, flags accounts for review/ban Idempotency key collision or replay abuse Attempted double-move or move injection Same key with a different move payload Reject on payload mismatch for a reused key; only an exact-payload retry is treated as safe to re-ack The only two sockets the Game Server manages for this match Sticky session only aware of 2 connections regardless of spectator count Every applied move published as an event here once One publish fans out to all subscribed edge nodes Regional edge cache absorbs most viewer connections locally A second edge region same broadcast independent scaling Up to 1M concurrent read-only connections across both edge nodes"},{"u":"topics/questions/online-chess.html","p":"Design Online Chess","i":"interview-moves","h":"Interview moves","t":"Interview moves 01 \"This is really a distributed consensus problem wearing a chess costume — the hard part isn't the rules engine, it's guaranteeing both clients converge on one board state even under packet loss\" — states the core insight unprompted, reframes the problem correctly 02 \"I'd put an idempotency key on every move message so a lost ack can be safely retried without the server re-applying or double-counting the move\" — names the exact mechanism that prevents the most common bug in this design 03 \"The game server has to be stateful with a sticky session — this isn't a stateless request/response API, and pretending otherwise breaks reconnect and failover\" — shows you're not defaulting to a generic load-balanced-stateless mental model 04 \"Spectators should never connect to the same server holding the players' sockets — a viral game with a million viewers can't be allowed to add latency to the two people actually playing\" — proactively separates read and write paths before being asked 05 \"A network partition between two players isn't just an error to catch — someone needs to define the adjudication rule, like a disconnect-clock timeout that awards the game\" — surfaces a product/policy decision most candidates treat as purely technical 06 \"Cheat detection runs as an async, post-game pipeline against engine analysis — it must never sit on the live move path or it adds latency to every legitimate player to catch a small minority\" — demonstrates separating a real requirement from the hot path it doesn't belong on"},{"u":"topics/questions/online-chess.html","p":"Design Online Chess","i":"follow-ups","h":"Follow-up questions","t":"Follow-up questions How do you guarantee the two players never see divergent board states? Single writer per game (the Game Server), atomic Redis updates keyed by game_id, and move_count as a monotonic version — no client ever applies a move locally before the server confirms it. What happens if a player's client reconnects mid-game after a dropped connection? The client reconnects to the same sticky Game Server (or a recovered one), sends its last known move_count, and the server replies with the authoritative current FEN to resync. How would you scale matchmaking if the player pool is very thin at extreme ratings? Widen both the rating window and the latency/region constraint on a timer so an isolated high- or low-rated player still gets matched, trading some match quality for bounded wait time. Why is cheat detection async instead of blocking moves in real time? Real-time engine analysis on every move would add latency to every legitimate player to catch a small minority — running it post-game against the full move log keeps the hot path fast. How do you prevent a hostile client from claiming an illegal move was applied? The server is the sole source of truth — it validates every move server-side before writing to Redis, and clients only ever render what the server acks or broadcasts, never their own local guess. What's the tradeoff in choosing Redis over Postgres for live game state? Redis gives the sub-millisecond atomic read-modify-write the move path needs at 350k moves/sec, at the cost of weaker durability — mitigated by the append-only Postgres move log as the recoverable source of truth."},{"u":"topics/questions/payment-system.html","p":"Design a Payment System","i":"","h":"Overview","t":"Questions Design a Payment System Tests whether you can coordinate state across multiple parties — buyer, seller, platform, external bank/card network — when the external confirmation is async, sometimes delayed, and sometimes duplicated. 45-55 min interview Hard Concept Map click to expand Say this in the first 2 minutes The core insight: this is a multi-party state coordination problem — buyer, seller/merchant, platform, and an external processor all need a consistent view, and the processor's confirmation arrives async, not synchronously Key scale number to anchor on: 10M transactions/day, but processor confirmation latency ranges from milliseconds (card) to seconds or minutes (bank transfer/ACH) — the design must not block on the slow end Idempotency is mandatory, not optional: every charge request carries a client-generated idempotency key, because a client retry after a timeout must never double-charge a buyer Component most candidates get wrong: balances must never be a mutable column — a double-entry, append-only ledger is what makes multi-party settlement (buyer debit, merchant credit, platform fee credit) provably net to zero Failure mode most candidates skip: processor webhooks can be delayed, retried, or arrive out of order — you need webhook dedup by processor event ID plus a nightly reconciliation job against the processor's own statement, not blind trust in the webhook stream Payment System Payment API Idempotency check on every request before any state changes step5-hld Idempotency-Key Required header UNIQUE DB constraint Fast lookup Short-circuits duplicate requests Payment Orchestration Owns the transaction state machine end to end step6-deepdive-idempotent State machine pending to authorized to completed or failed Single owner Avoids split-brain state ownership External Processor Card network or bank rail confirms asynchronously via webhook step4-dataflow Async confirm 200ms to 30s plus depending on rail Immediate ack Ack is not the final result Webhook Ingestion Verifies signature and dedups by processor event id step6-deepdive-webhooks HMAC verify Confirms the event is genuinely from the processor Event dedup Re-delivery becomes a safe no-op Ledger Service Append-only double-entry rows that net to zero per transaction step6-deepdive-ledger No mutable balance Balance is derived never stored as one field Sum to zero Debit and credits always net to zero Reconciliation Job Nightly batch cross-checks internal ledger against processor statement step4-dataflow Statement pull Full settlement report not just webhooks Auto vs page Matches auto-resolve mismatches page on-call"},{"u":"topics/questions/payment-system.html","p":"Design a Payment System","i":"step1-requirements","h":"Requirements","t":"Step 1 of 6 — Requirements Requirements Functional Buyer initiates a charge against a merchant for an order amount Payment is authorized and captured via an external processor (card network, bank rail) On success, funds are split: merchant payout amount, platform fee, both tracked separately Buyer, merchant, and platform can each query the current state of a transaction Refunds and disputes can be issued against a previously completed transaction Non-Functional Idempotent charge handling — a retried request must never double-charge the buyer 10M transactions/day, processor confirmation latency from ~200ms to 30+ seconds depending on rail Never lose or double-apply a webhook — dedup by processor event ID, tolerate out-of-order delivery Ledger must always net to zero across all parties on every settled transaction Nightly reconciliation against the processor's statement catches any drift within 24 hours What NOT to build: card tokenization/PCI-compliance implementation details (assume a PCI-compliant vault/tokenization provider sits in front of us), a fraud/risk scoring ML pipeline, and multi-currency FX conversion logic. This is transaction state coordination, ledger accounting, and processor reconciliation only. 10M transactions/day 200ms-30s+ processor confirmation latency range 24h idempotency key retention window nightly reconciliation job frequency"},{"u":"topics/questions/payment-system.html","p":"Design a Payment System","i":"step2-entities","h":"Core entities","t":"Step 2 of 6 — Core Entities Core entities Transaction Postgres transaction_id UUID idempotency_key TEXT, UNIQUE index buyer_id, merchant_id BIGINT amount, currency DECIMAL, TEXT status TEXT — state machine processor_ref TEXT, nullable — external charge id Because a charge is a financial commitment where the UNIQUE constraint on idempotency_key is what makes a retried request provably safe to replay instead of merely \"usually\" safe. Tradeoff: needs sharding by merchant_id or buyer_id at very high volume. LedgerEntry Postgres transaction_id UUID → Transaction entry_id UUID, append-only account_id BIGINT — buyer, merchant, or platform delta DECIMAL — signed entry_type TEXT — debit, credit, fee, refund Because a single payment moves money across 3+ parties at once — a mutable \"balance\" column per account can't express that atomically. Every entry is an immutable row, and every transaction's entries must sum to zero. Tradeoff: reading a balance means aggregating rows unless materialized. WebhookEvent Postgres processor_event_id TEXT, UNIQUE index transaction_id UUID → Transaction event_type TEXT — authorized, captured, failed received_at TIMESTAMPTZ raw_payload JSONB — signature-verified Because the processor can retry a webhook delivery or deliver it out of order — the UNIQUE constraint on processor_event_id is the dedup mechanism, so a re-delivered webhook is a no-op instead of a double-credit. Tradeoff: this table grows unbounded, needs periodic archival. Primary key: Transaction.transaction_id . Critical indexes: UNIQUE(idempotency_key) on Transaction makes retried charge requests safe; UNIQUE(processor_event_id) on WebhookEvent makes retried/duplicated webhook deliveries safe. LedgerEntry has no mutable field at all by design — it is insert-only."},{"u":"topics/questions/payment-system.html","p":"Design a Payment System","i":"step3-api","h":"API design","t":"Step 3 of 6 — API / Interface API design POST /api/v1/charges Auth: required · Header: Idempotency-Key Request { buyer_id, merchant_id, amount, currency, payment_method_token } Response { transaction_id, status: \"pending\" } — same body replayed if Idempotency-Key seen before GET /api/v1/transactions/{id} Auth: required Request path param: transaction_id Response { status, amount, processor_ref, updated_at } POST /webhooks/processor Auth: signature header (HMAC) Request (inbound) { event_id, event_type, transaction_ref, amount, ts } Response 200 immediately after dedup + enqueue — never blocks on downstream processing GET /api/v1/accounts/{id}/balance Auth: required Request path param: account_id (buyer, merchant, or platform) Response { available_balance, pending_balance, as_of } The Idempotency-Key header is mandatory on POST /charges — the client mints one UUID per logical charge attempt and reuses that exact key across every retry of that attempt, including after a timeout. Inbound webhooks carry their own event_id from the processor, which plays the identical dedup role on the receiving side."},{"u":"topics/questions/payment-system.html","p":"Design a Payment System","i":"step4-dataflow","h":"Data flow","t":"Step 4 of 6 — Data Flow Data flow WRITE PATH — charge creation, idempotency-checked Client/Merchant App Payment Orchestration External Processor ASYNC PATH — webhook confirmation, deduped by event id External Processor Webhook Ingestion Ledger Service READ PATH — balance queries Client/Merchant App Balance View Ledger (Postgres) Client/merchant app — charge creation and balance reads Balance view — materialized, absorbs read volume off the raw ledger External processor + ledger — durable sources of truth, one external, one internal Webhook ingestion + ledger write — decoupled from the synchronous charge call Client sends a charge with an idempotency key — generated once per attempt, reused verbatim on any retry Orchestration service creates the transaction in \"pending\" — before the processor has confirmed anything Processor call returns an immediate ack, not a final result — actual authorization/capture confirmation arrives later via webhook Webhook ingestion dedups by processor event ID — a re-delivered or out-of-order webhook is a safe no-op Ledger entries are written only after a deduped, verified webhook confirms success — never optimistically on the initial request Generates one Idempotency-Key per charge attempt reused on retry Creates transaction in 'pending' state owns the state machine External card network / bank rail — confirms asynchronously Sends a webhook once authorization/capture completes Verifies signature dedups by processor_event_id updates state Confirmed entry written only after webhook is deduped and verified Buyer merchant or platform checks current balance Materialized periodically-refreshed balance view Source of truth: SUM(delta) per account append-only"},{"u":"topics/questions/payment-system.html","p":"Design a Payment System","i":"step5-hld","h":"High-level design","t":"Step 5 of 6 — High-Level Design High-level design Client/Merchant idempotency key Payment API Idempotency-Key required Payment Orchestration pending → completed External Processor card / bank rail Webhook Ingestion verify + dedup + enqueue Payment Orchestration applies confirmed state Ledger Service Postgres, append-only Balance View derived, refreshed on write Reconciliation Job nightly, pulls statement API Client/merchant app — charge creation and state queries Balance view — materialized read path, off the raw ledger External processor + ledger — durable sources of truth Webhook ingestion + reconciliation — decoupled, tolerant of delay Component Role Tech + why Payment API Idempotency check, request intake Postgres-backed UNIQUE constraint on idempotency_key — because a retried request must be provably safe to replay, not just \"probably\" safe. Payment Orchestration Owns the transaction state machine Explicit state machine (pending → authorized → completed / failed) — because a payment moving through an external, async confirmation needs a single source of truth for \"where is this right now.\" External Processor Actual card/bank movement of funds Third-party integration, confirms via webhook not synchronous response — because bank-rail settlement can take seconds to minutes and blocking on it would tank throughput. Webhook Ingestion Verifies, dedups, and applies processor events Signature verification (HMAC) plus UNIQUE constraint on processor_event_id — because webhook delivery is at-least-once, never exactly-once, from the processor's side. Ledger Service Double-entry bookkeeping across all parties Append-only Postgres rows, never a mutable balance column — because one payment must atomically debit the buyer and credit both the merchant and the platform fee account. Reconciliation Job Cross-checks internal state against the processor Nightly batch pulling the processor's settlement statement API — because webhooks alone are not a durable enough guarantee for money movement. Scaling levers — cheapest to most complex Add read replicas for the materialized balance view low complexity Batch webhook processing with a dedicated consumer group low complexity Shard the Transaction/Ledger DB by merchant_id medium complexity Move idempotency key lookups to a fast key-value store in front of Postgres medium complexity Run reconciliation continuously in near-real-time streaming, not just nightly batch high complexity — last resort Bottleneck Triggers at Exact fix Idempotency key lookup contention TRIGGERS AT High concurrent retry volume on the same key during a processor slowdown Fast unique-index lookup path, short-circuit to cached response before any processor call Webhook ingestion burst TRIGGERS AT Processor replays a large batch after its own outage recovers Queue-backed ingestion with a dedicated consumer group, dedup before any downstream write Ledger write contention on a high-volume merchant TRIGGERS AT Many concurrent transactions crediting the same merchant account Shard by merchant_id; append-only inserts avoid row-level lock contention that a mutable balance would hit Confirmation handling: synchronous wait vs async webhook Synchronous wait Async webhook Reconciliation: real-time streaming vs periodic batch Real-time Periodic batch Initiates charges views transaction and balance state Client/Merchant Client Web / mobile / server-to-server Mints one idempotency key per charge attempt reused verbatim on every retry. 0ms (origin of request) Idempotency check on every request before touching state Payment API Service Stateless API layer Checks Idempotency-Key before any state changes — the first line of double-charge defense. ~5-10ms (idempotency lookup) Owns the transaction state machine end to end Payment Orchestration Service Stateless owns transaction state machine Single owner of pending → completed transitions — avoids split-brain state ownership. ~5-10ms (transaction insert) External card network / bank rail confirms async via webhook External Processor Database Card network / bank rail (Stripe-like) Confirms asynchronously via webhook — latency ranges 200ms to 30+ seconds depending on rail. 200ms-30s+ (async confirmation) Verifies HMAC signature dedups by processor_event_id Webhook Ingestion Queue Signature-verified inbound handler Dedups by processor_event_id — a re-delivered webhook becomes a safe no-op. ~1-2ms (dedup check) Updates transaction state once a webhook is confirmed Payment Orchestration (confirm) Service Same orchestration service confirm-path Applies confirmed state only after a deduped verified webhook — never optimistically. ~5-10ms (state update) Double-entry append-only — buyer debit merchant credit fee credit Ledger Service Database Postgres append-only rows Append-only debit/credit rows net to zero per transaction — no mutable balance field to race on. ~5-10ms (multi-row insert) Materialized per-account balance refreshed on ledger writes Balance View Cache Materialized view refreshed on write Balance reads hit this view never the raw ledger — keeps SUM(delta) off the hot read path. ~1-3ms (materialized read) Nightly job pulls processor statement cross-checks internal ledger Reconciliation Job Queue Nightly batch job Backstop the webhook stream alone can't provide — catches missed late or duplicated webhook drift. 24h max detection window"},{"u":"topics/questions/payment-system.html","p":"Design a Payment System","i":"step6-deepdive-idempotent","h":"Deep dive: idempotent charge flow with external processor","t":"Step 6 of 6 — Deep Dives Deep dive: idempotent charge flow with external processor Client Orchestration External Processor 1. Charge sent with Idempotency-Key: K-77ab 2. Transaction row created in \"pending\" — before processor confirms anything status = pending 3. Orchestration calls the processor, gets an immediate ack only ack ≠ final result — actual auth/capture confirmed later 4. Processor confirms asynchronously via webhook, possibly retried/duplicated 5. Webhook deduped by processor_event_id — a re-delivery is a no-op dedup check 6. On first-seen confirmation: transaction → \"completed\", ledger entry written client polls or receives a push — same idempotency key still resolves consistently Client / gateway Cache — hot path Database — source of truth Async — queue / worker transactions (idempotency columns) Postgres transaction_id UUID PRIMARY KEY idempotency_key TEXT NOT NULL, UNIQUE INDEX status TEXT — pending/authorized/completed/failed response_body JSONB — the exact response to replay The UNIQUE index on idempotency_key makes correctness structural at the database level: a second INSERT with the same key fails before any processor call is even attempted, and that failure is the signal to fetch and replay response_body. Client generates one idempotency key per logical charge attempt — created once, before the first network call Orchestration attempts an INSERT keyed on idempotency_key — the UNIQUE index makes a duplicate insert fail fast, before touching the processor Transaction is created in \"pending\" immediately — the processor hasn't confirmed anything yet, and doesn't need to for this row to exist Processor's webhook is the only thing that can move the transaction out of \"pending\" — and that webhook itself must be deduped by processor_event_id, since it can be retried or arrive out of order Only after a deduped, verified webhook confirms success does the ledger get a confirmed entry — never optimistically on the initial request The client's idempotency key and the processor's event ID solve two different halves of the same problem — one protects against the client retrying the request, the other protects against the processor retrying its confirmation. Skipping either one reopens a double-charge or double-credit path. Client lane Orchestration lane Processor lane INSERT with UNIQUE(idempotency_key) — this row is the lock UNIQUE(processor_event_id) — duplicate delivery fails the insert handled as a no-op"},{"u":"topics/questions/payment-system.html","p":"Design a Payment System","i":"step6-deepdive-ledger","h":"Deep dive: double-entry ledger for multi-party settlement","t":"Deep dive: double-entry ledger for multi-party settlement NAIVE — mutable balance columns (can't split atomically) buyer.balance -= 100 merchant.balance += 97 platform.balance += 3 3 independent UPDATEs — a partial failure leaves no trace of what should net to zero There is no row anywhere that says \"these three changes belong together\" LEDGER — one transaction, three linked rows, sums to zero transaction_id = T-501 buyer: delta = -100 merchant: delta = +97 platform fee: delta = +3 -100 + 97 + 3 = 0 — verified Cache — hot path Database — source of truth Never store per-account balance as a single mutable field — a 3+ party split (buyer, merchant, platform fee) has no way to be expressed as one atomic mutation across three separate columns Model one payment as multiple linked ledger entries sharing a transaction_id — debit buyer, credit merchant, credit platform fee, all inserted in a single DB transaction Enforce that every transaction's entries sum to exactly zero — this invariant is checkable and is what makes the accounting provably correct, not just probably correct Refunds and disputes become new offsetting entries, never mutated history — a refund is a fresh entry crediting the buyer and debiting the merchant, referencing the original transaction_id, so the full history stays intact for audit Derive each account's balance by summing its entries — materialize it for fast reads, but the ledger sum is always the source of truth A naive \"balance\" column per account fails specifically at 3+ parties: there is no single row that can represent \"these three changes are one atomic unit that must net to zero.\" The ledger's transaction_id grouping is what makes that invariant enforceable at write time instead of hoped for. A single UPDATE per account has no transactional link to the other two If this UPDATE fails after the buyer's succeeded money vanishes A third independent UPDATE — three separate race windows not one atomic move No single row proves the three updates are one transaction that nets to zero One transaction_id ties all three entries together INSERT delta=-100 for the buyer account INSERT delta=+97 for the merchant account INSERT delta=+3 for the platform fee account All three entries share one transaction_id and commit atomically"},{"u":"topics/questions/payment-system.html","p":"Design a Payment System","i":"step6-deepdive-webhooks","h":"Deep dive: webhook reliability and reconciliation","t":"Deep dive: webhook reliability and reconciliation WHY WEBHOOKS ALONE AREN'T ENOUGH Webhook never arrives network drop, endpoint down at delivery time Webhook arrives very late processor-side retry backoff Webhook arrives duplicated processor's own at-least-once retry policy Transaction sits in \"pending\" — or a duplicate webhook risks a double-credit if not deduped Timeout-based status check polls processor API if pending too long Nightly Reconciliation Job pulls processor statement/report API Match → auto-resolve. Mismatch → alert on-call Database — source of truth Async — queue / worker Webhook delivery is at-least-once at best, and not even guaranteed at all — the processor's endpoint call can fail outright, arrive minutes to hours late, or be delivered more than once A timeout-based status check is the first backstop — if a transaction has sat in \"pending\" past a defined SLA, the system proactively polls the processor's status API instead of waiting indefinitely for a webhook A nightly reconciliation job is the second, stronger backstop — it pulls a full settlement statement/report from the processor's API, not just recent webhook events, and diffs it against internal transaction state Matches auto-resolve silently — the vast majority of transactions agree between the two sources and require no action Mismatches are flagged, and where safe, auto-corrected — e.g. a transaction confirmed on the statement but stuck \"pending\" internally gets its webhook effect replayed; anything ambiguous (e.g. an internal \"completed\" the processor has no record of) pages a human instead of guessing Trusting the webhook stream alone means trusting that delivery is guaranteed, in-order, and exactly-once — none of which any processor actually promises. Reconciliation exists precisely because the webhook path is a convenience for latency, not a durability guarantee. Failure mode User impact Detection signal Mitigation Duplicate charge attempt from client retry Buyer charged twice for one logical purchase Two requests, same intent, different or missing idempotency keys Mandatory Idempotency-Key header with a UNIQUE DB constraint; client reuses the same key across all retries of one attempt Webhook never arrives or arrives after a long delay Transaction shows \"pending\" indefinitely, buyer/merchant unsure if it succeeded Transactions older than the expected confirmation SLA still in \"pending\" Timeout-based status check against the processor's API as an immediate backstop, plus nightly reconciliation as the guaranteed catch-all Ledger entry write fails partway through a multi-entry transaction Buyer debited but merchant/platform never credited, or vice versa — books don't net to zero A transaction_id whose ledger entries don't sum to zero, caught by a periodic integrity check Wrap all entries for one transaction in a single atomic DB transaction; if entries must span services, use a saga with explicit compensating entries on partial failure Case 1: delivery fails entirely no retry ever succeeds Case 2: delivery succeeds but very late — minutes to hours Case 3: delivery succeeds more than once for the same event A transaction can sit here far longer than a user should have to wait Backstop 1: a timeout-based status check calls the processor's own API directly Backstop 2: nightly job pulls the full settlement statement not just recent events Matches auto-resolve; unexplained mismatches page a human"},{"u":"topics/questions/payment-system.html","p":"Design a Payment System","i":"interview-moves","h":"Interview moves","t":"Interview moves 01 \"This is a multi-party state coordination problem before it's a scale problem — buyer, merchant, platform, and an external processor all need a consistent view, and the processor's confirmation is async\" — states the core insight unprompted 02 \"Every charge request carries a client-generated idempotency key with a UNIQUE constraint in the database — that's what makes a retry provably safe instead of just usually safe\" — names the specific, correct mechanism 03 \"Balances should never be a mutable column — I'd use a double-entry, append-only ledger so a single payment's debit and multiple credits are linked, atomic, and provably net to zero\" — shows the ledger pattern extends naturally to 3+ parties, not just two 04 \"The processor's webhook needs its own dedup, keyed on the processor's event ID — webhooks are delivered at-least-once, never exactly-once, so a retried delivery must be a safe no-op\" — distinguishes client-side idempotency from processor-side webhook dedup 05 \"I wouldn't trust the webhook stream alone for money movement — I'd add a nightly reconciliation job that pulls the processor's own statement and cross-checks it against internal state\" — proactively surfaces the reliability gap and names the concrete fix 06 \"Refunds and disputes are new offsetting ledger entries referencing the original transaction, never a mutation of history\" — demonstrates the ledger model isn't just for the happy path"},{"u":"topics/questions/payment-system.html","p":"Design a Payment System","i":"follow-ups","h":"Follow-up questions","t":"Follow-up questions What if two charge requests arrive with the same idempotency key at the exact same moment? The UNIQUE index on idempotency_key makes the database the arbiter — only one INSERT wins; the other fails and its handler fetches and returns the winner's stored response. How long do you retain idempotency keys and webhook event IDs? As long as the transaction row itself lives, since a legitimate late retry or delayed webhook after a long network partition should still resolve safely rather than be treated as new. Why not just use optimistic locking on mutable balance columns instead of a ledger? Optimistic locking could fix a two-party race, but it can't express a 3+ party atomic split, and it gives you no audit trail — the ledger solves both at once. What happens when reconciliation finds a mismatch? Auto-resolvable cases (e.g. a confirmed statement line with no local effect applied) get the missed webhook effect replayed automatically; anything ambiguous pages a human rather than guessing. How do you handle a partial refund? A new transaction referencing the original, with its own linked ledger entries — debit merchant, credit buyer, adjust the platform fee proportionally — never editing the original entries. How would this design change for a bank transfer that takes days to settle instead of a card charge that confirms in milliseconds? Same state machine and same webhook/reconciliation mechanics, just with a much longer expected \"pending\" window — the timeout-based status check threshold is rail-specific, not universal."},{"u":"topics/questions/price-tracking.html","p":"Design a Price Tracking Service","i":"","h":"Overview","t":"Questions Design a Price Tracking Service Tests whether you can keep a scraper fleet alive against sites actively trying to block it, not whether you can store a time series. 30-40 min interview Medium Concept Map click to expand Say this in the first 2 minutes The core insight: getting blocked by target sites is the primary operational risk, not storage or alert delivery — anti-detection is a first-class design concern Key scale number to anchor on: 50M tracked products polled on independent per-domain schedules means the scheduler itself is a hard distributed-systems problem Primary tradeoff being probed: crawl frequency vs politeness — hitting a domain too often gets the whole fleet IP-banned, not just one request Component most candidates forget: a per-domain rate limiter that throttles the scraper fleet itself, independent of the site's own limits Failure mode most candidates skip: a site changes its HTML structure — the scraper must fail loudly (alert a human) rather than silently store garbage prices Price Tracking Scheduler Decides when each product is due for a re-scrape step4-dataflow Cron-driven Cadence varies per target site Per-domain queues Isolates one blocked site from others Scraper Workers Fetches pages while evading anti-bot detection step6-deepdive-antibot Rotate + jitter Avoids looking like a bot Anti-detection first The actual hard problem here Redis Throttle Rate-limits requests per target site step5-hld Per-domain limits Keeps one site from getting hammered Backpressure Slows workers before getting blocked TimescaleDB Stores the price history time series step2-entities Time-series storage Optimized for timestamped writes Retention policy Old granular data gets downsampled Alert Engine Evaluates price-drop rules against new data step6-deepdive-alerts Rule evaluation Runs on each new price point Notification Svc Delivers the alert to the user"},{"u":"topics/questions/price-tracking.html","p":"Design a Price Tracking Service","i":"step1-requirements","h":"Requirements","t":"Step 1 of 6 — Requirements Requirements 1 2 3 4 5 6 Arc 1 (steps 1-5) — Satisfy Functional Requirements 1 2 3 4 5 6 Arc 2 (all 6 steps) — Satisfy Non-Functional Requirements Functional Track a product URL and poll its price on a schedule Store historical price points per product Alert a user when price drops below a threshold or by a percentage Show a price history chart to the user Non-Functional 50M tracked (user, product) pairs across ~200k distinct product URLs Alert delivery within 5 minutes of a detected price change Scraper fleet must not get IP-banned by target retailers Tolerate individual site layout changes without cascading failures What NOT to build: a general-purpose web crawler/search index, browser extension price-comparison shopping, or checkout/purchase automation. This is scheduled polling, price extraction, and threshold alerting only. 200k distinct product URLs tracked ~450/s scrape jobs dispatched (avg) 1-4x/day poll frequency per product <5 min alert delivery SLA"},{"u":"topics/questions/price-tracking.html","p":"Design a Price Tracking Service","i":"step2-entities","h":"Core entities","t":"Step 2 of 6 — Core Entities Core entities Four entities split by access pattern: config lookups, append-only history, and a separate table for the anti-bot layer's per-domain state. TrackedProduct Postgres id BIGINT url TEXT UNIQUE domain VARCHAR(255) poll_interval_min INT selector_config JSONB Because scheduling and CSS-selector config are read far more often than written — a relational store with an indexed domain column fits the scheduler's query pattern. Tradeoff: schema migration needed whenever a new retailer's extraction logic differs enough to need new fields. PricePoint TimescaleDB product_id BIGINT → TrackedProduct.id ts TIMESTAMPTZ price_cents INT in_stock BOOLEAN Because this is a pure append-only time series queried by product_id + time range for charting — a time-series-optimized store gives compression and range-query speed a general RDBMS table would need manual partitioning to match. Tradeoff: an extra system to operate versus reusing Postgres. PriceAlert Postgres id BIGINT user_id BIGINT product_id BIGINT → TrackedProduct.id threshold_type ENUM(absolute, pct_drop) threshold_value INT Because alert rules are low-volume structured config evaluated on every new price point — needs transactional guarantees so a user is never double-notified for the same drop. Tradeoff: a hot product with 10k alert subscribers means the evaluation fan-out happens here, not in the scraper. DomainThrottleState Redis domain VARCHAR(255) requests_this_window INT backoff_until TIMESTAMP, nullable Because the anti-bot layer needs a shared, low-latency counter that every scraper worker checks before every request — in-memory with TTL is the only store fast enough not to become the bottleneck at 450 req/s. Tradeoff: state resets on a Redis restart, briefly relaxing throttling. Primary key: TrackedProduct.id . Critical index: UNIQUE INDEX on url — prevents tracking the same product URL twice under different rows, and INDEX on (product_id, ts) on PricePoint for fast chart range queries."},{"u":"topics/questions/price-tracking.html","p":"Design a Price Tracking Service","i":"step3-api","h":"API design","t":"Step 3 of 6 — API / Interface API design Four endpoints — track a product, fetch price history, create an alert rule, and list a user's tracked products. No endpoint exposes scraping internals. POST /api/v1/products/track Auth: required Request { url } Response { product_id, domain, first_price_cents, poll_interval_min } GET /api/v1/products/{id}/history Auth: required Request ?from=&to= (ISO timestamps) Response { points: [{ ts, price_cents, in_stock }] } POST /api/v1/alerts Auth: required Request { product_id, threshold_type, threshold_value } Response { alert_id, created_at } DELETE /api/v1/products/{id}/track Auth: required (owner) Request path param only Response HTTP 204 — stops polling if no other user tracks this product"},{"u":"topics/questions/price-tracking.html","p":"Design a Price Tracking Service","i":"step4-dataflow","h":"Data flow","t":"Step 4 of 6 — Data Flow Data flow Scrape path (amber, async/scheduled) and user read path (mint) never touch the same hot component — a slow scrape never blocks a chart load. SCRAPE PATH — scheduled, async, ~450 jobs/s Scheduler cron-driven Job Queue Scraper Worker Target Site TimescaleDB READ PATH — user views chart Client Read API TimescaleDB Scrape path — scheduled, per-domain throttled Read path — direct query, fully decoupled from scraping Scheduler computes due jobs — every product whose poll_interval_min has elapsed since its last scrape Jobs land in per-domain queues — isolates a slow or blocking retailer from delaying every other domain Worker checks Redis throttle state — skips or delays the request if the domain is in backoff Worker fetches and extracts price — via the domain's configured CSS/JSON selector Price point written, alerts evaluated — a write to TimescaleDB triggers a synchronous check against PriceAlert rules for that product Computes due jobs from poll_interval_min per product Per-domain queues so one slow retailer can't starve others Checks Redis throttle state before every request External retailer site — the thing actively resisting us Writes the extracted price point evaluates alert rules User opens a product's price history view Stateless read API never touches the scrape queue Range query over price_point by product_id + ts"},{"u":"topics/questions/price-tracking.html","p":"Design a Price Tracking Service","i":"step5-hld","h":"High-level design","t":"Step 5 of 6 — High-Level Design High-level design Full system: scrape pipeline, anti-bot layer, alert evaluation, and the read path together. Scheduler cron-driven Per-Domain Queues Scraper Workers pool, stateless Redis Throttle Target Sites 200k+ domains TimescaleDB Alert Engine Notification Svc Client Read API Scheduler + queues + workers — the scrape pipeline Redis — shared per-domain throttle state TimescaleDB — append-only price history Target sites + end-user client Component Role Tech + why Scheduler Determines which products are due for a scrape Cron-driven job producer — because polling interval per product varies and needs to be computed cheaply at scale; tradeoff is a single logical scheduler needs its own HA setup. Per-Domain Queues Isolates scrape jobs by target domain Kafka topics keyed by domain — because one retailer's slowdown or ban must never delay jobs for every other retailer; tradeoff is far more partitions/topics to operate than one shared queue. Redis Throttle Shared per-domain rate limit state In-memory counters with TTL — because every worker in the fleet must see the same up-to-date backoff state with sub-ms latency; tradeoff is throttling briefly relaxes on a Redis restart. Alert Engine Evaluates price drops against user rules Synchronous check on write — because a 5-minute alert SLA doesn't tolerate a batch job; tradeoff is added latency on the scrape write path, kept small by indexing rules on product_id. Scaling levers — cheapest to most complex Add more scraper worker instances near-zero complexity Widen poll intervals for low-value/rarely-checked products low complexity, high cost payoff Rotate proxy pools and user agents per domain medium complexity Shard TimescaleDB by product_id range as history grows medium complexity Headless-browser rendering tier for JS-heavy sites high complexity — last resort, only for sites that require it Bottleneck Triggers at Exact fix Single domain gets IP-banned Backoff triggers, 429/403 rate climbs Rotate proxy pool for that domain; widen its poll interval automatically Alert engine lag Price-write-to-alert-check latency >5 min Partition alert evaluation by product_id hash across more consumers Selector drift (site redesign) Extraction returns null/garbage for a domain Circuit-break that domain's scraper, page an on-call human, don't write bad prices Crawl frequency vs politeness Aggressive polling Conservative, ban-averse Price data freshness Real-time Eventual (hours) Fires due jobs on each product's own interval One queue per domain isolates slow retailers Pool of stateless workers horizontally scaled Shared throttle state checked before every request External sites each with its own defenses Append-only price history range-query optimized Every new price point checked against active rules Email/push decoupled via its own queue Views tracked products and price charts Stateless reads Postgres config + TimescaleDB history"},{"u":"topics/questions/price-tracking.html","p":"Design a Price Tracking Service","i":"step6-deepdive-antibot","h":"Deep dive: scraper anti-detection techniques","t":"Step 6 of 6 — Deep Dives Deep dive: scraper anti-detection techniques Every layer exists to make the scraper fleet look like normal, distributed human traffic rather than a single bot hammering a site. ✓ Well-behaved fleet — sustained access Worker rotate + jitter Target Site looks organic 200 OK randomized headers, delays, IPs → sustained access ▸ Replay ✗ Naive fleet — banned within minutes Worker fixed pattern Target Site bot detection flags pattern IP Ban 403 forever predictable pattern → detected → banned ▸ Replay Rotate proxy IPs per domain — a pool of residential/datacenter proxies, never hammering from one IP Rotate user-agent strings — mimics a mix of real browsers instead of one static bot signature Jitter request timing — randomized delay instead of a suspiciously exact fixed interval Per-domain rate limiting — the scraper throttles itself below the site's own detection threshold, not just above it Respect robots.txt where reasonable — reduces adversarial posture and legal risk, though it isn't a technical requirement for public price pages This is an arms race, not a solved problem: sites tighten detection over time, so the anti-bot layer needs ongoing tuning per domain, not a one-time configuration. Rotates proxy IP + user agent per request Sees varied human-like request patterns Same IP same user agent fixed interval Flags the fixed-interval single-IP pattern as a bot IP added to a blocklist"},{"u":"topics/questions/price-tracking.html","p":"Design a Price Tracking Service","i":"step6-deepdive-alerts","h":"Deep dive: price alert evaluation","t":"Deep dive: price alert evaluation Two threshold types cover nearly every real use case, evaluated synchronously on every new price point. Absolute threshold \"Alert me when this drops below $50\" Simple comparison: new price_cents < threshold_value Best for a specific target price the user has in mind Percentage drop threshold \"Alert me on any 15%+ drop from the price I started tracking at\" Compares against a stored baseline price, not the previous point Best for catching sales without knowing a target price upfront New price point written — the scraper worker's write to TimescaleDB completes Alert Engine looks up active rules — indexed query on PriceAlert.product_id, typically a handful of rows Each rule evaluated against the new price — absolute or percentage-drop comparison Matching rules enqueue a notification — deduplicated so a user isn't alerted twice for the same drop within a cooldown window Scenario User impact Detection signal Mitigation Target site redesigns its HTML Price extraction silently returns wrong or null values Extracted price deviates >50% from trailing average, or selector match fails Circuit-break that domain, discard the bad point, page an on-call human to update the selector Domain IP-bans the whole fleet All products on that domain stop updating 429/403 rate spikes to 100% for the domain Exponential backoff, rotate to a fresh proxy pool, widen poll interval temporarily Redis throttle store down Risk of over-scraping and mass bans Redis health check failure Workers fail closed — pause scraping for affected domains rather than scrape unthrottled Alert Engine falls behind Users notified late, missing the 5-min SLA Consumer lag on the price-point write stream Scale out alert evaluation consumers, partitioned by product_id hash Duplicate product URLs tracked Wasted scrape capacity on the same product URL-normalization check at track time (strip tracking params, resolve redirects) UNIQUE constraint on normalized URL merges duplicate tracking requests onto one TrackedProduct row"},{"u":"topics/questions/price-tracking.html","p":"Design a Price Tracking Service","i":"interview-moves","h":"Interview moves","t":"Interview moves 01 \"Before I design storage, let me flag that the hard constraint here is staying unblocked — getting IP-banned breaks the product entirely, not just degrades it\" — reframes the problem correctly in the first minute 02 \"I'd queue scrape jobs per-domain, not in one global queue, so one retailer's slowdown or ban never delays every other domain\" — shows isolation instinct before being prompted 03 \"A shared Redis-backed throttle means every worker in the fleet respects the same per-domain rate limit, even across hundreds of instances\" — names the exact mechanism, not just the concept 04 \"If extraction starts returning null or wildly different prices, I want that to page a human, not silently write garbage into the price history\" — demonstrates data-integrity instincts 05 \"I'd separate the scrape pipeline from the read API entirely — a user loading a price chart should never be slowed down by scraper backpressure\" — correctly identifies the two independent hot paths 06 \"Poll interval shouldn't be fixed — a $2,000 item a user is actively watching deserves more frequent checks than a $10 item nobody's alerted on\" — connects a technical scheduling decision to product value"},{"u":"topics/questions/price-tracking.html","p":"Design a Price Tracking Service","i":"follow-ups","h":"Follow-up questions","t":"Follow-up questions How do you avoid tracking the same product twice under slightly different URLs? Normalize URLs at track time — strip tracking/query params, resolve redirects — then enforce a UNIQUE constraint on the normalized form before creating a new TrackedProduct. How would you prioritize which products to scrape first when the worker pool is saturated? Weight the per-domain queue by number of active alert subscribers and how overdue the product is relative to its poll interval, not simple FIFO. What if a site requires JavaScript rendering to show the real price? Route that domain to a separate headless-browser worker tier — it's far more expensive per request, so only sites that actually need it should use it. How do you handle currency and regional pricing differences? Store price_cents alongside a currency code and normalize to the user's display currency at read time using a periodically refreshed FX rate table, never at write time. Could you reduce cost by sharing scrape results across users tracking the same product? Yes — TrackedProduct is already deduplicated by normalized URL, so one scrape serves every user watching that product; only PriceAlert rules are per-user. How would you extend this to support 10x the tracked products? Shard TimescaleDB by product_id range, scale out per-domain queue partitions, and widen poll intervals for long-tail low-alert-subscriber products first."},{"u":"topics/questions/rate-limiter.html","p":"Design a Rate Limiter","i":"","h":"Overview","t":"Questions Design a Rate Limiter Tests whether you understand that rate limiting is fundamentally a distributed counting problem — get the shared-state model wrong and every instance silently multiplies the real limit. 30-40 min interview Medium Concept Map click to expand Say this in the first 2 minutes The core insight: local in-memory counters per instance are wrong at any scale >1 instance — 10 instances behind a load balancer each enforcing \"100/min\" independently allows 1000/min in aggregate Key scale number to anchor on: gateway sees ~200k req/s across ~50 backend instances, so the limiter check must add well under 1ms and never become the bottleneck it's supposed to protect Primary tradeoff being probed: token bucket (smooths bursts, allows saved-up capacity) vs sliding window (precise but costs more memory) vs fixed window (cheap but bursts at boundaries) Component most candidates forget: Redis itself needs to be fast and available for every request — if it's down, decide up front whether to fail open (allow) or fail closed (deny) Failure mode most candidates skip: the check-then-increment race — two concurrent requests both read count=99 under a limit of 100 and both get allowed, so the increment must be atomic, not read-modify-write in app code Rate Limiter API Gateway Terminates TLS and authenticates requests before the limiter check step5-hld Auth Resolves the scope identifier Routing Sends traffic to backend instances Rate Limit MW Stateless middleware that computes the key and calls Redis step4-dataflow Stateless pods Runs as a sidecar or in-process plugin Key builder Builds scope plus identifier plus window Redis Cluster Shared atomic counters that make the allow or deny decision race free step6-deepdive-tokenbucket Atomic INCR Single threaded no app level locking Lua scripts Read compute write in one round trip Postgres rules Durable rate limit rule config polled into memory every 30s step2-entities Cold config Never touched on the hot path Scope enum user api_key ip endpoint Metrics Alerting Async pipeline tracking deny rates and hot keys off the request path step5-hld Async counters Never adds latency to the decision Hot key detection Flags shards with imbalance Sliding Window Exact algorithm using a sorted set to avoid boundary bursts step6-deepdive-window ZADD plus ZCARD Sorted set per identifier Fixed window risk Allows 2x burst at the edge"},{"u":"topics/questions/rate-limiter.html","p":"Design a Rate Limiter","i":"step1-requirements","h":"Requirements","t":"Step 1 of 6 — Requirements Requirements 1 2 3 4 5 6 Arc 1 (steps 1-5) — Satisfy Functional Requirements 1 2 3 4 5 6 Arc 2 (all 6 steps) — Satisfy Non-Functional Requirements Functional Allow or deny a request based on a configurable rate limit Support multiple limit tiers — per-user, per-API-key, per-IP, per-endpoint Return 429 with a Retry-After header when denied Let limits be configured/updated without redeploying services Non-Functional ~200k req/s at the gateway across ~50 backend instances Limiter check adds <1ms p99 — it must never be the slow part Limit enforcement consistent across all instances, not per-node Highly available — a limiter outage should degrade, never take down the API What NOT to build: a general-purpose API gateway, DDoS mitigation at the network layer, or a billing/quota system. This is strictly the allow/deny decision and its supporting counter infrastructure — not WAF rules or usage-based invoicing. 100/min typical per-user limit ~200k/s total gateway throughput <1ms Redis INCR + EXPIRE round trip ~50 backend instances sharing state"},{"u":"topics/questions/rate-limiter.html","p":"Design a Rate Limiter","i":"step2-entities","h":"Core entities","t":"Step 2 of 6 — Core Entities Core entities Three entities — the rule config is cold and relational, the live counters are hot and ephemeral, so they live in different stores by access pattern. RateLimitRule Postgres id BIGINT scope ENUM(user, api_key, ip, endpoint) limit_count INT window_seconds INT algorithm ENUM(token_bucket, sliding_window, fixed_window) Because rule config changes rarely (ops-driven, not per-request) and needs relational integrity across scopes. Tradeoff: it's never on the hot path — it's read once and cached in-memory on each limiter node, refreshed every ~30s. Counter Redis key \"rl:{scope}:{id}:{window}\" count INT (atomic via INCR) ttl seconds, auto-expires the key Because every request needs an atomic read-and-increment across every instance in under a millisecond — Redis's single-threaded command execution makes INCR inherently race-free without app-level locking. Tradeoff: Redis becomes a single dependency every request now hits. TokenBucketState Redis key \"tb:{scope}:{id}\" tokens FLOAT, current bucket level last_refill_ts FLOAT, epoch ms Because token bucket needs two fields updated together (tokens consumed, refill timestamp advanced) — stored as a Redis hash and mutated via a Lua script so the read-compute-write is atomic in one round trip. Tradeoff: slightly more compute per request than a plain INCR. Primary key: Counter.key , composed as rl:{scope}:{identifier}:{window_start} . Critical index: none needed — Redis keys are the index, and TTL-based auto-expiry means no cleanup job is ever required."},{"u":"topics/questions/rate-limiter.html","p":"Design a Rate Limiter","i":"step3-api","h":"API design","t":"Step 3 of 6 — API / Interface API design The rate limiter is usually a middleware/sidecar, not a public API — but it still needs an internal check endpoint plus admin CRUD for rules. POST /internal/v1/check Auth: service-to-service Request { scope, identifier, endpoint } Response { allowed, remaining, retry_after_ms } GET /internal/v1/rules/{scope} Auth: service-to-service Request path param only Response { limit_count, window_seconds, algorithm } — cached client-side ~30s POST /api/v1/admin/rules Auth: required (admin) Request { scope, limit_count, window_seconds, algorithm } Response { rule_id } — HTTP 201, propagates on next refresh cycle PUT /api/v1/admin/rules/{id} Auth: required (admin) Request { limit_count?, window_seconds? } Response HTTP 200 — existing in-flight windows are not retroactively affected"},{"u":"topics/questions/rate-limiter.html","p":"Design a Rate Limiter","i":"step4-dataflow","h":"Data flow","t":"Step 4 of 6 — Data Flow Data flow Two outcomes for the same path — the ALLOW row (mint) and the DENY row (amber) — both hit Redis, they just diverge on the response. ALLOW PATH — under limit Client Gateway Rate Limit MW stateless Redis under limit Backend Svc DENY PATH — over limit Client Gateway Rate Limit MW Redis 429 + Retry-After returned to client CONFIG PATH — rule updates, off the hot path Postgres → rule cache Redis — shared atomic counter, source of truth for the decision Gateway + middleware — stateless, horizontally scalable Postgres — cold rule config, polled not queried per-request Gateway authenticates the request — resolves the identifier (user ID, API key, or IP) used as the rate-limit scope Middleware builds the counter key — e.g. rl:user:4471:2026-06-30T14:22 for a fixed window, or tb:user:4471 for token bucket Atomic Redis operation executes — INCR+EXPIRE or a Lua script, resolving in under 1ms with no read-modify-write race Decision made from the returned count — under limit → forward to backend; at/over limit → short-circuit with 429 Response includes Retry-After — computed from the window's remaining TTL or the bucket's refill rate, so clients can back off correctly Any authenticated API request Terminates TLS authenticates the request Extracts scope+identifier calls the limiter Atomic INCR (or Lua token-bucket script) resolves in <1ms Request proceeds to the actual backend Same client has burst past the limit Same gateway path Same atomic check count already at limit Returns count >= limit no backend call made Middleware polls/refreshes rules every ~30s never per-request"},{"u":"topics/questions/rate-limiter.html","p":"Design a Rate Limiter","i":"step5-hld","h":"High-level design","t":"Step 5 of 6 — High-Level Design High-level design Full system: gateway, rate-limit middleware, the Redis counter tier, and the cold config path together. Clients API Gateway Rate Limit MW stateless, x50 Redis Cluster counters + buckets Backend Svc horizontally scaled Postgres rule config Metrics/Alerting deny-rate, hot keys Middleware emits async deny/allow counters → Metrics Clients / gateway / middleware Redis — the shared source of truth for counts Backend services + Postgres rule config Metrics/alerting — decoupled from the decision path Component Role Tech + why Rate Limit MW Computes the key, calls Redis, returns allow/deny In-process middleware or sidecar — because an extra network hop per request would double the latency budget; tradeoff is it must be redeployed alongside the gateway. Redis Cluster Atomic shared counters across all instances Single-threaded command execution — because it makes INCR and Lua scripts atomic with zero app-level locking; tradeoff is Redis itself becomes a critical dependency for every request. Postgres Durable rule configuration Relational store — because rules are low-volume, ops-authored, and need integrity; tradeoff is it's never queried per-request, only polled every ~30s into an in-memory cache. Metrics/Alerting Tracks deny rates and hot keys Async counters shipped off the request path — because computing dashboards synchronously would add latency to a system whose entire job is being fast; tradeoff is metrics lag reality by a few seconds. Scaling levers — cheapest to most complex Cache rule config in-memory on each middleware node , refresh every 30s near-zero complexity Batch the metrics/alerting counters instead of firing one event per request low complexity Shard Redis by key hash across cluster nodes as counter volume grows medium complexity Move to a local+global two-tier limiter — approximate locally, sync to Redis periodically medium-high complexity Add per-region Redis clusters with async replication for multi-region deployments high complexity — last resort Bottleneck Triggers at Exact fix Redis single-node saturation >100k ops/sec on one node Move to Redis Cluster, shard counters by scope+identifier hash Hot key contention One identifier (e.g. a shared API key) dominates a shard Add a client-local pre-check cache with short TTL to absorb repeat hits before they reach Redis Redis unavailable Cluster failover or network partition Fail open for low-risk endpoints, fail closed for sensitive ones — decide per-endpoint up front, never a global default Algorithm choice: burst tolerance vs precision Token bucket (bursty, smooth) Sliding window log (precise, costly) Redis outage behavior Fail open (available) Fail closed (protective) All inbound API traffic TLS termination auth routing to N backend instances Runs as gateway plugin or sidecar not a separate network hop Cluster mode — sharded by key hash replicated per shard Forwarded only when the limiter allows Cold config source of truth for limits per scope Async batched — never blocks the allow/deny decision Emits deny events for monitoring dashboards"},{"u":"topics/questions/rate-limiter.html","p":"Design a Rate Limiter","i":"step6-deepdive-tokenbucket","h":"Deep dive: token bucket algorithm","t":"Step 6 of 6 — Deep Dives Deep dive: token bucket algorithm Tokens refill continuously into a fixed-capacity bucket; each request consumes one. This is what allows a client to burst up to the bucket size, then throttles to the steady refill rate. refill @ 100/60s = ~1.67/sec bucket: 65/100 tokens capacity 100 request consumes 1 Request cost: 1 token ▸ Replay consume Lua script runs atomically in Redis — reads tokens and last_refill_ts from the hash in one round trip Compute elapsed time — now - last_refill_ts , multiplied by the refill rate (e.g. 1.67 tokens/sec for 100/min) Add refilled tokens, capped at bucket capacity — a bucket never exceeds its max, so idle time doesn't grant unlimited future bursts Check if tokens >= cost — if yes, subtract and allow; if no, deny and return time-until-next-token as Retry-After Write back tokens and timestamp — same Lua script, same round trip, no separate write call Memory cost: one Redis hash per active identifier, ~100 bytes each. At 10M active users that's ~1GB — trivial for a Redis cluster, and idle users' keys just expire via TTL. Request arrives checks tokens >= 1"},{"u":"topics/questions/rate-limiter.html","p":"Design a Rate Limiter","i":"step6-deepdive-window","h":"Deep dive: fixed window boundary burst vs sliding window","t":"Deep dive: fixed window boundary burst vs sliding window Fixed windows are cheap (one INCR per key) but let a client burst up to 2x the limit right at the window edge. Sliding window log fixes it at the cost of storing a timestamp per request. ✗ Fixed window — boundary burst window N (0:00-1:00) window N+1 (1:00-2:00) 100 reqs at 0:59 + 100 reqs at 1:01 = 200 in 2 seconds, both windows say \"allowed\" ✓ Sliding window log — smooth rolling 60s window, evaluated per-request count = requests with ts in [now-60s, now] rate is exact at every instant, no edge to exploit One Redis sorted set per identifier, score = request timestamp. ZREMRANGEBYSCORE drops entries older than the window. ZCARD counts survivors; ZADD records this request. All three in one Lua script for atomicity. Cost: O(log n) per op, one entry per request. Fixed window is a single integer — real memory tradeoff. Scenario User impact Detection signal Mitigation Redis cluster unavailable Every request blocked on a dependency that's down Limiter error rate spikes, Redis health check failing Fail open on low-risk read endpoints, fail closed on write/payment endpoints — decided per-route in config, not improvised at incident time Hot key from a shared API key or NAT'd IP One Redis shard gets disproportionate load, adds latency for unrelated keys on that shard Per-shard ops/sec imbalance in Redis metrics Add a short-TTL local cache in the middleware to absorb bursts before they reach Redis Clock skew between limiter nodes Token bucket refill math drifts, inconsistent allow/deny across nodes NTP drift alert; anomalous allow-rate variance per node Use Redis server TIME command inside the Lua script instead of each node's local clock Rule config propagation lag A newly lowered limit isn't enforced for up to 30s after the change Admin audit log timestamp vs enforcement timestamp gap Push-based invalidation (pub/sub) instead of pure polling for urgent limit changes Sliding window log memory growth High-limit identifiers (e.g. 100k/min) blow up sorted-set size Redis memory usage per key alert Switch high-volume scopes to sliding window counter (approximate) instead of the exact log variant"},{"u":"topics/questions/rate-limiter.html","p":"Design a Rate Limiter","i":"interview-moves","h":"Interview moves","t":"Interview moves 01 \"The first thing that breaks a rate limiter design is per-instance in-memory counters — with N backend nodes you're actually enforcing N times the stated limit\" — names the single most common mistake before being asked 02 \"The check-and-increment has to be one atomic operation, not a read followed by a write, or two concurrent requests both slip through under the limit\" — shows you understand the race condition, not just the happy path 03 \"I'd use token bucket for a public-facing API since it tolerates legitimate bursts, but sliding window log for something like a login endpoint where boundary bursts are a security problem\" — ties the algorithm choice to a concrete requirement, not a default 04 \"When Redis is down, failing open vs failing closed isn't one global answer — I'd fail open on read traffic and fail closed on anything that touches money or auth\" — demonstrates the failure mode isn't binary, it's per-endpoint risk 05 \"Rule config lives in Postgres but is polled into an in-memory cache every 30 seconds — the hot path never makes a database call, only Redis\" — separates cold config from hot enforcement unprompted 06 \"I'd put the rate limit check in the request path but everything downstream of the decision — metrics, alerting — goes through an async pipeline so it never adds latency\" — reuses the hot-path/side-effect isolation pattern that shows up across every well-designed system"},{"u":"topics/questions/rate-limiter.html","p":"Design a Rate Limiter","i":"follow-ups","h":"Follow-up questions","t":"Follow-up questions Why not just use a local in-memory counter per instance with sticky sessions? It works only if every request from a given user always lands on the same instance, which load balancers don't guarantee cheaply — and it silently breaks the moment you autoscale or a node restarts. How do you rate limit by IP when clients sit behind a shared NAT or corporate proxy? Pure IP-based limiting punishes everyone behind that IP. Prefer API-key or user-ID scoping where possible, and treat IP-based limits as a coarser, higher-threshold safety net layered on top. How would you support per-endpoint limits without a rule lookup on every request? Bake the endpoint into the counter key itself (e.g. rl:user:4471:POST:/orders) and load all rules for a scope into the middleware's in-memory cache at startup/refresh, not per-request. What's the tradeoff between fixed window, sliding window log, and sliding window counter? Fixed window: cheapest, allows boundary bursts. Sliding log: exact, but O(n) memory per identifier. Sliding counter: approximates the log using two fixed windows weighted by time — good middle ground. How do you rate limit across multiple regions with regional Redis clusters? Either accept eventual consistency with async cross-region replication (limit is approximate but latency stays low), or route all counting for a given identifier to one home region at the cost of extra hops. How would you let a client know how close they are to their limit before being denied? Return X-RateLimit-Limit, X-RateLimit-Remaining, and X-RateLimit-Reset headers on every response, not just on the 429 — lets well-behaved clients self-throttle proactively."},{"u":"topics/questions/robinhood.html","p":"Design Robinhood (Trade Execution)","i":"","h":"Overview","t":"Questions Design Robinhood (Trade Execution) Tests whether you treat trade execution as a financial-correctness problem first and a scale problem second — a network retry must never turn into a duplicate buy or sell. 45-55 min interview Hard Concept Map click to expand Say this in the first 2 minutes The core insight: order execution must be idempotent and exactly-once — a client retry after a timeout must never re-execute the same trade Key scale number to anchor on: market open/close creates a ~100x QPS spike in seconds, not minutes — this is a burst problem, not a steady-growth problem Portfolio reads outnumber writes roughly 1000:1, so the read path (balance/positions) and the write path (order placement) need completely different scaling strategies Component most candidates get wrong: account balance must never be a single mutable column — it has to be derived from an append-only ledger or concurrent updates silently lose money Failure mode most candidates skip: an exchange execution confirmation that's delayed or lost leaves an order stuck in \"submitted\" — you need a reconciliation job, not just hope Robinhood Order Service Validates and idempotency-checks every incoming order step6-deepdive-idempotency Idempotency key A retry never double-executes a trade Validation first Rejects bad orders before they reach an exchange Order Router Sends validated orders to the right exchange step5-hld Exchange routing Picks the venue for execution Exec confirmation Waits for the fill to come back Ledger DB Append-only record of every balance-changing event step6-deepdive-ledger Append-only Never mutates a historical entry Source of truth Portfolio state is derived from it Portfolio Service Computes current holdings and buying power step2-entities Derived state Rebuildable from the ledger Portfolio Cache Serves fast reads to the app Market Data Feed Streams live prices the trigger for order bursts step6-deepdive-marketburst Bursty load Volatility spikes order volume suddenly Backpressure plan System must not fall over under a burst"},{"u":"topics/questions/robinhood.html","p":"Design Robinhood (Trade Execution)","i":"step1-requirements","h":"Requirements","t":"Step 1 of 6 — Requirements Requirements 1 2 3 4 5 6 Arc 1 (steps 1-5) — Satisfy Functional Requirements 1 2 3 4 5 6 Arc 2 (all 6 steps) — Satisfy Non-Functional Requirements Functional User submits a market or limit order to buy/sell a security Order is validated (buying power), routed to an exchange, and executed User can view current portfolio: positions, cost basis, buying power User sees live market data (quotes) for symbols they hold or watch Non-Functional Exactly-once order execution — a retried request must never double-execute Handle a ~100x QPS spike at market open/close within seconds, not minutes Portfolio reads vastly outnumber order writes — roughly 1000:1 Order-to-confirmation latency target <500ms end to end under normal load What NOT to build: KYC/account-opening and identity verification flows, options or margin trading mechanics (assume simple cash equities only), and tax-document generation (1099-B, wash-sale tracking). This is order placement, execution, and portfolio state only. 22M active users 1000:1 portfolio reads vs order writes 100x QPS spike at market open/close <500ms order-to-confirmation latency target"},{"u":"topics/questions/robinhood.html","p":"Design Robinhood (Trade Execution)","i":"step2-entities","h":"Core entities","t":"Step 2 of 6 — Core Entities Core entities Every entity here is chosen to make financial correctness structurally hard to violate — not just \"usually correct.\" Order Postgres order_id UUID idempotency_key TEXT, UNIQUE index account_id BIGINT symbol, side, qty, order_type order intent status TEXT — state machine cached_response JSONB, nullable Because a trade is a financial commitment where ACID guarantees and a unique constraint on idempotency_key matter far more than raw write throughput. Tradeoff: needs sharding by account_id at very high order volume. LedgerEntry Postgres account_id BIGINT → Account entry_id UUID, append-only delta DECIMAL — signed reason TEXT — order_fill, deposit, fee created_at immutable, never updated Because balance must never be a single mutable field a concurrent transaction can silently overwrite — every change is a new immutable row, and truth is the sum. Tradeoff: reading a balance means aggregating rows unless it's materialized. Portfolio Redis cache account_id key buying_power derived, Postgres-computed positions materialized view, symbol → qty/cost basis ttl short TTL, ~1-2s Because portfolio reads outnumber writes 1000:1 — a short-TTL cache in front of a Postgres-materialized balance absorbs almost all read traffic without serving stale-for-long data. Tradeoff: a fill can lag the cache by up to one TTL window. Primary key: Order.order_id . Critical index: UNIQUE(idempotency_key) on Order — this single constraint is what makes retried requests safe to replay instead of re-executing. LedgerEntry has no primary mutable field at all by design; it is insert-only."},{"u":"topics/questions/robinhood.html","p":"Design Robinhood (Trade Execution)","i":"step3-api","h":"API design","t":"Step 3 of 6 — API / Interface API design Order placement carries a client-generated idempotency key on every request; market data is a push feed since polling a websocket-worthy stream wastes both round trips and freshness. POST /api/v1/orders Auth: required · Header: Idempotency-Key Request { symbol, side, qty, order_type, limit_price? } Response { order_id, status: \"submitted\" } — same body replayed if Idempotency-Key seen before GET /api/v1/orders/{id}/status Auth: required Request path param: order_id Response { status, filled_qty, avg_fill_price, updated_at } GET /api/v1/portfolio Auth: required Request none — scoped to authenticated account Response { buying_power, positions: [{symbol, qty, cost_basis}] } WEBSOCKET /ws/market-data Auth: required Message in { subscribe: [symbols] } Message out { symbol, price, ts } pushed on every quote change The Idempotency-Key header is mandatory, not optional, on POST /orders — the client generates one UUID per logical order attempt and reuses the exact same key on every retry of that attempt, including after a timeout."},{"u":"topics/questions/robinhood.html","p":"Design Robinhood (Trade Execution)","i":"step4-dataflow","h":"Data flow","t":"Step 4 of 6 — Data Flow Data flow Order submission (blue, low volume, idempotency-checked) and portfolio reads (mint, ~1000x the volume) never touch the same hot path. WRITE PATH — order submission, idempotency-checked Client App Order Service Idempotency Check Postgres READ PATH — portfolio reads, ~1000x write volume Client App Portfolio Cache cache miss / TTL expiry only Postgres Client app — order submission and portfolio view Idempotency check + portfolio cache — the hot-path components Postgres — order, ledger, and derived balance, source of truth Client submits an order with an idempotency key — generated once per attempt, reused verbatim on any retry Order Service validates buying power — rejects immediately if the account can't cover the order Idempotency check runs before execution — a seen key short-circuits straight to the cached response, no re-execution Order and ledger entry commit atomically — both succeed or both fail, never one without the other Portfolio reads almost always hit cache — Postgres is only touched on a cache miss or TTL expiry Generates one Idempotency-Key per order attempt reused on retry Validates buying power routes to exchange UNIQUE(idempotency_key) — replays cached response on retry Order row + ledger entry committed atomically Loads positions and buying power on app open Short-TTL cache absorbs ~99.9% of portfolio reads Balance derived by summing the ledger then materialized"},{"u":"topics/questions/robinhood.html","p":"Design Robinhood (Trade Execution)","i":"step5-hld","h":"High-level design","t":"Step 5 of 6 — High-Level Design High-level design Order flow, exchange confirmation, and market data are three separate pipelines that only meet at the portfolio and audit log. Client App idempotency key/order Order Service idempotency key Order Validation buying power check Order Router routes to venue Exchange external Exec Confirmation Kafka consumer Portfolio Service writes ledger entry Ledger DB Postgres, append-only Portfolio Cache Redis, short TTL Market Data Feed websocket push Audit Log append-only Client app — order entry and portfolio view Portfolio cache — the 1000:1 hot read path Ledger DB, exchange, audit log — durable sources of truth Exec confirmation + market data — decoupled async paths Component Role Tech + why Order Service Idempotency check, order intake Postgres-backed with a UNIQUE constraint on idempotency_key — because a retried request must be provably safe to replay, not just \"probably\" safe. Order Validation Buying power check Reads the materialized balance, not the raw ledger, on the hot path — because summing the full ledger per order would be too slow under burst load. Order Router Routes validated orders to an exchange venue Stateless routing layer — because it should be trivially horizontally scalable ahead of a market-open spike. Exec Confirmation Consumer Applies exchange fills Kafka consumer group — because fills arrive asynchronously and must be processed at-least-once with dedup, not blocked on a synchronous exchange response. Portfolio Service Applies fills to the ledger, derives balance Ledger pattern over Postgres — because a single mutable balance field is unsafe under concurrent writes (see deep dive). Market Data Feed Live quote push WebSocket fan-out — because polling a fast-moving price stream wastes both requests and freshness. Scaling levers — cheapest to most complex Pre-scale infrastructure ahead of known market-open/close times low complexity Add Redis read replicas for the portfolio cache per region low complexity Widen the Kafka consumer group for execution confirmations medium complexity Shard the Order/Ledger DB by account_id medium complexity Move buying-power checks to a fully in-memory precomputed store refreshed on every ledger write high complexity — last resort Bottleneck Triggers at Exact fix Market-open order burst ~100x normal QPS arriving within seconds Scheduled pre-scaling before 9:30am ET, not reactive autoscaling Order DB write contention High concurrent order volume on popular symbols Shard by account_id; ledger inserts avoid row-level lock contention Portfolio cache stampede Mass TTL expiry right after a market-wide price move Jittered TTLs plus request coalescing on cache miss Order execution: exactly-once vs at-least-once Exactly-once At-least-once Portfolio balance freshness: strong vs cached-with-short-TTL Strong Cached, short TTL Submits orders views portfolio receives live quotes Idempotency check on every request before touching state Checks buying power derived from the ledger Sends validated order to the exchange for matching External venue that matches and fills the order Fill events streamed back from the exchange Applies the fill: writes ledger entry updates order status Append-only ledger + materialized balance source of truth Short-TTL derived balance absorbs the 1000:1 read volume Live quotes streamed to clients holding or watching the symbol Immutable record of every order/state transition for compliance"},{"u":"topics/questions/robinhood.html","p":"Design Robinhood (Trade Execution)","i":"step6-deepdive-idempotency","h":"Deep dive: idempotency key flow","t":"Step 6 of 6 — Deep Dives Deep dive: idempotency key flow The client generates the key once per order attempt. A timeout-triggered retry reuses that exact key — the server recognizes it and returns the cached response instead of executing a second trade. Client Order Service 1. First attempt — key K-9f21 sent POST /orders Idempotency-Key: K-9f21 2. Server stores key + begins execution store key, execute trade 3. Response lost / client times out before it arrives 4. Client retries — SAME key K-9f21 POST /orders Idempotency-Key: K-9f21 (identical) 5. Key already seen — cached response replayed, NO re-execution Client / gateway Cache — hot path orders (idempotency columns) Postgres order_id UUID PRIMARY KEY idempotency_key TEXT NOT NULL, UNIQUE INDEX status TEXT — submitted/filled/rejected response_body JSONB — the exact response to replay created_at TIMESTAMPTZ The UNIQUE index on idempotency_key is what makes correctness structural: the second INSERT attempt with the same key fails at the database level, and that failure is the signal to fetch and return response_body instead of executing anything. Client generates one idempotency key per logical order attempt — a UUID created client-side before the first network call Server attempts an INSERT keyed on idempotency_key — the UNIQUE index makes a duplicate insert fail fast, before any exchange interaction Trade executes exactly once — only the winning INSERT proceeds to validation and routing Response is persisted alongside the order — not just returned to the caller, but stored for replay On retry with the same key, the conflict is caught — server fetches the stored response_body and returns it, skipping execution entirely The key must be generated once per user intent and reused across retries of that same intent — if the client mints a new key on every retry, the UNIQUE index provides zero protection and duplicate trades become possible again. Client lane Server lane INSERT with UNIQUE(idempotency_key) — this row is now the lock"},{"u":"topics/questions/robinhood.html","p":"Design Robinhood (Trade Execution)","i":"step6-deepdive-ledger","h":"Deep dive: portfolio consistency via ledger pattern","t":"Deep dive: portfolio consistency via ledger pattern A single mutable balance column is unsafe under concurrency. An append-only ledger with a derived balance is not. NAIVE — mutable balance column (lost update) accounts.balance = 1000 Txn A: read 1000, -200 Txn B: read 1000, -300 UPDATE balance=800, then UPDATE balance=700 — should be 500 Txn A's $200 debit is silently lost — final balance is wrong LEDGER — append-only, derived balance ledger_entries (append-only) INSERT delta=-200 INSERT delta=-300 SUM(delta) = 500 — correct Cache — hot path Database — source of truth Never store balance as a single mutable field — a naive UPDATE accounts SET balance = balance - X under concurrency has a read-then-write gap where two transactions can both read the same stale value Model every change as an append-only ledger entry — a deposit, fee, or trade fill is an INSERT of a signed delta, never an overwrite Derive balance by summing the ledger — correctness follows from arithmetic on immutable rows, not from lock timing Materialize the derived balance for hot-path reads — recompute periodically or on every write and cache it, so buying-power checks don't sum the whole ledger per request Reconcile derived balance against the ledger sum on a schedule — catches drift from a lost materialization write before a user notices The lost-update problem isn't a rare edge case here — every order fill, fee, and deposit is a concurrent write candidate on a busy account, and a single mutable balance column will eventually silently drop one of them. Both transactions read balance = 1000 before either writes Txn A reads 1000 computes 1000-200=800 Txn B reads 1000 too — same stale value — computes 1000-300=700 Whichever UPDATE commits last wins — the other write is silently lost Every change is a new immutable row never an overwrite Both inserts succeed independently — no read-then-write race Both inserts succeed independently — no read-then-write race Balance is always the sum of every entry never overwritten"},{"u":"topics/questions/robinhood.html","p":"Design Robinhood (Trade Execution)","i":"step6-deepdive-marketburst","h":"Deep dive: market-hours burst traffic","t":"Deep dive: market-hours burst traffic Traffic is flat for most of the day, then jumps ~100x within seconds at 9:30am ET — reactive autoscaling reacts on a timescale of minutes, far too slow for a spike that arrives in seconds. QPS time of day flat baseline traffic 9:30am ET market open ~100x baseline within seconds 4:00pm ET close second, smaller spike reactive autoscale still spinning up here Baseline QPS holds steady for most of the trading day — normal reactive autoscaling would be adequately sized here At 9:30am ET, order and quote-subscription volume jumps ~100x in seconds — not over minutes, so metric-triggered autoscaling (CPU/queue-depth thresholds) hasn't even finished evaluating before the spike has already landed Reactive autoscaling's reaction time (minutes to provision + warm up) exceeds the spike's arrival time (seconds) — by the time new capacity is healthy, the worst of the burst has already been served in a degraded state or dropped Pre-scale on a schedule instead — provision extra capacity a fixed window (e.g. 15-30 min) before every known market-open and market-close time, the same way ticket-sale platforms pre-scale ahead of a published on-sale time Scale back down on a schedule after the post-open volume settles — avoids paying for peak capacity all day Autoscaling triggered by observed load is fundamentally reactive — it is the wrong tool for a spike whose timing is known in advance. Market open/close times are published and fixed, so this should be a scheduled pre-scale, not a monitored threshold. Failure mode User impact Detection signal Mitigation Duplicate order on client retry Double buy/sell executed, wrong position and balance Two requests, same intent, different idempotency keys or none at all Mandatory Idempotency-Key header with a UNIQUE DB constraint; client reuses the same key on every retry of one attempt Exchange confirmation delayed or lost Order stuck showing \"submitted\" indefinitely, user unsure if it filled Orders older than expected fill SLA still in \"submitted\" state Reconciliation job periodically cross-checks all \"submitted\" orders against the exchange's order status API and corrects state Portfolio balance drift from a lost ledger write Displayed balance disagrees with the true ledger sum Scheduled diff between materialized balance and freshly summed ledger Periodic reconciliation job recomputes SUM(delta) and alerts (and self-heals the materialized value) on any mismatch"},{"u":"topics/questions/robinhood.html","p":"Design Robinhood (Trade Execution)","i":"interview-moves","h":"Interview moves","t":"Interview moves 01 \"This is a financial-correctness problem before it's a scale problem — the thing I'd design around first is exactly-once order execution\" — states the core insight unprompted 02 \"Every order request carries a client-generated idempotency key with a UNIQUE constraint in the database — that's what makes a retry provably safe instead of just usually safe\" — names the specific, correct mechanism 03 \"Account balance should never be a single mutable column — I'd use an append-only ledger and derive the balance, because a naive UPDATE has a lost-update race under concurrency\" — shows you know why the pattern exists, not just that it exists 04 \"Portfolio reads and order writes are roughly 1000:1 here, so I'd design completely different scaling strategies for each rather than one general solution\" — demonstrates read/write path separation 05 \"Market open at 9:30am ET is a known, scheduled event, not a random spike — I'd pre-scale ahead of it rather than rely on reactive autoscaling, which is too slow for a spike measured in seconds\" — proactively surfaces the burst-traffic problem with a concrete fix 06 \"I'd add a reconciliation job that cross-checks orders stuck in 'submitted' against the exchange, because a lost confirmation shouldn't require a user complaint to surface\" — shows operational maturity beyond the happy path"},{"u":"topics/questions/robinhood.html","p":"Design Robinhood (Trade Execution)","i":"follow-ups","h":"Follow-up questions","t":"Follow-up questions What if two requests arrive with the same idempotency key at the exact same moment? The UNIQUE index on idempotency_key makes the database the arbiter — only one INSERT wins; the other fails and its handler fetches and returns the winner's stored response. How long do you retain idempotency keys? Keep them as long as the order row itself lives, since a legitimate late retry (e.g. after a long network partition) should still resolve safely rather than be treated as a brand-new order. Why not just use optimistic locking on a mutable balance column instead of a ledger? Optimistic locking fixes the race but not the audit trail — a ledger also gives you a full, immutable history for disputes, compliance, and reconciliation, which a single column can never provide. How do you keep the materialized balance in sync with the ledger cheaply? Update it incrementally on every ledger insert within the same transaction, then run a periodic full-sum reconciliation job as a correctness backstop, not the primary mechanism. What if pre-scaling for market open isn't enough for an unusually volatile day? Layer reactive autoscaling on top of the scheduled pre-scale as a secondary defense, and apply load-shedding/backpressure on non-critical reads (e.g. serve slightly stale portfolio data) before ever rejecting order writes. How would you extend this design to support limit orders that don't fill immediately? The order stays in a \"working\" state until the exchange sends a fill or cancel event; the same execution-confirmation consumer and reconciliation job handle it, just over a longer, indefinite window."},{"u":"topics/questions/strava.html","p":"Design Strava","i":"","h":"Overview","t":"Questions Design Strava Tests whether you can turn noisy GPS streams into clean segment times and live leaderboards without falling into the trap of treating this as just another CRUD app. 30-40 min interview Medium Concept Map click to expand Say this in the first 2 minutes The core insight: this is a write-heavy time-series + geospatial problem, not a social-feed problem — GPS ingestion and segment matching dominate the design Key scale number to anchor on: a single activity can carry 3,000-10,000 GPS points, and raw tracks are append-only, never updated in place Primary tradeoff being probed: exact segment matching (expensive, precise) vs approximate spatial-index matching (cheap, tolerant of GPS noise) Component most candidates forget: a spatial index (geohash/R-tree) for segment matching — without it, matching degrades to a full scan per activity Failure mode most candidates skip: leaderboard re-rank storms when a popular segment gets thousands of new times in a single morning commute window Strava Upload Svc Accepts the GPS track writes durable storage then enqueues matching step4-dataflow Fast 202 Responds before matching runs Durable first S3 and Postgres both committed Segment Matcher Snaps a noisy GPS track onto known segments using a spatial index step6-deepdive-matching Geohash or R-tree Bounds the candidate search region Curve similarity Frechet distance rejects false matches Cassandra Wide-column store holding ordered segment efforts per partition step2-entities segment_id partition Fastest-N query stays in one partition Source of truth Leaderboard rebuilds from here Redis Leaderboard Sorted set serving live segment rankings under 100ms step6-deepdive-leaderboard ZADD/ZRANK O(log N) writes and rank lookups Rebuildable Regenerated from Cassandra if lost Leaderboard Svc Stateless reader serving top-N and personal rank queries step3-api Read-only No business logic beyond auth Scope param all_time vs following filters Feed Fan-out Async pipeline distributing kudos comments and followed activity step5-hld Decoupled Never blocks the upload response Social layer Sits on top of activity data"},{"u":"topics/questions/strava.html","p":"Design Strava","i":"step1-requirements","h":"Requirements","t":"Step 1 of 6 — Requirements Requirements 1 2 3 4 5 6 Arc 1 (steps 1-5) — Satisfy Functional Requirements 1 2 3 4 5 6 Arc 2 (all 6 steps) — Satisfy Non-Functional Requirements Functional Upload a GPS-tracked activity (run, ride) after recording Match the activity against known segments and compute segment times Maintain per-segment leaderboards (all-time, this-year, following) Show a social feed of followed athletes' recent activities with kudos/comments Non-Functional ~15M activities uploaded/day, each with thousands of GPS points Segment matching completes within seconds of upload, not real-time Leaderboard reads <100ms p99 — read-heavy, tolerates eventual consistency Activity uploads durable and never lost, even if downstream processing lags What NOT to build: live tracking / real-time location sharing during an activity, route planning or turn-by-turn navigation, or a full e-commerce/gear-shop integration. This is upload → match → rank → feed only. ~15M/day activities uploaded ~5-10k GPS points per activity <5s segment match latency, post-upload <100ms leaderboard read p99"},{"u":"topics/questions/strava.html","p":"Design Strava","i":"step2-entities","h":"Core entities","t":"Step 2 of 6 — Core Entities Core entities Four entities across three stores, split by write pattern — activity metadata, raw GPS blobs, matched segment efforts, and leaderboard state each behave differently. Activity Postgres id BIGINT (Snowflake) athlete_id BIGINT → Athlete.id type ENUM(run, ride, swim) distance_m INTEGER gps_blob_key VARCHAR → S3 object key Because metadata is small, structured, and queried relationally (by athlete, by date, by type) — Postgres fits. Tradeoff: the raw GPS payload itself must live elsewhere or this table bloats fast. GpsTrack S3 activity_id object key prefix points compressed polyline (lat, lng, elev, ts) Because a track is an immutable, write-once blob of 5-10k points — object storage is far cheaper than a row-oriented DB for this access pattern. Tradeoff: no partial reads without decompressing the whole blob. SegmentEffort Cassandra segment_id partition key elapsed_time_s clustering key, INT athlete_id BIGINT activity_id BIGINT Because writes are append-only and the read pattern is \"give me the fastest N efforts for this segment\" — a wide-column store with segment_id as partition key makes that a sequential scan within a partition. Tradeoff: cross-segment queries (an athlete's all efforts) need a second table keyed by athlete_id. LeaderboardEntry Redis segment:{id} sorted set key member athlete_id score elapsed_time_s Because leaderboard reads need <100ms p99 and \"top N\" / \"my rank\" are O(log N) operations on a sorted set — Cassandra alone can't serve that fast enough for hot segments. Tradeoff: Redis is a derived cache, so it must be rebuildable from SegmentEffort if lost. Primary key: Activity.id (Snowflake). Critical index: composite (segment_id, elapsed_time_s) on SegmentEffort — every leaderboard rebuild and \"top N\" query depends on this ordering."},{"u":"topics/questions/strava.html","p":"Design Strava","i":"step3-api","h":"API design","t":"Step 3 of 6 — API / Interface API design Four endpoints — upload, fetch activity, segment leaderboard, and the social feed. POST /api/v1/activities Auth: required Request { type, gps_points[], started_at } Response HTTP 202 { activity_id, status: \"processing\" } GET /api/v1/activities/{id} Auth: required (owner or follower) Request path param only Response { distance_m, elapsed_time_s, segment_efforts[], polyline_url } GET /api/v1/segments/{id}/leaderboard Auth: optional Request ?scope=all_time|following&limit=25 Response { entries: [{ rank, athlete_id, elapsed_time_s }] } GET /api/v1/feed Auth: required Request ?cursor=&limit=20 Response { activities: [{ athlete, type, distance_m, kudos_count }], next_cursor }"},{"u":"topics/questions/strava.html","p":"Design Strava","i":"step4-dataflow","h":"Data flow","t":"Step 4 of 6 — Data Flow Data flow Write path (blue) accepts the raw track and returns immediately; the read path (mint) serves leaderboards from Redis; matching happens fully async and never blocks the upload response. READ PATH — leaderboard Client Leaderboard Svc stateless Redis ZSET miss Cassandra WRITE PATH — upload Client Upload Svc S3 Postgres ASYNC PATH — matching + leaderboard, never blocks the upload response Queue → Segment Matcher → ZADD Read path — Redis sorted set first Write path — durable, returns before matching Matching — fully decoupled, seconds not milliseconds Client uploads raw GPS track — Upload Svc validates and mints an activity_id Track written to S3, metadata to Postgres — both durable before responding 202 returned immediately — client sees \"processing,\" never waits on matching Matching job enqueued — spatial index snaps the track against nearby known segments Segment efforts written to Cassandra, then ZADD into Redis — leaderboard updates within seconds, not on the critical path App requesting a segment's leaderboard Stateless API layer no business logic beyond auth ZRANGE top N — sub-5ms this is the hot path Rebuild source if a Redis ZSET is cold or evicted Watch/app finishes recording and uploads the GPS track Validates payload mints activity_id accepts synchronously Compressed polyline stored as an immutable blob Metadata row written 202 returned to client before matching runs Upload Svc enqueues a matching job after the 202 is already sent"},{"u":"topics/questions/strava.html","p":"Design Strava","i":"step5-hld","h":"High-level design","t":"Step 5 of 6 — High-Level Design High-level design Full system: upload, matching, leaderboard, and social feed paths together with every supporting component. Clients API Gateway Upload Svc stateless Leaderboard Svc stateless S3 Postgres Redis Match Queue Segment Matcher geohash/R-tree Cassandra SegmentEffort Feed Fan-out Client / gateway Postgres + S3 + Cassandra — durable stores Redis — leaderboard sorted sets Match queue + matcher + feed fan-out Component Role Tech + why Upload Svc Accepts GPS track, writes S3 + Postgres, enqueues matching Stateless service — because it needs to scale horizontally with upload volume; tradeoff is all state pushed to S3/Postgres, adding a network hop per upload. Segment Matcher Snaps a noisy GPS track onto known segments Geohash/R-tree spatial index — because candidate segments must be found in a bounded region, not a full table scan; tradeoff is index maintenance cost as new segments are created. Cassandra Stores ordered segment efforts, source of truth for rankings Wide-column store partitioned by segment_id — because \"top N for this segment\" is a sequential read within one partition; tradeoff is cross-segment athlete queries need a second table. Redis Serves live leaderboard reads Sorted set (ZSET) — because ZADD/ZRANGE/ZRANK are all O(log N), fast enough for <100ms p99; tradeoff is it's a rebuildable cache, not the durable source. Scaling levers — cheapest to most complex Add Postgres + Cassandra read replicas near-zero complexity Shard Redis leaderboards by segment_id hash low complexity, isolates hot segments Scale matching queue consumers horizontally medium complexity Pre-aggregate \"this year\" / \"this month\" leaderboard windows instead of computing on read medium complexity Geo-partition the spatial index by region as segment count grows high complexity — last resort Bottleneck Triggers at Exact fix Matching queue backlog Consumer lag grows during peak commute hours Auto-scale matcher workers on queue depth; batch spatial index lookups Hot segment leaderboard writes A popular climb gets 1000s of ZADDs in one morning Buffer ZADDs per segment and flush in small batches instead of one write per effort Cassandra partition hotspot One segment_id partition grows far larger than peers Add a time-bucket suffix to segment_id partition key to split by month/year Segment matching: precision vs speed Exact geometric match Approximate spatial-index match Leaderboard consistency Strong Eventual Mobile app / GPS watch sync client Auth rate limiting routing Stateless Stateless serves reads only Immutable compressed polylines Activity + athlete metadata ZRANGE top N sub-5ms hot path Upload Svc enqueues a matching job after responding Spatial index lookup + geometric snapping Ordered efforts per segment partition key = segment_id Kudos comments followed-athlete activity stream"},{"u":"topics/questions/strava.html","p":"Design Strava","i":"step6-deepdive-matching","h":"Deep dive: segment matching algorithm","t":"Step 6 of 6 — Deep Dives Deep dive: segment matching algorithm Raw GPS points are noisy — matching snaps them onto a canonical segment path using a spatial index instead of comparing against every known segment. RAW GPS TRACK — noisy, ~5-10k points CANDIDATE LOOKUP Spatial Index geohash/R-tree Candidates 2-3 segments SNAPPED RESULT — matched to canonical segment path Frechet-distance check confirms best-fit candidate, rejects the rest Bucket track points into geohash cells — bounds the search to segments physically near the route Query spatial index for candidate segments — returns a handful, not every segment in the system Run a curve-similarity check per candidate — Frechet distance (or similar) scores how closely the noisy track follows each candidate's canonical path Accept the best-fit match above a distance threshold — reject candidates that don't clear it, avoiding false positives on parallel roads Compute elapsed time from the snapped entry/exit points — not the raw first/last GPS timestamps, which can be off by a few seconds GPS drift near tunnels, tree cover, or urban canyons is the real failure mode: a track can appear to cut through a building. The curve-similarity threshold exists specifically to reject a false match rather than award an impossible segment time. Geohash cells the track passes through are queried for nearby segments 2-3 candidate segments near this bounding box"},{"u":"topics/questions/strava.html","p":"Design Strava","i":"step6-deepdive-leaderboard","h":"Deep dive: leaderboard update flow","t":"Deep dive: leaderboard update flow Once a segment effort is computed, the leaderboard must re-rank without ever making the athlete wait on it. Segment Matcher effort computed Cassandra SegmentEffort Redis ZADD O(log N) Rank Updated ZRANK O(log N) Notify if top-10 Cache — hot path Database — source of truth Async — queue / worker Matcher computes elapsed_time_s for the segment — happens seconds after upload, fully async Write to Cassandra first — durable source of truth, survives a Redis restart ZADD into the segment's Redis sorted set — O(log N), the set stays sorted automatically, no batch re-rank job needed ZRANK gives the athlete's live position — same O(log N) cost whether the set has 100 or 100,000 entries Conditional notification fires — only if the new rank cracks the top 10, avoiding notification spam on low-traffic segments Scenario User impact Detection signal Mitigation Redis leaderboard node down Leaderboard reads fail or return stale data ZRANGE error rate spike Rebuild the sorted set from Cassandra on the fly; serve from a replica in the meantime Matching queue backlog during peak hours Segment times appear minutes late, not seconds Consumer lag alert on match queue Auto-scale matcher workers; surface \"processing\" status in the app instead of failing silently False segment match from GPS drift Athlete awarded an impossible time (e.g. tunnel shortcut) Elapsed time far below physically possible pace for that segment Reject matches below a minimum-plausible-speed threshold; flag for manual review Hot segment write storm Redis CPU spikes, other segments' reads slow down Per-key command rate on segment:{id} exceeds baseline 10x Buffer and batch ZADDs per segment instead of one write per finished activity Cassandra partition growth on a legendary segment Leaderboard rebuild for that segment gets slow Partition size metric crosses configured threshold Add a time-bucket suffix to the partition key to split by year Matcher confirms this activity completed a known segment Durable write source of truth for this effort ZADD segment: id elapsed_time_s athlete_id Sorted set auto-reorders — no separate re-rank pass needed Only fires if the athlete's new rank crosses a notable threshold"},{"u":"topics/questions/strava.html","p":"Design Strava","i":"interview-moves","h":"Interview moves","t":"Interview moves 01 \"This is really a write-heavy time-series and geospatial problem wearing a social-app costume — I'll design the ingestion and matching pipeline first\" — reframes the problem correctly before diving into CRUD 02 \"I'll return a 202 the instant the track is durably stored, and run segment matching fully async — the athlete shouldn't wait seconds for their upload to complete\" — separates the durable write from the expensive computation 03 \"Raw GPS is noisy, so matching isn't a database lookup — it's a spatial index query followed by a curve-similarity check to reject false positives\" — shows awareness that this isn't a simple point-lookup problem 04 \"Redis sorted sets give me ZADD and ZRANK in O(log N), so I never need a separate batch re-rank job — the leaderboard is always sorted\" — picks the right data structure for the access pattern, not just \"cache it\" 05 \"A legendary climb near a major city could get thousands of efforts in one morning — I'd batch ZADDs per segment rather than writing on every single finish\" — anticipates a hot-key problem unprompted 06 \"Cassandra stays the source of truth and Redis is a rebuildable cache — if I lose the sorted set, I can regenerate it from segment_id partition scans\" — states a concrete recovery story instead of assuming the cache never fails"},{"u":"topics/questions/strava.html","p":"Design Strava","i":"follow-ups","h":"Follow-up questions","t":"Follow-up questions How do you handle an athlete uploading a manually-edited or spoofed GPS file? Apply plausibility checks (max speed for activity type, elevation consistency) at matching time and flag outliers for review rather than blocking the upload outright. Why Cassandra for segment efforts instead of Postgres? Writes are append-only and reads are \"ordered scan within one partition\" — Cassandra's partition model fits that natively without the row-lock contention a relational table would see at this write volume. How would you support \"following\" leaderboards, not just all-time? Maintain a separate Redis sorted set per (segment_id, athlete's follow-graph) is too expensive — instead fetch the all-time top N, filter client-side against the follow list, and fall back to a targeted ZSCORE lookup for followed athletes not in that page. What happens if two athletes finish a segment with the exact same elapsed time? Redis ZSET breaks ties lexicographically by member, which is arbitrary — add a secondary tiebreaker (earlier activity timestamp wins) enforced at write time before the ZADD. How do you keep the spatial index from growing unbounded as new segments are created? Geo-partition the index by region/city so a lookup only touches the local shard, and archive segments with near-zero traffic out of the hot index into cold storage. How would you scale this to 10x the daily upload volume? Shard the match queue and Cassandra by segment_id hash, add more matcher workers scaled on queue depth, and pre-bucket hot segments' Redis keys across multiple nodes."},{"u":"topics/questions/ticketmaster.html","p":"Design Ticketmaster","i":"","h":"Overview","t":"Questions Design Ticketmaster Tests whether you can solve distributed locking under extreme write contention — millions of people fighting over the same 20,000 seats in the same 60 seconds. 40-50 min interview Hard Concept Map click to expand Say this in the first 2 minutes The core insight: this isn't a CRUD problem, it's a distributed locking problem — the hard part is preventing two people from both \"successfully\" reserving the same seat Key scale number to anchor on: a single stadium on-sale can see 3-5M waiting-room requests in the first 60 seconds for 20,000 seats — a 200:1+ contention ratio Primary tradeoff being probed: pessimistic locking (simple, safe, contends hard) vs optimistic locking (scales better, needs conflict retry UX) Component most candidates forget: a TTL-based hold expiry — if a lock never releases on abandoned checkout, seats die forever Failure mode most candidates skip: double-booking during a lock-service failover — must be architecturally impossible, not just unlikely Ticketmaster Waiting Room Token-bucket queue that admits clients at a controlled rate step5-hld Thundering herd Absorbs 3-5M requests before they reach Redis Admit rate Tuned to match measured downstream capacity Seat Map Svc Stateless read service that merges Postgres rows with Redis lock state step4-dataflow Read-only merge Durable status plus live hold state Stateless Scales horizontally with no coordination Reservation Svc Orchestrates lock acquire payment and confirm step6-deepdive-locking Idempotent ops Can restart mid-flow without losing lock state Fixed lock order Sorts seat_ids to avoid deadlock Redis Cluster Holds the distributed per-seat lock using atomic SETNX with a TTL step6-deepdive-holdstate SETNX plus TTL Atomic claim and self-cleanup in one op Hard dependency Needs Sentinel or Cluster HA on the write path Postgres Durable seat status and reservation records with ACID guarantees step2-entities Off hot path Only written to on confirm never during hold Audit trail Supports refunds and disputes Payment Gateway External processor charged only after a lock is confirmed held step5-hld PCI compliance Not worth building in-house Retry-safe webhook Confirm cannot double-charge on retry"},{"u":"topics/questions/ticketmaster.html","p":"Design Ticketmaster","i":"step1-requirements","h":"Requirements","t":"Step 1 of 6 — Requirements Requirements Functional Browse events, view an interactive seat map with live availability Hold a seat (or group of seats) for a short checkout window Complete payment and convert a hold into a confirmed reservation Release a hold automatically if checkout is abandoned or times out Non-Functional Zero double-booking — the seat map's correctness beats every other property Survive a thundering-herd spike: 3-5M requests/60s for one on-sale event Seat map view stays <200ms p99 even under peak load Hold-to-confirm consistency is strong; browse-availability can be a few seconds stale What NOT to build: dynamic/surge pricing engines, a resale/secondary marketplace, venue seating-chart design tools, or a recommendation feed. This is seat inventory + locking + checkout only. 3-5M requests in first 60s of a hot on-sale 20k seats in a large stadium event 8 min hold TTL before auto-release 250:1 peak demand-to-inventory ratio"},{"u":"topics/questions/ticketmaster.html","p":"Design Ticketmaster","i":"step2-entities","h":"Core entities","t":"Step 2 of 6 — Core Entities Core entities Event Postgres id BIGINT venue_id BIGINT → Venue.id on_sale_at TIMESTAMP status ENUM(scheduled, on_sale, sold_out) Because event metadata is low-write, read-heavy, and needs relational joins to venue/seat-map data. Tradeoff: not built for the write throughput of seat state itself, which is why that lives elsewhere. Seat Postgres id BIGINT event_id BIGINT → Event.id section, row, seat_num VARCHAR status ENUM(available, held, reserved) price_cents INT Because status is the durable source of truth and must survive a Redis failure — but every hot check/set during the on-sale rush hits Redis first, never this table directly. Tradeoff: two systems to keep in sync. SeatLock Redis seat_id key hold_token UUID value ttl 480s expiry Because a SETNX-with-TTL is a single atomic operation that both claims the seat and guarantees self-cleanup — no cron job needed to sweep abandoned holds. Tradeoff: Redis becomes a hard dependency on the write path, so it must be highly available, not just fast. Reservation Postgres id BIGINT user_id BIGINT seat_ids BIGINT[] payment_status ENUM(pending, paid, failed) Because a confirmed purchase needs ACID guarantees and an audit trail (refunds, disputes, support). Tradeoff: writes here only happen after payment succeeds, so it's never on the hot contention path. Primary key: Seat.id . Critical index: UNIQUE INDEX on (event_id, section, row, seat_num) — the seat map render is a range scan on this index, and it must stay a covering index so it never touches the row-level lock state."},{"u":"topics/questions/ticketmaster.html","p":"Design Ticketmaster","i":"step3-api","h":"API design","t":"Step 3 of 6 — API / Interface API design GET /api/v1/events/{event_id}/seats Auth: required Request path param only Response [{ seat_id, section, row, seat_num, status, price_cents }] POST /api/v1/seats/hold Auth: required Request { event_id, seat_ids[] } Response { hold_token, expires_at } or 409 Conflict if any seat unavailable POST /api/v1/reservations/confirm Auth: required Request { hold_token, payment_method_id } Response { reservation_id, status } or 410 Gone if hold expired DELETE /api/v1/seats/hold/{hold_token} Auth: required Request path param only Response HTTP 204, seats immediately return to available"},{"u":"topics/questions/ticketmaster.html","p":"Design Ticketmaster","i":"step4-dataflow","h":"Data flow","t":"Step 4 of 6 — Data Flow Data flow READ PATH — seat map view Client Seat Map Svc stateless Redis lock state Postgres WRITE PATH — hold a seat Client Reservation Svc Redis SETNX + TTL on confirm Postgres ASYNC — abandoned holds self-expire, no worker needed Redis TTL expiry → seat free Read path — merges durable state with live locks Write path — atomic lock acquire is the whole game TTL expiry — self-healing, no cleanup job required Client requests a hold on N seats — one API call, all-or-nothing Reservation Svc attempts SETNX on every seat_id — atomic per-seat, in a fixed lock order to avoid deadlock Any seat fails to lock → roll back all acquired locks — returns 409, nothing partially held All locks acquired → hold_token + 8-minute TTL returned — checkout page opens with a visible countdown Confirm within TTL → Postgres write + Redis locks deleted — or TTL fires and seats silently return to available Loads the interactive seat map for an event Stateless read service — merges Postgres rows with Redis lock state Fast check of which seats are currently held Base seat rows — layout price durable status Selects seats and requests a checkout hold Validates the request then attempts the lock acquire SET seat_id hold_token NX EX 480 — atomic claim-and-expire Only written to on confirm — hold itself never touches Postgres Redis TTL fires key vanishes seat becomes available again automatically"},{"u":"topics/questions/ticketmaster.html","p":"Design Ticketmaster","i":"step5-hld","h":"High-level design","t":"Step 5 of 6 — High-Level Design High-level design Clients Waiting Room token bucket admit API Gateway Seat Map Svc stateless Reservation Svc stateless Redis Cluster Postgres Payment Svc Payment Gateway Notification Queue Clients + gateway Waiting Room, Payment, Notifications Redis — the distributed lock Postgres — durable state Component Role Tech + why Waiting Room Admits clients into the booking flow at a controlled rate Token-bucket admission queue — because 3-5M simultaneous requests would melt any downstream tier regardless of how it's built; tradeoff is added perceived wait time for legitimate users. Redis Cluster Holds the distributed per-seat lock with TTL In-memory KV with atomic SETNX — because the entire correctness guarantee rests on one operation being atomic and sub-millisecond; tradeoff is Redis becomes a hard single point of failure on the write path, so it needs Sentinel/Cluster HA. Reservation Svc Orchestrates lock acquire, payment, and confirm Stateless service, horizontally scaled — because it can be replaced or restarted mid-flow without losing lock state (that lives in Redis, not the process); tradeoff is every operation needs to be idempotent. Postgres Durable seat status and reservation records Relational store with row-level constraints — because a confirmed sale needs ACID guarantees for refunds/audits; tradeoff is it's deliberately kept off the hot contention path. Payment Gateway Charges the card only after locks are confirmed External processor (Stripe-style) — because PCI compliance and fraud detection are not worth building in-house; tradeoff is an external network call sits inside the confirm flow's latency budget. Scaling levers — cheapest to most complex Add Postgres read replicas for seat map browsing near-zero complexity Tighten the waiting-room admit rate to match measured Redis throughput low complexity, biggest lever for a hot on-sale Shard Redis by event_id so one event's contention can't starve others medium complexity Move seat map reads onto a denormalized cache refreshed every 2-3s medium complexity Pre-warm dedicated Redis + service capacity ahead of a known on-sale time high complexity — operational, not architectural Bottleneck Triggers at Exact fix Redis lock contention TRIGGERS AT Lock acquire p99 >50ms Shard by event_id; move to per-event Redis instances for the largest on-sales Thundering herd at on-sale start TRIGGERS AT Request rate spikes 250x baseline in <10s Waiting room admits at a fixed rate; everyone else queues client-side Payment gateway latency TRIGGERS AT Confirm p99 >3s, holds expiring mid-checkout Extend TTL once payment is submitted; async webhook confirms instead of blocking Locking strategy: pessimistic vs optimistic Pessimistic (Redis lock) Optimistic (version check) Seat availability consistency Strong (lock state) Eventual (browse view) Millions of clients hitting refresh at on-sale time Clients Client Browser/mobile app seat map UI Entry point for the thundering herd — millions of simultaneous refreshes at on-sale time. n/a — origin of the request Queues clients admits at a controlled rate matching downstream capacity Waiting Room Queue Token-bucket admission queue 3-5M simultaneous requests would melt any downstream tier regardless of how it's built; tradeoff is added perceived wait time for legitimate users. adds seconds-to-minutes of queue wait at peak ~0ms once admitted Auth rate limiting routing API Gateway Service Auth rate limiting routing Single entry point that authenticates requests and enforces per-user rate limits before they reach any stateful component. ~1-5ms Read-only merges Postgres + Redis Seat Map Svc Service Stateless service horizontally scaled Read-only merge of durable Postgres rows with live Redis lock state — stateless so it scales horizontally without coordination. ~10-30ms p99 Owns the lock acquire/release lifecycle Reservation Svc Service Stateless service horizontally scaled Can be replaced or restarted mid-flow without losing lock state since that lives in Redis not the process; tradeoff is every operation needs to be idempotent. ~5-15ms plus downstream lock/DB calls Distributed lock state — SETNX with TTL per seat Redis Cluster Cache In-memory KV with atomic SETNX The entire correctness guarantee rests on one operation being atomic and sub-millisecond; tradeoff is Redis becomes a hard single point of failure on the write path so it needs Sentinel/Cluster HA. sub-millisecond per SETNX Durable seat status reservations primary + replicas Postgres Database Relational store with row-level constraints A confirmed sale needs ACID guarantees for refunds/audits; tradeoff is it's deliberately kept off the hot contention path. ~5-20ms per write Charges the card only after locks are confirmed held Payment Svc Service Stateless orchestrator over external gateway Only triggers a charge once locks are confirmed held keeping payment off the hot contention path entirely. ~10ms plus gateway round trip External processor — Stripe/Braintree Payment Gateway Service External processor (Stripe-style) PCI compliance and fraud detection are not worth building in-house; tradeoff is an external network call sits inside the confirm flow's latency budget. ~200-800ms round trip Confirmation emails calendar invites — off the critical path Notification Queue Queue Async message queue + worker consumers Confirmation emails and calendar invites are off the critical path so they're queued instead of blocking the confirm response. fire-and-forget consumed within seconds"},{"u":"topics/questions/ticketmaster.html","p":"Design Ticketmaster","i":"step6-deepdive-locking","h":"Deep dive: optimistic vs pessimistic locking","t":"Step 6 of 6 — Deep Dives Deep dive: optimistic vs pessimistic locking ✓ Pessimistic — Redis SETNX lock User A User B Redis lock A wins, B rejected instantly B never sees a stale success — rejected in <5ms ▸ Replay ✗ Optimistic — version-check on write User A User B DB row, version=1 A commits, B's write rejected B did full checkout UX before discovering the conflict ▸ Replay Optimistic locking scales beautifully when contention is rare — but a hot on-sale is the opposite of rare contention. Retrying failed writes at 250:1 demand-to-inventory ratio just means most users pay the full cost of checkout UX before being told \"sorry, taken.\" Pessimistic locking rejects instantly instead, which is why this design defaults to it for the hold step. First request to arrive Arrives microseconds later SETNX is atomic — only one caller can ever win Reads version=1 writes version=2 Also reads version=1 writes version=2 Both read the same version — only the first write wins the second gets a version conflict AFTER doing full work"},{"u":"topics/questions/ticketmaster.html","p":"Design Ticketmaster","i":"step6-deepdive-holdstate","h":"Deep dive: seat hold state machine","t":"Deep dive: seat hold state machine Available SETNX succeeds Held payment succeeds Reserved TTL expires or user cancels Released back to Available — same seat, new lock cycle refund Available / Released — no active Redis key Held — Redis key with live TTL Reserved — durable, Postgres source of truth Available → Held — atomic SETNX in Redis; seat map reflects this within one poll cycle Held → Reserved — only fires after the payment gateway confirms the charge; Postgres write and Redis delete happen in the same request Held → Released — TTL expiry (no action needed) or explicit cancel; either way the Redis key simply stops existing Released → Available — there's no separate storage state for this; absence of a Redis key already means available Reserved → Released — a refund path, gated behind support tooling, not exposed on the hot path at all The dangerous transition is Held → Reserved: if the payment call succeeds but the Postgres write fails (or times out) before the Redis lock is deleted, the seat can appear inconsistently sold. Fix: wrap the Postgres commit and Redis delete in a single idempotent operation keyed by hold_token, and make the payment webhook retry-safe so a retried confirm can't double-charge. Scenario User impact Detection signal Mitigation Redis primary fails mid-lock-acquire In-flight holds may be lost, seats briefly show available incorrectly Redis failover event + hold-acquire error rate spike Sentinel/Cluster promotes a replica in <10s; Reservation Svc retries the SETNX against the new primary Payment succeeds but confirm write fails User charged but seat not reserved Payment webhook count > confirmed reservation count Idempotent confirm keyed by hold_token; reconciliation job matches payments to reservations hourly TTL fires while user is mid-payment-form Seat released, then a race lets someone else grab it before this user submits Confirm attempts against an already-expired hold_token Extend TTL by 90s the moment payment submission begins, before calling the gateway Thundering herd bypasses the waiting room Redis lock-acquire latency spikes, legitimate holds start timing out SETNX p99 latency alert Hard rate limit at the gateway independent of the waiting room; shed load with 503 rather than let Redis degrade Two seats in one order lock in different order across two retries Deadlock between two multi-seat holds competing for overlapping seats Lock-acquire timeout rate rising for multi-seat orders Always acquire locks in a fixed, sorted seat_id order across every request Default state visible on seat map as bookable Redis lock exists with active TTL not yet paid Postgres write committed Redis lock deleted TTL fires or explicit DELETE — no distinct storage state just absence of a lock"},{"u":"topics/questions/ticketmaster.html","p":"Design Ticketmaster","i":"interview-moves","h":"Interview moves","t":"Interview moves 01 \"The core problem here isn't the seat map, it's distributed locking under extreme contention — I want to design the lock first\" — immediately signals you've identified the actual hard problem, not the decoy 02 \"At 250:1 demand-to-inventory, optimistic locking would mean most users discover a conflict after doing real work — I'm going pessimistic with a Redis SETNX\" — justifies a specific tradeoff with a specific number 03 \"Every hold needs a TTL, or one abandoned checkout tab kills a seat forever — this has to be self-healing, not dependent on a cleanup job\" — shows you think about failure modes unprompted 04 \"I'd put a waiting room in front of the booking flow so the thundering herd never even reaches Redis directly\" — demonstrates layered defense, not just a single strong component 05 \"When acquiring locks for multiple seats in one order, I always sort seat_ids first to avoid a classic deadlock between two overlapping multi-seat holds\" — a concrete correctness detail most candidates miss entirely 06 \"The Held → Reserved transition is the one place a bug causes real money damage, so I'd make the Postgres write and Redis delete a single idempotent operation keyed by hold_token\" — pinpoints the exact risky transition and states the fix precisely"},{"u":"topics/questions/ticketmaster.html","p":"Design Ticketmaster","i":"follow-ups","h":"Follow-up questions","t":"Follow-up questions Why not just use a Postgres row lock (SELECT ... FOR UPDATE) instead of Redis? It works at low contention, but holding a transaction open across an 8-minute checkout window would exhaust connection pools instantly at this scale. Redis decouples the lock lifetime from any DB transaction. How do you handle a group of friends trying to sit together? The hold API accepts an array of seat_ids and locks all-or-nothing — if any seat in the group fails to lock, every acquired lock in that request is rolled back immediately. What stops a bot from holding thousands of seats and never checking out? Per-user rate limits on hold requests, CAPTCHA at the waiting-room entry, and a max-seats-per-hold cap (typically 8) enforced server-side before the lock is even attempted. How would you extend this to support 10x the scale (a global on-sale)? Shard Redis by event_id so hot events don't starve others, run per-region waiting rooms, and pre-provision dedicated capacity ahead of any known high-demand on-sale time. How do you keep the seat map view from showing stale availability? Short polling (2-3s) or a lightweight WebSocket push on state change; the view is allowed to be a few seconds stale since the lock itself is the real source of truth at hold time. What happens if the payment gateway times out but may have actually charged the card? Never assume failure on timeout — poll the gateway's idempotency-keyed status endpoint before releasing the hold, so a slow success can't result in a charged-but-unseated customer."},{"u":"topics/questions/tinder.html","p":"Design Tinder","i":"","h":"Overview","t":"Questions Design Tinder Tests whether you treat recommendation as a geospatial + preference filter problem — narrow a city down to a few hundred candidates before any ranking model ever runs. 40-50 min interview Hard Concept Map click to expand Say this in the first 2 minutes The core insight: candidate discovery is geospatial retrieval first, ranking second — you never run a model over an entire city's users Key scale number to anchor on: 75M DAU swiping ~150 times/day each is ~130k swipes/sec at peak, but geo-filtered candidate pools per query are only a few hundred rows Primary tradeoff being probed: precomputed daily candidate batches vs on-demand geo queries — batching wins on read latency, loses on freshness Component most candidates forget: the swipe write path needs idempotency — a retried swipe must never double-count or trigger a duplicate match Failure mode most candidates skip: a match is a two-writer race — both users can swipe right in the same instant, so match detection needs an atomic check, not a read-then-write Tinder Deck Svc Stateless service serving precomputed candidate batches to swipe on step4-dataflow Cache-first Reads the daily candidate batch Geo fallback Falls to live query on a miss Swipe Svc Records swipes and atomically checks for a mutual like step6-deepdive-statemachine Idempotency key Prevents a retried swipe from double-counting Inline match check Reads reverse pair in the same request Candidate Cache Redis store of each user daily precomputed candidate batch step6-deepdive-geohash Nightly rebuild Batch job refreshes it once a day Fast reads Low tens of ms per deck fetch Redis GEO In-memory geospatial index answering radius queries in milliseconds step6-deepdive-geohash GEOADD Places a user by lat and lng Ring expansion Widens search until enough candidates found Cassandra Append-only wide-column store for the massive swipe write volume step2-entities Partition key swiper_id spreads writes evenly 130k writes per sec Peak swipe throughput target Match DB Postgres table enforcing exactly-once match creation on a sorted pair step6-deepdive-statemachine Unique constraint Sorted user pair blocks duplicates Race-safe Losing insert treated as a success"},{"u":"topics/questions/tinder.html","p":"Design Tinder","i":"step1-requirements","h":"Requirements","t":"Step 1 of 6 — Requirements Requirements 1 2 3 4 5 6 Arc 1 (steps 1-5) — Satisfy Functional Requirements 1 2 3 4 5 6 Arc 2 (all 6 steps) — Satisfy Non-Functional Requirements Functional Build and edit a profile (photos, bio, preferences, location) Fetch a deck of nearby candidate profiles to swipe on Record swipe (like/pass) and detect mutual likes as a match Notify both users in real time when a match occurs Non-Functional 75M DAU, ~150 swipes/user/day → ~130k swipes/sec at peak Deck fetch latency <200ms p99, feels instant while swiping Never show the same profile twice in one \"seen\" window Match detection must be exactly-once — no missed or duplicate matches What NOT to build: the deep learning ranking model internals, in-app chat/messaging infra, payments for premium tiers, or content moderation/photo-verification pipelines. This is candidate discovery, swiping, and match detection only. 75M daily active users ~130k/s swipe writes/sec (peak) <200ms deck fetch latency target (p99) 6 chars geohash precision (~1.2km cell)"},{"u":"topics/questions/tinder.html","p":"Design Tinder","i":"step2-entities","h":"Core entities","t":"Step 2 of 6 — Core Entities Core entities Profile data is relational, location is geospatial, swipes are an append-heavy log — three access patterns, three stores. Profile Postgres user_id BIGINT name, age, bio TEXT / SMALLINT photo_urls TEXT[] → S3 keys pref_age_min/max, pref_gender SMALLINT / ENUM Because profile edits are low-frequency but must be strongly consistent — a user's own view of their profile can never look stale. Tradeoff: not built for the read-heavy deck-serving path, so it's replicated into the candidate index instead of queried live. UserLocation Redis GEO user_id member key lng, lat GEOADD coordinates ttl ~24h, refreshed on app open Because candidate discovery is fundamentally a radius query and Redis GEO answers it in low single-digit milliseconds against millions of members. Tradeoff: a node failure loses recent position updates, acceptable since location is re-pushed on next app open. Swipe Cassandra swiper_id partition key swipee_id clustering key action ENUM(like, pass, superlike) ts TIMESTAMP Because writes are massive (130k/sec) and append-only with a simple key-value access pattern — exactly what a wide-column store is optimized for. Tradeoff: cross-partition queries (e.g. \"who liked me\") need a second table keyed by swipee_id. Match Postgres match_id UUID user_a_id, user_b_id BIGINT, sorted pair matched_at TIMESTAMP Because a match is a low-volume, high-value event that must never be created twice for the same pair — a unique constraint on the sorted (user_a_id, user_b_id) pair enforces exactly-once at the DB level. Tradeoff: an extra write on the already-hot swipe path, mitigated by only triggering it on a detected mutual like. Primary key: Swipe(swiper_id, swipee_id) composite. Critical index: UNIQUE INDEX on Match(user_a_id, user_b_id) sorted-pair constraint — this is what makes match creation idempotent under a concurrent double-like race."},{"u":"topics/questions/tinder.html","p":"Design Tinder","i":"step3-api","h":"API design","t":"Step 3 of 6 — API / Interface API design Deck fetch and swipe are REST; match notifications push over a persistent connection since a match must reach both users instantly. GET /api/v1/deck Auth: required Request { lat, lng, count } (defaults to cached daily batch) Response { profiles: [{ user_id, name, age, photos, distance_km }] } POST /api/v1/swipes Auth: required Request { swipee_id, action: like|pass|superlike, idempotency_key } Response { matched: boolean, match_id? } POST /api/v1/profile/location Auth: required Request { lat, lng } Response 204, refreshes Redis GEO TTL WEBSOCKET /ws/matches Auth: required Message out { event: \"new_match\", match_id, other_user } Cadence pushed the instant Match Svc commits a row The swipe endpoint requires an idempotency_key : a mobile client on a flaky connection will retry a POST it thinks failed, and a retried \"like\" must never be double-counted or trigger a phantom second match check."},{"u":"topics/questions/tinder.html","p":"Design Tinder","i":"step4-dataflow","h":"Data flow","t":"Step 4 of 6 — Data Flow Data flow Deck read path (mint) pulls from a precomputed candidate cache; swipe write path (blue) records the action and checks for a match inline. READ PATH — deck fetch Client App Deck Svc stateless Candidate Cache Redis miss Redis GEO WRITE PATH — swipe + match check Client App Swipe Svc Cassandra Match Svc → push Deck read — precomputed batch first, live geo fallback Swipe write — logged then checked for a match inline Match — only fires on a detected mutual like Client requests a deck — Deck Svc first checks the precomputed candidate cache for this user Cache hit (common case) — batch was built overnight by the recommendation pipeline, returned in low tens of ms Cache miss (new/relocated user) — falls through to a live Redis GEO radius query, expanding rings until enough candidates are found Client swipes right — Swipe Svc writes the row to Cassandra, keyed by (swiper_id, swipee_id) Inline match check — Swipe Svc reads for the reverse pair; if it's also a like, Match Svc commits a Match row and pushes to both sockets Requests the next batch of profiles to swipe on Stateless — applies preference filters to a precomputed batch Precomputed daily candidate batch per user refreshed overnight Live radius query expanding geohash rings on cache miss only Sends like/pass with an idempotency key Writes the swipe then atomically checks for a mutual like Append-only swipe log 130k writes/sec at peak Only invoked when the reverse swipe already exists as a like"},{"u":"topics/questions/tinder.html","p":"Design Tinder","i":"step5-hld","h":"High-level design","t":"Step 5 of 6 — High-Level Design High-level design Full system: deck serving, swipe/match, and the offline recommendation pipeline that fills the candidate cache. Client App API Gateway Deck Svc stateless Swipe Svc stateless Candidate Cache Cassandra Redis GEO Match DB Notification Svc Rec Pipeline geo filter → rank Client + gateway Candidate cache — precomputed per-user batches Cassandra + Redis GEO + Match DB Rec pipeline + notifications — decoupled from hot path Component Role Tech + why Deck Svc Serves the next batch of candidates Stateless, reads precomputed cache — because computing ranked candidates per-request at 75M DAU is far too expensive; tradeoff is up to a day of staleness on the ranking. Redis GEO Radius query for candidate discovery In-memory geospatial index — because both the nightly batch job and live cache-miss fallback need sub-10ms proximity queries; tradeoff is no durability per location ping. Swipe Svc Records swipes, detects mutual likes Stateless, idempotent writes keyed by (swiper, swipee) — because a retried request must never double-write or double-match; tradeoff is an extra read before every match-triggering write. Rec Pipeline Builds each user's daily candidate batch Offline batch job — geo-filter first (Redis GEO) to a few thousand rows, then rank with a lightweight model — because running ML inference over an entire city per request would never hit <200ms. Scaling levers — cheapest to most complex Add Redis GEO and Candidate Cache read replicas per region near-zero complexity Shard Cassandra by swiper_id (already partition-friendly) low complexity Increase batch size / precompute frequency for high-density metros medium complexity Shard the Rec Pipeline by geo-region so one metro's batch job never blocks another's medium complexity Dynamic geohash cell-splitting in dense urban cores high complexity — last resort Bottleneck Triggers at Exact fix Candidate cache miss storm Cache hit rate drops below ~95% Widen precompute coverage; serve a slightly stale batch instead of falling through to live query for everyone Dense urban geohash cell One cell has far more users than surrounding cells (e.g. NYC vs suburbs) Finer geohash precision (7-char) in dense cells only, coarser (5-char) in sparse ones Cassandra write hotspot A viral profile gets swiped on far more than average Partition key is swiper_id not swipee_id, so writes are naturally spread — hotspot risk is on the read side (who-liked-me), addressed with a separate denormalized table Candidate freshness: precomputed batch vs live query Precomputed (fast) Live (fresh) Match detection consistency Strong Eventual Deck fetch swipes and match socket all originate here Auth rate limiting routing Serves precomputed batches falls back to live geo query Writes swipes checks for mutual likes Per-user precomputed candidate batch rebuilt nightly Append-only swipe log Live radius index shared by cache-miss and the offline batch job Sorted-pair unique constraint prevents duplicate matches Fans out to both users' open sockets Nightly batch: geo-filter then rank with a lightweight model"},{"u":"topics/questions/tinder.html","p":"Design Tinder","i":"step6-deepdive-geohash","h":"Deep dive: geo-filter before you rank","t":"Step 6 of 6 — Deep Dives Deep dive: geo-filter before you rank A city is divided into geohash cells. Candidate discovery filters by cell first, shrinking the pool to a few hundred rows — only then does a ranking model run. CITY GRID — each cell ≈ 1.2km × 1.2km (6-char geohash) ▸ Simulate user location ▸ Simulate candidate retrieval (expand outward) User location lands in a cell — GPS coordinate encoded to a 6-char geohash, GEOADD places the user in Redis GEO Nightly batch job runs per user — starts from their home cell and expands ring by ring until ~2,000 candidates are collected Hard filters applied first — age range, gender preference, max distance, already-swiped exclusion — all cheap boolean checks on the geo-filtered set Lightweight ranking model scores the survivors — activity recency, mutual-interest signals, photo quality — runs over hundreds, never millions, of rows Top ~150 written to the candidate cache — this is exactly what Deck Svc serves the next day, no per-request ranking cost Running the ranking model before the geo filter is the classic mistake: scoring every user in a metro against every other user is O(n²) and will never fit a nightly batch window, let alone a <200ms request. Geo-filter to a few thousand candidates first — ranking only ever touches that shrunk set."},{"u":"topics/questions/tinder.html","p":"Design Tinder","i":"step6-deepdive-statemachine","h":"Deep dive: swipe state machine and match race","t":"Deep dive: swipe state machine and match race Every profile a user sees moves through seen → liked/passed, and a match only fires when both directions resolve to \"liked.\" Seen in deck, not yet acted on Liked swipe right Passed swipe left, terminal reverse swipe checked Pending Check reverse pair read Matched both liked other side hasn't swiped yet → stays Liked, waits Seen — no write yet, purely a \"don't show again\" flag Matched — only reachable via an atomic reverse-pair check Transition Reversible? Guard condition Seen → Liked / Passed No — a swipe is final for that pair Idempotency key on the swipe row prevents a client retry from double-recording Liked → Pending Check N/A — internal step Swipe Svc reads Cassandra for the (swipee_id, swiper_id) reverse row inline, same request Pending Check → Matched No — terminal Reverse row exists and is \"like\" — Match Svc does a conditional INSERT on the sorted pair, unique constraint rejects a duplicate The match race is the sharpest edge case in this system: both users can swipe right within milliseconds of each other, so two concurrent requests may both detect \"the other side already liked me.\" The unique constraint on the sorted (user_a_id, user_b_id) pair in Postgres is what prevents two Match rows — the losing INSERT fails cleanly and that request just treats it as \"match already exists,\" same outcome delivered to the user. Scenario User impact Detection signal Mitigation Redis GEO node failure Live candidate fallback degrades; precomputed decks unaffected GEO query error rate spike Replica promotion; users re-push location on next app open, self-healing Candidate cache cold for a user First deck fetch falls through to a slower live query Cache miss rate per-user spike on new signups On signup, trigger an on-demand mini batch instead of waiting for the nightly job Simultaneous double-like race Risk of duplicate match notification without a guard Unique constraint violation rate on Match table (expected, monitored) Conditional INSERT on sorted pair; losing writer treats the constraint error as a successful match Cassandra partition hot from a high-swipe-volume user Elevated write latency for that partition Per-partition write latency p99 alert Partition key is swiper_id, which is naturally load-balanced — pathological cases get a daily swipe cap Notification Svc outage Match created but not pushed in real time WebSocket delivery failure rate Match row is durable in Postgres regardless — client polls/reconciles on next app open, no data loss"},{"u":"topics/questions/tinder.html","p":"Design Tinder","i":"interview-moves","h":"Interview moves","t":"Interview moves 01 \"Before I design ranking, let me separate candidate discovery from candidate scoring — geo-filter to a few thousand users first, then only rank that shrunk set\" — states the core insight unprompted, prevents an O(n²) design 02 \"I'd precompute each user's daily candidate batch offline rather than run the recommendation model on every deck request — that's the only way to hit <200ms at 75M DAU\" — justifies a caching decision with a concrete latency number 03 \"A swipe needs an idempotency key — a mobile client retrying a failed request must never double-write or trigger a phantom match check\" — shows awareness of real-world client behavior, not just the happy path 04 \"Match detection is a two-writer race — I'd enforce it with a unique constraint on the sorted user pair rather than a read-then-write check, so it's atomic at the database level\" — names the exact concurrency mechanism, not a vague \"handle it\" 05 \"Swipes and profile edits have wildly different access patterns — 130k writes/sec of append-only swipes go to Cassandra, low-frequency strongly-consistent profile data stays in Postgres\" — demonstrates picking storage per access pattern, not one store for everything 06 \"If the candidate cache is cold for a brand-new signup, I'd trigger an on-demand mini batch rather than make them wait for the next nightly run\" — proactively covers a cold-start edge case most candidates miss"},{"u":"topics/questions/tinder.html","p":"Design Tinder","i":"follow-ups","h":"Follow-up questions","t":"Follow-up questions How do you prevent showing the same profile twice in one session? The candidate batch excludes anyone already in the user's Swipe table; \"seen\" state is also cached client-side for the current session to avoid a re-fetch showing stale entries. How would you handle a user who travels to a new city? Location POST refreshes the Redis GEO key immediately; if the candidate cache has no batch for the new region, trigger an on-demand mini geo-filter job instead of waiting overnight. How do you scale the \"who liked me\" feature given Swipe is keyed by swiper_id? Maintain a second denormalized table keyed by swipee_id, written asynchronously off the same swipe event — trades a bit of write amplification for a fast reverse lookup. How would you rate-limit or throttle abusive swiping behavior? A daily swipe cap enforced at Swipe Svc, backed by a Redis counter with a 24h TTL — cheap to check inline before the write, resets automatically. Why not rank candidates in real time instead of precomputing batches? Real-time ranking over millions of geo-filtered users per request can't hit <200ms at 75M DAU; precomputing trades some freshness for a latency floor the live path can't reach. How do you extend this to support video profiles or stories? Store media in S3/CDN as with photos, keep only URLs in Profile — the deck-serving and matching logic is entirely unaffected since it never touches media bytes directly."},{"u":"topics/questions/uber.html","p":"Design Uber","i":"","h":"Overview","t":"Questions Design Uber Tests whether you split a write-heavy geospatial stream (driver location) from a read-heavy geospatial query (ride matching) — and whether you can reason about ETA and surge pricing as their own deep problems, not afterthoughts. 45-55 min interview Hard Concept Map click to expand Say this in the first 2 minutes The core insight: driver location is a write-heavy geospatial stream, ride matching is a read-heavy geospatial query — different access patterns need different stores and different optimization strategies Key scale number to anchor on: 3M active drivers pinging every ~4s is 750k location writes/sec at peak — an entirely separate hot path from the 5M trips/day it eventually feeds Primary tradeoff being probed: durability vs write speed for driver location — a dropped ping is irrelevant since a fresher one lands in 4s Component most candidates gloss over: ETA is a live composition of routing, traffic, and driver behavior, not a single distance formula — and surge pricing is a separate signal fed by the same demand data Consistency model most candidates get wrong: trip state needs strong consistency (no double-dispatch), but surge pricing is fine with data that's a few seconds stale — don't apply one consistency bar to both Uber Location Svc Ingests a constant write-heavy stream of driver GPS pings step4-dataflow Write-heavy Every driver pings every few seconds Redis GEO In-memory geospatial index for fast lookups Matching Svc Finds nearby available drivers for a ride request step6-deepdive-geohash Geohash grid Buckets drivers into searchable cells Read-heavy A different scaling problem than location writes Trip Service Owns the ride lifecycle as an explicit state machine step6-deepdive-statemachine State machine requested to matched to in-progress to completed Postgres Durable source of truth for trip state ETA Svc Estimates arrival time from live traffic and route data step5-hld Deep dive component Not an afterthought a real subsystem Feeds matching ETA informs which driver gets picked Surge Pricing Adjusts price in real time based on local supply and demand step6-deepdive-surge Kafka Streams demand signals per geo cell Per-region multiplier Computed continuously not per-request"},{"u":"topics/questions/uber.html","p":"Design Uber","i":"step1-requirements","h":"Requirements","t":"Step 1 of 6 — Requirements Requirements Functional Rider requests a trip from their current location to a destination System matches the request to the nearest available driver Track trip state from requested through completed Show a live ETA to both rider and driver Apply a surge multiplier when local demand outstrips supply Non-Functional 3M active drivers citywide-aggregate, location pings every ~4s Match latency target <2s from request to driver offer Trip state transitions are strongly consistent — no double-dispatch, no lost trips Surge pricing can tolerate a few seconds of staleness — it's a signal, not a ledger Survive a single region/AZ outage without losing in-flight trips What NOT to build: driver onboarding or background-check workflows, in-app payment processing internals (assume a payments service exists and is called), or driver earnings/payroll systems. This is trip request, matching, ETA, and surge pricing only. 5M trips/day 3M active drivers 750k/s location writes/sec at peak <2s match latency target"},{"u":"topics/questions/uber.html","p":"Design Uber","i":"step2-entities","h":"Core entities","t":"Step 2 of 6 — Core Entities Core entities Trip Postgres trip_id UUID rider_id BIGINT driver_id BIGINT, nullable status TEXT — state machine surge_multiplier DECIMAL, locked at request version INT — optimistic lock Because a trip is money and legal liability — exactly-once state transitions matter more than raw throughput. The version column prevents two matching attempts from double-assigning the same trip. Tradeoff: needs sharding by city_id at high trip volume. DriverLocation Redis GEO driver_id member key lng, lat GEOADD coordinates status available / en_route / offline ttl ~15s Because positions are ephemeral and hit 750k writes/sec at peak — durability of any single ping is worthless, only the current position and sub-ms radius query speed matter. Tradeoff: a node failure loses recent positions, self-heals within one ~4s ping cycle. SurgeCell Redis geohash_cell 7-char geohash string open_requests rolling counter available_drivers rolling counter multiplier DECIMAL, recomputed ~5s Because demand density per cell is a pricing signal, not a source of truth — a few seconds of staleness is fine and cheap to compute from streaming counters. Tradeoff: rapid demand spikes lag the actual multiplier by one aggregation window. Primary key: Trip.trip_id with a version column for optimistic locking. Critical index: Redis GEO's internal sorted-set on driver coordinates for matching; a separate per-cell counter structure for surge — two different access patterns on the same underlying geohash key space."},{"u":"topics/questions/uber.html","p":"Design Uber","i":"step3-api","h":"API design","t":"Step 3 of 6 — API / Interface API design POST /api/v1/trips/request Auth: required (rider) Request { pickup_geo, dropoff_geo, ride_type } Response { trip_id, status: \"requested\", surge_multiplier, eta_seconds } WEBSOCKET /ws/driver/{id}/location Auth: required (driver) Message in { lat, lng, heading } every ~4s Message out { trip_offer } when matched, 15s accept window POST /api/v1/trips/{id}/transition Auth: required (driver or rider) Request { to_status, version } Response 200 on success, 409 on version conflict GET /api/v1/trips/{id}/eta Auth: required (rider or driver) Response { eta_seconds, distance_m, degraded: bool } Cadence polled every ~5s or pushed on recompute WebSocket for location, REST for everything else: location is a continuous high-frequency push (750k/s in aggregate) where a persistent connection avoids per-ping HTTP overhead across 3M drivers; trip requests and transitions are low-frequency and benefit from REST's explicit request/response and status-code semantics."},{"u":"topics/questions/uber.html","p":"Design Uber","i":"step4-dataflow","h":"Data flow","t":"Step 4 of 6 — Data Flow Data flow WRITE PATH — driver location, ~750k/s peak Driver App Location Svc Redis GEO READ PATH — ride matching Rider App Trip Service Matching Svc Redis GEO Driver/rider apps — persistent WebSocket for location, REST for requests Redis GEO — one index, written by the location path, read by the matching path Driver pings location every ~4s — over the already-open WebSocket connection Location Svc writes to Redis GEO — GEOADD refreshes position and TTL, also streams to Kafka for ETA and surge aggregation Rider requests a trip — Trip Service creates the trip row in \"requested\" state, reads the current surge multiplier Matching Svc queries Redis GEO — finds nearby available drivers by expanding outward from the rider's geohash cell Best candidate is offered the trip — pushed over their WebSocket connection with a 15s accept window Sends a GPS ping every ~4s over a persistent WebSocket Thin ingestion — writes position forwards to Kafka for ETA/surge GEOADD writes current position TTL ~15s Requests a trip from current location to a destination Creates the trip row kicks off matching Expands outward ring by ring from the rider's cell Same GEO index the location write path populates"},{"u":"topics/questions/uber.html","p":"Design Uber","i":"step5-hld","h":"High-level design","t":"Step 5 of 6 — High-Level Design High-level design Rider App Driver App Trip Service state machine Postgres Location Svc Matching Svc Redis GEO Kafka ETA Svc Surge Pricing Notification Svc Rider + driver apps Redis GEO — single geospatial index, shared by matching and surge Postgres — transactional trip state, source of truth Kafka + ETA/Surge/Notification — decoupled from the hot path Component Role Tech + why Trip Service Owns trip state machine Postgres-backed, optimistic locking — because exactly-once transitions matter more than raw throughput; tradeoff is sharding needed at high trip volume. Location Service Ingests driver pings, fans out Stateless WebSocket terminator — writes Redis GEO synchronously, streams to Kafka async so ETA/surge never block the write path. Matching Svc Nearest-driver query Geohash ring expansion over Redis GEO — turns 2D proximity into a cheap prefix lookup; tradeoff is edge effects at cell boundaries. Redis GEO Live driver positions In-memory geospatial index — 750k writes/sec of ephemeral data needs sub-ms radius queries; tradeoff is no durability guarantee per ping. ETA Service Composes distance, traffic, driver load into a live estimate Streaming consumer off Kafka — because inputs update independently and continuously; falls back to a cached estimate when degraded. Surge Pricing Svc Demand-density multiplier per cell Rolling counters in Redis, recomputed every ~5s — a pricing signal can tolerate staleness that trip state cannot. Scaling levers — cheapest to most complex Add Redis GEO read replicas per region low complexity Cache ETA estimates for a few seconds to absorb repeat polling low complexity Shard trips by city_id medium complexity Batch client-side location writes over the 4s interval under network stress medium complexity Dynamic geohash cell-splitting in dense downtown cores high complexity — last resort Bottleneck Triggers at Exact fix Hot geohash cell (downtown rush hour) TRIGGERS AT One dense cell gets far more requests/drivers than others Dynamic cell-splitting — finer geohash precision in dense areas Location write throughput TRIGGERS AT >100k writes/sec regionally Shard Redis GEO by region; batch client-side writes under load Trip DB write contention TRIGGERS AT High trip volume in one city during surge Shard by city_id; optimistic locking avoids row-level blocking Driver location data: durability vs write speed Durable Fast, ephemeral Surge pricing data: strong vs eventual consistency Strong Eventual Requests trips views ETA and fare Rider App Client Mobile client REST + push Entry point for trip requests ETA and fare display — low-frequency actions suited to REST. ~50-150ms per request (network + gateway) Sends location receives trip offers Driver App Client Mobile client persistent WebSocket Continuous high-frequency location stream needs a persistent connection not per-ping HTTP overhead. ~4s ping interval offer push near-instant Owns the trip state machine Trip Service Service Postgres-backed optimistic locking Exactly-once transitions matter more than raw throughput; tradeoff is sharding needed at high trip volume. ~10-30ms per transition (single-row write) ACID-compliant trip records Postgres Database ACID-compliant relational store Trip state is money and legal liability — exactly-once state transitions matter more than raw throughput. ~5-15ms per write (single row) Ingests location writes GEO + streams to Kafka Location Service Service Stateless WebSocket terminator Writes Redis GEO synchronously streams to Kafka async so ETA/surge never block the write path. ~1-5ms to Redis async to Kafka Nearest-driver query expanding geohash rings Matching Service Service Geohash ring expansion over Redis GEO Turns 2D proximity into a cheap prefix lookup; tradeoff is edge effects at cell boundaries. ~5-20ms per query (ring expansion) Live driver positions shared read/write index Redis GEO Cache In-memory geospatial index 750k writes/sec of ephemeral data needs sub-ms radius queries; tradeoff is no durability guarantee per ping. sub-ms per GEOADD/GEORADIUS Every ping streams here for ETA + surge aggregation Kafka Queue Streaming log location + request events Decouples the hot write path from ETA and surge aggregation so neither blocks the location write. ~5-20ms produce async consume Composes distance live traffic driver load into a live ETA ETA Service Service Streaming consumer off Kafka Inputs (distance traffic driver load) update independently and continuously; falls back to a cached estimate when degraded. ~50-100ms live instant on cache fallback Aggregates open-requests / available-drivers per geohash cell Surge Pricing Service Service Rolling counters in Redis recomputed ~5s A pricing signal can tolerate staleness that trip state cannot. ~5s recompute window sub-ms read Pushes trip offers and state changes to both apps Notification Service Queue Async push fan-out to rider/driver apps Decoupled from the hot path so a slow push never blocks trip state transitions or matching. ~100-300ms push delivery"},{"u":"topics/questions/uber.html","p":"Design Uber","i":"step6-deepdive-geohash","h":"Deep dive: geohash grid for ride matching","t":"Step 6 of 6 — Deep Dives Deep dive: geohash grid for ride matching CITY GRID — each cell ≈ 150m × 150m (7-char geohash) ▸ Simulate driver ping ▸ Simulate ride request (expand outward) Driver ping lands in a cell — GPS coordinate encoded to a geohash string, GEOADD places the driver at that key Ride request arrives with its own cell — rider's pickup location encoded the same way Query expands outward ring by ring — own cell first, then the 8 neighbors, then the next ring, until the candidate count is enough Rank candidates — actual road distance and current driver idle time, not just geohash proximity Offer and lock — top candidate gets a 15s accept window; next candidate tried on decline or timeout Geohash precision is a tradeoff, not a constant: 7 characters gives ~150m cells, tight enough for dense urban matching but requiring more ring expansions in sparse suburbs. Fewer characters (coarser cells) reduce ring-expansion cost but blur \"nearby\" into cells that can be a kilometer wide — precision should flex with local driver density, not be fixed citywide."},{"u":"topics/questions/uber.html","p":"Design Uber","i":"step6-deepdive-statemachine","h":"Deep dive: trip state machine","t":"Deep dive: trip state machine Requested trip created Accepted driver assigned En Route Pickup driver→rider Arrived Pickup at rider's location En Route Dest rider onboard Completed terminal Cancelled terminal — only from Requested or Accepted Transition Reversible? Guard condition Requested → Accepted Yes (reverts to Requested on driver decline/timeout) Matching service confirms an available driver within the accept window Requested / Accepted → Cancelled No — terminal Rider or system-initiated cancellation before pickup Accepted → En Route Pickup Yes (can revert to Accepted on GPS jitter, not further back) Driver app confirms navigation started toward rider En Route Pickup → Arrived Pickup No — irreversible Geofence match on driver's location vs pickup point Arrived Pickup → En Route Dest No — irreversible Driver confirms rider onboard (explicit tap, not just proximity) En Route Dest → Completed No — terminal Geofence match on destination plus explicit driver confirmation Once a trip reaches \"Arrived Pickup,\" cancellation is no longer offered to the rider without a fee — the driver has already committed travel time and fuel. This must be enforced at the state machine level, not just hidden in the UI, or a race condition could let a free cancellation slip through after the driver has arrived."},{"u":"topics/questions/uber.html","p":"Design Uber","i":"step6-deepdive-surge","h":"Deep dive: surge pricing pipeline","t":"Deep dive: surge pricing pipeline Kafka location + request events Density Aggregator per-cell counters, ~5s window SurgeCell (Redis) ratio per geohash cell Threshold Tiering e.g. ratio>1.5 → 1.3x Trip Service reads the current multiplier at request time and locks it into the trip row Trip Service Kafka + aggregator — continuous streaming computation SurgeCell in Redis — cheap to read, tolerant of a few seconds of lag Trip Service — the one place the multiplier becomes strongly consistent, frozen at request time Every location ping and ride request streams to Kafka — the same event stream that feeds ETA also feeds surge Density Aggregator maintains rolling counters per cell — open_requests and available_drivers, refreshed on a ~5s window Ratio crosses a threshold — e.g. requests-to-drivers ratio above 1.5 triggers the next pricing tier for that cell Trip Service reads the multiplier at request time — and freezes it into the trip row so the price shown to the rider never changes mid-trip Multiplier decays as supply catches up — the same rolling window that triggered surge naturally relaxes it Why staleness is fine here: surge pricing only needs to be \"roughly right, updated frequently\" — a multiplier computed on data that's 3-5 seconds old still reflects real demand pressure, and the cost of being briefly wrong (slightly over- or under-priced) is far lower than the cost of making every rider request block on a strongly consistent citywide count. Contrast with trip state, where a stale read could double-dispatch a driver — that's why trip transitions use Postgres with optimistic locking while surge uses eventually-consistent rolling counters in Redis. Failure mode Rider/driver impact Detection signal Mitigation Redis GEO index goes stale Driver appears available but has lost connection — offer sent into the void Missed heartbeat — driver's GEO TTL key not refreshed within ~15s TTL expiry auto-removes the driver from the index; offer times out after 15s and re-offers to the next candidate ETA service slow or degraded Trip request flow would stall waiting on a live ETA ETA Svc p99 latency or error rate crosses a circuit-breaker threshold Degraded mode returns a cached/static distance-based estimate immediately; request flow never blocks on ETA Trip stuck mid-state (driver app crash) Rider sees no progress; driver never confirms next transition No state transition or location update for a trip past a timeout window (e.g. 3 min) Automatic timeout flags the trip for ops review; on-call runs a manual resolution SOP — contact both parties, then force-transition to Cancelled or Completed based on evidence Every driver ping and every ride request streams here Rolling window counters keyed by geohash cell open_requests / available_drivers per cell Ratio crosses a threshold → applies a bounded tier Reads and freezes the multiplier for this specific trip"},{"u":"topics/questions/uber.html","p":"Design Uber","i":"interview-moves","h":"Interview moves","t":"Interview moves 01 \"Before I design anything, let me separate driver location from ride matching — one is a write-heavy geospatial stream, the other is a read-heavy geospatial query, and they need different stores\" — states the core insight unprompted 02 \"Finding the nearest driver can't be a full scan across 3M drivers — I'd geohash-index positions so proximity becomes a ring-expansion lookup\" — signals you know the core data structure problem 03 \"Trip state transitions need to be atomic and exactly-once — I'd use optimistic locking with a version column to prevent double-dispatch\" — names a specific, correct mechanism 04 \"ETA isn't one distance calculation — it's a live composition of routing, traffic, and driver load, and it needs a degraded fallback so a slow ETA service never blocks a ride request\" — shows systems thinking over ML hand-waving, and names the failure mode unprompted 05 \"Surge pricing can run on eventually-consistent demand data — a few seconds of staleness in a pricing signal is fine, unlike trip state where staleness causes double-dispatch\" — demonstrates you apply different consistency models to different data on purpose, not by default 06 \"Once a trip hits 'Arrived Pickup,' free cancellation should be blocked at the state machine level, not just hidden in the UI\" — demonstrates invariants hold even under racy client behavior"},{"u":"topics/questions/uber.html","p":"Design Uber","i":"follow-ups","h":"Follow-up questions","t":"Follow-up questions How do you prevent two riders from being matched to the same driver simultaneously? Optimistic locking on the driver assignment — the UPDATE only succeeds if the driver is still unassigned; a losing request immediately retries the next candidate. What happens if a driver's app crashes mid-trip? A timeout on state transitions and location updates flags the trip for ops review; an on-call SOP contacts both parties and force-resolves the trip to Cancelled or Completed based on available evidence. Why is it safe for surge pricing to use stale data when trip state can't be? Surge is a pricing signal where being briefly wrong just means a slightly off multiplier; trip state staleness can cause a double-dispatch, which is a hard correctness violation, not a soft one. How would you detect a driver who appears available but has actually lost connection? A TTL-based heartbeat on the driver's Redis GEO key — if it's not refreshed within ~15s, the key expires and the driver silently drops out of the matching pool. What's the fallback if the ETA service is slow or down? A circuit breaker trips and the request flow returns a cached or static distance-based estimate immediately rather than blocking the rider on a live computation. How would you cap surge so it doesn't feel exploitative? A hard multiplier ceiling per tier, shown transparently to the rider before they confirm, with the multiplier frozen into the trip at request time so it can't change mid-ride."},{"u":"topics/questions/url-shortener.html","p":"Design a URL Shortener","i":"","h":"Overview","t":"Questions Design a URL Shortener Tests whether you can identify the real hard problem — ID generation, not redirect logic — and build a read-heavy system around it. 30-40 min interview Easy Concept Map click to expand Say this in the first 2 minutes The core insight: ID generation is the hard problem here, not the redirect — the redirect is a one-line cache lookup Key scale number to anchor on: reads outnumber writes roughly 100:1, so every decision optimizes the redirect path Primary tradeoff: base62 counter vs hash vs Snowflake for ID generation — collision risk vs coordination cost Component most candidates forget: a CDN in front of the redirect service, not just a Redis cache behind it Failure mode most candidates skip: what happens to click analytics if the async pipeline backs up — it must never block the redirect URL Shortener Redirect Svc Stateless lookup-and-redirect service on the hot read path step5-hld Stateless pods Scales horizontally with zero shared state Thin logic Just a cache/DB lookup + 302 Shorten Svc Validates URL and requests a new short code on write step3-api ID generation Base62 counter vs hash vs Snowflake Alias check Custom aliases live in a separate table Redis Cache Hot short_code to long_url mappings sub-ms lookups step6-deepdive-cache Cache-aside Miss falls through to Postgres then backfills LRU eviction Keeps only the hottest codes in memory Postgres Source of truth with a unique index on short_code step2-entities Unique index Every redirect is a point lookup never a scan Read replicas Absorb cache-miss reads at scale CDN Edge-caches the 302 response so popular links skip origin step4-dataflow Edge cache Repeat hits on a link never reach the service Purge on delete Explicit DEL keeps CDN and cache consistent"},{"u":"topics/questions/url-shortener.html","p":"Design a URL Shortener","i":"step1-requirements","h":"Requirements","t":"Step 1 of 6 — Requirements Requirements Functional Shorten a long URL into a unique short code Redirect a short code to its original long URL Support optional custom aliases Support optional link expiration Non-Functional 100M URLs created, 10M DAU Redirect latency <10ms p99 (cached) Read:write ratio ~100:1 99.99% availability for redirects What NOT to build: real-time click analytics dashboards, link-in-bio pages, QR code generation, or team/workspace permissions. Scope this to shorten + redirect + basic expiration only. ~12/s write QPS (shorten) ~1,200/s read QPS (redirect) ~50GB URL storage (5 yrs) 7 chars base62 short code"},{"u":"topics/questions/url-shortener.html","p":"Design a URL Shortener","i":"step2-entities","h":"Core entities","t":"Step 2 of 6 — Core Entities Core entities Url Postgres id BIGINT short_code VARCHAR(10) UNIQUE long_url TEXT user_id BIGINT, nullable created_at TIMESTAMP expires_at TIMESTAMP, nullable Because writes are low-volume (~12/s) and need a strong uniqueness constraint on short_code — a relational store with a unique index is the simplest correct fit. Tradeoff: a single primary needs read replicas once traffic grows past one node's capacity. CustomAlias Postgres alias VARCHAR(50) url_id BIGINT → Url.id Because a separate namespace prevents a user-chosen alias from ever colliding with a generated short_code. Tradeoff: redirect lookups check two tables instead of one — negligible cost since both are indexed. Primary key: Url.id is the Snowflake/counter-generated integer. Critical index: UNIQUE INDEX on short_code — every redirect is a point lookup against this index, so it must never require a table scan."},{"u":"topics/questions/url-shortener.html","p":"Design a URL Shortener","i":"step3-api","h":"API design","t":"Step 3 of 6 — API / Interface API design POST /api/v1/shorten Auth: optional (anonymous allowed) Request { long_url, custom_alias?, expires_at? } Response { short_url, short_code, expires_at } GET /{short_code} Auth: none Request path param only Response HTTP 302 + Location header. 404 if not found/expired DELETE /api/v1/urls/{short_code} Auth: required (owner only) Request path param only Response HTTP 204 on success REST fits cleanly here — no persistent connection is needed since both shorten and redirect are single-request-response operations with no ongoing state to push."},{"u":"topics/questions/url-shortener.html","p":"Design a URL Shortener","i":"step4-dataflow","h":"Data flow","t":"Step 4 of 6 — Data Flow Data flow READ PATH — redirect Client CDN Redirect Svc stateless Redis hot cache miss Postgres source of truth WRITE PATH — shorten Client API Gateway Shorten Svc stateless pods Postgres write Read path — cache-first, CDN in front Write path — low volume, strong uniqueness Client requests /{short_code} — hits CDN edge first CDN cache hit — returns cached 302 immediately, request never reaches origin CDN miss → Redirect Svc → Redis — checks in-memory cache for short_code Redis miss → Postgres — indexed lookup on short_code, result written back to Redis 302 returned to client — browser follows to long_url Browser or app following a shortened link Caches the 302 response at the edge — repeat hits never reach origin Thin stateless lookup-and-redirect service Cache hit avoids the Postgres round-trip — ~1ms vs ~10ms+ Source of truth — only touched on a cache miss Submits a long URL to be shortened Single entry point — auth rate limiting Validates URL requests an ID checks custom alias namespace Persists the new short_code → long_url row"},{"u":"topics/questions/url-shortener.html","p":"Design a URL Shortener","i":"step5-hld","h":"High-level design","t":"Step 5 of 6 — High-Level Design High-level design Clients CDN API Gateway Redirect Svc stateless Shorten Svc stateless Redis cache Postgres primary + replicas Client / gateway CDN — edge cache Redis — hot mappings Postgres — source of truth Component Role Tech + why Redirect Svc Looks up short_code, issues HTTP redirect Go — because the hot path needs low per-request overhead; tradeoff is a smaller ecosystem than Node for this thin a service. Shorten Svc Validates URL, requests an ID, writes row Stateless pods — because horizontal scaling needs zero coordination between instances; tradeoff is none, this is the easy side. Cache Serves hot short_code lookups Redis — because O(1) key lookup at sub-ms latency is exactly the read pattern; tradeoff is dataset must fit in RAM. Database Source of truth for URL mappings Postgres — because writes need a strong unique constraint on short_code; tradeoff is a single-writer bottleneck above ~10k writes/s. CDN Edge-caches redirect responses Cloudflare/CloudFront — because it eliminates the network round-trip entirely for popular links; tradeoff is stale-cache risk on delete. Scaling levers — cheapest to most complex Add Redis read replicas for the cache layer near-zero complexity Add Postgres read replicas to absorb cache-miss reads low complexity Put a CDN in front of the redirect service low complexity, high payoff Switch ID generation from DB auto-increment to Snowflake medium complexity Shard Postgres by short_code prefix high complexity — last resort Bottleneck Triggers at Exact fix DB read overload TRIGGERS AT >50k RPS hitting Postgres directly Redis cluster absorbs the majority; add read replicas for remaining misses Hot short code (viral link) TRIGGERS AT Single code getting millions of RPS CDN caches the redirect response at edge; Redis local replica per region Write throughput TRIGGERS AT >10k shortens/sec Switch DB auto-increment to Snowflake; shard Postgres by user_id range New short URL — write visibility on next read Strong Eventual Reads after cache miss — replica lag vs primary Strong Eventual Browsers and apps — both shorten and redirect requests originate here Clients Client Browser / mobile app Originates both shorten and redirect requests — no server logic here. 0ms (origin of request) Caches redirect responses at edge absorbing the bulk of read traffic CDN CDN Cloudflare / CloudFront Edge-caches the 302 response so popular links never reach origin. ~5-20ms (edge cache hit) Routes to shorten or redirect service; handles auth + rate limiting API Gateway Service Managed gateway (auth rate limit) Single entry point for writes — keeps auth/rate-limit logic out of app services. ~1-2ms overhead Stateless scales horizontally Redirect Svc Service Go stateless pods Hot path needs minimal per-request overhead — thin lookup-and-redirect only. ~1-3ms cache hit ~15-25ms miss Stateless scales horizontally Shorten Svc Service Stateless pods Validates URL requests an ID writes the row — horizontally scalable no shared state. ~5-10ms (DB write) Hot short_code → long_url mappings LRU eviction Redis Cache Redis LRU eviction O(1) key lookup at sub-ms latency — exactly the redirect read pattern. ~1-3ms Source of truth primary + read replicas Postgres Database Postgres primary + read replicas Strong unique constraint on short_code needs a relational store with an index. ~15-25ms (indexed lookup)"},{"u":"topics/questions/url-shortener.html","p":"Design a URL Shortener","i":"step6-deepdive-idgen","h":"Deep dive: ID generation","t":"Step 6 of 6 — Deep Dives Deep dive: ID generation Base62 counter Simple: increment a counter, encode to base62 No collisions by construction Single point of contention — one DB sequence Codes are sequential — guessable, minor info leak MD5/SHA hash of URL No coordination needed — pure function of input Collision risk requires a uniqueness check on every write anyway Truncating the hash for a short code raises collision odds further At this scale (~12 writes/sec), a Postgres sequence with base62 encoding is genuinely sufficient — reach for Snowflake only once write throughput or multi-region writes make a single sequence a bottleneck (see the Bitly page for that scale)."},{"u":"topics/questions/url-shortener.html","p":"Design a URL Shortener","i":"step6-deepdive-cache","h":"Deep dive: cache hit vs miss","t":"Deep dive: cache hit vs miss ✓ Cache hit — ~1-3ms Redirect GET code Redis key found ~1-3ms key exists → 302 immediately ▸ Replay ✗ Cache miss — ~15-25ms Redirect GET code Redis MISS SET back Postgres ~15-25ms miss → query → cache → return ▸ Replay Scenario User impact Detection signal Mitigation Redis down Latency spikes 10-20x, all reads hit DB Cache miss rate >80% alert Circuit breaker to protect DB; auto-scale replicas Postgres primary down Writes fail, cache misses fail Write error spike + replication lag Promote a read replica automatically CDN misconfigured All traffic hits origin, 10x spike Origin request rate anomaly Rate limit at gateway, auto-scale redirect pods Malicious/phishing URL submitted Users redirected to unsafe sites Safe Browsing API flag on shorten Block at write time, allow reports, auto-disable flagged codes Redirect Svc queries Redis In-memory lookup no disk I/O Redirect Svc queries Redis Falls through to Postgres SETs result back Indexed lookup slower than memory"},{"u":"topics/questions/url-shortener.html","p":"Design a URL Shortener","i":"interview-moves","h":"Interview moves","t":"Interview moves 01 \"Before I design anything, let me clarify the read/write ratio — this is 100:1 read-heavy which changes the entire caching strategy\" — anchors every downstream decision to a stated number 02 \"The interesting problem here is ID generation, not the redirect — the redirect is a one-line cache lookup\" — shows you know where the complexity actually lives 03 \"I'd put a CDN in front of the redirect service, not just Redis behind it — that's a second, cheaper layer of caching\" — most candidates stop at one cache tier 04 \"Custom aliases go in a separate table so they can never collide with a generated short_code\" — a subtlety that separates senior from mid-level answers 05 \"At this write volume, a Postgres sequence is genuinely fine — I'd only reach for Snowflake once a single sequence becomes a bottleneck\" — resists over-engineering for the stated scale 06 \"What happens to a short code after it expires — do we hard-delete immediately or soft-delete and sweep in a background job?\" — raises a lifecycle question interviewers often plant deliberately"},{"u":"topics/questions/url-shortener.html","p":"Design a URL Shortener","i":"follow-ups","h":"Follow-up questions","t":"Follow-up questions How would you prevent short code enumeration attacks? Rate limit per IP/user at the gateway. 62^7 ≈ 3.5 trillion combinations already makes brute-force scanning impractical without added protection. What if the same long URL is submitted twice? Default: generate a new code each time. Dedup by hashing long_url adds a read on every write to save storage — a real tradeoff, not a free win. How do you handle 10x the current scale? Shard Postgres by short_code prefix, switch ID generation to Snowflake, extend the code to 8 characters, make CDN mandatory rather than optional. 301 or 302 redirect? 302 by default — keeps every click hitting the server. 301 lets browsers cache it, which is faster but kills click visibility after the first visit. See the Bitly page for the full tradeoff. How would you implement expiration efficiently? Store expires_at in both Postgres and as a Redis TTL. The TTL handles the hot-path check for free; a daily background job hard-deletes expired rows in batches. How do you keep the CDN and Redis cache consistent after a delete? Short TTL (~60s) on both as a safety net, plus an explicit purge/DEL call fired synchronously on delete rather than relying on TTL expiry alone."},{"u":"topics/questions/web-crawler.html","p":"Design a Web Crawler","i":"","h":"Overview","t":"Questions Design a Web Crawler Tests whether you treat the URL frontier and dedup index as the hard distributed-systems problems they are, instead of hand-waving \"we just fetch pages with HTTP.\" 40-50 min interview Hard Concept Map click to expand Say this in the first 2 minutes The core insight: this is a distributed BFS over the web, constrained by politeness — the frontier and the dedup index are the hard data-structure problems, not the HTTP fetching Key scale number to anchor on: 10B pages to crawl, ~5,000 pages/sec sustained across the fleet, which is fetch-worker-bound, not bandwidth-bound Primary tradeoff being probed: dedup accuracy vs memory — a naive hash-set of 10B seen URLs doesn't fit in memory, so a Bloom filter trades a tunable false-positive rate for a 20-30x memory win Component most candidates forget: politeness — the frontier must be sharded/queued per domain so the fetcher fleet never hammers one site, no matter how parallel it is Failure mode most candidates skip: a crawler trap — a site that generates near-infinite URLs via query parameters or calendar links can consume the entire fetch budget if not capped Web Crawler URL Frontier One priority queue per domain gated by a politeness delay step6-deepdive-frontier Per-domain queues One slow site never blocks the rest Politeness delay Caps request rate per domain Fetcher Worker Pool Distributed workers that actually fetch pages step4-dataflow DNS Cache Avoids a lookup on every fetch Robots.txt Cache Checked before a fetch is allowed Dedup / Bloom Svc Cheap membership check against billions of seen URLs step6-deepdive-bloom Bloom filter Fraction of the memory of a real set Gates re-entry Decides what re-enters the frontier Parser / Link Extractor Pulls new links out of fetched content step5-hld Feeds itself New links flow back into the frontier Content Store Write-once storage for crawled pages Recrawl Scheduler Decides how often each page deserves to be revisited step6-deepdive-recrawl Change-rate aware A news homepage recrawls far more than a static page Fetch budget Avoids wasting it on unchanged pages"},{"u":"topics/questions/web-crawler.html","p":"Design a Web Crawler","i":"step1-requirements","h":"Requirements","t":"Step 1 of 6 — Requirements Requirements 1 2 3 4 5 6 Arc 1 (steps 1-5) — Satisfy Functional Requirements 1 2 3 4 5 6 Arc 2 (all 6 steps) — Satisfy Non-Functional Requirements Functional Given a set of seed URLs, discover and fetch reachable pages web-wide Extract links from fetched pages and feed newly discovered URLs back into the crawl queue Respect each site's robots.txt and per-domain crawl-delay directives Avoid re-fetching a URL already crawled, and re-crawl pages at a frequency matched to how often they change Non-Functional 10B pages in the crawlable set, sustained fetch rate of ~5,000 pages/sec fleet-wide No single domain receives more than one request per configured politeness delay Dedup index must hold ~10B seen URLs without a linear memory blow-up Crawler must self-limit against traps (infinite URL spaces) without operator intervention What NOT to build: full-text search indexing/ranking (a downstream consumer of the crawl output, not this system), JavaScript-rendering or headless-browser crawling (assume static HTML fetches only), or image/video content extraction. This is URL discovery, fetching, and storage of raw content — not search relevance. 10B pages in the crawlable web set ~5,000/s sustained fetch rate, fleet-wide 1 req / delay politeness cap per domain ~10B keys dedup index size (seen URLs)"},{"u":"topics/questions/web-crawler.html","p":"Design a Web Crawler","i":"step2-entities","h":"Core entities","t":"Step 2 of 6 — Core Entities Core entities Three entities split by access pattern: the frontier is a hot, constantly-reordered queue; crawled content is write-once and huge; domain policy is small, cached, and read on every single fetch. URLFrontierEntry Redis url_hash CHAR(64) — SHA-256(url) domain TEXT — shard key next_allowed_time TIMESTAMP — priority score priority FLOAT — freshness/depth weighted depth INT — hops from seed Because the frontier is popped and re-scored constantly at fleet scale — an in-memory priority structure keyed by per-domain next-allowed-time is what makes politeness enforcement O(log n) instead of a scan. Tradeoff: a node failure can lose recent re-prioritization, self-heals on next discovery pass. CrawledPage Postgres url_hash CHAR(64) UNIQUE domain, fetched_at TEXT, TIMESTAMP content_key VARCHAR(200) → S3 object change_rate FLOAT — observed edits/week next_recrawl_at TIMESTAMP Because metadata needs indexed lookups by URL and by recrawl due-time, while raw HTML is large and touched only by the parser and rare re-fetches — splitting them keeps the hot metadata table small. Tradeoff: fetching full content is always a second hop to blob storage. DomainPolicy Redis (cached) domain TEXT crawl_delay_ms INT — from robots.txt disallow_patterns TEXT[] fetched_at TIMESTAMP — TTL ~24h Because every single fetch decision reads this — re-parsing robots.txt per request would be both slow and itself impolite. Tradeoff: a stale cached policy briefly under- or over-restricts a domain until the TTL expires and it's refetched. Primary key: CrawledPage.url_hash , a SHA-256 hash of the canonicalized URL (used everywhere instead of the raw URL string). Critical index: next_recrawl_at on CrawledPage and the frontier's per-domain next_allowed_time score — both are what turn \"what should we fetch right now\" into an O(log n) pop instead of a scan."},{"u":"topics/questions/web-crawler.html","p":"Design a Web Crawler","i":"step3-api","h":"API design","t":"Step 3 of 6 — API / Interface API design Two operator endpoints: seed submission , page lookup . One policy lookup + one internal claim endpoint for fetchers. The fleet never hits the frontier over a public API. POST /api/v1/seeds Auth: required (operator) Request { urls: [...], priority?: \"high\"|\"normal\" } Response { accepted: N, deduped: M } — deduped counts URLs already seen GET /api/v1/pages/{urlHash} Auth: required (internal consumer) Request path param only Response { url, fetched_at, content_key, links_found: N } GET /api/v1/domains/{domain}/policy Auth: internal only Request path param only Response { crawl_delay_ms, disallow_patterns[], cached_at } POST /internal/fetch-tasks/claim Auth: internal (fetcher worker) Request { worker_id, batch_size } Response { tasks: [{url, url_hash, domain}] } — only domains past their politeness delay The claim endpoint is the only way a fetcher worker gets a URL — it never reads the frontier directly. That single choke point is where politeness is enforced: the scheduler behind it will not hand out a URL for a domain whose next_allowed_time hasn't passed yet, no matter how many idle workers are asking."},{"u":"topics/questions/web-crawler.html","p":"Design a Web Crawler","i":"step4-dataflow","h":"Data flow","t":"Step 4 of 6 — Data Flow Data flow Write path (blue) is a fetch-and-parse cycle that feeds itself — new links flow back into the frontier (amber). Read path (mint) is a page lookup, fully decoupled from crawling. WRITE PATH — fetch and parse, ~5,000/s URL Frontier Fetcher Fleet Parser Content Store ASYNC PATH — newly discovered URLs loop back new URLs, deduped against Bloom filter first READ PATH — page lookup by consumers Consumer Page Lookup Svc Postgres Frontier — politeness-gated queue Content store + Postgres metadata Downstream consumers — fully decoupled from crawling Fetcher claims a URL — only from a domain whose politeness delay has already elapsed Fetcher retrieves the page — respects robots.txt disallow rules pulled from the cached DomainPolicy Parser extracts links and normalizes them — strips tracking params, resolves relative paths to absolute URLs Each extracted URL is checked against the Bloom filter — likely-seen URLs are dropped before touching the frontier Genuinely new URLs are pushed back into the frontier — this is what makes the crawl a self-feeding BFS instead of a fixed list Per-domain queues keyed by earliest allowed next-crawl-time Distributed pool claims work only for domains past their delay Extracts links normalizes URLs checks dedup Raw HTML to S3 metadata row to Postgres Downstream indexer or operator tooling Stateless read API over CrawledPage metadata Indexed by url_hash"},{"u":"topics/questions/web-crawler.html","p":"Design a Web Crawler","i":"step5-hld","h":"High-level design","t":"Step 5 of 6 — High-Level Design High-level design Seeds enter the frontier, DNS is resolved and cached ahead of fetch time, a distributed fetcher pool respects politeness, and a dedup service gates what re-enters the frontier at all. Seed URLs operator input URL Frontier sharded by domain DNS Cache Fetcher Worker Pool respects per-domain rate limit Robots.txt Cache politeness rules Parser / Link Extractor Content Store blob storage Dedup / Bloom Svc new, deduped URLs loop back to frontier Recrawl Scheduler Seed input — operator-facing Robots.txt cache + dedup/Bloom service — hot-path gates Content store — durable raw HTML Frontier, fetcher pool, recrawl scheduler — the crawl loop itself Component Role Tech + why URL Frontier Decides what to fetch next, enforces politeness Sharded priority queues keyed by (domain, next_allowed_time) — because a priority pop must be O(log n) at fleet scale while guaranteeing no domain is over-hit; tradeoff is added coordination cost across shards. DNS Cache Resolves hostnames ahead of fetch time Local resolver cache with TTL honoring DNS records — because a cold DNS lookup per fetch would add 20-200ms to every request at 5,000 req/s; tradeoff is occasional stale resolution on IP changes. Fetcher Worker Pool Executes HTTP fetches, respects rate limits Horizontally scaled stateless workers pulling from the claim API — because raw fetch throughput scales linearly with workers, the frontier is the actual bottleneck to guard; tradeoff is workers must never bypass the claim gate. Dedup / Bloom Service Rejects already-seen URLs before they re-enter the frontier Bloom filter backed by a durable store for positive-match confirmation — because a real 10B-entry hash set doesn't fit in memory; tradeoff is a tunable false-positive rate that can silently skip a genuinely new URL. Content Store Stores raw fetched HTML Object storage (blob) — because write-once, rarely-read pages at web scale are exactly what object storage is priced for; tradeoff is no query capability, metadata must live in Postgres. Robots.txt Cache Serves politeness rules without a live fetch per request Redis, ~24h TTL per domain — because re-fetching robots.txt on every crawl decision would itself be impolite and slow; tradeoff is up to 24h staleness if a site changes its rules. Scaling levers — cheapest to most complex Add more fetcher workers horizontally, bounded by per-domain politeness caps low complexity Shard the frontier by domain hash across more nodes as URL volume grows low complexity Batch DNS resolution ahead of the fetch queue to hide resolver latency medium complexity Resize or rotate the Bloom filter as the seen-URL set grows past its sized capacity medium complexity Move to a distributed, coordinated multi-datacenter crawl for geo-local fetch latency high complexity — last resort Bottleneck Triggers at Exact fix Frontier hot-shard on one domain TRIGGERS AT A single large domain (e.g. a huge e-commerce site) dominates one shard's queue Sub-shard large domains by URL-path prefix while keeping one politeness clock per domain Bloom filter false-positive creep TRIGGERS AT Filter fill ratio grows past its sized capacity Periodic resize or rotate to a scalable variant (counting Bloom filter / cuckoo filter) DNS resolution latency TRIGGERS AT Fetcher pool stalls waiting on cold lookups Pre-resolve domains ahead of the claim queue, cache aggressively within TTL bounds Crawl freshness vs crawl budget Recrawl aggressively Conserve fetch budget Dedup accuracy vs memory — Bloom filter false-positive rate Exact, memory-heavy Probabilistic, compact Operator-submitted starting URLs Per-domain queues keyed by next-allowed-time priority-weighted by change rate Resolves and caches hostnames ahead of fetch to hide DNS latency Distributed workers claim URLs only for domains past their delay Cached robots.txt rules and crawl-delay ~24h TTL Extracts links normalizes URLs strips tracking params Raw HTML written once rarely re-read except by re-parses Bloom filter gates every candidate URL before it can re-enter the frontier Recrawl priority recomputed from observed change rate"},{"u":"topics/questions/web-crawler.html","p":"Design a Web Crawler","i":"step6-deepdive-frontier","h":"Deep dive: URL frontier and politeness","t":"Step 6 of 6 — Deep Dives Deep dive: URL frontier and politeness The frontier isn't one global queue — it's effectively one priority queue per domain, and the scheduler will only ever pop from a domain that's past its politeness delay. PER-DOMAIN QUEUES — scored by earliest allowed next-crawl-time domain: news-site.com next_allowed: now - 2s → eligible domain: shop-example.com next_allowed: now + 8s → blocked domain: slow-api.org next_allowed: now + 45s → blocked Scheduler pops only eligible domains skips blocked ones entirely Fetcher Fleet claims one URL at a time re-scores next_allowed_time = now + crawl_delay after every claim Result: 500 idle fetcher workers cannot force a fetch against a domain still inside its delay window Cache — hot path Async — queue / worker Each domain gets its own logical queue — scored by next_allowed_time , the earliest moment a fetch is allowed again Scheduler merges all per-domain queues — but only exposes entries whose domain's time has already passed Fetcher claims one URL — via the claim endpoint, never by reading the frontier directly Domain's next_allowed_time is pushed forward — by the domain's crawl_delay (from DomainPolicy), immediately after the claim Blocked domains simply wait — no matter how many fetcher workers are idle, a domain inside its delay window yields nothing Politeness has to be enforced at the frontier/scheduler level, not left to individual fetcher workers — if each worker independently decided \"am I allowed to hit this domain,\" a large fleet would still race past the limit in aggregate before any single worker noticed. 12 URLs queued next_allowed_time already passed — eligible now Still inside its crawl-delay window — not eligible yet Large crawl-delay directive from robots.txt — long wait Merges all per-domain queues pops only eligible entries After a claimed fetch this domain's next_allowed_time is pushed forward by its crawl-delay"},{"u":"topics/questions/web-crawler.html","p":"Design a Web Crawler","i":"step6-deepdive-bloom","h":"Deep dive: dedup at scale with Bloom filters","t":"Deep dive: dedup at scale with Bloom filters A naive hash-set of 10B seen URLs at ~64 bytes/entry (hash + overhead) is well over half a terabyte — too large to keep resident for a sub-millisecond membership check on every discovered link. 10B expected inserted URLs (n) ~17.1 Gb bit array size (m) at target FP rate 7 optimal hash functions (k) 0.1% target false-positive rate (p) Sized from the standard formulas m = -(n · ln p) / (ln 2)² and k = (m/n) · ln 2 . At n=10B and p=0.1%, that's m ≈ 1.44×10¹¹ bits ≈ 18GB . Versus roughly 640GB to store 10B full 64-byte hash-set entries — close to a 35x memory reduction for a 1-in-1000 false-positive chance. Candidate URL Bloom Filter ~18GB bit array any bit unset → definitely not seen Push to Frontier all bits set → maybe seen Postgres url_hash lookup, unique index confirmed dup false positive → push anyway Client / gateway Cache — hot path Database — source of truth Candidate URL hashed by k independent functions — each sets/checks one bit position in the shared bit array Any unset bit means definitely not seen — the vast majority of genuinely new URLs resolve here in O(k), no external lookup needed All bits set means maybe seen — this is the only case that pays for a definitive lookup, keeping the expensive path rare by design Durable store gives the authoritative answer — a Postgres unique-index lookup on url_hash confirms or refutes the match A false positive silently drops one new URL — accepted at a fixed, tunable rate rather than paying for a fully durable set The false-positive rate isn't a bug to eliminate, it's a dial to set deliberately — going from 0.1% to 0.01% roughly doubles the bit array size. State the accepted rate out loud rather than promising zero false positives, since a real hash set at this scale isn't a serious option. New URL discovered by the parser k=7 hash functions check bits in the array Fast path — most links are genuinely new Definitive check only on a possible match not on every URL"},{"u":"topics/questions/web-crawler.html","p":"Design a Web Crawler","i":"step6-deepdive-recrawl","h":"Deep dive: recrawl scheduling and freshness","t":"Deep dive: recrawl scheduling and freshness Pages don't deserve equal recrawl cadence. News homepage changes hourly; static docs change yearly. Identical polling wastes the fetch budget on both. Recrawl Fetch content hash compare Change-Rate Estimator Freshness Score next_recrawl_at URL Frontier re-enqueued at due time High-change page (news homepage): next_recrawl_at = now + hours Low-change page (static doc): next_recrawl_at = now + weeks Cache — hot path Database — source of truth Async — queue / worker Every recrawl computes a content hash — compared against the hash stored from the previous fetch Change-rate is updated as a rolling estimate — a page that changed on this check nudges its estimated change frequency up, one that didn't nudges it down Freshness score blends change-rate with importance — a high-traffic page that rarely changes can still outrank a low-traffic page that changes constantly next_recrawl_at is recomputed — shorter intervals for volatile pages, longer for stable ones, always within a min/max bound Page re-enters the frontier at its due time — competing for fetch budget against both first-time discoveries and other due recrawls Recrawl and first-discovery share one frontier and one fetch budget on purpose — treating them as separate systems would let an aggressive recrawl policy starve discovery of brand-new pages, or vice versa. Failure mode User impact Detection signal Mitigation Crawler trap (infinite URL generation) One malicious/misconfigured site consumes the entire fetch budget URL count for a single domain grows far past historical norms URL normalization (strip session/tracking params) plus a hard max-depth and max-URLs-per-domain cap Fetcher stuck on a slow-responding server Worker capacity silently drains as connections hang Per-request latency and open-connection count spike for one domain Per-request timeout plus a circuit breaker that stops sending new requests to a domain after repeated timeouts Bloom filter false-positive rate grows too high Genuinely new URLs get silently skipped at a rising rate Fill ratio (bits set / total bits) crosses a monitored threshold Periodic resizing, or migrate to a scalable variant like a counting Bloom filter or cuckoo filter that supports deletion and cleaner growth Each recrawl compares a content hash/checksum against the last fetch Exponential moving average of how often the page's content actually changes Combines change rate page importance and staleness into one score Recomputed next_recrawl_at pushed back into CrawledPage Feeds back into the same frontier used for first-time discovery"},{"u":"topics/questions/web-crawler.html","p":"Design a Web Crawler","i":"interview-moves","h":"Interview moves","t":"Interview moves 01 \"The hard problems here are the URL frontier and dedup index, not the HTTP fetching itself — any worker can issue a GET request\" — states the core insight unprompted and reframes the whole problem correctly 02 \"Politeness has to be enforced at the frontier or scheduler level, not trusted to individual fetcher workers, or a large fleet still races past the limit in aggregate\" — shows you understand distributed rate-limiting, not just single-node rate-limiting 03 \"A real hash set of 10 billion seen URLs is hundreds of gigabytes — I'd use a Bloom filter and explicitly state the false-positive rate I'm accepting\" — quantifies the tradeoff instead of just naming the data structure 04 \"A crawler trap — infinite URLs from query parameters or calendar links — will silently eat the whole fetch budget unless I cap max-depth and max-URLs-per-domain up front\" — proactively surfaces a failure mode most candidates only discover when prompted 05 \"Recrawl frequency should be driven by each page's observed change rate, not a flat schedule — a news homepage and a static doc don't deserve the same fetch budget\" — ties freshness directly to a measurable signal 06 \"I'd put a timeout and a per-domain circuit breaker on every fetch, because one slow-responding server can otherwise drain fetcher capacity indefinitely\" — names a concrete resilience mechanism instead of hand-waving \"handle timeouts\""},{"u":"topics/questions/web-crawler.html","p":"Design a Web Crawler","i":"follow-ups","h":"Follow-up questions","t":"Follow-up questions How do you avoid crawling the same content reachable via two different URLs? URL normalization first (sort query params, strip session IDs, lowercase host) collapses many duplicates before dedup even runs; a content-hash comparison catches the rest post-fetch. What happens when the Bloom filter reports a false positive? A genuinely new URL is silently never crawled — no error, no retry. It's mitigated by periodic resizing/rotation and accepted as a fixed, tunable cost rather than something to eliminate entirely. How would you prioritize which domains get crawled first when the frontier has a backlog? A priority score blending page importance (e.g. inbound link count), change rate, and staleness — high-value, fast-changing, overdue pages surface first within each domain's politeness budget. How do you detect and stop a crawler trap in real time, not after the fact? Track URL count and unique-path-depth per domain against a running baseline; crossing a hard cap on max-URLs-per-domain or max-depth halts further enqueuing from that domain automatically. Why keep DNS resolution as a separate cached step instead of resolving inline per fetch? A cold DNS lookup can add tens to hundreds of milliseconds — at thousands of fetches/sec that latency compounds across the fleet, so pre-resolving and caching within TTL bounds is a cheap win. How would this design change for a scoped, single-site crawl instead of web-scale? The Bloom filter and sharded frontier become overkill — a single in-memory set and one politeness queue suffice; the design only earns its complexity at the 10B-URL, multi-domain scale."},{"u":"topics/questions/whatsapp.html","p":"Design WhatsApp","i":"","h":"Overview","t":"Questions Design WhatsApp Tests whether you can guarantee at-least-once, in-order message delivery over millions of long-lived connections without losing a single message when a recipient is offline. 40-50 min interview Hard Concept Map click to expand Say this in the first 2 minutes The core insight: delivery guarantees (at-least-once) and per-conversation ordering are the hard problems here — not the chat UI Key scale number to anchor on: ~150M daily active users, each holding one long-lived WebSocket connection concurrently at peak Primary tradeoff being probed: strong per-message delivery guarantees vs the cost of holding millions of persistent connections in memory Component most candidates forget: a durable per-user offline queue — messages must survive a dead connection, not just a dead request Failure mode most candidates skip: what happens when a connection service instance dies mid-session with thousands of sockets attached to it WhatsApp Connection Svc Stateful service holding millions of long-lived WebSockets step5-hld Stateful tier The one genuinely stateful piece in this design ~50k sockets/node Per-connection memory is the binding constraint Message Svc Assigns ordered message IDs and orchestrates fan-out to recipients step4-dataflow Stateless Scales trivially behind the connection tier Durable write first Never acks send before Cassandra commits Redis Session routing table and per-user offline message queue step6-deepdive-offline Not durable copy Rebuilt from Cassandra if ever lost Sub-ms lookup Routes to the right connection server instantly Cassandra Durable append-only message log partitioned by conversation step2-entities TIMEUUID clustering Gives ordered writes with zero read-time sorting Source of truth Fallback if the Redis queue is ever lost Postgres Tracks each device and its per-device sync cursor step2-entities Hot lookup Checked on every send so cache-backed Strong consistency Needed for exact device-to-server routing Push Notif Svc Wakes a fully offline device through APNs or FCM step5-hld Best-effort A wake-up nudge not a delivery guarantee Last resort Only needed when no socket is open at all"},{"u":"topics/questions/whatsapp.html","p":"Design WhatsApp","i":"step1-requirements","h":"Requirements","t":"Step 1 of 6 — Requirements Requirements Functional Send and receive 1:1 text messages in near real time Deliver messages to offline users once they reconnect Show delivery receipts — sent, delivered, read Sync message history across a user's multiple devices Non-Functional 150M DAU, ~40B messages/day End-to-end delivery latency <100ms p99 when both users are online At-least-once delivery — a message must never silently vanish Per-conversation ordering preserved even under retries and reconnects What NOT to build: group chats, voice/video calling, end-to-end encryption key exchange (Signal protocol), status/stories, or media transcoding pipelines. This is 1:1 text messaging with delivery guarantees and multi-device sync only. ~460k/s messages/sec sent (avg, 40B/day) ~50M concurrent WebSocket connections at peak <100ms p99 delivery latency, both online ~5KB/conn connection service memory overhead/socket"},{"u":"topics/questions/whatsapp.html","p":"Design WhatsApp","i":"step2-entities","h":"Core entities","t":"Step 2 of 6 — Core Entities Core entities Message Cassandra conversation_id TEXT (partition key) message_id TIMEUUID (clustering key) sender_id BIGINT body TEXT (encrypted blob) status ENUM(sent,delivered,read) Because writes are append-only at ~460k/s and reads are always \"give me a conversation's recent messages in order\" — a wide-column store with conversation_id as partition key and a TIMEUUID clustering key gives ordered writes with zero read-time sorting. Tradeoff: cross-conversation queries (like global search) need a separate index. OfflineQueue Redis user_id BIGINT (key prefix) message_id TIMEUUID (list member) enqueued_at TIMESTAMP Because a recipient's offline window can last hours to days, and re-scanning all of Cassandra on reconnect to find \"what's new\" would be prohibitively slow — a per-user pending-ID list gives an O(1) push and a direct fan-out on reconnect. Tradeoff: Redis is not the durable copy, so it's rebuilt from Cassandra if it's ever lost, never treated as source of truth. Session Postgres device_id BIGINT user_id BIGINT conn_server_id VARCHAR, nullable last_seen_message_id TIMEUUID Because multi-device sync needs a strongly consistent record of which connection server a device is currently attached to and how far it has read — a relational store fits the low write volume and the need for exact device-to-server routing. Tradeoff: this table is a hot lookup on every send, so it must stay in a cache-backed read path. Primary key: (conversation_id, message_id) composite on Message. Critical index: the Redis per-user pending list — the offline-delivery hot path is a direct key lookup by user_id and must never fall back to scanning Cassandra."},{"u":"topics/questions/whatsapp.html","p":"Design WhatsApp","i":"step3-api","h":"API design","t":"Step 3 of 6 — API / Interface API design WS /v1/connect Auth: required (token on handshake) Client → Server frame { type: \"send\", conversation_id, client_msg_id, body } Server → Client frame { type: \"message\"|\"receipt\", message_id, status, ts } POST /api/v1/messages/{message_id}/receipt Auth: required Request { status: \"delivered\"|\"read\" } Response HTTP 204 — fans out receipt to sender's active devices GET /api/v1/conversations/{conversation_id}/messages Auth: required Request ?before=message_id&limit=50 Response { messages: [...], has_more } — paginated history from Cassandra PUT /api/v1/devices/{device_id}/sync-cursor Auth: required Request { last_seen_message_id } Response HTTP 204 — advances this device's multi-device read cursor"},{"u":"topics/questions/whatsapp.html","p":"Design WhatsApp","i":"step4-dataflow","h":"Data flow","t":"Step 4 of 6 — Data Flow Data flow SEND PATH — sender writes a message Sender Connection Svc stateful, sticky Message Svc Cassandra durable log RECEIVE PATH — fan out to recipient Message Svc Session Router Redis-backed online Connection Svc Recipient OFFLINE PATH — recipient not connected anywhere Redis Offline Queue → Push Svc Send path — durable write before ack Receive path — session-routed fan-out Offline — queued, delivered on reconnect Sender writes over WebSocket — Connection Svc forwards the frame to Message Svc Message Svc assigns an ordered ID and writes to Cassandra — the message is not acked as \"sent\" until this commits Session Router looks up the recipient — checks which connection server, if any, holds their live socket Online: direct push — the owning connection server writes the frame straight down the socket, no polling Offline: queued instead — message ID pushed onto the recipient's Redis list; a push notification is fired so the OS wakes the app Sender's app holding an open WebSocket Terminates the WebSocket forwards the frame Assigns ordered message_id validates sender Durable write — message is not acked to sender until this commits After the durable write commits looks up where the recipient is connected Session table lookup: which connection server holds this user's socket? Pushes the message frame down the recipient's live socket Recipient's app; fires a delivered receipt on frame receipt Session Router finds no live connection message pushed to the durable offline queue instead"},{"u":"topics/questions/whatsapp.html","p":"Design WhatsApp","i":"step5-hld","h":"High-level design","t":"Step 5 of 6 — High-Level Design High-level design Client Devices Load Balancer Connection Svc stateful, in-memory Message Svc stateless Cassandra Redis sessions + offline queue Push Notif Svc Postgres devices + sync cursors APNs / FCM Client / load balancer Redis — sessions + offline queue Cassandra + Postgres Push notification fan-out Component Role Tech + why Connection Svc Holds the long-lived WebSocket, forwards frames both ways Stateful service on a language with cheap concurrency (e.g. Go/Erlang-style event loop) — because ~50M concurrent sockets means per-connection memory overhead is the binding constraint; tradeoff is that instances are stateful and harder to deploy/scale than the rest of the fleet. Redis (sessions + offline queue) Routes messages to the right connection server; buffers messages for offline users In-memory key-value store — because session lookup and queue push/pop must be sub-millisecond on the hot path; tradeoff is it's not the durable copy, so it's rebuildable from Cassandra, never authoritative. Cassandra Durable, ordered message log per conversation Wide-column store — because writes are append-only at massive scale and reads are always range-scans within one partition; tradeoff is weaker support for ad-hoc cross-conversation queries. Push Notif Svc Wakes a fully offline device via the OS push channel Thin fan-out layer over APNs/FCM — because the OS-level push channel is the only way to reach an app that isn't even running; tradeoff is push delivery itself is best-effort, so it's a wake-up nudge, not the guarantee. Scaling levers — cheapest to most complex Add more Connection Svc instances behind the load balancer near-zero complexity, horizontal by socket count Shard Redis session table by user_id hash low complexity Add Cassandra nodes, tune replication factor per region medium complexity Move Message Svc fan-out onto a queue for burst absorption medium complexity Geo-partition Connection Svc clusters by region with cross-region routing high complexity — last resort Bottleneck Triggers at Exact fix Connection Svc instance overload TRIGGERS AT >50k sockets or CPU/memory saturation on one node Load balancer stops routing new connects to it; existing sockets drain and reconnect elsewhere Redis session table hot key TRIGGERS AT Single shard CPU pegged from routing lookups Consistent-hash shard by user_id across more Redis nodes Cassandra write hot partition TRIGGERS AT One conversation_id gets disproportionate write volume Rare for 1:1 chat, but bound message size and rate-limit per conversation as a safety valve Delivery guarantee: at-least-once vs exactly-once At-least-once (dedupe on client) Exactly-once (costly coordination) Connection state placement Stateless everywhere Stateful connection tier Sender and recipient devices each holding one WebSocket per device Client Devices Client Mobile/desktop apps one persistent WebSocket per device Each device keeps its own socket and sync cursor so multi-device delivery and read state stay independent per device. ~0ms — origin of the request not a hop TLS termination sticky routing to a connection server Load Balancer Service L4/L7 LB with sticky routing Terminates TLS and routes each socket consistently to one connection server so the stateful tier behind it stays coherent. ~1-3ms TLS + routing overhead Holds long-lived sockets ~50k connections per instance Connection Svc Service Stateful service cheap-concurrency runtime (e.g. Go/Erlang-style event loop) ~50M concurrent sockets means per-connection memory overhead is the binding constraint; tradeoff is these instances are stateful and harder to deploy/scale than the rest of the fleet. ~1-5ms frame forwarding Assigns ordered message IDs orchestrates fan-out Message Svc Service Stateless service Stateless so it scales trivially; assigns ordered message_id and orchestrates the fan-out to Cassandra Redis and Session Router. ~2-5ms ID assignment + orchestration Durable append-only message log partitioned by conversation Cassandra Database Wide-column store Writes are append-only at massive scale and reads are always range-scans within one partition; tradeoff is weaker support for ad-hoc cross-conversation queries. ~5-15ms durable write commit Session routing table: user/device → connection server instance Redis Cache In-memory key-value store Session lookup and queue push/pop must be sub-millisecond on the hot path; tradeoff is it's not the durable copy so it's rebuildable from Cassandra never authoritative. <1ms session lookup / queue op Wakes the OS app when a device is offline and a message is queued Push Notif Svc Queue Thin fan-out layer over APNs/FCM The OS-level push channel is the only way to reach an app that isn't even running; tradeoff is push delivery itself is best-effort so it's a wake-up nudge not the guarantee. ~100ms-1s best-effort wake-up Tracks each user's device list and per-device read cursor Postgres Database Relational store Multi-device sync needs a strongly consistent record of which connection server a device is attached to and how far it has read; low write volume fits a relational store but this table is a hot lookup on every send so it must stay cache-backed. ~2-8ms hot lookup APNs / FCM — third-party push gateways APNs / FCM Queue Third-party OS push gateways External push infrastructure is the only channel that can wake a fully-closed app process on iOS/Android. ~100ms-several sec provider-dependent"},{"u":"topics/questions/whatsapp.html","p":"Design WhatsApp","i":"step6-deepdive-receipts","h":"Deep dive: delivery receipt state machine","t":"Step 6 of 6 — Deep Dives Deep dive: delivery receipt state machine Sent Cassandra write committed Delivered recipient device ACKed frame Read recipient opened the chat reconnect can jump Sent → Read directly if unread while offline Sent — set the moment Message Svc's write to Cassandra commits, before any fan-out happens Delivered — recipient's Connection Svc pushes the frame and the client sends an application-level ACK back over the same socket Read — fired only when the recipient actually opens that conversation thread, a separate explicit client action from delivery Receipt fan-out to sender — each transition is itself a small message routed back to every one of the sender's active devices Multi-device reconciliation — a device that was offline during a transition catches up via its stored sync cursor rather than replaying every intermediate receipt Read receipts must never be inferred from delivery — a message can be pushed to a locked phone in someone's pocket. Treating \"delivered\" as \"read\" is the single most common correctness bug candidates propose here."},{"u":"topics/questions/whatsapp.html","p":"Design WhatsApp","i":"step6-deepdive-offline","h":"Deep dive: offline message queue and reconnect flow","t":"Deep dive: offline message queue and reconnect flow WHILE OFFLINE — message arrives, no live socket found Cassandra durable write Session Router no live socket Redis Offline Queue APNs / FCM ON RECONNECT — queued messages flush in order Client Connection Svc Redis Offline Queue drain in ID order Client ordered replay Cassandra is the fallback source of truth if the Redis queue itself is ever lost or evicted Durable write, unaffected by connection state Offline queue + push wake-up Reconnect drains queue in order TIMEUUID clustering keys make the drain order deterministic, but the client must still dedupe by message_id — a redelivery after a partial ACK (connection dropped mid-flush) is expected under at-least-once, not a bug. Scenario User impact Detection signal Mitigation Connection Svc instance crashes with live sockets attached Thousands of users appear offline instantly Instance health check fails, socket count drops to zero Clients auto-reconnect with backoff; Session Router evicts stale entries on TTL, messages queue normally in the meantime Redis offline queue node failure Pending-message pointers for affected users are lost Redis node health alert, queue read errors Rebuild queue by scanning each affected user's recent Cassandra partition since their last sync cursor Push notification not delivered by APNs/FCM Offline user doesn't get woken up promptly Push provider error/bounce rate Message still sits durably in Cassandra + offline queue; delivered on next app foreground regardless of push success Duplicate delivery after flaky reconnect User sees the same message twice Client-side dedupe counter spikes Client dedupes by message_id before rendering — expected behavior under at-least-once, not treated as an incident Out-of-order arrival across two devices Message order looks inconsistent between a phone and a linked desktop Per-device sync cursor drifts from Cassandra's clustering order Client always renders by TIMEUUID clustering order, never by arrival order Message committed to Cassandra as usual Session Router finds no connection server owns this user message_id pushed onto the recipient's pending list Silent push wakes the OS-level app process Device opens a new WebSocket presents its last-seen cursor Reattaches socket updates Redis session mapping Drains the pending list resolves each ID against Cassandra Messages streamed back in original send order receipts fire per message"},{"u":"topics/questions/whatsapp.html","p":"Design WhatsApp","i":"interview-moves","h":"Interview moves","t":"Interview moves 01 \"The hard part here isn't chat UI, it's guaranteeing at-least-once delivery with per-conversation ordering across connects, disconnects, and multiple devices\" — names the actual problem before diving into components 02 \"I'd never ack a send back to the sender until the message is durably written to Cassandra — the WebSocket ack and the durability guarantee are two separate events\" — shows the write path is correctness-first, not latency-first 03 \"Delivered and read are two distinct, explicit client actions — I will not infer 'read' from 'delivered,' that's the most common correctness bug in this design\" — preempts a classic interviewer probe 04 \"Connection Svc is the one genuinely stateful tier in this system — everything else stays stateless so it scales trivially behind a load balancer\" — demonstrates the architecture isn't uniformly stateless out of habit 05 \"Redis holds the offline queue for speed, but Cassandra is always the fallback source of truth if that queue is ever lost — I never treat the cache as durable\" — states a clear durability boundary unprompted 06 \"At-least-once means the client has to dedupe by message_id — I'd rather redeliver a message the client already has than risk losing one\" — picks the correct side of the tradeoff and defends it"},{"u":"topics/questions/whatsapp.html","p":"Design WhatsApp","i":"follow-ups","h":"Follow-up questions","t":"Follow-up questions How do you handle a user with two devices connected simultaneously? Both devices get their own socket and their own session entry; the Message Svc fans out to every active device, and each tracks its own sync cursor independently. Why Cassandra over Postgres for the message log? Write volume (~460k/s) and the append-only, partition-scoped access pattern favor a wide-column store; Postgres would need heavy sharding to match that write throughput. How do you prevent a message from being lost if Connection Svc crashes mid-delivery? The message is already durably written to Cassandra before any push attempt, so a crashed connection server just means the client reconnects and the normal offline-drain path re-delivers it. How would you add group chat on top of this design? Fan out the same send path to N recipients instead of one, and change conversation_id semantics to a group ID — the durability, ordering, and offline-queue mechanics stay identical. How do you scale to 10x the concurrent connections? Add more Connection Svc instances (horizontal, bounded by memory per socket), shard the Redis session table further, and geo-partition connection clusters by region. What happens if the sender and recipient are on the same Connection Svc instance? Still routes through Message Svc for the durable write and ID assignment — no shortcut bypassing Cassandra, since correctness must hold even when this coincidence doesn't."},{"u":"topics/questions/yelp.html","p":"Design Yelp","i":"","h":"Overview","t":"Questions Design Yelp Tests whether you treat \"search nearby businesses\" as a geo-indexing and ranking problem first, and reviews as ordinary CRUD second. 30-40 min interview Medium Concept Map click to expand Say this in the first 2 minutes The core insight: geo search + ranking is the hard part of this system, reviews and business profiles are standard CRUD on top Key scale number to anchor on: 12M businesses indexed, ~8k search QPS average with 5-10x spikes in dense metros at peak hours Primary tradeoff being probed: a precomputed geo index (fast reads, stale on writes) vs querying live (always fresh, slow at scale) Component most candidates forget: search ranking is a multi-signal blend — distance alone produces bad results next to a 2.1-star business Failure mode most candidates skip: a dense downtown core can have 50x the business density of a suburb, breaking uniform-grid geo indexes Yelp Search Svc Builds the geo and keyword filter then executes a ranked query step6-deepdive-ranking Stateless Ranking logic lives outside the index Cache check Redis checked before Elasticsearch Write Svc Validates and persists business listing and review writes step2-entities One review rule Unique constraint per user per business Sync commit Postgres write before async reindex Elasticsearch Search engine combining geo-radius filter with rank scoring step6-deepdive-geoindex geo_point field Native radius query support Read-optimized copy Can briefly lag the source of truth Redis Caches hot search queries and the denormalized rating summary step2-entities Query cache Keyed on geohash cell plus category Rating cache Avoids a COUNT AVG per page load Postgres Transactional source of truth for Business and Review rows step2-entities ACID commit Strong consistency on ownership edits Denormalized rating avg_rating stored on Business Kafka Reindexer Propagates writes into the search index asynchronously step4-dataflow CDC pipeline Change events drive the reindex Eventual freshness Search can lag by seconds"},{"u":"topics/questions/yelp.html","p":"Design Yelp","i":"step1-requirements","h":"Requirements","t":"Step 1 of 6 — Requirements Requirements 1 2 3 4 5 6 Arc 1 (steps 1-5) — Satisfy Functional Requirements 1 2 3 4 5 6 Arc 2 (all 6 steps) — Satisfy Non-Functional Requirements Functional Search businesses by category/keyword near a location View a business profile — hours, photos, rating, reviews Leave a review with a star rating and text Business owners create and edit their business listing Non-Functional 12M businesses indexed, ~8k search QPS average Search latency <200ms p99, including ranking 99.9% availability for search and reads; writes can tolerate less Search results eventually consistent — a new review can lag minutes, not hours What NOT to build: real-time table reservations, food delivery/ordering, ad auction/bidding platform, or a review-fraud ML pipeline in depth. This is search + profile + reviews only — mention fraud detection exists, don't design it. 12M businesses indexed ~8k/s search QPS (avg, peaks 5-10x) ~1.5k/s new reviews/sec (global peak) <200ms search p99 target"},{"u":"topics/questions/yelp.html","p":"Design Yelp","i":"step2-entities","h":"Core entities","t":"Step 2 of 6 — Core Entities Core entities Business profile and reviews are relational; the geo index is a separate derived structure optimized purely for radius queries. Business Postgres business_id UUID name, category TEXT lat, lng DOUBLE PRECISION avg_rating NUMERIC(2,1), denormalized hours, phone JSONB Because a business listing needs strong consistency on ownership edits and structured fields for filtering — a relational store fits. Tradeoff: lat/lng columns alone can't serve a fast radius query at 12M rows, hence the separate geo index. Review Postgres review_id UUID business_id UUID → Business user_id, stars UUID, SMALLINT text, created_at TEXT, TIMESTAMP Because reviews are ordinary write-once, read-many CRUD with a strict one-review-per-user-per-business constraint — a relational store with a unique index enforces that cheaply. Tradeoff: aggregate rating recompute needs a background job, not a live join, at high review volume. GeoIndexEntry Elasticsearch business_id keyword geo_point geo_point field category, avg_rating keyword, float — denormalized review_count int — denormalized Because search needs a combined geo-radius filter, keyword match, and rank score in one query — a search engine with native geo queries beats a hand-rolled quadtree service for this workload. Tradeoff: it's a read-optimized copy, updated async, so it can briefly lag the source of truth. RatingSummary Redis business_id hash key avg_rating, count float, int Because the profile page reads avg_rating on every view — caching the aggregate avoids a COUNT/AVG query per page load. Tradeoff: invalidated and recomputed async after each new review, so it's momentarily stale. Primary key: Business.business_id . Critical index: the Elasticsearch geo_point field on GeoIndexEntry — every search query starts with a geo-distance filter, and it must never fall back to a full scan."},{"u":"topics/questions/yelp.html","p":"Design Yelp","i":"step3-api","h":"API design","t":"Step 3 of 6 — API / Interface API design Four endpoints — search, profile read, review write, and business listing write. All request-response, no persistent connections needed. GET /api/v1/search?lat&lng&q&category&radius_km Auth: optional Request query params only, radius_km defaults to 5 Response { results: [{ business_id, name, distance_km, avg_rating, category }], next_cursor } GET /api/v1/businesses/{id} Auth: none Request path param only Response { name, hours, avg_rating, photos, top_reviews[5] } POST /api/v1/businesses/{id}/reviews Auth: required (user) Request { stars, text } Response 201 on success, 409 if user already reviewed this business POST /api/v1/businesses Auth: required (owner) Request { name, category, lat, lng, hours, phone } Response { business_id } — triggers async geo-index write"},{"u":"topics/questions/yelp.html","p":"Design Yelp","i":"step4-dataflow","h":"Data flow","t":"Step 4 of 6 — Data Flow Data flow Search reads (mint) hit the geo index directly; business/review writes (blue) land in Postgres first, then propagate to the index async. READ PATH — search Client Search Svc ranks results Redis miss Elasticsearch WRITE PATH — new business / review Client Write Svc Postgres Kafka Elasticsearch Read path — cache then geo index Write path — strong consistency in Postgres first Reindex — decoupled, eventually consistent Client searches by location + keyword — hits Search Svc with lat/lng, radius, category Redis checked for a hot query cache — keyed on geohash cell + category + query text Cache miss → Elasticsearch geo query — filters by radius, scores by the ranking blend Business/review writes land in Postgres first — the transactional source of truth, response returns immediately Change event flows through Kafka to reindex — search results reflect the update within seconds, not synchronously User searches 'coffee near me' Parses query builds geo + keyword filter applies ranking Caches hot queries — popular category near a popular geohash cell Geo-radius + keyword + rank query source of truth for search Owner edits listing or user posts a review Validates input enforces one-review-per-user constraint Source of truth — ACID commit Change event queued for async reindex decoupled from the write's response Reindexer consumes the event upserts the geo document"},{"u":"topics/questions/yelp.html","p":"Design Yelp","i":"step5-hld","h":"High-level design","t":"Step 5 of 6 — High-Level Design High-level design Full system: search, profile, and review flows together with the async reindexing pipeline that keeps them in sync. Clients API Gateway Search Svc stateless Write Svc stateless Redis Elasticsearch Postgres Kafka Reindexer Client / gateway Redis — hot query + rating cache Postgres + Elasticsearch Kafka + Reindexer — keeps search eventually consistent Component Role Tech + why Search Svc Builds geo filter, executes ranked query Stateless service in front of Elasticsearch — because ranking logic (distance decay, rating, recency) needs to live outside the index itself for easy iteration; tradeoff is an extra network hop vs querying ES directly. Elasticsearch Geo-radius + keyword + rank query engine Inverted index with native geo_point support — because it combines full-text relevance and geo-distance scoring in one query; tradeoff is operational complexity of running and resharding a cluster. Postgres Transactional source of truth Relational store with unique constraints — because business ownership and one-review-per-user rules need real ACID guarantees; tradeoff is it cannot serve the search query pattern directly at this scale. Kafka + Reindexer Propagates writes into the search index Log-based CDC pipeline — because it decouples index freshness from write latency, so a slow reindex never blocks a review submission; tradeoff is search results can lag the source of truth by seconds. Scaling levers — cheapest to most complex Add Redis + Postgres read replicas near-zero complexity Cache hot search queries by geohash cell + category low complexity, high payoff in dense metros Add Elasticsearch replica shards for read throughput medium complexity Scale Kafka consumer group partitions for reindex throughput medium complexity Geo-shard Elasticsearch by region so a metro's index fits in memory high complexity — last resort Bottleneck Triggers at Exact fix Elasticsearch query latency p99 >200ms during peak search hours Add replica shards; cache the top N hottest queries in Redis Dense downtown core One metro core has 50x the business density of a suburb Variable-depth geo cells (quadtree-style) instead of a uniform grid in that region Reindex consumer lag New reviews/listings take >60s to appear in search Scale out consumer partitions; batch ES bulk upserts larger Search index freshness vs write throughput Strong Eventual (seconds) Business/review write consistency Strong Eventual Search profile views and review submissions all originate here Auth rate limiting routing Geo + keyword filter ranking blend Business writes review writes Hot query cache rating summary cache Geo-radius + keyword + rank query engine Business Review — transactional source of truth Change-data-capture events for reindexing Consumes CDC events upserts geo documents"},{"u":"topics/questions/yelp.html","p":"Design Yelp","i":"step6-deepdive-geoindex","h":"Deep dive: quadtree vs geohash for the geo index","t":"Step 6 of 6 — Deep Dives Deep dive: quadtree vs geohash for the geo index Both turn a 2D radius query into a fast lookup — they differ in how they handle uneven business density across a city. Quadtree — adapts to density ▸ Simulate downtown density Geohash — uniform grid ▸ Simulate downtown density Approach When it wins Tradeoff Quadtree Uneven density — dense downtown cores next to sparse suburbs, which is exactly a city's business distribution More complex to implement and rebalance; tree traversal is slower than a string-prefix lookup Geohash Simplicity and uniform load — easy to shard by prefix, trivial to reason about, works well when density is roughly even A single dense cell becomes a hotspot with no built-in way to subdivide it further In practice: Elasticsearch's geo_point uses a geohash-like structure internally, but the engine shards and balances automatically — most teams don't hand-roll either structure. The interview signal is knowing why density matters, not implementing a quadtree from scratch."},{"u":"topics/questions/yelp.html","p":"Design Yelp","i":"step6-deepdive-ranking","h":"Deep dive: search ranking signals","t":"Deep dive: search ranking signals Distance alone produces bad results — a 4.8-star cafe 400m away should usually beat a 2.1-star cafe 150m away. Ranking blends four signals into one score. Geo filter narrows the candidate set — Elasticsearch geo-radius query returns everything within the search radius first, typically a few hundred to a few thousand businesses Distance decay score — closer businesses score higher, but the curve flattens past ~1km so distance stops dominating once \"nearby enough\" Star rating score — average rating weighted by review count, so a single 5-star review doesn't outrank a business with 500 reviews at 4.6 Review recency score — a business with recent reviews signals it's currently open and active, a small boost over one with reviews from 3 years ago Text relevance score — keyword match against name, category, and review text (BM25-style), only applied when the query has search terms Weighted blend produces final rank — score = w1·distance_decay + w2·rating_score + w3·recency + w4·relevance , weights tuned per query type (browse vs keyword search) Weighting is context-dependent: a \"pizza near me\" query should weight distance heavily since intent is clearly proximity-driven; a \"best pizza in [neighborhood]\" query should weight rating and relevance more heavily since the user already named a location. Scenario User impact Detection signal Mitigation Elasticsearch cluster degraded Search latency spikes or times out Query latency p99 alert, node health check failures Fall back to a cached \"top businesses near this geohash cell\" result set; auto-scale ES nodes Reindex pipeline stalls New businesses/reviews invisible in search for minutes+ Kafka consumer lag climbing on the reindex topic Scale reindexer consumers; page on-call if lag exceeds 5 minutes Rating cache goes stale Profile page shows outdated star rating briefly Redis TTL expiry mismatch vs actual review write timestamp Short TTL (~5 min) plus explicit cache invalidation fired on every new review Review bombing (coordinated fake reviews) A business's rating swings sharply in a short window Anomalous review velocity + rating delta on a single business Rate-limit reviews per user/IP, flag for fraud review, exclude flagged reviews from avg_rating pending review Hot downtown geo cell Search queries in dense metro cores are slower than suburbs Per-region query latency breakdown shows an outlier cell Finer-grained subdivision (quadtree-style) for that region only"},{"u":"topics/questions/yelp.html","p":"Design Yelp","i":"interview-moves","h":"Interview moves","t":"Interview moves 01 \"Before I design anything, let me split this into two problems — geo search is the hard part, business profiles and reviews are standard CRUD\" — states the core insight unprompted and sets scope 02 \"I wouldn't run a geo-radius query against Postgres at 12M rows — I'd maintain a dedicated search index that's async-updated from the source of truth\" — names the read/write split explicitly 03 \"Ranking can't be distance-only — a 2-star business next door shouldn't outrank a 4.8-star business three blocks away, so I'd blend distance, rating, recency, and relevance\" — shows the ranking is a designed system, not an afterthought 04 \"A uniform geohash grid breaks down in a dense downtown core — I'd want variable-depth cells there, closer to a quadtree, while suburbs stay coarse\" — proactively surfaces the density problem 05 \"Reviews go straight into Postgres for strong consistency on the one-review-per-user rule, then flow into the search index async — a slow reindex should never block a review submission\" — separates write correctness from read freshness 06 \"I'd flag review-bombing as a real failure mode — a sudden spike in reviews with an extreme rating delta on one business should be rate-limited and queued for fraud review, not trusted immediately\" — demonstrates abuse-case thinking unprompted"},{"u":"topics/questions/yelp.html","p":"Design Yelp","i":"follow-ups","h":"Follow-up questions","t":"Follow-up questions How do you handle autocomplete/typeahead for the search box? A separate trie or Elasticsearch completion suggester over business names and categories, served from an in-memory cache — a different latency budget than full search. How would you support filtering by \"open now\"? Store structured hours as a JSONB field, evaluate against the request's local time server-side, and add it as a post-filter after the geo/rank query rather than indexing every possible time slot. How do you prevent duplicate business listings for the same physical location? Fuzzy-match name + geo-proximity + phone number at creation time, flag likely duplicates for manual merge rather than auto-rejecting, since false positives block legitimate new businesses. How would this change to support 10x the scale? Geo-shard Elasticsearch by region so each shard's working set fits in memory, add more Redis cache capacity for hot queries, and move to variable-depth cells more broadly, not just downtown cores. Why not compute avg_rating live with a JOIN instead of denormalizing it? A profile page and every search result would need a COUNT/AVG aggregate over potentially thousands of reviews — denormalizing into a cached summary makes that a point read instead. How do you rank sponsored/ad businesses without making search feel untrustworthy? Keep organic ranking signals untouched, inject sponsored results into clearly labeled slots, and cap how much an ad boost can override a very low organic rating."},{"u":"topics/questions/youtube-top-k.html","p":"Design Top-K Trending Videos (YouTube)","i":"","h":"Overview","t":"Questions Design Top-K Trending Videos (YouTube) Tests whether you know that exact top-K at global scale is a myth — the real design is a probabilistic counting structure feeding a bounded heap, not a giant counter-per-video table. 35-45 min interview Medium Concept Map click to expand Say this in the first 2 minutes The core insight: exact top-K over hundreds of millions of videos and billions of daily events doesn't scale — you need approximate counting (Count-Min Sketch) instead of an atomic counter per video Key scale number to anchor on: 5B views/day is ~58k events/sec average, with the top-K query needing to answer in under 100ms Primary tradeoff being probed: exactness vs memory — a sketch trades a small, bounded overestimation error for constant memory regardless of how many distinct videos exist Component most candidates forget: a bounded min-heap of size K sitting on top of the sketch — the sketch alone doesn't give you \"top-K,\" it only gives you fast estimates you still have to rank Failure mode most candidates skip: a single viral video can overload one counter shard — needs local pre-aggregation before it ever touches the shared sketch YouTube Top-K Kafka Durable ordered event log partitioned by video_id step5-hld Partitioned One video co-locates on one partition Fire-and-forget Ingestion never blocks on counting View Counter Svc Pre-aggregates locally before touching the shared sketch step6-deepdive-heavyhitters Local batching Absorbs a viral video before it hits the sketch Added lag A few seconds before a count reflects everywhere Count-Min Sketch Fixed d by w matrix estimating counts regardless of cardinality step6-deepdive-cms d hash rows One increment per row per event Take the min Tightest upper bound across collisions Top-K Min-Heap Size-K heap tracking only the current best candidates step6-deepdive-heavyhitters Bounded memory Never grows past K entries Sketch is not enough Still needs ranking on top of estimates Snapshot Job Periodically extracts the heap and writes it durably step5-hld Bounded staleness Freshness limited by snapshot interval Decouples reads Millions of readers never hit the live sketch Query Service Serves every trending request from the durable snapshot step2-entities snapshot_age_ms Exposes staleness instead of hiding it Never touches sketch Read path fully decoupled from ingestion"},{"u":"topics/questions/youtube-top-k.html","p":"Design Top-K Trending Videos (YouTube)","i":"step1-requirements","h":"Requirements","t":"Step 1 of 6 — Requirements Requirements 1 2 3 4 5 6 Arc 1 (steps 1-5) — Satisfy Functional Requirements 1 2 3 4 5 6 Arc 2 (all 6 steps) — Satisfy Non-Functional Requirements Functional Track view events per video in near real time Return the top-K trending videos for a given window (e.g. 1h, 24h, all-time) Support trending broken out by region/category (same mechanism, partitioned key) Serve the trending list to millions of concurrent readers Non-Functional 5B view events/day, ~58k events/sec average, much higher at peak Top-K query answers in <100ms Counting must be approximate but bounded — no unbounded memory growth per video Tolerate a small, known overestimation error in view counts in exchange for constant memory What NOT to build: no personalized recommendations, no per-user watch history or \"recommended for you\" ranking, no video upload/transcoding/storage pipeline. This is purely: count view events at scale, then serve an approximate top-K. 5B/day view events ingested <100ms top-K query latency target 10-100 typical K size ~58k/s avg ingestion rate (peaks far higher)"},{"u":"topics/questions/youtube-top-k.html","p":"Design Top-K Trending Videos (YouTube)","i":"step2-entities","h":"Core entities","t":"Step 2 of 6 — Core Entities Core entities The raw event lives briefly in Kafka, the running counts live in an ephemeral probabilistic structure in Redis, and only the periodic winner snapshot is durably persisted. ViewEvent Kafka video_id STRING ts epoch millis region STRING, optional source watch_page | embed | mobile Because a view is a fire-and-forget fact, not a record needing a primary key or update — it's a message on a log, partitioned by video_id so all events for one video land on the same partition for local pre-aggregation. Tradeoff: no durability guarantee beyond Kafka's own retention window. CountMinSketch Redis d rows × w cols INT counter matrix hash_seeds d independent hash fns window_id 1h bucket | all-time Because tracking an exact counter per video would need one key per distinct video — hundreds of millions of them — while the sketch uses a fixed-size matrix regardless of cardinality. Tradeoff: counts are estimates, always biased upward, never downward. TopKSnapshot Postgres snapshot_id UUID window, region composite index ranked_videos JSON[video_id, est_count] computed_at TIMESTAMP Because query traffic for \"what's trending\" is bursty and read-heavy — millions of readers should never hit the sketch directly. A periodic snapshot (every few seconds to a minute) gives a durable, queryable, cacheable answer. Tradeoff: freshness is bounded by the snapshot interval, not truly real-time. Primary key: none on ViewEvent — it's an immutable log message, not a row. Critical index: TopKSnapshot(window, region) — this is what the read path hits, never the sketch or the raw stream."},{"u":"topics/questions/youtube-top-k.html","p":"Design Top-K Trending Videos (YouTube)","i":"step3-api","h":"API design","t":"Step 3 of 6 — API / Interface API design Ingestion is fire-and-forget (Kafka producer, not a synchronous REST call); reads are cheap GETs against the snapshot, with an optional WebSocket for a live-updating trending rail. POST /api/v1/views/track Auth: service-to-service Request { video_id, ts, region, source } Response 202 Accepted — enqueued to Kafka, not synchronously counted GET /api/v1/trending?window=1h&region=US&k=25 Auth: none (public read) Request window, region, k as query params Response { videos: [{video_id, est_views, rank}], snapshot_age_ms } GET /api/v1/trending?window=all&k=100 Auth: none (public read) Request window=all uses the persistent global sketch, not a time bucket Response { videos: [{video_id, est_views, rank}], computed_at } WEBSOCKET /ws/trending/{window} Auth: none (public read) Message out { videos: [...] } pushed on every new snapshot Cadence once per snapshot interval, not per view event The track endpoint returns 202 immediately and never blocks on counting logic — the whole point of decoupling ingestion from aggregation is that a burst of views never backs up the client-facing write path."},{"u":"topics/questions/youtube-top-k.html","p":"Design Top-K Trending Videos (YouTube)","i":"step4-dataflow","h":"Data flow","t":"Step 4 of 6 — Data Flow Data flow The write path (amber) streams every view event through Kafka into the sketch asynchronously; the read path (mint) only ever touches the periodic snapshot, never the live sketch. WRITE PATH — view events, ~58k/s avg Watch Page Kafka Counter Service CMS in Redis READ PATH — trending query Client / App Query Service TopKSnapshot DB Kafka + Counter Service — decoupled, high-throughput write path Redis CMS — the hot, ephemeral counting structure Snapshot DB — what every read actually hits View fires — the watch page/app emits a view event once playback crosses a minimum threshold Event lands on Kafka — partitioned by video_id so one video's events stay ordered and co-located Counter Service pre-aggregates — batches counts locally per partition before incrementing the shared sketch Sketch cells increment — one increment per hash function row, not one key per video Snapshot job reads the sketch — periodically extracts the current top-K and writes it durably Query Service serves reads — every trending request hits the snapshot, completely decoupled from ingestion load Any surface a view can originate from Durable ordered partitioned by video_id Pre-aggregates locally before touching the shared sketch Count-Min Sketch — fixed-size matrix one per window Requests top-K for a window/region Reads only the durable snapshot never the live sketch Periodic durable top-K cheap to serve at massive fan-out"},{"u":"topics/questions/youtube-top-k.html","p":"Design Top-K Trending Videos (YouTube)","i":"step5-hld","h":"High-level design","t":"Step 5 of 6 — High-Level Design High-level design View events stream in, get pre-aggregated, increment a Count-Min Sketch, and feed a bounded min-heap; a periodic job snapshots the heap to durable storage for the query service to serve. Client Apps Kafka View Counter Svc local pre-aggregation Count-Min Sketch Redis, per window Top-K Min-Heap Snapshot Job TopKSnapshot DB Query Svc served to millions of readers Client apps — every view event origin Kafka, Counter Svc, Snapshot Job — decoupled async pipeline Count-Min Sketch — the hot approximate-counting core Snapshot DB — durable, what queries actually hit Component Role Tech + why View Counter Svc Local pre-aggregation before the shared sketch Stateful stream consumer, batches per partition — because incrementing a shared Redis structure once per raw event would itself become the bottleneck under a viral spike; tradeoff is a few seconds of added lag before a count reflects everywhere. Count-Min Sketch Approximate per-video counting at fixed memory In-memory counter matrix in Redis — because exact counters need one key per distinct video (hundreds of millions), while a sketch is O(width × depth) regardless of cardinality; tradeoff is a bounded overestimation error. Top-K Min-Heap Maintains the current best K candidates Size-K min-heap, updated on sketch increments — because the sketch alone only answers \"what's this video's estimated count,\" not \"who's in the top K\"; tradeoff is the heap itself must be resharded/merged across counter-service instances. Snapshot Job Durable, queryable top-K per window/region Scheduled job writing to Postgres every few seconds — because millions of concurrent readers should never hit the live sketch or heap directly; tradeoff is a small, bounded staleness window. Scaling levers — cheapest to most complex Increase snapshot job frequency for fresher trending results low complexity Add read replicas / CDN cache in front of the snapshot query low complexity Shard the Counter Service by video_id partition to parallelize pre-aggregation medium complexity Widen the sketch matrix (more columns/rows) to tighten the error bound medium complexity Per-shard local heaps merged centrally for true horizontal scale of the heap itself high complexity — last resort Bottleneck Triggers at Exact fix Hot video overloads one shard A single viral video sends disproportionate events to one partition Local pre-aggregation in the Counter Service before the shared sketch ever sees the event Kafka consumer lag under spike Ingestion rate temporarily exceeds Counter Service throughput Let the snapshot go stale gracefully; expose snapshot_age_ms so staleness is visible, not silent Sketch memory growth Long-running windows never reset their matrix Rotate to a fresh sketch per window boundary (e.g. new hourly sketch every hour), discard the expired one Top-K freshness: real-time vs snapshot-batched Real-time Snapshot-batched Sketch accuracy vs memory footprint Tiny memory, looser bound Wider matrix, tighter bound Emits a view event per qualifying playback Durable ordered partitioned by video_id Local batching absorbs hot videos before the shared sketch Fixed-size counter matrix — d hash rows x w columns Size-K min-heap — evicts the current lowest estimate on a bigger candidate Extracts the heap on a fixed interval writes it durably Durable queryable cacheable top-K per window/region Reads only the snapshot — never the live sketch or heap"},{"u":"topics/questions/youtube-top-k.html","p":"Design Top-K Trending Videos (YouTube)","i":"step6-deepdive-cms","h":"Deep dive: Count-Min Sketch mechanics","t":"Step 6 of 6 — Deep Dives Deep dive: Count-Min Sketch mechanics A view event is hashed by d independent hash functions, each mapping to one column in its own row of a d × w counter matrix. One event increments d cells at once — one per row. To estimate a count, hash it the same way and take the minimum across those d cells — collisions can only inflate a cell, never deflate it, so the min is the tightest available upper bound. 4 HASH ROWS × 10 COLUMNS — one event, one cell per row view event ▸ Simulate view event (increment) ▸ Simulate estimate read (take MIN) Event arrives for a video — the video_id is fed into d independent hash functions Each hash picks one column per row — d cells total are touched, one per row, possibly shared with other videos All d cells increment by one — a single view event is a single write fanned out across rows Estimate reads hash the same video_id — landing on the same d cells deterministically Take the MIN across those cells — collisions only inflate a cell, so the minimum is the tightest upper bound on the true count Because two different videos can collide into the same cell, every cell is a sum of possibly multiple videos' counts — that's exactly why you take the min across rows instead of trusting any single cell: it's the row least corrupted by collisions. A single incoming view event for one video"},{"u":"topics/questions/youtube-top-k.html","p":"Design Top-K Trending Videos (YouTube)","i":"step6-deepdive-heavyhitters","h":"Deep dive: heavy hitters — exact vs approximate top-K","t":"Deep dive: heavy hitters — exact vs approximate top-K Exact top-K : full count per video + full sort. Falls apart at hundreds of millions of videos. Approximate : fixed sketch size + fixed-size heap — bounded memory. Exact top-K (impossible at this scale) One counter per distinct video — memory grows with the number of videos, not a fixed budget Every query needs a full sort (or a full scan) across all counters to find the top K A single global atomic counter per video becomes a hot lock under viral load Memory for hundreds of millions of videos is untenable to keep hot in memory Approximate top-K (what actually ships) Count-Min Sketch: fixed d × w matrix, independent of how many distinct videos exist A bounded min-heap of size K tracks only current candidates, updated incrementally as counts change No global lock — only local shard pre-aggregation plus periodic merges Error is small, known, and one-sided (only ever overestimates) View Event Stream Count-Min Sketch Min-Heap (size K) Top-K List error bound: overestimate by at most ε·N with probability 1−δ ε shrinks as matrix width w grows — w ≈ e/ε δ shrinks as matrix depth d grows — d ≈ ln(1/δ) wider + deeper matrix = tighter bound, more memory Cache — hot path Database — source of truth Async — queue / worker Stream has unbounded cardinality — hundreds of millions of distinct video_ids, no way to hold one counter each affordably Sketch compresses cardinality to fixed memory — d × w cells absorb the entire stream, accepting collisions as the cost Heap tracks only K live candidates — every sketch update checks if a video's new estimate beats the heap's current minimum Losing candidates are evicted — the heap never grows past size K, so its memory cost is constant Error is tunable, not accidental — picking w and d before launch is a deliberate memory-vs-accuracy trade, not a bug to fix later The error bound is one-sided by construction — a video's estimated count can be inflated by collisions but is mathematically guaranteed to never be undercounted, which is exactly why taking the min across rows is safe and why a video can rank into the top K it shouldn't, but never rank out of one it deserves. Every view event all videos unbounded cardinality Fixed-size counter matrix regardless of cardinality Only K candidates ever held in memory at once K videos ranked by estimated count"},{"u":"topics/questions/youtube-top-k.html","p":"Design Top-K Trending Videos (YouTube)","i":"step6-deepdive-windowed","h":"Deep dive: windowed vs all-time top-K","t":"Deep dive: windowed vs all-time top-K \"Trending in the last hour\" and \"most-viewed of all time\" are different questions that need different data structures — a single persistent sketch can't cheaply answer both. WINDOWED — \"top-K in the last 1h\" Bucket t-55m Bucket t-50m ... Bucket t-0m Merge last 12 buckets merged CMS = elementwise sum 1h Top-K Result ALL-TIME — \"top-K ever\" Persistent Global Sketch never resets, only grows All-Time Top-K Result Cache — hot path Database — source of truth Windowed queries need decay — a video's popularity 3 hours ago shouldn't count toward \"trending now,\" so old buckets must expire Time-bucketed sketches solve this — open a fresh small sketch every few minutes, merge the last N buckets by elementwise sum for the current window All-time queries need permanence — a single sketch that's never reset, growing (in accuracy demand, not physical size) over the video's life One structure can't do both cheaply — resetting periodically (needed for freshness) destroys history; never resetting (needed for permanence) can't express \"just the last hour\" Two structures, two purposes — bucketed+merged sketches for any rolling window, one persistent sketch (or exact aggregate, since all-time cardinality growth is slower) for all-time All-time counts are often worth computing exactly via a batch job (e.g. nightly aggregation from durable logs) instead of a sketch — the write rate against all-time is the same, but the tolerance for a slower, exact answer is much higher than for \"trending right now.\" Failure mode User impact Detection signal Mitigation Hot video overloads counter shard Ingestion latency spikes for that video's partition Per-partition consumer lag diverges from the rest Local pre-aggregation in the Counter Service absorbs the burst before it reaches the shared sketch Kafka consumer lag during traffic spike Trending list is stale, not wrong snapshot_age_ms exceeds the staleness SLA Degrade gracefully — serve the last good snapshot with its age clearly surfaced, never block reads on catch-up Sketch memory growth across window boundaries Memory footprint creeps if old buckets aren't cleared Redis memory usage trends upward without plateauing Hard TTL / explicit reset per bucket boundary — a new bucket sketch always starts from zero A fresh sketch is opened every 5 minutes Rolling window merges the last 12 buckets The most recent 5-minute bucket Merges 12 five-minute sketches into one logical 1h view — oldest bucket expires as a new one opens Top-K for the rolling 1h window One sketch never reset running since the video's existence Top-K across the video's entire lifetime"},{"u":"topics/questions/youtube-top-k.html","p":"Design Top-K Trending Videos (YouTube)","i":"interview-moves","h":"Interview moves","t":"Interview moves 01 \"Exact top-K at this scale is a trap — with hundreds of millions of distinct videos, I can't afford a counter per video, so I'd reach for approximate counting instead\" — states the core insight unprompted 02 \"I'd use a Count-Min Sketch: fixed-size matrix, d hash functions per event, take the min across rows to estimate any video's count\" — names the exact data structure and how reads work 03 \"The sketch alone doesn't give me top-K — I still need a bounded min-heap of size K sitting on top of it\" — shows you understand the sketch and the ranking layer are two separate concerns 04 \"The error is tunable and one-sided — wider and deeper matrices shrink epsilon and delta, at the cost of more memory\" — demonstrates you can reason about the accuracy/memory knob quantitatively 05 \"Windowed and all-time top-K need different structures — a bucketed, merged sketch for the rolling window, a persistent one for all-time, because one structure can't reset and never-reset at the same time\" — proactively surfaces a design fork most candidates miss 06 \"A single viral video shouldn't be able to overload one shard — I'd pre-aggregate locally before ever touching the shared sketch\" — surfaces the hot-partition failure mode before being asked"},{"u":"topics/questions/youtube-top-k.html","p":"Design Top-K Trending Videos (YouTube)","i":"follow-ups","h":"Follow-up questions","t":"Follow-up questions Why not just use an exact counter with atomic increments per video? Memory scales linearly with distinct video count — hundreds of millions of hot keys is untenable to keep in fast storage, and a full sort for top-K would be too slow regardless. Can the Count-Min Sketch ever undercount a video? No — collisions only add extra weight to a cell, they never remove it, so every cell is an upper bound and the minimum across rows is the tightest available upper bound. How do you size the sketch's width and depth? Width (w) controls the error magnitude epsilon, roughly e/epsilon; depth (d) controls the failure probability delta, roughly ln(1/delta) — pick both from your acceptable error and confidence targets. What happens if the snapshot job falls behind? The trending list goes stale, not wrong or unavailable — the API exposes snapshot age so clients can decide whether to show a \"may be outdated\" indicator. How would you support trending broken out by region or category? Key the sketch and heap by (region, window) or (category, window) instead of a single global instance — same mechanism, partitioned differently, more memory in exchange for finer granularity. Could a malicious actor game the trending list with bot views? Yes without mitigation — add a lightweight anomaly filter upstream of the Counter Service (rate limits per viewer/IP, watch-time thresholds) before events ever reach the sketch."},{"u":"topics/sd-introduction.html","p":"Introduction","i":"","h":"Overview","t":"Foundations What is a System Design Interview What interviewers are actually evaluating — and what separates a pass from a hire. 10 min read Start here The interviewer's mental checklist Can you drive the conversation without being prompted at every step? Do you clarify before designing — or do you jump straight to solutions? Can you make decisions under ambiguity and defend them with tradeoffs? Do you know what breaks at scale, and how to fix it? Are you someone this team would trust to own a complex system?"},{"u":"topics/sd-introduction.html","p":"Introduction","i":"mental-model","h":"Mental model","t":"Mental model A system design interview is not a quiz — it's a 45-minute simulation of your first week on the job, where your manager says \"we need to build X\" and watches how you think. There is no single correct answer. What's being evaluated is your engineering judgment : how you break down a vague problem, what questions you ask, which tradeoffs you reason about, and whether you can communicate a coherent design under time pressure."},{"u":"topics/sd-introduction.html","p":"Introduction","i":"core-concepts","h":"What you're actually scored on","t":"What you're actually scored on Problem clarity — scoping questions before the whiteboard Jumps to solution Scopes first Structured thinking — framework vs stream of consciousness Rambles Follows a framework Scale awareness — reasons about QPS/storage/latency before choosing Guesses Does the math Tradeoff fluency — names cost, not just the tool Name-drops tech Justifies + costs it Signal What it looks like Depth on demand When the interviewer probes a component, you can go 2 levels deeper Communication You narrate your thinking out loud so the interviewer follows along"},{"u":"topics/sd-introduction.html","p":"Introduction","i":"how-it-works","h":"Interview structure","t":"Interview structure Clarify 5 min Estimate 3 min High-level design 15 min Deep dive bottlenecks 15 min Wrap-up 5 min Client / gateway Cache — hot path Clarify: Nail down functional requirements, users, scale, and what you're NOT building Estimate: Back-of-envelope QPS, storage, and bandwidth — drives your component choices High-level design: Core components, APIs, data model, data flow Deep dive: Interviewer picks the hardest part; you go deep on bottlenecks and failure modes Wrap-up: Summarize tradeoffs, mention what you'd improve with more time Nail down functional requirements users scale and what you're explicitly NOT building Back-of-envelope QPS storage and bandwidth — these numbers justify every component choice that follows Sketch the core components APIs and data flow at a level everyone in the room can follow Interviewer steers you into the hardest part — this is where signal is actually collected Summarize tradeoffs made and what you'd improve with more time — leaves a strong last impression"},{"u":"topics/sd-introduction.html","p":"Introduction","i":"tradeoffs","h":"What trips candidates up","t":"What trips candidates up Strong candidates Ask about scale before drawing anything Explicitly state why they chose a technology Surface tradeoffs without being asked Drive the interview — don't wait for prompts Say \"I'd handle X like this, but there's a cost...\" Common failure modes Jump to drawing components before clarifying scope Name-drop tech without justifying the choice Only talk about happy-path flows Wait to be asked before mentioning a problem Treat it like a coding problem with one right answer"},{"u":"topics/sd-introduction.html","p":"Introduction","i":"when-to-use","h":"Interview formats by company","t":"Interview formats by company ✓ FAANG / MANGA (Google, Meta, Apple, Amazon, Netflix): 45-60 min, one problem, emphasis on scale and deep dives. Expect follow-ups on every decision. ⚠ Mid-size (Stripe, Airbnb, Uber, Databricks): Often more focused — may be a specific sub-problem. More emphasis on API design and data modeling. ⚠ Nvidia / Apple ML roles: May include ML system design (model serving, inference pipeline, feature store). Same framework, different components."},{"u":"topics/sd-introduction.html","p":"Introduction","i":"interview-moves","h":"Interview moves","t":"Interview moves 01 \"Before I start, let me clarify a few things\" — opens every interview; signals structured thinking immediately 02 \"I'll assume 10M DAU unless you'd like a different scale\" — shows you think in numbers, gives the interviewer a chance to redirect 03 \"I'm going to start with the high-level and then we can double-click on any component\" — frames the interview, shows you can manage time 04 \"I chose X here. The tradeoff is Y — I'm comfortable with that given our scale\" — every technology choice should sound like this 05 \"The bottleneck here is [X]. Here's how I'd address it...\" — say this before the interviewer has to ask; shows you see failure modes proactively"},{"u":"topics/sd-introduction.html","p":"Introduction","i":"follow-ups","h":"Common follow-up questions","t":"Common follow-up questions How is a system design interview different from a coding interview? No single correct answer. It tests ambiguity handling, breadth of knowledge, and communication — not algorithmic precision. You're evaluated on process and judgment, not output. What if I don't know a specific technology? Say so, and reason from first principles: \"I haven't used Kafka directly, but I'd think about this as a durable message queue — here's what I'd need from it...\" Interviewers care more about your thinking than your memorized tool list. How much should I draw vs talk? Draw the high-level architecture diagram early — it anchors the conversation. Narrate while drawing. Use the diagram to point to components when you deep-dive. Don't draw every detail upfront. What if the interviewer keeps redirecting me? They're telling you what they care about. Pivot immediately, go deep, then say \"should I continue here or move on to X?\" They're testing whether you can follow their lead. How long should I spend on requirements? 5 minutes max. Cover: core features (2-3), excluded features (1-2), users, scale, and SLA. Then start designing. Over-spending on requirements is a red flag for time management. Is there a minimum bar for knowing distributed systems theory? Yes: CAP theorem, eventual vs strong consistency, and common failure modes. You don't need to cite papers — you need to reason correctly about tradeoffs when they come up."},{"u":"topics/tech/api-gateway.html","p":"API Gateway","i":"","h":"Overview","t":"Key Technologies API Gateway A single front door that centralizes auth, rate limiting, routing, and request shaping so backend services don't each reimplement them. 12-18 min read Easy-Medium Say this in the first 2 minutes The gateway exists to centralize cross-cutting concerns — auth, rate limiting, routing — so every backend service isn't reimplementing them It validates JWTs/OAuth tokens once at the edge; backend services trust a verified identity header instead of re-checking credentials Rate limiting belongs at the edge (token bucket or sliding window) — stops abusive traffic before it reaches any backend It becomes a single point of failure and a latency bottleneck if not designed for horizontal scale — mention this proactively Request transformation (protocol translation, payload shaping) lets backends evolve independently of the public-facing contract"},{"u":"topics/tech/api-gateway.html","p":"API Gateway","i":"mental-model","h":"Mental model","t":"Mental model An API gateway is a building's front desk: it checks ID once, decides which floor you're allowed on, and meters how many people go up at a time — so no individual office has to re-verify every visitor itself."},{"u":"topics/tech/api-gateway.html","p":"API Gateway","i":"core-concepts","h":"Core responsibilities","t":"Core responsibilities Responsibility What it does Authentication Validates JWT/OAuth token signature and expiry once; forwards a trusted identity header downstream Rate limiting Token bucket or sliding window per client/API key, rejecting excess requests before they reach backends Routing Path-based ( /users/* → user service) or header-based (API version, tenant) routing to the right backend Request transformation Protocol translation (REST↔gRPC), request/response reshaping, header injection Observability Centralized logging, tracing, and metrics for every request crossing the edge TLS termination Decrypts HTTPS once at the edge; internal traffic can run plain HTTP/gRPC inside a trusted network"},{"u":"topics/tech/api-gateway.html","p":"API Gateway","i":"how-it-works","h":"Request flow","t":"Request flow Client + JWT API Gateway 1. Auth check (JWT) 2. Rate limit check 3. Route by path 4. Transform request 401 / 429 rejected here — never reaches backends User Service Order Service Payment Service Gateway — auth, rate limit, route, transform Backend services — trust the gateway's verified identity Authenticate: validate JWT signature and expiry against the issuer's public key — reject with 401 before touching any backend Rate limit: check the client's token bucket / sliding window — reject with 429 if exceeded Route: match the request path/headers to the target backend service Transform: reshape the request (strip internal headers, translate protocol) and forward with a trusted identity header Aggregate (optional): some gateways fan out to multiple services and merge responses (BFF pattern) for mobile-optimized payloads Client sends request with a bearer token 1. Verify JWT signature + expiry 2. Check rate limit bucket 3. Route by path Trusts the identity header from the gateway — no re-auth needed Trusts the identity header from the gateway Trusts the identity header from the gateway"},{"u":"topics/tech/api-gateway.html","p":"API Gateway","i":"tradeoffs","h":"Tradeoffs","t":"Tradeoffs Wins Auth/rate-limiting logic lives in one place, not duplicated across N services Backend services can change internal protocols without breaking public clients Centralized observability — every request is logged/traced at one chokepoint Simplifies client code — one endpoint instead of N service URLs Costs Single point of failure if not deployed redundantly across instances/regions Adds a network hop of latency to every request Can become a bottleneck under load if it does too much synchronous work Another piece of infrastructure to operate, version, and deploy safely Mitigate the SPOF/bottleneck risk: deploy multiple stateless gateway instances behind a load balancer, keep gateway logic lightweight (no heavy business logic), and consider regional gateways for global traffic to cut cross-region latency."},{"u":"topics/tech/api-gateway.html","p":"API Gateway","i":"when-to-use","h":"When to use","t":"When to use ✓ USE: any system with more than a couple of backend services that all need auth, rate limiting, or a unified public API surface. ✗ AVOID: putting heavy business logic or stateful processing inside the gateway — it should stay thin; logic belongs in backend services. ⚠ WATCH OUT: a misconfigured gateway (bad routing rule, broken auth check) takes down access to every backend simultaneously — treat gateway config changes with the same rigor as a DB migration."},{"u":"topics/tech/api-gateway.html","p":"API Gateway","i":"interview-moves","h":"Interview moves","t":"Interview moves 01 \"I'd centralize JWT validation and rate limiting at the gateway so none of the five backend services reimplement that logic\" — names the core justification immediately 02 \"The gateway becomes a single point of failure, so I'd run multiple stateless instances behind a load balancer, and add regional gateways if we're global\" — proactively addresses the obvious follow-up 03 \"Rate limiting happens at the edge with a token bucket per API key, so abusive traffic never reaches the backend services at all\" — shows where defense should live 04 \"I'd keep the gateway thin — routing, auth, rate limiting only — and push business logic into the services so the gateway doesn't become a deployment bottleneck\" — architectural discipline"},{"u":"topics/tech/api-gateway.html","p":"API Gateway","i":"follow-ups","h":"Common follow-up questions","t":"Common follow-up questions How does the gateway avoid becoming a bottleneck? Keep it stateless and horizontally scaled behind a load balancer; avoid synchronous heavy work (DB calls, business logic). Cache auth verification keys locally instead of calling an auth service per request. What's the difference between an API gateway and a load balancer? A load balancer distributes traffic across instances of one service. A gateway routes across many different services and adds auth, rate limiting, and transformation — often sits in front of multiple load balancers. How would you implement rate limiting at the gateway? Token bucket or sliding window counter keyed by API key/user ID, backed by Redis for shared state across gateway instances. Return 429 with a Retry-After header when exceeded. How do backend services trust the identity the gateway asserts? The gateway signs an internal trusted header (or re-issues a short-lived internal JWT) after validating the original token — services verify that signature instead of re-validating the original credential. What is the Backend-for-Frontend (BFF) pattern and how does it relate? A BFF is a gateway variant tailored per client type (mobile vs web) that aggregates multiple backend calls into one optimized response — reduces client-side round trips and over-fetching. How do you handle gateway config changes safely? Treat routing/auth rules as versioned config deployed through CI/CD with canary rollout and automated rollback — a bad rule can silently break every service behind it at once."},{"u":"topics/tech/cassandra.html","p":"Cassandra","i":"","h":"Overview","t":"Key Technologies Cassandra A wide-column, AP-leaning store built for write-heavy, high-availability workloads — schema is designed around the query, not the entity. 15-20 min read Medium-Hard Say this in the first 2 minutes Cassandra is query-first schema design — you model tables around the exact queries you'll run, denormalizing aggressively, because there are no joins Partition key decides which node owns the data; clustering columns decide sort order within that partition Consistency is tunable per-query (ONE, QUORUM, ALL) — it's not a fixed property of the database Writes are fast by design: append to commit log + memtable, no read-before-write, no random disk I/O on the write path It's AP-leaning on the CAP spectrum — favors availability and partition tolerance over strict consistency by default"},{"u":"topics/tech/cassandra.html","p":"Cassandra","i":"mental-model","h":"Mental model","t":"Mental model Cassandra is a write-optimized ledger, not a queryable spreadsheet. You don't ask it new questions after the fact — you decide the questions up front, build a table per question, and it answers each one in constant time because the data is already laid out for that exact lookup."},{"u":"topics/tech/cassandra.html","p":"Cassandra","i":"core-concepts","h":"Wide-column model","t":"Wide-column model Concept What it means Partition key Determines which node(s) own the row — all rows with the same partition key live together, on the same node Clustering columns Sort order of rows within a partition — enables efficient range scans (e.g. latest 50 events for a user) Denormalization Same data duplicated across multiple tables, one per query pattern — storage is cheap, joins are not available No joins Query patterns must be known at schema design time; changing access patterns later often means a new table Tunable consistency Cassandra default posture — consistency vs availability (CAP) Strong consistency Availability Consistency level Behavior ONE Acked by a single replica — fastest, weakest guarantee, used for high-throughput non-critical writes QUORUM Acked by majority of replicas (e.g. 2 of 3) — common production default, balances latency and consistency ALL Acked by every replica — strongest read-after-write guarantee, lowest availability (any replica down blocks the request) Read QUORUM + write QUORUM gives you strong consistency for that specific query, since the read and write replica sets are guaranteed to overlap by at least one node. This is set per-query, not globally."},{"u":"topics/tech/cassandra.html","p":"Cassandra","i":"how-it-works","h":"Write path","t":"Write path Write request INSERT Commit log sequential disk write Memtable in-memory, sorted flush SSTable immutable on-disk file merge Compaction reclaims space Write acked after commit log + memtable — no read-before-write Commit log — durability, replayed on crash recovery Memtable — in-memory, what makes writes fast SSTable + compaction — eventual on-disk durable form Commit log: write appended sequentially to disk first — guarantees durability if the node crashes before flush Memtable: write also applied to an in-memory sorted structure — this is what makes the write path so fast, no random I/O Ack: write is acknowledged to the client once both succeed, satisfying the requested consistency level across replicas Flush: when the memtable fills up, it's flushed to disk as an immutable SSTable Compaction: background process merges SSTables over time, discarding overwritten or tombstoned (deleted) data Write request arrives at a coordinator node Append-only sequential write — durability before the memtable ack In-memory sorted structure — write is acked once both commit log and memtable succeed Memtable flushed to an immutable file on disk once it's full Background process merges SSTables discards overwritten/deleted data"},{"u":"topics/tech/cassandra.html","p":"Cassandra","i":"tradeoffs","h":"Tradeoffs","t":"Tradeoffs Wins Extremely fast writes — no read-before-write, sequential disk I/O Linear horizontal scalability — add nodes, capacity grows proportionally No single point of failure — masterless, peer-to-peer architecture Multi-region active-active writes are a first-class capability Costs No joins — every new query pattern often needs a new denormalized table Eventual consistency by default — strong consistency costs latency (QUORUM/ALL) Deletes create tombstones that must be compacted away — can degrade read performance if mismanaged Schema changes are harder once query patterns shift — redesign means backfilling new tables"},{"u":"topics/tech/cassandra.html","p":"Cassandra","i":"when-to-use","h":"When to use","t":"When to use ✓ USE: write-heavy, high-availability workloads with known query patterns — time-series/IoT sensor data, activity feeds, messaging history. ✗ AVOID: ad-hoc analytical queries, workloads needing joins, or anything where the access pattern isn't known upfront and changes frequently. ⚠ WATCH OUT: heavy delete-and-rewrite workloads generate tombstones that slow reads until compaction catches up — design around updates/appends where possible."},{"u":"topics/tech/cassandra.html","p":"Cassandra","i":"interview-moves","h":"Interview moves","t":"Interview moves 01 \"I'd design the schema around our three known query patterns first, denormalizing into separate tables, since Cassandra has no joins\" — shows you understand query-first modeling, the core Cassandra skill 02 \"For this time-series workload — writes far outnumber reads, need horizontal scale and multi-region availability — Cassandra fits better than Postgres\" — justifies the choice with the actual access pattern 03 \"I'd use QUORUM consistency on both reads and writes here since we need read-after-write correctness for this specific table\" — shows consistency is a per-query dial, not a fixed property 04 \"The write path never does a read-before-write — that's specifically why Cassandra writes are so much faster than a B-tree-indexed relational table\" — internals-level justification"},{"u":"topics/tech/cassandra.html","p":"Cassandra","i":"follow-ups","h":"Common follow-up questions","t":"Common follow-up questions Why is Cassandra's write path so much faster than a typical relational DB? No read-before-write and no random disk I/O — it's a sequential append to a commit log plus an in-memory write, with reconciliation (compaction) deferred to the background. How do you choose a partition key? High cardinality to spread load evenly across nodes, but matching your primary query's filter (e.g. user_id) so a single query hits one partition instead of scanning the whole cluster. What's a tombstone and why does it matter? A marker written on delete instead of immediate removal — reads must skip past tombstones until compaction physically removes them. Too many tombstones in a partition slows reads significantly. How does Cassandra achieve high availability with no master? Peer-to-peer gossip protocol — every node can accept reads/writes for the data it owns or coordinates. Replication factor (commonly 3) spreads copies so node failure doesn't mean data loss. What happens if you need a new query pattern you didn't design for? Usually means creating a new denormalized table for that pattern and backfilling it — there's no ad-hoc join to fall back on. This is the main schema-evolution cost of the model. Cassandra vs DynamoDB — when would you pick one over the other? Cassandra for multi-region active-active writes outside AWS or to avoid vendor lock-in. DynamoDB for zero-ops on AWS with comparable performance and built-in autoscaling."},{"u":"topics/tech/dynamodb.html","p":"DynamoDB","i":"","h":"Overview","t":"Key Technologies DynamoDB A fully managed, partition-key-sharded NoSQL store built for predictable-access-pattern workloads at massive scale with zero ops overhead. 12-18 min read Medium Say this in the first 2 minutes Partition key hashes to a physical partition — pick a high-cardinality key or you create a hot partition GSI = different partition/sort key, its own throughput, eventually consistent; LSI = same partition key, alternate sort key, can be strongly consistent, must exist at table creation Two capacity modes: provisioned (predictable cost, needs autoscaling) vs on-demand (pay-per-request, handles spiky traffic with zero planning) Single-table design is the advanced DynamoDB pattern — multiple entity types in one table to support several access patterns with one query each It's fully managed — no ops overhead, but you trade away joins and ad-hoc queries for that simplicity"},{"u":"topics/tech/dynamodb.html","p":"DynamoDB","i":"mental-model","h":"Mental model","t":"Mental model DynamoDB is a giant filing cabinet sorted by a label you choose up front. If you know exactly which drawer (partition key) you need, retrieval is instant — but you can't flip through every drawer looking for something; you must have planned your labeling system before you needed it."},{"u":"topics/tech/dynamodb.html","p":"DynamoDB","i":"core-concepts","h":"Partition keys and hot partitions","t":"Partition keys and hot partitions Concept What it means Partition key Hashed to determine the physical partition — all items with the same key live together Sort key (optional) Orders items within a partition, enables range queries (e.g. all orders for a user, newest first) Hot partition Low-cardinality or skewed partition key (e.g. a single popular tenant_id) concentrates traffic on one physical partition, throttling it Choosing a key Pick high-cardinality, evenly-accessed values — composite keys (e.g. userId#date ) spread load when a natural key alone is too coarse GSI vs LSI Global Secondary Index (GSI) Local Secondary Index (LSI) Partition key Different from the base table Same as the base table Sort key Different attribute, your choice Alternate attribute, same partition Consistency Eventually consistent only Can be strongly consistent When created Anytime, even after table creation Only at table creation — cannot be added later Capacity Its own provisioned throughput Shares the base table's throughput LSI's \"must exist at table creation\" constraint is the single biggest reason teams default to GSIs — you rarely know every access pattern upfront, and GSIs let you add new query paths as requirements evolve."},{"u":"topics/tech/dynamodb.html","p":"DynamoDB","i":"how-it-works","h":"Partition routing and capacity modes","t":"Partition routing and capacity modes PutItem userId: \"u882\" Hash(partition key) internal routing Partition A even load Partition B even load Partition C hot — throttled Healthy partitions — high-cardinality key spreads load Hot partition — low-cardinality key concentrates traffic Provisioned + auto-scaling You set read/write capacity units; auto-scaling adjusts within bounds based on utilization Cheaper at steady, predictable traffic Requires capacity planning and tuning scaling policies Can still throttle on sudden spikes faster than auto-scaling reacts On-demand Pay per request, no capacity planning needed Instantly absorbs traffic spikes — scales to previous peak automatically More expensive per-request at sustained high, predictable volume Best for unpredictable or new workloads where you don't know the traffic shape yet Write request includes the item's partition key e.g. userId = 'u882' DynamoDB hashes the partition key to deterministically pick a physical partition High-cardinality keys spread evenly — healthy partition Healthy evenly loaded partition A low-cardinality key (e.g. status field) concentrates traffic here — throttles regardless of total table capacity"},{"u":"topics/tech/dynamodb.html","p":"DynamoDB","i":"tradeoffs","h":"Tradeoffs","t":"Tradeoffs Wins Fully managed — no servers, patching, or cluster ops Single-digit millisecond latency at virtually unlimited scale Seamless horizontal scaling via automatic partition splitting Built-in global tables for multi-region active-active replication Costs No joins, no ad-hoc queries — access patterns must be designed upfront AWS vendor lock-in Hot partitions throttle even when overall table capacity is fine Single-table design (the advanced pattern) has a real learning curve and reduces query flexibility for ease of scaling"},{"u":"topics/tech/dynamodb.html","p":"DynamoDB","i":"when-to-use","h":"When to use","t":"When to use ✓ USE: massive-scale workloads with well-known, stable access patterns — session stores, shopping carts, gaming leaderboards, IoT device state — especially already on AWS. ✗ AVOID: ad-hoc analytical queries, evolving/unknown access patterns, or anything requiring complex joins across entities. ⚠ WATCH OUT: a low-cardinality partition key (e.g. status: \"active\"/\"inactive\") creates a hot partition that throttles regardless of how much total table capacity you provision."},{"u":"topics/tech/dynamodb.html","p":"DynamoDB","i":"interview-moves","h":"Interview moves","t":"Interview moves 01 \"I'd pick userId as the partition key here — high cardinality, evenly distributed — instead of something like region, which would create hot partitions\" — shows you understand the hot-partition failure mode concretely 02 \"I'd start with on-demand capacity since we don't know the traffic shape yet, and switch to provisioned with autoscaling once we have steady-state data\" — pragmatic, justified capacity choice 03 \"For this 'get all orders by status' query I'd add a GSI rather than an LSI, since we'll likely need new indexes later and LSIs must be defined at table creation\" — correct, specific tradeoff reasoning 04 \"I'd briefly mention single-table design as an option here, but keep it simple with separate tables unless we have a specific reason to optimize for fewer round trips\" — shows awareness without over-engineering"},{"u":"topics/tech/dynamodb.html","p":"DynamoDB","i":"follow-ups","h":"Common follow-up questions","t":"Common follow-up questions What is single-table design and why would you use it? Storing multiple entity types in one DynamoDB table using generic partition/sort key names, so a single query can retrieve related entities together. Reduces round trips but adds real schema complexity. How do you fix a hot partition? Add a random or calculated suffix to the partition key to spread writes (write sharding), or redesign the key to a higher-cardinality attribute that better matches actual access patterns. Why can't you add an LSI after table creation? LSIs share the base table's partition structure and storage internally — DynamoDB needs to know about every LSI when items are first written so it can maintain them inline, hence the upfront requirement. DynamoDB vs Cassandra — how do you choose? DynamoDB for zero-ops on AWS with comparable performance. Cassandra for multi-cloud/on-prem flexibility or when you specifically need tunable consistency per query rather than DynamoDB's simpler consistency model. How does DynamoDB handle strong consistency? Reads default to eventually consistent (cheaper, faster) but support a strongly consistent read flag that reads from the leader replica — costs roughly 2x the read capacity of an eventually consistent read. What's DynamoDB Streams used for? A change log of item-level modifications, consumable by Lambda or Kinesis — common for triggering downstream processing, replicating to Elasticsearch, or building event-driven workflows off table changes."},{"u":"topics/tech/elasticsearch.html","p":"Elasticsearch","i":"","h":"Overview","t":"Key Technologies Elasticsearch A distributed search engine built on an inverted index — fed from your real database, never the source of truth itself. 15-20 min read Medium Say this in the first 2 minutes Elasticsearch is a secondary, read-optimized index — never the system of record It's fast at full-text search because of the inverted index: term → list of doc IDs, not row scans Data lands in ES via CDC or dual-write from the real DB, with a near-real-time refresh delay (~1s default) Sharding (primary + replica) gives both horizontal scale and read availability A relational DB's native full-text search (e.g. Postgres tsvector) is \"good enough\" up to ~10M rows — justify ES with relevance scoring, fuzzy match, or facets, not just \"search\""},{"u":"topics/tech/elasticsearch.html","p":"Elasticsearch","i":"mental-model","h":"Mental model","t":"Mental model A relational DB index speeds up \"find the row where id = 5.\" An inverted index flips that: it answers \"find every document containing this word\" by storing the word once and pointing to every document it appears in — like a book's back-of-the-page index instead of reading cover to cover."},{"u":"topics/tech/elasticsearch.html","p":"Elasticsearch","i":"core-concepts","h":"Inverted index","t":"Inverted index A forward index maps doc → words. An inverted index maps word → docs. Search becomes a set lookup instead of a scan. Document \"fast distributed search\" Analyzer tokenize + normalize Inverted index fast → [doc7, doc12, doc88] distribut → [doc2, doc7, doc41] search → [doc7, doc9, doc12] QUERY TIME Query \"fast search\" Intersect + score (BM25) doc7 (both terms) ranks highest Inverted index — term → doc ID postings list Query execution — set intersection, not a table scan ES vs a database's native search Need Pick Exact match, small dataset (<10M rows) DB native (WHERE, LIKE, tsvector) — one less system to operate Relevance-ranked full-text search Elasticsearch — BM25 scoring out of the box Faceted search / aggregations at scale Elasticsearch — built for filter + aggregate combos Fuzzy matching, typo tolerance, autocomplete Elasticsearch — edit-distance and n-gram analyzers Strong read-your-writes consistency DB native — ES refresh interval adds lag (see below) Raw document submitted for indexing Analyzer: lowercases removes stopwords stems words (running → run) Each term now maps to the list of document IDs containing it Query 'fast search' is analyzed the same way as documents Intersect posting lists for each term score by relevance (BM25) return ranked results"},{"u":"topics/tech/elasticsearch.html","p":"Elasticsearch","i":"how-it-works","h":"Sharding","t":"Sharding Query index: products Primary shard 0 routes by doc_id hash Primary shard 1 Primary shard 2 Replica 0' Replica 1' Replica 2' Primary shards — fixed at index creation, can't reshard without reindexing Replica shards — read scale + failover Primary shard count is set at index creation and is expensive to change — pick it based on projected data volume, not current volume. Replica count can be changed anytime. Client request routed by the coordinating node to relevant shards Primary shard 0 — owns a routed subset of docs e.g. hash(doc_id) % 3 == 0 Primary shard 1 Primary shard 2 Replica of shard 0 — serves reads promoted if primary node fails Replica of shard 1 Replica of shard 2"},{"u":"topics/tech/elasticsearch.html","p":"Elasticsearch","i":"tradeoffs","h":"Tradeoffs","t":"Tradeoffs Wins Relevance-ranked full-text search out of the box (BM25) Fast faceted/aggregation queries across large datasets Horizontally scales via sharding, built-in replication Fuzzy matching, autocomplete, geo queries built in Costs Near-real-time only — default 1s refresh interval before new docs are searchable Operationally heavy: JVM tuning, shard sizing, cluster management No real transactions or strong consistency — eventually consistent by design Must stay in sync with the real DB — adds a CDC/dual-write pipeline to maintain"},{"u":"topics/tech/elasticsearch.html","p":"Elasticsearch","i":"when-to-use","h":"When to use","t":"When to use ✓ USE: product search with relevance ranking, log/event analytics, autocomplete, or any query mixing full-text + filters + aggregations at scale. ✗ AVOID: as your primary transactional store — no real ACID guarantees, and losing the index just means rebuilding it from the source of truth. ⚠ WATCH OUT: read-your-write expectations break — a user who just created a record may not see it in search results for up to the refresh interval (~1s default, tunable but costs indexing throughput)."},{"u":"topics/tech/elasticsearch.html","p":"Elasticsearch","i":"interview-moves","h":"Interview moves","t":"Interview moves 01 \"Postgres stays the source of truth; I'd feed Elasticsearch via CDC so search never blocks the write path\" — the single most important sentence in any ES design 02 \"Given we need typo-tolerant search with relevance ranking, a DB LIKE query won't cut it — that's exactly what ES's inverted index and BM25 scoring are for\" — justifies the technology with the specific requirement 03 \"There's a near-real-time gap — if the product spec needs read-your-writes, I'd flag that ES isn't the right fit for that specific flow\" — shows you understand the consistency tradeoff, not just the happy path 04 \"I'd size primary shards for projected 2-year data volume since resharding means reindexing\" — operational foresight"},{"u":"topics/tech/elasticsearch.html","p":"Elasticsearch","i":"follow-ups","h":"Common follow-up questions","t":"Common follow-up questions How do you keep Elasticsearch in sync with the source database? CDC (Debezium reading the DB's write-ahead log) into a queue, then a consumer indexes into ES. Dual-write is simpler but risks the two stores drifting apart on partial failure. What happens if Elasticsearch goes down? Search degrades or fails, but the source DB is untouched since ES isn't authoritative. Recovery is a reindex from the DB or CDC replay — no data is permanently lost. Why can't you just reshard an ES index like you would a database? Primary shard count is fixed at index creation because document routing (hash → shard) depends on it. Changing it requires creating a new index with the new shard count and reindexing all data. How does Elasticsearch rank results? BM25 by default — scores based on term frequency, inverse document frequency, and field length normalization. Custom scoring functions (boosts, decay by recency) can be layered on top. When would plain Postgres full-text search be enough? Up to roughly 10M rows with moderate query volume and no need for complex facets — Postgres tsvector + GIN index handles it without adding an entire extra system to operate. What's the refresh interval and why does it matter? New/updated documents aren't searchable until the next refresh (default ~1s) — it batches index segment writes for efficiency. Lowering it improves freshness but increases indexing overhead."},{"u":"topics/tech/flink.html","p":"Flink","i":"","h":"Overview","t":"Key Technologies Flink A true stream processing engine — continuous computation over unbounded data, with windowing and checkpointed state for low-latency exactly-once results. 15-20 min read Hard Say this in the first 2 minutes Flink treats streams as the primitive — batch is just a stream with a known end, not the other way around Windowing buckets an infinite stream into finite chunks for aggregation: tumbling (fixed, non-overlapping), sliding (fixed, overlapping), session (gap-based, dynamic) Keyed state lets each key (e.g. user_id) maintain its own running state across events, partitioned like Kafka partitions Checkpointing periodically snapshots all operator state — recovery replays from the last checkpoint, giving exactly-once semantics Reach for Flink over Kafka Streams or Spark Streaming when you need true low-latency processing with complex, large keyed state"},{"u":"topics/tech/flink.html","p":"Flink","i":"mental-model","h":"Mental model","t":"Mental model Batch processing is reading a finished book cover to cover. Stream processing is reading a book as pages arrive forever — you maintain a running understanding (state) and periodically save your place (checkpoint) so a crash doesn't mean starting over."},{"u":"topics/tech/flink.html","p":"Flink","i":"core-concepts","h":"Windowing","t":"Windowing Windows bucket an unbounded stream into finite chunks so aggregations (count, sum, average) have a defined scope. Event stream (time →) Tumbling — fixed, non-overlapping [0-5min] [5-10min] [10-15min] Sliding — fixed, overlapping (size 5min, slide 2.5min) Session — gap-based, closes after inactivity gap → Window type Behavior Use case Tumbling Fixed size, non-overlapping — each event belongs to exactly one window \"Requests per 5-minute bucket\" Sliding Fixed size, overlapping by a slide interval — an event can belong to multiple windows \"Rolling 5-min average, updated every 30s\" Session Dynamic size, closes after a gap of inactivity — no fixed boundary \"Group a user's clicks into one session\""},{"u":"topics/tech/flink.html","p":"Flink","i":"how-it-works","h":"Stream → window → sink, with checkpointing","t":"Stream → window → sink, with checkpointing Event stream Kafka source keyBy(userId) keyed state Tumbling window count() per 1min Sink DB / dashboard Checkpoint barrier → durable state snapshot Keyed state — per-key, partitioned like Kafka partitions Windowing — buckets the stream for aggregation Checkpointing — periodic durable snapshot for recovery Source: consume an unbounded stream, typically from Kafka keyBy: partition the stream by key so each key's state and processing is independent and parallelizable Window: bucket events by time (tumbling/sliding/session) and apply an aggregation function Checkpoint: Flink periodically injects a barrier into the stream; as it flows through each operator, that operator snapshots its state to durable storage Recover: on failure, restart from the last checkpoint and replay the source from that offset — combined with idempotent sinks, this gives exactly-once results Unbounded source — e.g. a Kafka topic of click events Partitions the stream by key (e.g. user_id) — each key maintains independent state Buckets events into time windows applies the aggregation function Window results written out — DB dashboard alert system Periodic snapshot of all operator state — barriers flow through the pipeline so every operator's state is captured at a consistent point"},{"u":"topics/tech/flink.html","p":"Flink","i":"tradeoffs","h":"Tradeoffs","t":"Tradeoffs Wins True low-latency stream processing — milliseconds, not micro-batches Rich, large keyed state managed natively with efficient checkpointing Exactly-once semantics via checkpoint barriers, even across complex pipelines Flexible windowing (event-time, watermarks for out-of-order events) Costs Steep operational learning curve — cluster, state backends, checkpoint tuning Heavier to run than Kafka Streams for simple stateless transformations Large state can make checkpoints slow if not tuned (incremental checkpointing helps) Smaller ecosystem/talent pool than Spark"},{"u":"topics/tech/flink.html","p":"Flink","i":"when-to-use","h":"When to use","t":"When to use ✓ USE: true low-latency stream processing with complex stateful logic — fraud detection, real-time recommendations, sessionization, anomaly detection. ✗ AVOID: simple stateless filtering/transformation embedded in one service — Kafka Streams is a lighter-weight library with no separate cluster to operate. ⚠ WATCH OUT: out-of-order or late-arriving events need watermarks and an explicit lateness policy, or window results will be silently wrong/incomplete."},{"u":"topics/tech/flink.html","p":"Flink","i":"interview-moves","h":"Interview moves","t":"Interview moves 01 \"Given we need sub-second fraud signals with large per-user state, I'd reach for Flink over Spark Streaming's micro-batch model\" — justifies Flink with the specific latency and state requirement 02 \"I'd use a sliding window here since the dashboard needs a rolling average that updates more often than the window size\" — shows you can match window type to the actual product requirement 03 \"Checkpointing gives us exactly-once recovery — if a task manager crashes, we restart from the last checkpoint and replay from that Kafka offset\" — concrete fault-tolerance explanation 04 \"I'd flag that late-arriving events need a watermark strategy — otherwise a window might close and emit results before all its events have actually arrived\" — surfaces a subtlety most candidates miss"},{"u":"topics/tech/flink.html","p":"Flink","i":"follow-ups","h":"Common follow-up questions","t":"Common follow-up questions How does Flink achieve exactly-once semantics? Checkpoint barriers flow through the pipeline; every operator snapshots state when the barrier passes. On failure, the job restarts from the last consistent checkpoint and replays the source from that point. Flink vs Spark Streaming — what's the core difference? Flink is a native stream processor with true per-event processing. Spark Structured Streaming uses micro-batching — processes small batches on an interval, which adds latency Flink avoids. Flink vs Kafka Streams — when would you pick Flink? Kafka Streams is a lightweight library embedded in your app, simpler to deploy. Flink is a separate cluster better suited for complex stateful jobs, large state, and advanced windowing/watermark needs. What's a watermark and why does it matter? A watermark is Flink's estimate of \"we've seen all events up to time T\" — it lets windows close and emit results despite some events arriving out of order, with a configurable allowed lateness. How does keyed state scale? State is partitioned by key, similar to Kafka partitions — each parallel task owns a subset of keys and their state, so scaling out adds more parallel state partitions. What happens if checkpoints become too slow due to large state? Use incremental checkpointing (RocksDB state backend) which only persists state deltas since the last checkpoint, rather than a full snapshot every time."},{"u":"topics/tech/kafka.html","p":"Kafka","i":"","h":"Overview","t":"Key Technologies Kafka A distributed, partitioned, replicated commit log — partitions are the unit of parallelism, consumer groups are the unit of scale. 15-20 min read Medium Say this in the first 2 minutes A topic is split into partitions — ordering is guaranteed only within a partition, never across the whole topic Partition count is your parallelism ceiling: more consumers than partitions in a group just sit idle Each partition is consumed by exactly one consumer within a consumer group — that's how horizontal scaling works At-least-once + idempotent consumer is the practical default; exactly-once (idempotent producer + transactions) is for when duplicates are unacceptable and can't be deduped downstream Consumer lag (how far behind the latest offset a group is) is your single most important Kafka health metric"},{"u":"topics/tech/kafka.html","p":"Kafka","i":"mental-model","h":"Mental model","t":"Mental model A Kafka topic is a set of append-only logs, not a queue. Consumers don't remove messages — they track their own bookmark (offset) and can rewind it. That's what makes replay, multiple independent consumers, and durability all fall out of the same simple structure."},{"u":"topics/tech/kafka.html","p":"Kafka","i":"core-concepts","h":"Partitions and consumer groups","t":"Partitions and consumer groups Concept What it means Partition An ordered, append-only log. The unit of parallelism — more partitions = more concurrent consumers possible Ordering guarantee Only within a single partition. Across partitions, no ordering is guaranteed at all Partition key Hash of the key picks the partition — same key always lands on the same partition, preserving per-key order Consumer group A set of consumers sharing the work of a topic — Kafka assigns each partition to exactly one consumer in the group Consumer lag Difference between the latest produced offset and a consumer group's committed offset — the key health signal Rebalancing Reassigning partitions across consumers when one joins/leaves the group — briefly pauses consumption Picking partition count: if you expect to scale to 12 consumers eventually, provision at least 12 partitions up front — you can add partitions later, but that breaks key-based ordering for existing keys since the hash-to-partition mapping changes."},{"u":"topics/tech/kafka.html","p":"Kafka","i":"how-it-works","h":"Producer → partitions → consumer group","t":"Producer → partitions → consumer group Producer hash(key) → partition Partition 0 offsets 0..n Partition 1 Partition 2 Consumer 1 reads partition 0 Consumer 2 reads partition 1 Consumer 3 reads partition 2 consumer group: order-processing Partitions — ordered logs, the parallelism unit Consumer group — 1:1 partition-to-consumer mapping Produce: producer hashes the message key, deterministically picks a partition Append: message is written to the end of that partition's log, replicated to followers before ack (if acks=all ) Assign: Kafka's group coordinator assigns each partition to exactly one consumer in the group Consume + commit: consumer reads sequentially, processes, commits its offset — restart resumes from the last committed offset Scale: adding a 4th consumer to a 3-partition topic leaves it idle — partition count is the hard ceiling on parallelism Producer hashes the message key to pick a partition Partition 0 — append-only log ordered within itself Partition 1 — independent ordered log Partition 2 — independent ordered log Consumer 1 — owns partition 0 exclusively within this group Consumer 2 — owns partition 1 exclusively within this group Consumer 3 — owns partition 2 exclusively within this group Consumer group boundary — Kafka guarantees each partition maps to exactly one consumer inside it"},{"u":"topics/tech/kafka.html","p":"Kafka","i":"exactly-once","h":"Delivery guarantees","t":"Delivery guarantees At-least-once + idempotent consumer Default and simplest — retries on failure may redeliver a message Consumer dedups by a unique key (e.g. UPSERT on order_id) so reprocessing is harmless Lower overhead, easier to reason about, scales further Good enough for the vast majority of real systems Exactly-once semantics (EOS) Idempotent producer (sequence numbers dedupe retries) + transactional writes across produce + offset commit Needed when duplicates genuinely can't be tolerated and can't be deduped downstream (e.g. financial ledger entries) Higher latency and throughput cost — full transaction coordination per batch Only spans Kafka-to-Kafka; doesn't make external side effects (an API call) exactly-once Most interview answers should default to \"at-least-once with an idempotent consumer\" and only reach for EOS when explicitly pressed on duplicate-sensitive correctness (e.g. payments)."},{"u":"topics/tech/kafka.html","p":"Kafka","i":"tradeoffs","h":"Tradeoffs","t":"Tradeoffs Wins Decouples producers from consumers — independent scaling and deploys Durable, replayable log — new consumers can read from the beginning Massive throughput — sequential disk I/O, batched writes Multiple independent consumer groups can read the same topic Costs No ordering across partitions — must design partition keys carefully Operational complexity: brokers, ZooKeeper/KRaft, partition rebalancing Rebalancing pauses consumption briefly when group membership changes Overkill for simple point-to-point task queues (<10k msgs/sec) — SQS/RabbitMQ is simpler"},{"u":"topics/tech/kafka.html","p":"Kafka","i":"when-to-use","h":"When to use","t":"When to use ✓ USE: event-driven fan-out (one event, many independent consumers), audit/event log, decoupling write-heavy producers from slower consumers, >100k events/sec. ✗ AVOID: simple request-response or point-to-point task queues at modest scale — a managed queue (SQS) is operationally simpler and sufficient. ⚠ WATCH OUT: growing consumer lag means consumers can't keep up with producers — alert on it before it becomes hours of backlog, not after."},{"u":"topics/tech/kafka.html","p":"Kafka","i":"interview-moves","h":"Interview moves","t":"Interview moves 01 \"I'd partition by user_id so all events for one user stay ordered, while different users process in parallel\" — shows you understand the ordering-vs-parallelism tradeoff explicitly 02 \"Partition count sets the parallelism ceiling — I'd provision for 2-3x our expected consumer count up front since adding partitions later breaks existing key-to-partition mapping\" — concrete capacity planning 03 \"At-least-once with an idempotent consumer is the default here — exactly-once only matters if duplicates are truly unrecoverable, like a payment ledger\" — calibrates the right level of guarantee instead of over-engineering 04 \"I'd alert on consumer lag, not just broker uptime — a healthy cluster with a backed-up consumer group is still a production incident\" — operational maturity"},{"u":"topics/tech/kafka.html","p":"Kafka","i":"follow-ups","h":"Common follow-up questions","t":"Common follow-up questions Why does Kafka only guarantee ordering within a partition? Each partition is a single append-only log handled by one broker leader at a time. Guaranteeing global order across partitions would mean serializing all writes through one node, killing throughput. What causes consumer rebalancing and why is it disruptive? Triggered when a consumer joins, leaves, or is considered dead (missed heartbeat). All partitions get reassigned, briefly pausing consumption across the whole group during the rebalance. How do you choose the number of partitions? Base it on target throughput ÷ per-partition throughput, and your expected max consumer count, since consumers beyond partition count sit idle. Err generous — you can add but not safely shrink. What's the difference between Kafka and a traditional message queue? A queue removes a message once consumed — one logical consumer. Kafka retains messages for a configured period; multiple independent consumer groups can each read the full stream at their own pace. How does Kafka achieve durability? Each partition is replicated across multiple brokers (replication factor, typically 3). A write is acknowledged once it's written to the leader and, with acks=all , replicated to enough followers. When would you NOT use Kafka? Low-volume task queues, simple background jobs, or when you need true exactly-once delivery to an external non-Kafka system without building idempotency yourself — a managed queue or workflow engine is simpler."},{"u":"topics/tech/postgresql.html","p":"PostgreSQL","i":"","h":"Overview","t":"Key Technologies PostgreSQL The default choice until a specific requirement forces a distributed store — vertical scaling, read replicas, and partitioning carry it further than most interviews assume. 15-20 min read Medium Say this in the first 2 minutes Default to Postgres unless a specific requirement forces a distributed DB — most systems never hit its ceiling B-tree is the default index; GIN is for full-text search and JSONB containment queries Streaming replication (physical, byte-for-byte) for read replicas; logical replication for selective, cross-version replication Sync replication guarantees zero data loss on failover at a latency cost; async is faster but risks losing the last unreplicated writes Don't reach for sharding until vertical scaling + read replicas + partitioning are exhausted — that ceiling is much higher than people assume"},{"u":"topics/tech/postgresql.html","p":"PostgreSQL","i":"mental-model","h":"Mental model","t":"Mental model Postgres is a single very capable office that you can keep expanding — more staff (read replicas), better filing (indexes), bigger filing cabinets (partitioning) — before you ever need to open a second office (sharding) with all the coordination overhead that implies."},{"u":"topics/tech/postgresql.html","p":"PostgreSQL","i":"core-concepts","h":"Indexing strategies","t":"Indexing strategies Index type Use case B-tree (default) Equality and range queries on ordered data — the default for almost every column you index GIN Full-text search (tsvector), JSONB containment queries ( @> ), array membership GiST Geometric data, range types, nearest-neighbor search Partial index Indexes only rows matching a WHERE clause (e.g. WHERE deleted_at IS NULL ) — smaller, faster for that subset Covering index Includes extra columns via INCLUDE so the query is answered from the index alone — avoids a heap fetch A partial index on WHERE status = 'pending' on a table where 95% of rows are 'completed' can be 20x smaller than a full index — and faster for the queries that actually filter on that condition."},{"u":"topics/tech/postgresql.html","p":"PostgreSQL","i":"how-it-works","h":"Replication: write path vs read path","t":"Replication: write path vs read path WRITE PATH App (write) Primary WAL streamed out streaming replication Read replica 1 async, ~10-50ms lag Read replica 2 sync, zero data loss READ PATH App (read) reads routed to replicas — primary handles writes only Primary — single writer, source of truth Replicas — scale reads, replay the write-ahead log (WAL) Replication type Behavior Streaming (physical) Byte-for-byte WAL replay — replica is an exact copy, must match Postgres major version Logical Replicates at the row/table level — supports selective replication and cross-version upgrades Synchronous Primary waits for replica ack before committing — zero data loss, higher write latency Asynchronous Primary commits immediately, replica catches up — lower latency, risks losing last writes on failover All writes go to the primary — single writer Single source of truth — all writes serialize here Replays the WAL — typically tens of ms behind primary with async replication Synchronous replica — primary waits for ack before committing zero data loss on failover Reads can be routed to any replica for horizontal read scale"},{"u":"topics/tech/postgresql.html","p":"PostgreSQL","i":"scaling-ceiling","h":"When Postgres scales \"enough\"","t":"When Postgres scales \"enough\" Default bias — stay on Postgres vs reach for a distributed DB Stick with Postgres Go distributed Vertical scaling first: modern hardware (64+ cores, TBs of RAM, NVMe) carries a single Postgres instance through tens of thousands of writes/sec for many workloads Read replicas next: offload read traffic horizontally — most systems are read-heavy, so this alone buys huge headroom Partitioning next: split a huge table by range (date) or hash on a single instance — keeps indexes small and vacuum fast without distributing anything Only then, sharding/distributed DB: reach for Citus, CockroachDB, or a NoSQL store when write volume genuinely exceeds one primary, or you need true multi-region active-active writes Common interview trap: jumping straight to \"we'll shard the database\" for a system that would comfortably run on a single well-tuned Postgres instance with replicas. Justify the jump with a specific number, not a vibe."},{"u":"topics/tech/postgresql.html","p":"PostgreSQL","i":"tradeoffs","h":"Tradeoffs","t":"Tradeoffs Wins Full ACID transactions, mature query planner, joins that actually work JSONB gives semi-structured flexibility without leaving SQL Enormous ecosystem — extensions, tooling, managed offerings everywhere Vertical + replica + partition scaling goes further than most systems need Costs Single-writer architecture — write throughput ultimately caps on one primary Multi-region active-active writes aren't native — needs an external solution Vacuum/bloat management requires ongoing operational attention Horizontal write scaling (sharding) isn't built-in — needs Citus or app-level sharding"},{"u":"topics/tech/postgresql.html","p":"PostgreSQL","i":"when-to-use","h":"When to use","t":"When to use ✓ USE: the default starting point for almost any system — relational data, need for transactions, unclear future access patterns. ✗ AVOID (alone): when you have a confirmed requirement for multi-region active-active writes or write volume that demonstrably exceeds single-primary capacity even after partitioning. ⚠ WATCH OUT: async replication means failover can lose the last few writes — if that's unacceptable, you need at least one synchronous replica, accepting the latency cost."},{"u":"topics/tech/postgresql.html","p":"PostgreSQL","i":"interview-moves","h":"Interview moves","t":"Interview moves 01 \"I'd default to Postgres here and only introduce a distributed store if a specific requirement — write volume or multi-region — forces it\" — the single highest-signal sentence in this whole topic 02 \"Before sharding, I'd exhaust read replicas and partitioning — those alone often carry a system another order of magnitude in scale\" — shows the scaling ladder, not a binary choice 03 \"For this JSONB column with containment queries, I'd add a GIN index rather than a B-tree, since B-tree can't efficiently support @> lookups\" — concrete indexing knowledge 04 \"Given we can't lose a single committed write here, I'd make at least one replica synchronous, accepting the added write latency\" — explicit tradeoff reasoning, not just a feature list"},{"u":"topics/tech/postgresql.html","p":"PostgreSQL","i":"follow-ups","h":"Common follow-up questions","t":"Common follow-up questions When would you actually need to shard Postgres? When sustained write throughput exceeds what a single well-tuned primary can handle even after partitioning — typically tens of thousands of writes/sec sustained, or a hard multi-region active-active write requirement. What's the difference between partitioning and sharding? Partitioning splits a table across multiple physical segments on the same instance (still one writer). Sharding splits data across multiple independent database instances/nodes. How do you decide between synchronous and asynchronous replication? Synchronous when zero data loss on failover is a hard requirement (financial transactions) — accept the latency cost. Asynchronous when slightly stale failover is acceptable for lower write latency. What is replication lag and how do you handle it? The delay between a write on the primary and its visibility on a replica. For read-your-writes needs, route that specific read to the primary or track a \"read after write\" hint to wait for replica catch-up. What's the difference between streaming and logical replication? Streaming replicates the entire WAL byte-for-byte (must match major version, used for HA/read replicas). Logical replicates row changes selectively, enabling cross-version replication and use with CDC tools like Debezium. How would you handle a table that's grown to hundreds of millions of rows? Range or hash partition it (e.g. by month for time-series data) to keep individual indexes and vacuum operations fast, before considering sharding across instances."},{"u":"topics/tech/redis.html","p":"Redis","i":"","h":"Overview","t":"Key Technologies Redis An in-memory data structure server — not just a cache. Strings, hashes, sorted sets, streams, persistence, and cluster sharding. 15-20 min read Medium Say this in the first 2 minutes Redis is a data structure server, not a generic cache — the structure you pick (ZSET, Hash, Stream) often eliminates app-side logic Single-threaded event loop means every command is atomic — no locking needed for single-key ops Persistence is opt-in and tunable: RDB for fast restarts, AOF for durability, or both Cluster mode shards via 16384 hash slots, not consistent hashing — rebalancing moves whole slots Cache stampede on a hot key expiring is a real failure mode — mitigate with jitter or locking, not just TTL"},{"u":"topics/tech/redis.html","p":"Redis","i":"mental-model","h":"Mental model","t":"Mental model Redis is RAM with a vocabulary. A plain key-value cache only knows GET and SET; Redis adds verbs — push, increment, intersect, rank — so the data itself carries structure instead of your application re-deriving it from a blob on every read."},{"u":"topics/tech/redis.html","p":"Redis","i":"core-concepts","h":"Data structures","t":"Data structures Structure Interview use case String Session token → user_id, short_code → long_url, simple counters via INCR Hash User profile object — set/get individual fields without serializing the whole blob List Recent activity feed, simple FIFO/LIFO task queue (LPUSH/RPOP) Set Unique visitor IDs, tag membership, blocked-user lookups (O(1) SISMEMBER) Sorted Set (ZSET) Leaderboards (score = points), rate limiters (score = timestamp), priority queues HyperLogLog Approximate unique counts (DAU, unique page views) in ~12KB regardless of cardinality Stream Append-only event log with consumer groups — lightweight Kafka alternative for smaller scale Cache-aside: hit vs miss The dominant Redis pattern in interviews. Watch both paths run side by side — the latency gap is the whole argument for caching. ✓ Cache hit — ~1ms App GET user:42 Redis key found ~1ms key exists → return immediately ▸ Replay ✗ Cache miss — ~10ms+ App GET user:42 Redis MISS SET back DB ~10ms+ miss → query DB → cache result → return ▸ Replay Eviction policies When Redis hits maxmemory , the eviction policy decides what gets dropped. Policy Behavior noeviction Reject writes with an error once memory is full — reads still work. Default; safest for data you can't lose. allkeys-lru Evict the least-recently-used key across the entire keyspace, regardless of TTL volatile-lru Evict least-recently-used key, but only among keys with a TTL set — keys without expiry are protected allkeys-lfu Evict least-frequently-used key — better than LRU for workloads with stable hot keys and occasional scans volatile-ttl Evict the key with the nearest expiration time first volatile-lru is the common production default: it protects keys you intentionally made permanent (e.g. config) while still reclaiming space from expiring cache entries. App requests GET user:42 In-memory key lookup — O(1) no disk I/O App requests GET user:42 Key not found — falls through to DB then SETs the result back Disk read / index scan — much slower than memory"},{"u":"topics/tech/redis.html","p":"Redis","i":"persistence","h":"Persistence: RDB vs AOF","t":"Persistence: RDB vs AOF RDB — point-in-time snapshot Compact single binary file, fast to load on restart Low runtime overhead — forks a child process to write Great for backups and fast cluster node recovery Data loss window: everything since the last snapshot (e.g. 5 min) AOF — append-only command log Logs every write command — replay on restart rebuilds exact state Configurable fsync: always (durable, slow), everysec (default, ~1s loss window), no Larger file, slower restart — needs periodic rewrite/compaction Much smaller data-loss window than RDB alone Production default is usually both: AOF for durability, RDB snapshots for fast full-cluster bootstrap and backups. Pure in-memory with no persistence is fine only if Redis is a pure cache backed by a real source of truth."},{"u":"topics/tech/redis.html","p":"Redis","i":"how-it-works","h":"Cluster mode — hash slot sharding","t":"Cluster mode — hash slot sharding CLIENT REQUEST — CRC16(key) mod 16384 picks the slot Client Master A slots 0 – 5460 Master B slots 5461 – 10922 Master C slots 10923 – 16383 async replication Replica A' standby Replica B' standby Replica C' standby Nodes gossip cluster state over a separate bus port — each node knows every slot owner without a central coordinator Masters — own a slot range, serve reads/writes Replicas — async copy, promoted on master failure Slot assignment: 16384 total hash slots are statically divided across master nodes at cluster setup Routing: client hashes the key (CRC16 mod 16384), connects to the master owning that slot directly — no proxy hop Replication: each master streams writes asynchronously to 1+ replicas for failover, not for read scaling by default Gossip + failover: nodes exchange heartbeats; if a master misses enough pings, the cluster votes and promotes its replica Resharding: adding/removing a node moves whole slot ranges, not individual keys — predictable but can be a multi-minute operation at scale Client computes the slot for the key and routes directly to the owning master Owns hash slots 0-5460. Handles all reads/writes for keys in this range. Owns hash slots 5461-10922. Owns hash slots 10923-16383. Replicates Master A. Gossip protocol detects A's failure and promotes this replica. Replicates Master B. Replicates Master C."},{"u":"topics/tech/redis.html","p":"Redis","i":"tradeoffs","h":"Tradeoffs","t":"Tradeoffs Wins Sub-millisecond latency — everything is in RAM Rich data structures eliminate app-side aggregation logic Atomic single-key operations with no external locking Pub/sub and Streams give you messaging for free Cluster mode scales horizontally with predictable sharding Costs Dataset must fit in RAM — expensive at large scale vs disk-based stores Single-threaded command execution — one slow command (e.g. KEYS *) blocks everything Without persistence, a crash loses all data Cluster mode loses multi-key transactions across different slots Operational overhead: failover, resharding, memory tuning"},{"u":"topics/tech/redis.html","p":"Redis","i":"when-to-use","h":"When to use","t":"When to use ✓ USE: sub-ms hot-path reads, leaderboards/ranking, rate limiting, session storage, pub/sub fan-out, or any workload that maps cleanly onto one of Redis's native structures. ✗ AVOID: as a primary system of record for data you can't afford to lose without persistence configured, or for datasets that don't fit in available RAM cost-effectively. ⚠ WATCH OUT: a single very hot key (celebrity user, trending item) can saturate one shard even with cluster mode — needs client-side local caching or key splitting."},{"u":"topics/tech/redis.html","p":"Redis","i":"interview-moves","h":"Interview moves","t":"Interview moves 01 \"I'd justify Redis over Memcached here because we need a ZSET for the leaderboard\" — Memcached is pure key-value; Redis's structures often remove a whole layer of app logic 02 \"I'll enable AOF with everysec fsync so we lose at most ~1 second of writes on a crash\" — shows you know persistence is opt-in and tunable, not automatic 03 \"To avoid a cache stampede when this key expires, I'd add jitter to the TTL or use a mutex lock so only one request repopulates it\" — a senior-level failure mode most candidates miss 04 \"Cluster mode shards by hash slot, not consistent hashing — resharding moves whole slot ranges, which is more predictable but coarser-grained\" — shows real depth beyond \"Redis can cluster\" 05 \"Single-threaded means I need to avoid O(N) commands like KEYS or large SMEMBERS in the hot path — I'd use SCAN instead\" — operational awareness"},{"u":"topics/tech/redis.html","p":"Redis","i":"follow-ups","h":"Common follow-up questions","t":"Common follow-up questions What is a cache stampede and how do you prevent it? Many requests hit a DB simultaneously when a hot key expires. Prevent with a short-lived lock (only one request repopulates) or TTL jitter so keys don't all expire at the same instant. Why is Redis single-threaded, and is that a problem? Avoids locking overhead and context switches for in-memory ops, which is faster for typical small commands. It's a problem only for O(N) commands on large collections — use SCAN/pagination instead. How does Redis Cluster differ from Redis Sentinel? Sentinel provides HA/failover for a single primary-replica set with no sharding. Cluster mode adds sharding across the 16384 hash slots plus its own failover via gossip — use Cluster when data exceeds one node's RAM. Can you do transactions in Redis? MULTI/EXEC queues commands and executes them atomically, but without rollback on individual command failure. In cluster mode, all keys in a transaction must hash to the same slot (use hash tags {tag} ). How would you implement a rate limiter with Redis? Sliding window with a ZSET: score = request timestamp, trim entries older than the window, count remaining. Or simpler token bucket via INCR + EXPIRE for fixed windows. What happens on a Redis Cluster master failure? Other masters detect missed heartbeats via gossip, reach quorum, and promote the failed master's replica. Writes to that slot range fail for the brief detection+failover window (typically a few seconds)."},{"u":"topics/tech/zookeeper.html","p":"ZooKeeper","i":"","h":"Overview","t":"Key Technologies ZooKeeper A coordination service for distributed systems — leader election, distributed config, and service discovery, built on a simple hierarchical znode tree. 12-18 min read Medium Say this in the first 2 minutes ZooKeeper's value isn't the database — it's the coordination primitives: leader election, distributed locks, config propagation Leader election uses ephemeral sequential znodes — lowest sequence number wins, and the watch mechanism notifies the next-in-line on failure Ephemeral nodes disappear automatically when a client disconnects — that's the basis for liveness-based service discovery Watches let clients react to changes (config update, node death) without polling It's increasingly replaced by etcd/Consul in newer systems, but the coordination patterns it embodies are exactly what interviewers want you to reason through — name ZooKeeper or etcd, the pattern is what matters"},{"u":"topics/tech/zookeeper.html","p":"ZooKeeper","i":"mental-model","h":"Mental model","t":"Mental model ZooKeeper is a shared bulletin board with a strict filing system: everyone can post a note in a folder, see who else posted, and get tapped on the shoulder the instant something changes — which turns \"who's in charge right now\" into a simple, race-free read of who has the lowest ticket number."},{"u":"topics/tech/zookeeper.html","p":"ZooKeeper","i":"core-concepts","h":"Znode tree and node types","t":"Znode tree and node types Znode type Behavior Persistent Exists until explicitly deleted — used for stable config and structure Ephemeral Automatically deleted when the creating client's session disconnects — the basis for liveness detection Sequential ZooKeeper appends a monotonically increasing counter to the name — guarantees a global, gapless ordering Ephemeral + Sequential Combines both — the standard building block for leader election and distributed locks Watch A one-time trigger a client sets on a znode — fires on change, client must re-register to keep watching"},{"u":"topics/tech/zookeeper.html","p":"ZooKeeper","i":"how-it-works","h":"Leader election via ephemeral sequential znodes","t":"Leader election via ephemeral sequential znodes /election persistent znode /election/n_000001 LEADER — lowest seq /election/n_000002 watches n_000001 /election/n_000003 watches n_000002 each candidate watches only its immediate predecessor If the leader's client disconnects → n_000001 is ephemeral → auto-deleted n_000002's watch fires → it checks: am I now lowest? → becomes leader No central coordinator decides — each node self-checks its rank Current leader — lowest sequence number under /election Candidates — watch only their immediate predecessor Register: each candidate creates an ephemeral sequential znode under a shared parent (e.g. /election/n_ ) Compare: a candidate lists its siblings; if its own sequence number is lowest, it's the leader Watch the predecessor, not the leader: every other candidate sets a watch only on the znode immediately before its own — this avoids every node waking up on every change (the \"herd effect\") Failover: if the leader's session disconnects (crash, network partition), its ephemeral znode is automatically deleted Promote: the watch on the deleted znode fires for the next-in-line candidate, which re-checks its rank and becomes leader if it's now lowest Persistent parent znode — the election 'room' all candidates register under Lowest sequence number among siblings — this client becomes leader Watches the znode immediately before it (n_000001) not the leader directly — avoids a herd effect Watches n_000002 the node just before it in sequence On reconnect or restart the old session's ephemeral znodes are already gone"},{"u":"topics/tech/zookeeper.html","p":"ZooKeeper","i":"patterns","h":"Other coordination patterns","t":"Other coordination patterns Pattern How ZooKeeper implements it Distributed configuration Config stored in a persistent znode; clients set a watch and get notified instantly when it changes — no polling Service discovery Each service instance registers an ephemeral znode on startup — it vanishes automatically if the instance crashes or loses connectivity, giving accurate liveness without heartbeats to a separate system Distributed lock Same ephemeral-sequential pattern as leader election — lowest sequence number holds the lock, releases by disconnecting or deleting its znode Barrier / group membership Clients register under a shared znode; the count of children tells every member who else is currently part of the group"},{"u":"topics/tech/zookeeper.html","p":"ZooKeeper","i":"tradeoffs","h":"Tradeoffs","t":"Tradeoffs Wins Battle-tested, strongly consistent coordination primitives Watch mechanism avoids polling for config/leadership changes Ephemeral nodes give accurate liveness detection for free Underpins major systems (Kafka historically, HBase, Hadoop) Costs Operationally heavy — JVM-based, needs an odd-numbered quorum (3, 5) of dedicated nodes Not designed for high-volume data storage — small values only, coordination metadata etcd (Raft, simpler ops model, gRPC API) has displaced it in many newer systems (Kubernetes uses etcd, not ZK) Kafka itself removed its ZooKeeper dependency (KRaft mode) — a sign of the broader trend"},{"u":"topics/tech/zookeeper.html","p":"ZooKeeper","i":"when-to-use","h":"When to use","t":"When to use ✓ USE: any distributed system needing leader election, distributed locks, or coordinated config — whether via ZooKeeper, etcd, or Consul, the pattern is the point. ✗ AVOID: as a general-purpose data store — it's built for small amounts of coordination metadata, not application data at volume. ⚠ WATCH OUT: a watch only fires once — if you don't re-register it immediately after handling the event, you can miss the next change (a classic correctness bug)."},{"u":"topics/tech/zookeeper.html","p":"ZooKeeper","i":"interview-moves","h":"Interview moves","t":"Interview moves 01 \"I wouldn't hand-roll leader election with a database row lock — I'd reach for ZooKeeper or etcd, since getting this correct under network partitions is genuinely hard\" — the core justification for using a coordination service at all 02 \"Each candidate watches only its immediate predecessor, not the whole election znode — that avoids a thundering herd of every node waking up on every leadership change\" — shows real depth on the mechanism, not just the term 03 \"Service instances register an ephemeral znode on startup — if one crashes, that znode disappears automatically, giving us liveness without a separate heartbeat system\" — connects the primitive to a concrete use case 04 \"I'd mention etcd is the more common modern choice — Kubernetes uses it — but the coordination pattern, ephemeral-node-based leader election, is identical to what ZooKeeper pioneered\" — shows you're current without dismissing the underlying concept"},{"u":"topics/tech/zookeeper.html","p":"ZooKeeper","i":"follow-ups","h":"Common follow-up questions","t":"Common follow-up questions Why use ephemeral sequential znodes specifically for leader election, instead of just ephemeral? Sequential gives a deterministic, race-free ordering (lowest number wins) without any node needing to coordinate who goes first — pure sequential ephemeral alone can't resolve ties between simultaneous registrations. What happens during a network partition? ZooKeeper uses ZAB (a Paxos-like consensus protocol) requiring a quorum majority to make progress — the minority partition can't elect a new leader or accept writes, avoiding split-brain. How is ZooKeeper different from etcd? etcd uses Raft (generally considered easier to reason about than ZAB), exposes a gRPC/HTTP API instead of a custom protocol, and has become the default in cloud-native systems like Kubernetes. Why did Kafka remove its ZooKeeper dependency? KRaft mode moved metadata management into Kafka itself using a Raft-based quorum of brokers, removing the operational burden of running and scaling a separate ZooKeeper cluster. How would you implement a distributed lock with ZooKeeper? Same ephemeral-sequential pattern as leader election: create a sequential znode, you hold the lock if you're lowest, watch your predecessor, and the lock releases automatically if your session dies. What's the \"herd effect\" and how does ZooKeeper avoid it? If every client watched the same znode, one change would wake up and re-trigger logic in all of them simultaneously. Watching only your immediate predecessor means a change wakes at most one other client."}];
